{"id": "direct_ft_train_00001", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS AND THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is false: the White box has only one statement, so it cannot have at least two false statements. Thus the Black box is all-false.\n\nSince at least one box must be all-true, at least one of B1 or W1 must be true.\n\n- If the gems were in BLACK, W1 would be false because the White box does not contain the gems. Then B1 would have to be true, but Blue would be the only all-true box and would not contain the gems, contradiction.\n- If the gems were in WHITE, W1 could be true or false, but both lead to contradictions: if W1 is false, B1 would need to be true while Blue lacks the gems; if W1 is true, B1 cannot consistently describe a unique all-true box containing the gems.\n- Therefore the gems must be in BLUE.\n\nWith gems in BLUE, W1 is false because the White box does not contain the gems. K1 is false. So B1 must be true, and indeed Blue is the only all-true box and contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.5, "ast_prob": 0.454, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00002", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE OR THE BLACK BOX IS BLACK\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "- The BLACK box is black, so the second part of W1 is true. Therefore W1 is true.\n- WHITE has only statement W1, so WHITE is a true box.\n- “A box next to the BLUE box is true” means WHITE is true, so K1 is true.\n- Thus BLACK is also a true box.\n- There must be at least one all-false box, so BLUE must be the false box.\n- Therefore B1 must be false, meaning the gems cannot be in a true box; since BLUE is the only false box, the gems must be in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.55, "ast_prob": 0.193, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00003", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS WHITE AND THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The WHITE box is the middle box and is white, so W1 (“The white box is blue”) is false.  \nThe BLACK box is the right box, not the middle box, so K1 (“The black box is the middle box”) is false.  \n\nSince at least one box must be all true, and W1 and K1 are false, the BLUE box must be the all-true box. Therefore B1 is true.  \nB1 says the white box is white (true) and the box with no false statements has the gems. The only box with no false statements is BLUE, so BLUE must contain the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.26, "ast_prob": 0.374, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00004", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE WHITE BOX HAS AT MOST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EITHER EXACTLY THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE OR THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the three statements be B1, W1, K1.\n\n- W1 says it is not true that the White box has at most one true statement. Since White contains only W1, it can have at most one true statement in every possible case, so W1’s claim is false. Thus **W1 = F**.\n- B1 is true exactly when Blue contains the gems.\n- K1 says either all three statements are false, or the box with two false statements contains the gems. But no box has two false statements because each box has exactly one statement. So K1 reduces to “all three statements are false.”\n  - If Blue did not contain the gems, then B1 = F and W1 = F, so K1 would be true exactly if K1 were false — a contradiction.\n  - Therefore Blue must contain the gems, making B1 = T.\n- With B1 = T, it is not true that all three statements are false, so K1 = F.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.722, "ast_prob": 0.327, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00005", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE NOT IN THE BLACK BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Black is the rightmost box, so the only box next to it is WHITE.  \nThus B1 and K1 both mean “the gems are in WHITE.”  \nW1 means “the gems are not in BLACK.”\n\n- If gems were in WHITE: B1=T, W1=T, K1=T. No box is all-false → violates rules.\n- If gems were in BLACK: B1=F, W1=F, K1=F. No box is all-true → violates rules.\n- If gems were in BLUE: B1=F, W1=T, K1=F. WHITE is all-true; BLUE and BLACK are all-false → satisfies all rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.609, "ast_prob": 0.453, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00006", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the boxes each contain one statement, so no box can have two false statements. Therefore W1's claim that “the box with two false statements has the gems” is false.\n\nK1 says “this box is the middle box,” but BLACK is the right box, so K1 is false.\n\nIf the gems were in WHITE or BLACK, then no box would have all statements true: BLUE would be false, WHITE would be false because no box has two false statements, and BLACK would also be false. This violates the rule that at least one box has all statements true.\n\nTherefore the gems must be in BLUE, making B1 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.35, "ast_prob": 0.145, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00007", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is false because the BLACK box is on the right, not the middle.  \nB1 refers to “the box with two false statements,” but each box has only one statement, so no such box exists; therefore B1 is false.  \nW1 is true only if the gems are in the BLUE box, because then the WHITE and BLACK boxes are both empty.  \nSince at least one box must be all-true, and both B1 and K1 are false, W1 must be true.  \nThus the gems must be in the BLUE box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.275, "ast_prob": 0.227, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00008", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EXACTLY TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(B1, W1, K1\\) be the truth values of the three statements.\n\n- \\(W1\\) says “the top statement of each box is true,” meaning \\(B1 \\land W1 \\land K1\\).  \n  If \\(W1\\) were true, then \\(B1\\) and \\(K1\\) would also be true, giving 3 true statements. But then \\(K1\\) (“exactly two statements are true”) would be false, contradiction.  \n  So \\(W1 = F\\).\n\n- With \\(W1\\) false, at least one of \\(B1,K1\\) must be true because at least one box is all-true.\n\n- \\(K1\\) says exactly two statements are true. Since \\(W1\\) is false, \\(K1\\) can be true only if \\(B1\\) is also true.\n\n- If \\(B1\\) were false, then \\(W1=F\\) and \\(K1=F\\), so no statement would be true, violating the “at least one all-true” rule.  \n  Therefore \\(B1 = T\\).\n\n- Now \\(B1=T\\) and \\(W1=F\\). If \\(K1\\) were true, both Blue and Black would have no false statements, so there would be two “boxes with no false statements.” But \\(B1\\) refers to “THE box with no false statements,” requiring uniqueness. So \\(K1\\) cannot be true; hence \\(K1=F\\).\n\nThus \\(B1=T\\), \\(W1=F\\), \\(K1=F\\). The only all-true box is Blue, and \\(B1\\) says that box has the gems, so the gems are in Blue.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.724, "ast_prob": 0.394, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00009", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS WHITE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- B1 = “The top statement of each box is true” → B1 is true only if B1, W1, and K1 are all true.\n- W1 = “The other two boxes relative to the Blue box are empty” → means White and Black are empty, so the gems are in Blue.\n- K1 = “The Blue box is white” → false, because the Blue box is Blue.\n\nIf K1 is false, then B1 cannot be true, because B1 would require K1 to be true.  \nSo B1 = F.\n\nSince at least one statement must be true, W1 must be true.  \nW1 = T means the White and Black boxes are empty, so the gems must be in the Blue box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.391, "ast_prob": 0.267, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00010", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "- K1 says \"The BLACK box is the middle box.\" The BLACK box is actually rightmost, so **K1 = F**. Thus the BLACK box is all-false.\n\n- B1 says the other two boxes relative to BLUE are empty. Since exactly one box has gems, this is true exactly when the gems are in **BLUE**. So **B1 = (gem = BLUE)**.\n\n- W1 says a box next to WHITE is true. WHITE’s neighbors are BLUE and BLACK. BLACK is not true because K1 is false. So W1 is true exactly when BLUE is true, which is exactly when B1 is true.\n\n- If the gems were in WHITE or BLACK, then B1 would be false, BLUE would be false, W1 would be false, and WHITE would be false. Then all three boxes would be false, violating the rule that at least one box is all-true.\n\n- Therefore gems must be in BLUE. Then B1 = T and W1 = T, while K1 = F.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.617, "ast_prob": 0.296, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00011", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 says “the statement above this one is true,” but there is no statement above B1, so B1 is false.\n\nSince K1 says “the top statement of each box is true,” K1 would require B1, W1, and K1 all to be true. But B1 is false, so K1 is false.\n\nNow we have B1 = false and K1 = false. At least one box must be all-true, so W1 must be true. W1 says “the blue box contains the gems,” so the gems are in the BLUE box.\n\nThis gives: BLUE all-false, WHITE all-true, BLACK all-false, satisfying the rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.678, "ast_prob": 0.188, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00012", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY OR THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Each box has only one statement, so a box is “all true” if its single statement is true, and “all false” if its single statement is false.\n\n- K1 says the black box has exactly two false statements, but the black box has only one statement, so K1 cannot be true. Thus K1 = F.\n- Therefore, at least one of B1 or W1 must be true.\n- W1 is true exactly when the gems are in the blue box.\n- B1 says the other two boxes are empty OR the blue box contains gems. Since exactly one box contains gems, “the other two boxes are empty” is equivalent to “the blue box contains the gems.” So B1 is true exactly when the gems are in the blue box.\n- If the gems were not in the blue box, both B1 and W1 would be false, and K1 is already false, leaving no true box. So the gems must be in the blue box.\n- Then B1 = T, W1 = T, and K1 = F.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.597, "ast_prob": 0.152, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00013", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS WHITE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n\n1. The WHITE box is in the middle, so W1 (“This box is the middle box”) is TRUE.\n2. The middle box is white, so B1 (“The white box is white”) is TRUE.\n3. At least one statement must be FALSE. Since B1 and W1 are both true, K1 must be FALSE.\n4. K1 says “The blue box is empty.” If K1 is false, the blue box is NOT empty.\n5. Since exactly one box contains the gems, the gems must be in the BLUE box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.289, "ast_prob": 0.335, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00014", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Each box has only one statement, so “all true” means that statement is true, and “all false” means that statement is false.\n\nLet:\n- B1 = truth of BLUE statement\n- W1 = truth of WHITE statement\n- K1 = truth of BLACK statement\n\n- B1 says “A box next to the BLUE box is true.” The only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so B1 = W1.\n- W1 says “The top statement of each box is true,” meaning B1, W1, and K1 are all true. So W1 is true only if B1 and K1 are true: W1 = B1 ∧ K1.\n- K1 says “The BLUE box contains the gems,” so K1 = (gems in BLUE).\n\nCombining B1 = W1 and W1 = B1 ∧ K1 gives only possible truth patterns:\n- B1=F, W1=F, K1=F\n- B1=F, W1=F, K1=T\n- B1=T, W1=T, K1=T\n\nAt least one box must be true and at least one must be false, so the only valid pattern is B1=F, W1=F, K1=T.\n\nThus the gems are in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.38, "ast_prob": 0.397, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00015", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 says both boxes next to White (Blue and Black) contain gems. Since exactly one box contains gems, K1 must be false.\n\nW1 says every box’s top statement is true. But K1 is false, so W1 cannot be true; therefore W1 is false.\n\nAt least one statement must be true, and the only remaining candidate is B1, so B1 must be true.\n\nB1 says the gems are in a box that is true. Since W1 and K1 are false, only the Blue box can be the true box containing the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.595, "ast_prob": 0.482, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00016", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX HAS AT MOST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n\n1. Each box has exactly one statement, so a “true box” means its statement is true, and a “false box” means its statement is false.\n\n2. W1 says the BLACK box has at most two true statements. The BLACK box has only one statement, K1, so it can have 0 or 1 true statements — both are at most 2. Thus W1 is always true.\n\n3. K1 says a box next to the BLACK box is true. The only box next to BLACK is WHITE. Since W1 is true, WHITE is true, so K1 is true.\n\n4. Now W1 = T and K1 = T. The rules require at least one all-false box, so BLUE must be the false box. Therefore B1 = F.\n\n5. B1 says the gems are in a true box. Since B1 is false, the gems are not in a true box. The only false box is BLUE, so the gems are in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.622, "ast_prob": 0.468, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00017", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is true because the White box is indeed the middle box.  \nK1 says all statements on the White box are true, and since W1 is true, K1 is also true.  \nThus both White and Black boxes are all-true.  \nThe rules require at least one all-false box, so the Blue box must be all-false, meaning B1 is false.  \nB1 says the gems are in a box that is true. The true boxes are White and Black, so if B1 is false, the gems must be in the Blue box, which is false.  \nThis makes everything consistent.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.521, "ast_prob": 0.174, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00018", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT MOST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS BLACK\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n\n1. The WHITE box contains only W1. Since one statement cannot be more than two true statements, W1 must be true. If W1 were false, the WHITE box would have 0 true statements, making “at most two true statements” true — contradiction.\n2. The BLACK box is black, so K1 is true.\n3. At least one box must be entirely false. Since WHITE and BLACK are already true boxes, the BLUE box must be the all-false box. Therefore B1 must be false.\n4. B1 says “THIS BOX IS EMPTY.” Since B1 is false, the BLUE box is not empty. With exactly one box containing gems, the gems must be in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.664, "ast_prob": 0.155, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00019", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE AND BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(G_B, G_W, G_K\\) mean the gems are in Blue, White, or Black.\n\n- Since Black is the rightmost box, the only box next to it is White. So:\n  - \\(K1\\) is true iff the gems are in White: \\(K1 = G_W\\).\n- \\(B1\\) says Black is empty: \\(B1 = \\neg G_K\\).\n- \\(W1\\) says: “The gems are in a false box, and boxes next to Black contain gems.” So \\(W1 =\\) (gem box’s statement is false) \\(\\land K1\\).\n\nCheck cases:\n\n1. Gems in Black:  \n   \\(B1 = F\\), \\(K1 = F\\), \\(W1 = F\\). All three false, violating “at least one all-true.”\n\n2. Gems in White:  \n   \\(K1 = T\\). But then “gems in a false box” would require \\(W1\\) to be false, making \\(W1 = \\neg W1\\), impossible.\n\n3. Gems in Blue:  \n   \\(B1 = T\\), \\(K1 = F\\), \\(W1 = F\\). This satisfies exactly one gem box, at least one true box, and at least one false box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.445, "ast_prob": 0.158, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00020", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Each box has exactly one statement, so a box is “true” iff its single statement is true, and “false” iff its single statement is false.\n\n- K1 says “the box with two false statements has the gems.” But no box has two statements, so no box can have two false statements. Therefore K1 is false.\n- W1 says “a box next to the white box is true.” The boxes next to white are BLUE and BLACK, so W1 = B1 OR K1. Since K1 is false, W1 = B1.\n- At least one statement must be true; K1 is false, so B1 must be true. That also makes W1 true.\n- Thus BLUE contains the gems. K1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.758, "ast_prob": 0.311, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00021", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE OR THIS BOX HAS EXACTLY ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Black is the rightmost box, so the only box next to it is WHITE. Thus K1 is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.\n\nLet T mean “the box containing the gems is all-true.”\n\n- B1 says T.\n- W1 says: T OR “WHITE has exactly one false statement.”\n  Since WHITE contains only W1, “exactly one false statement” is equivalent to W1 being false.\n  So W1 = T OR (not W1). If W1 were false, then the right side would be true, contradiction. Therefore W1 is true, and T is true.\n\nSo the gems must be in a true box.\n\nTest gem locations:\n- BLACK: impossible, because K1 would be false, so BLACK would not be true.\n- WHITE: K1 true, W1 true, and B1 true because WHITE is true. Then all three boxes would be true, leaving no all-false box.\n- BLUE: B1 true makes BLUE true, W1 true because the gems are in a true box, and K1 false makes BLACK all-false. This satisfies all rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.721, "ast_prob": 0.486, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00022", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO FALSE STATEMENTS AND ALL STATEMENTS ON THIS BOX ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 must be false: the White box has only one statement, so it cannot have “exactly two false statements,” and the self-reference cannot make that false conjunct true.\n\nTherefore the Black box is all-false.\n\nW1 is also false: with exactly one gem, the two boxes next to White cannot both contain gems.\n\nSo the only possible all-true box is Blue. For Blue to be all-true, B1 must be true, meaning the other two boxes are empty, so the gems must be in Blue.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.635, "ast_prob": 0.5, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00023", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS AND THE WHITE BOX HAS EXACTLY ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE OR THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 refers to a statement above it on the White box, but the White box contains only W1 itself, so there is no such above statement. Thus “the statement above this one is true” is false, making W1 = false or false = false.\n\nSince W1 is false, the White box has zero true statements, so the White-box part of B1 is false. Therefore B1 is false regardless of gem location.\n\nWe need at least one all-true box, and B1 and W1 are already false, so K1 must be true. K1 says the Blue box contains the gems, so the gems are in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.436, "ast_prob": 0.427, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00024", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS AND THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is false, because “boxes next to the white box” are BLUE and BLACK, and exactly one box can contain the gems—so both cannot contain gems.\n\nSince K1 is false, B1 (“the top statement of each box is true”) is also false, because K1 is one of the top statements and is not true.\n\nAt least one box must be all-true. BLUE and BLACK are already all-false, so WHITE must be all-true. Therefore W1 is true, meaning the BLUE box contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.725, "ast_prob": 0.336, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00025", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX HAS EXACTLY ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is the only statement in the WHITE box, so there is no statement above it inside that box. Therefore, “the statement above this one is true” is false, making W1 true.\n\nSince WHITE has exactly one true statement (W1), B1 is true.\n\nBLUE is then all-true. To satisfy the rule that at least one box is all-false, BLACK must be all-false, so K1 must be false.\n\nK1 says “The BLUE box is empty.” Since K1 is false, the BLUE box is not empty, so the gems are in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.661, "ast_prob": 0.241, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00026", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS OR THE BLACK BOX HAS EXACTLY ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER THIS BOX HAS AT MOST ONE TRUE STATEMENT OR A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT LEAST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "We have one statement per box, so a box is true if its statement is true, false if its statement is false.\n\n- W1 says: “Either this box has at most one true statement or a box next to this box is true.” Since WHITE has only one statement, it always has at most one true statement. Therefore W1 is always TRUE.\n\n- K1 says: “At least two statements are false.” Since W1 is true, at least two false statements would require both B1 and K1 to be false. But if K1 is false, then at least two statements are not false, so K1 cannot be false. Thus K1 must be FALSE.\n\n- Since K1 is false and W1 is true, there is exactly one false statement so far. To keep K1 false, we cannot have B1 also false, so B1 must be TRUE.\n\n- B1 says: “Either this box contains the gems or the black box has exactly one true statement.” We already know K1 is false, so “black box has exactly one true statement” is false. Therefore the first part must be true: BLUE contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.698, "ast_prob": 0.266, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00027", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] AT MOST THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "There are 3 statements total, so “at most three statements are false” is always true. Thus B1 = T.\n\nSince the Blue box contains only B1, and B1 is true, W1 (“All statements on the Blue box are true”) is also true. Thus W1 = T.\n\nBlue and White are therefore all-true boxes. To satisfy the rule that at least one box is all-false, the Black box must be all-false, so K1 = F.\n\nK1 = “The Blue box is empty” is false, meaning the Blue box is not empty. Since exactly one box contains the gems, the gems must be in the Blue box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.281, "ast_prob": 0.322, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00028", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS NOT EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 refers to a statement above it, but there is no statement above it, so B1 is false.  \nW1 says the white box is blue, but the white box is not the blue box, so W1 is false.  \nK1 says the blue box is not empty, which is true exactly when the gems are in the BLUE box.  \n\nSince at least one box must have all statements true, and B1 and W1 are both false, K1 must be true. Therefore the gems are in the BLUE box.  \nThis satisfies the rules: BLUE is all-false, WHITE is all-false, and BLACK is all-true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.735, "ast_prob": 0.333, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00031", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT LEAST THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the three statements be B1, W1, and K1.\n\n- K1 says “At least three statements are false.” Since there are exactly 3 statements total, K1 being true would require K1 itself to be false, a contradiction. Therefore K1 = F.\n- B1 says “The statement above this one is true,” but there is no statement above B1, so B1 cannot be true. Therefore B1 = F.\n- Since at least one box must have all statements true, and both B1 and K1 are false, W1 must be true. Therefore W1 = T.\n- W1 says the other two boxes relative to BLUE are empty, meaning WHITE and BLACK are empty. This is true only if the gems are in BLUE.\n- Check: BLUE is all-false, WHITE is all-true, BLACK is all-false, satisfying the global rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.612, "ast_prob": 0.168, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00032", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS WHITE AND THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO FALSE STATEMENTS AND THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EITHER THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS OR THIS BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because “the blue box is white” is impossible: the left box is blue, so the first part of the conjunction is false, making the whole statement false.\n\nW1 is false because the black box contains only one statement, K1, so it cannot have exactly two false statements. Thus W1’s first conjunct is false, making W1 false.\n\nK1 says: “Either the blue box contains the gems or this box has at least two true statements.” Since the black box has only one statement, “this box has at least two true statements” is false. So K1 is true exactly when the blue box contains the gems.\n\nAt least one box must be all-true, and B1 and W1 are both false, so K1 must be true. Therefore the blue box must contain the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.467, "ast_prob": 0.415, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00033", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLACK BOX ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(W1\\) be equivalent to \\(K1\\), since the only statement on the BLACK box is \\(K1\\).\n\n- If \\(K1\\) were true, then \\(W1\\) would also be true. To satisfy “at least one box all false,” \\(B1\\) would have to be false. Then there would be only one false statement total, so no box could have two false statements, making \\(K1\\) false—contradiction.  \n- Therefore \\(K1 = F\\), so \\(W1 = F\\).  \n- Since \\(W1\\) and \\(K1\\) are false, at least one box must be all true, so \\(B1\\) must be true.  \n- \\(B1\\) being true means the WHITE and BLACK boxes are empty, so the gems must be in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.691, "ast_prob": 0.286, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00034", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 and K1 both say “the blue box is the middle box,” but the blue box is on the left, so both statements are false.\n\nSo B1 = F and K1 = F.\n\nWe need at least one statement true. The only possible true statement is W1, which says the blue box contains the gems. Therefore the gems must be in the blue box, making W1 = T.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.217, "ast_prob": 0.11, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00035", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let W1 and K1 have the same truth value:  \n- W1 says “The Black box is true,” which is exactly K1.  \n- K1 says “W1 is true,” so K1 is true iff W1 is true.  \nThus W1 = K1.\n\nSince at least one statement must be true and at least one must be false, B1 must have the opposite truth value from W1/K1.\n\nIf W1 = K1 = T, then both White and Black have no false statements, so “THE box with no false statements” would not be unique. That makes B1 lack a determinate truth value under the puzzle’s wording, so this case is invalid.\n\nTherefore B1 = T and W1 = K1 = F. The only box with no false statements is Blue, so Blue has the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.755, "ast_prob": 0.102, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00036", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT LEAST THREE STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let  \n- B1 = “The other two boxes are empty” → true iff the gems are in BLUE.  \n- W1 = “The top statement of each box is true” → true iff B1, W1, and K1 are all true.  \n- K1 = “At least three statements are true” → true iff all three statements B1, W1, and K1 are true.\n\nSo W1 and K1 have the same truth condition: they are true only if all three statements are true.  \nIf B1 were false, then W1 and K1 would both be false, making every statement false, violating the rule that at least one box has all statements true.  \nTherefore B1 must be true, meaning the gems are in BLUE.\n\nWith B1 true, W1 and K1 could be both true or both false. But both true would leave no false statement, violating the rule that at least one box has all statements false.  \nThus W1 and K1 must both be false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.746, "ast_prob": 0.195, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00037", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EXACTLY THREE STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the three statements be B1, W1, and K1.\n\n- K1 says “Exactly three statements are true.” But the rules require at least one box to have all statements false, so at least one statement must be false. Therefore K1 cannot be true: K1 = F.\n- B1 says “The top statement of each box is true,” meaning B1, W1, and K1 would all have to be true. Since K1 is false, B1 must also be false: B1 = F.\n- At least one box must have all statements true. Since B1 and K1 are false, the all-true box must be WHITE, so W1 = T.\n- W1 says “The BLUE box contains the gems,” so the gems are in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.388, "ast_prob": 0.5, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00038", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE AND THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- Blue = left, White = middle, Black = right.\n- A “true box” means all statements on that box are true.\n- K1: “The blue box is the middle box” is false because Blue is left, not middle.\n\nSo Black has only K1, and K1 is false, so Black is an all-false box.\n\nLet G = “Blue contains the gems.”\nThen:\n- W1 = G\n- White is all-true iff W1 is true iff G is true.\n- B1 = “A box next to Blue is true AND Blue contains the gems” = “White is true AND G” = G ∧ G = G.\n\nSo B1 = G and W1 = G.\n\nWe need at least one all-true box. If G were false, then B1 and W1 would both be false, so Blue and White would both be all-false, and Black is already all-false. That would leave no all-true box, violating the rules.\n\nThus G must be true:\n- Blue contains the gems.\n- W1 = T\n- B1 = T\n- K1 = F\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.341, "ast_prob": 0.402, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00040", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE BLUE BOX IS BLACK\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EITHER THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX OR THE BLACK BOX IS WHITE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is true: the BLUE box is not black.  \nK1 is true: the WHITE box is the middle box.  \n\nSo WHITE and BLACK are true boxes. The rules require at least one all-false box, so BLUE must be false. Therefore B1 is false: the gems are not in a true box. Since WHITE and BLACK are true, the only false box is BLUE, so the gems must be in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.359, "ast_prob": 0.25, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00041", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS BLUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is true exactly when the gems are in the BLUE box, because then both other boxes are empty.  \nW1 is false, since the BLACK box is black, not blue.  \nK1 is false, since the BLACK box is the right box, not the middle box.  \n\nIf the gems were in WHITE or BLACK, then B1 would be false, and W1 and K1 would also be false, making all three boxes all-false and violating the rule that at least one box must be all-true.  \nSo the gems must be in BLUE, making B1 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.392, "ast_prob": 0.112, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00042", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT AT MOST TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY OR THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT AT MOST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let B1, W1, K1 be the truth values of the three statements.\n\n- K1 says “it is not true that at most two statements are false,” meaning all three statements would have to be false. If K1 were true, then K1 itself would be false, a contradiction. So K1 = F.\n- B1 says “it is not true that at most two statements are true,” meaning all three statements would have to be true. If B1 were true, then K1 would also have to be true, which is impossible. So B1 = F.\n- Since at least one statement must be true and both B1 and K1 are false, W1 must be true.\n- W1 is true, and its second part “the statement above this one is true” refers to B1, which is false. Therefore its first part must be true: the other two boxes relative to Blue are empty, meaning White and Black are empty.\n- Since exactly one box contains the gems, the gems must be in Blue.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.473, "ast_prob": 0.496, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00043", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT LEAST THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because the blue box is on the left, not the middle.\n\nW1 says at least three statements are false. Since there are exactly three statements total, this would require all three to be false, including W1 itself, which is impossible. So W1 is false.\n\nBlue and White boxes are therefore all-false boxes. To satisfy the rule that at least one box is all-true, the Black box must be all-true, so K1 is true.\n\nK1 says the other two boxes relative to the blue box are empty. For K1 to be true, White and Black must both be empty, so the gems must be in Blue.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.254, "ast_prob": 0.202, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00045", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 cannot be true, because then no box would have two false statements, W1 would be false, K1 would be false, and the gem location would be unconstrained — so the puzzle would not be uniquely solvable.\n\nTherefore B1 is false, meaning the statement above B1 is false. That makes the Blue box the box with two false statements. For W1 to be true, that box must contain the gems, so the gems are in Blue. Then W1 is true, which makes the White box true, so K1 is also true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.534, "ast_prob": 0.283, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00046", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location be BLUE, WHITE, or BLACK.\n\n- W1 and K1 both say: “The BLUE box is empty.”  \n  So W1 and K1 always have the same truth value.\n- If gems are in WHITE or BLACK, then BLUE is empty, so W1 = K1 = TRUE.\n- If gems are in BLUE, then BLUE is not empty, so W1 = K1 = FALSE.\n\nCheck each gem location:\n\n1. If gems are in WHITE:  \n   W1 = K1 = TRUE.  \n   B1 says the gems are in a true box; WHITE is true, so B1 = TRUE.  \n   All three boxes are true, so there is no all-false box. Invalid.\n\n2. If gems are in BLACK:  \n   W1 = K1 = TRUE.  \n   B1 says the gems are in a true box; BLACK is true, so B1 = TRUE.  \n   All three boxes are true again. Invalid.\n\n3. If gems are in BLUE:  \n   W1 = K1 = FALSE.  \n   B1 says the gems are in a true box. If B1 = TRUE, then BLUE is a true box, making B1 consistent.  \n   Then BLUE is all-true, while WHITE and BLACK are all-false. This satisfies the rules.\n\nTherefore, the gems are in BLUE, B1 is true, W1 is false, K1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.627, "ast_prob": 0.448, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00047", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX AND THE BLACK BOX HAS AT MOST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EXACTLY TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE AND EXACTLY ONE STATEMENT IS FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "There are exactly three statements total, so the number of false statements cannot be both 2 and 1. Therefore W1 is false.\n\nB1 includes “THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX,” but the BLACK box is on the right, not the middle. Since one part of the conjunction is false, B1 is false.\n\nBlue and White boxes are therefore not all-true. To satisfy “at least one box has all statements true,” the BLACK box must be all-true, so K1 must be true. K1 says the BLUE box contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.444, "ast_prob": 0.185, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00048", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX HAS AT MOST TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "White has only one statement, W1. If W1 were false, the white box would have exactly one false statement, so “at most two false statements” would be true — contradiction. Thus W1 = T. Therefore all statements on the white box are true, so K1 = T.\n\nIf the gems were in white or black, that box would be a true box, making B1 true and the blue box all-true. Then every box would be all-true, violating the requirement that at least one box is all-false. So the gems must be in blue. To satisfy the all-false requirement, blue must be all-false, so B1 = F.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.684, "ast_prob": 0.189, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00049", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EXACTLY THREE STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is unconditionally false: the BLACK box is on the right, not the middle.  \nSo the BLACK box is all-false, satisfying the “at least one all-false” rule.\n\nW1 says “Exactly three statements are true.” Since K1 is false, all three statements cannot be true, so W1 must be false.\n\nWe still need at least one all-true box. Since W1 and K1 are false, the only possible all-true box is BLUE, so B1 must be true.\n\nB1 says the other two boxes relative to BLUE are empty, which means the gems are in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.723, "ast_prob": 0.48, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00050", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 refers to a statement above it, but the White box has only one statement and the boxes are arranged left/middle/right, so there is no statement above W1. Thus W1 is false.\n\nB1 says the other two boxes are empty, so B1 is true exactly when the gems are in BLUE.  \nK1 says all statements on BLUE are true, so K1 has the same truth value as B1.\n\nIf the gems were not in BLUE, then B1 and K1 would both be false, and W1 is already false, leaving no all-true box. Therefore the gems must be in BLUE, making B1 true and K1 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.754, "ast_prob": 0.493, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00051", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- B1 = “The top statement of each box is true” → B1 asserts (B1 and W1 and K1).\n- W1 = “The other two boxes relative to the blue box are empty” → W1 asserts the gems are in BLUE, since the other two boxes are WHITE and BLACK.\n- K1 = “All statements on the white box are true” → K1 asserts W1 is true.\n\nSo:\n- W1 is true iff gem = BLUE.\n- K1 is true iff W1 is true, so K1 = W1.\n- B1 is true iff B1 and W1 and K1 are all true.\n\nIf gem = BLUE:\n- W1 = T, K1 = T.\n- B1 can be T or F by the self-reference, but B1 = T would make all three statements true, violating the rule that at least one statement is false.\n- Therefore B1 = F. This gives exactly one false statement and two true statements, satisfying the rules.\n\nIf gem ≠ BLUE:\n- W1 = F, K1 = F.\n- Then B1 must also be F, so all three statements are false, violating the rule that at least one statement is true.\n\nThus the only valid solution is gem = BLUE, B1 = F, W1 = T, K1 = T.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.607, "ast_prob": 0.131, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00052", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLACK\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is true exactly when the gems are in the BLUE box.  \nW1 says the WHITE box is black, but the WHITE box is white, so W1 is false.  \nK1 says the BLACK box is the middle box, but the BLACK box is on the right, so K1 is false.  \n\nSince at least one box must be all-true, and both WHITE and BLACK are false boxes, the BLUE box must be the all-true box. Therefore B1 is true, meaning the gems are in the BLUE box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.319, "ast_prob": 0.349, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00053", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EXACTLY ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location be Blue, White, or Black.\n\n- B1 is true exactly when the White box has the gems.\n- K1 is true exactly when the Black box has the gems, because then Blue and White are both empty.\n- So B1 and K1 cannot both be true.\n\nCheck W1, which says exactly one statement overall is true:\n\n- If gems are in White: B1 = T, K1 = F. Then W1 would need to be F to make exactly one true, but if W1 is F there is exactly one true, making W1 T. Contradiction.\n- If gems are in Black: B1 = F, K1 = T. Same contradiction.\n- Therefore gems must be in Blue.\n\nWith gems in Blue: B1 = F and K1 = F. To satisfy “at least one box has all statements true,” W1 must be T. With W1 true, exactly one statement total is true, so W1 is consistent.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.686, "ast_prob": 0.394, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00054", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX HAS EXACTLY ONE FALSE STATEMENT AND THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT LEAST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let B1 mean the BLUE box’s statement. Since BLUE contains only B1, “this box has exactly one false statement” is true exactly when B1 is false.\n\nB1 also says “the other two boxes relative to the BLACK box are empty,” meaning BLUE and WHITE are empty, which is equivalent to gems being in BLACK.\n\nSo:\n- If gems were in BLACK, B1 would be equivalent to “B1 is false and true,” impossible.\n- Therefore gems are not in BLACK, and B1 must be false.\n\nNow gems are in BLUE or WHITE.\n\nIf gems were in WHITE, then W1 would be true, so only B1 would be false unless K1 is also false. That creates a self-contradiction for K1:\n- K1 true → only one false statement → K1 should be false.\n- K1 false → two false statements → K1 should be true.\n\nSo gems cannot be in WHITE.\n\nThus gems are in BLUE. Then W1 is false. With B1 and W1 both false, there are at least two false statements, so K1 is true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.669, "ast_prob": 0.203, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00055", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS BLACK\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is on the BLUE box and says “THIS BOX IS BLACK,” which is false.  \nW1 says “THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE,” referring to B1, so W1 is also false.  \nK1 says the two boxes other than BLUE are empty, which is true only if the gems are in BLUE.  \nSince at least one box must be all-true, K1 must be true, so the gems are in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.599, "ast_prob": 0.358, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00056", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EXACTLY ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem locations determine W1 and K1:\n\n- W1 = “A box next to the BLACK box contains the gems.” The only box next to BLACK is WHITE, so W1 is true iff gem = WHITE.\n- K1 = “THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS,” so K1 is true iff gem = BLACK.\n\nTest gem = BLUE:\n- W1 = F, K1 = F.\n- If B1 = T, then exactly one statement total is true: B1 only. Consistent.\n- If B1 = F, then zero statements are true, violating the rule that at least one box is all-true.\nSo gem = BLUE gives a valid unique solution: B1 = T, W1 = F, K1 = F.\n\nTest gem = WHITE:\n- W1 = T, K1 = F.\n- B1 would need to be F for exactly one true statement, but then B1 itself would be true. Contradiction.\nNo consistent assignment.\n\nTest gem = BLACK:\n- W1 = F, K1 = T.\n- Same contradiction for B1. No consistent assignment.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.652, "ast_prob": 0.45, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00057", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY AND THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS NOT EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location be either BLUE, WHITE, or BLACK.\n\n- W1 says “The Blue box is not empty.” This is true only if the gems are in BLUE.\n- B1 says the boxes other than White are empty AND the White box is true. The boxes other than White are Blue and Black. Both would be empty only if gems are in WHITE. But “White box is true” would require W1 true, meaning gems are in BLUE. These cannot both happen, so B1 is always false.\n- K1 says the boxes next to White contain gems. The boxes next to White are Blue and Black, so this would require both Blue and Black to contain gems. But only one box contains gems, so K1 is always false.\n\nThus Blue and Black are always all-false. To satisfy “at least one box is all-true,” the White box must be all-true, so W1 must be true. That happens only if the gems are in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.23, "ast_prob": 0.43, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00058", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let B1 mean “BLUE contains the gems.”  \nLet W1 and K1 both mean “the top statement of each box is true,” i.e. B1 ∧ W1 ∧ K1.\n\nSince W1 and K1 have identical content, they must have the same truth value: W1 = K1.\n\nSo:\n- If B1 is false, then W1 = K1 = false, so no box would have all true statements. This violates the rule.\n- Therefore B1 must be true, so the gems are in BLUE.\n- With B1 true, W1 = K1 = true would make every box all-true, leaving no all-false box. This violates the rule.\n- Therefore W1 = K1 = false.\n\nThis satisfies: BLUE is all-true, WHITE is all-false, BLACK is all-false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.588, "ast_prob": 0.32, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00059", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Black box has only one statement, so it cannot have two false statements. Therefore W1 is false, making WHITE all-false.\n\nSince at least one box must be all-true, either B1 or K1 must be true.\n\nIf K1 were true, the gems would be in BLACK. Then BLACK would be all-true. If B1 were also true, there would be two all-true boxes, so “the box with no false statements” would not be unique, making B1 false—contradiction. If B1 were false instead, then BLACK would be the unique no-false box and would contain the gems, making B1 true—also contradiction. So K1 cannot be true.\n\nThus K1 is false, so the gems are not in BLACK. Therefore B1 must be true. Then BLUE is the unique no-false box and must contain the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.669, "ast_prob": 0.249, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00060", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EXACTLY THREE STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(B1, W1, K1\\) be the truth values of the three statements.\n\n- \\(W1\\) says: “The top statement of each box is true,” i.e. \\(B1 \\land W1 \\land K1\\).\n- \\(K1\\) says: “Exactly three statements are true,” and since there are exactly three statements total, this is also \\(B1 \\land W1 \\land K1\\).\n\nSo \\(W1\\) and \\(K1\\) have the same truth condition. If either were true, all three statements would have to be true, leaving no all-false box. But the rules require at least one all-false box, so:\n\n- \\(W1 = F\\)\n- \\(K1 = F\\)\n\nSince at least one box must be all-true, and \\(W1\\) and \\(K1\\) are false, \\(B1\\) must be true:\n\n- \\(B1 = T\\)\n\nNow \\(B1\\) says the gems are in a true box. With \\(B1=T\\), \\(W1=F\\), \\(K1=F\\), the only true box is BLUE. Therefore the gems must be in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.331, "ast_prob": 0.363, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00061", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT MOST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is true because the WHITE box is indeed the middle box.\n\nLet W1 be true iff the BLUE box has the gems, and K1 be true iff at most one statement overall is true.\n\nSince B1 is true, the total number of true statements is at least 1. If K1 were true, then B1 and K1 would both be true, making “at most one statement is true” false — contradiction. So K1 must be false.\n\nFor K1 to be false, more than one statement must be true. Since B1 is already true, W1 must also be true. Therefore the BLUE box contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.739, "ast_prob": 0.285, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00062", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 and K1 both say “The BLUE box contains the gems,” so they always have the same truth value: true only if the gems are in BLUE.\n\nW1 says “The WHITE box is BLUE,” but the middle box is WHITE, not BLUE, so W1 is false.\n\nIf the gems were not in BLUE, then B1 and K1 would both be false, and W1 is already false, so every box would be all-false. That would violate the rule that at least one box has all statements true.\n\nTherefore the gems must be in BLUE. Then B1 and K1 are true, W1 is false, which satisfies all rules: BLUE is all-true, WHITE is all-false, BLACK is all-true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.323, "ast_prob": 0.288, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00063", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX HAS AT LEAST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let each statement be evaluated against the actual gem location and the truth values of the statements.\n\n- B1 says the other two boxes relative to Blue are empty. Since exactly one box has gems, B1 is true only if the gems are in BLUE.\n- W1 says Blue has at least one true statement. Blue has only B1, so W1 is true exactly when B1 is true.\n- K1 says “the box with two false statements has the gems.” But each box has exactly one statement, so no box has two false statements. Therefore K1 is false.\n\nIf the gems are in WHITE or BLACK, then B1 is false, so W1 is also false, and K1 is false — leaving no box all-true, which violates the rules.\n\nIf the gems are in BLUE, then B1 is true, W1 is true, and K1 is false. Blue is all-true, White is all-true, and Black is all-false, satisfying the rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.29, "ast_prob": 0.26, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00064", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS WHITE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT MOST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is true because the white box is indeed white.\n\nSuppose K1 were true. Then at most one statement would be true. But W1 is true, and K1 itself would also be true, giving at least two true statements — a contradiction. Therefore K1 is false.\n\nSince K1 is false, the statement “at most one statement is true” is false, meaning more than one statement is true. W1 is already true, so B1 must also be true.\n\nB1 true means the blue box contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.267, "ast_prob": 0.115, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00065", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(G\\) be the box with the gems.\n\n- B1: “The black box is empty” is true iff \\(G \\neq \\text{BLACK}\\).\n- K1: “A box next to this box contains the gems” refers to BLACK’s only neighbor, WHITE, so K1 is true iff \\(G = \\text{WHITE}\\).\n\nW1 cannot be true: if it were true, the gem-box would have two false statements. But B1 is about BLACK and K1 is about WHITE, so no single box could have two false statements unless W1 itself were false, a contradiction. Therefore W1 is false.\n\nSince W1 is false, “the box with two false statements” must exist and must not contain the gems. It cannot be BLACK, because that would require B1 to be false, meaning BLACK contains the gems, contradicting W1 being false. It cannot be BLUE, since no other statement is about BLUE. So the box with two false statements is WHITE. Thus WHITE does not contain the gems, and K1 is also false.\n\nSo gems are not in WHITE or BLACK; therefore gems are in BLUE. Then B1 is true, K1 is false, and W1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.727, "ast_prob": 0.405, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00066", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n- B1 = “A box next to this box is true” → Blue is only next to White, so B1 is true exactly when W1 is true.\n- W1 = “The White box is empty” → W1 is true exactly when the gems are not in White.\n- K1 = “The other two boxes are empty” → K1 is true exactly when the gems are in Black.\n\nTest the three possible gem locations:\n\n1. Gems in BLUE:\n   - W1 = True, because White is empty.\n   - B1 = True, because W1 is true.\n   - K1 = False, because Blue is not empty.\n   - Truth values: B1=T, W1=T, K1=F → has at least one true and at least one false. Consistent.\n\n2. Gems in WHITE:\n   - W1 = False, because White is not empty.\n   - B1 = False, because W1 is false.\n   - K1 = False, because Black is not empty.\n   - All false → violates the rule that at least one box is all-true.\n\n3. Gems in BLACK:\n   - W1 = True, because White is empty.\n   - B1 = True, because W1 is true.\n   - K1 = True, because Blue and White are empty.\n   - All true → violates the rule that at least one box is all-false.\n\nTherefore, the only consistent solution is gems in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.697, "ast_prob": 0.349, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00067", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The boxes are arranged left to right: BLUE, WHITE, BLACK.  \n- W1 says “the statement above this one is true,” but no statement is above it, so W1 is false.  \n- Therefore the WHITE box is not all-true.  \n- BLACK is at the right end, so its only adjacent box is WHITE. Since WHITE is not true, B1 is false.  \n- At least one box must be all-true. BLUE and WHITE are both false, so BLACK must be all-true: K1 is true.  \n- K1 says the other two boxes relative to BLUE are empty, so WHITE and BLACK are empty. Therefore the gems must be in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.648, "ast_prob": 0.498, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00068", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER THE BLUE BOX HAS AT LEAST ONE FALSE STATEMENT OR THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "- K1 says the blue box is the middle box. The blue box is left, so K1 is false.\n- W1 says the boxes next to the black box contain gems. The black box is rightmost, so only the white box is next to it. Thus W1 means the white box contains the gems.\n- B1 says: “Blue has at least one false statement OR blue contains the gems.” Since blue has only B1, “blue has at least one false statement” means B1 is false.\n  - If the gems are not in blue, then B1 = (B1 is false), which is impossible.\n  - Therefore the gems must be in blue, making B1 true.\n- Then W1 is false and K1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.538, "ast_prob": 0.413, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00069", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE AND ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is fixed true, because the WHITE box is literally the middle box.  \nSo the WHITE box is an all-true box, satisfying the “at least one all-true” rule.\n\nK1 says: “THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE AND ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE.”  \nSince W1 is true, the WHITE box is true. Therefore K1 reduces to: “All statements on the BLUE box are true,” i.e. K1 has the same truth value as B1.\n\nSo B1 and K1 must be either both true or both false.  \nIf both were true, then no box would be all-false, violating the rule that at least one box has all false statements.  \nTherefore B1 and K1 must both be false.\n\nB1 = “THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY” is false, so the BLUE box is not empty.  \nSince exactly one box contains the gems, the gems must be in the BLUE box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.3, "ast_prob": 0.358, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00070", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE OR AT MOST ONE STATEMENT IS FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE AND THIS BOX HAS AT LEAST ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let each box’s truth value be the truth value of its single statement.\n\n1. K1 says: “The gems are in a box that is false, and this box has at least one false statement.” Here “this box” is the BLACK box.  \n   - If K1 were true, BLACK would have no false statement, making “this box has at least one false statement” false — contradiction.  \n   - So K1 must be False.  \n   - Since K1 is false, the second half is actually true (Black has K1 as a false statement), so the first half must be false: the gems are NOT in a false box.  \n   - Therefore the gems must be in a true box.\n\n2. Since K1 is false, BLACK is a false box, so the gems cannot be in BLACK.\n\n3. If the gems were in WHITE, then W1 (“Blue contains the gems”) would be false. With K1 also false, there would already be two false statements, so B1’s “at most one statement is false” claim would be false, and the box next to BLUE (WHITE) is also false. Thus B1 would be false too — leaving no true box, violating the rule.  \n   So the gems cannot be in WHITE.\n\n4. Therefore the gems must be in BLUE. Then W1 is true. BLUE’s neighbor, WHITE, is true, so B1 is true. K1 is false as already shown.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.725, "ast_prob": 0.35, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00071", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The BLUE box is on the left, WHITE in the middle, BLACK on the right.\n\n- B1: “THE WHITE BOX IS BLUE” is false, because the WHITE box is white, not blue.\n- K1: “THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX” is false, because the BLUE box is on the left.\n- W1: “THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS” is true only if the gems are in the BLUE box.\n\nIf the gems were in WHITE or BLACK, then W1 would also be false, meaning every statement in all three boxes would be false. That would violate the rule that at least one box must have all statements true.\n\nTherefore the gems must be in the BLUE box, making W1 true. Then:\n- BLUE box: B1 false → all-false\n- WHITE box: W1 true → all-true\n- BLACK box: K1 false → all-false\n\nThis satisfies the rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.244, "ast_prob": 0.418, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00072", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n- B1: “Boxes next to the BLUE box contain gems” → true only if WHITE contains the gems.\n- W1: “Boxes next to the BLUE box contain gems” → true only if WHITE contains the gems.\n- K1: “This box is empty” → true only if BLACK does not contain the gems.\n\nTest the possible gem locations:\n\n1. If gems are in BLUE:\n   - B1 = False, because WHITE is empty.\n   - W1 = False, because WHITE is empty.\n   - K1 = True, because BLACK is empty.\n   - At least one true and at least one false: satisfied.\n\n2. If gems are in WHITE:\n   - B1 = True, W1 = True, K1 = True.\n   - No false statement, violating the rule that at least one box is all-false.\n\n3. If gems are in BLACK:\n   - B1 = False, W1 = False, K1 = False.\n   - No true statement, violating the rule that at least one box is all-true.\n\nTherefore, the only valid solution is gems in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.403, "ast_prob": 0.119, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00073", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX HAS AT MOST ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX HAS EXACTLY ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n- B1 = truth value of BLUE box statement\n- W1 = truth value of WHITE box statement\n- K1 = truth value of BLACK box statement\n\nStep 1: B1 says “The black box has at most one false statement.” Since the black box has only one statement, it can have at most one false statement. So B1 is always TRUE.  \nThus B1 = T.\n\nStep 2: W1 says “The black box has exactly one true statement.” Since the black box has only one statement, W1 is true exactly when K1 is true.  \nThus W1 = K1.\n\nStep 3: The rules require at least one all-true box and at least one all-false box. Since each box has only one statement, we need at least one TRUE statement and at least one FALSE statement. B1 is TRUE, so we need at least one false statement. Since W1 = K1, the only way to have a false statement is W1 = K1 = FALSE.  \nThus W1 = F and K1 = F.\n\nStep 4: K1 says “The gems are in a box that is FALSE.” A false box here means a box whose statement is false. With K1 = F, the gems cannot be in a false box.  \n- BLUE box is true, so it is not false.  \n- WHITE box is false, so gems there would make K1 true.  \n- BLACK box is false, so gems there would make K1 true.  \nTherefore the gems must be in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.688, "ast_prob": 0.325, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00074", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(K1\\) be true. Then the Black box would be true, making \\(B1\\) (“The Black box is true”) also true. That would give at least two boxes with no false statements: Blue and Black. But \\(K1\\) says **the** box with no false statements has the gems, implying uniqueness. That contradiction means \\(K1\\) cannot be true.\n\nSo:\n- \\(K1 = F\\)\n- Black box is false, so \\(B1 = F\\)\n- Since at least one box must be all-true, and both \\(B1\\) and \\(K1\\) are false, \\(W1\\) must be true.\n- \\(W1\\) true means White and Black are empty; therefore the gems must be in Blue.\n\nFinal check: Blue is all-false, White is all-true, Black is all-false. This satisfies the rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.771, "ast_prob": 0.398, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00075", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Interpreting the spatial clues literally: the White box is in the middle, not physically below the Blue box, and it contains only one statement. So there is no statement above W1, making W1 false.\n\n- W1 = F → the White box is all-false.\n- B1 says a box next to Blue is true; the only box next to Blue is White, but White is false, so B1 = F → Blue box is all-false.\n- At least one box must be all-true, so Black must be all-true → K1 = T.\n- K1 says the other two boxes relative to Blue are empty, so White and Black are empty → the gem must be in Blue.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.665, "ast_prob": 0.409, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00076", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT LEAST THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 refers to the Blue box having exactly two true statements, but Blue has only one statement, so B1 cannot be true. Therefore B1 = F.\n\nW1 says “at least three statements are false.” Since there are exactly three statements total, W1 would be true only if all three statements were false. But if W1 itself were true, it would not be false, so W1 cannot be true. Therefore W1 = F.\n\nWe already have at least one all-false box. The rules require at least one all-true box. The only remaining box is Black, so K1 must be true. K1 says the Blue box contains the gems, so the gems are in Blue.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.555, "ast_prob": 0.219, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00077", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is false, because the BLUE box is on the left, not the middle. So the WHITE box is all-false.\n\nK1 refers to the statement above it, which is W1. Since W1 is false, K1 is also false. So the BLACK box is all-false.\n\nSince at least one box must be all-true, and both WHITE and BLACK contain false statements, the BLUE box must be all-true. Therefore B1 is true.\n\nB1 says: “The box with no false statements has the gems.” The only box with no false statements is BLUE, so the gems must be in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.468, "ast_prob": 0.434, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00078", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- B1 is true iff the WHITE box does **not** contain the gems.\n- W1 is true iff the BLUE box’s statement is true, i.e. iff B1 is true.\n- K1 is true iff the BLACK box contains the gems.\n\nCheck each possible gem location:\n\n1. Gem in BLUE:  \n   - White is empty → B1 = T  \n   - W1 says B1 is true → W1 = T  \n   - Black is empty → K1 = F  \n   Result has both true and false statements, satisfying the rules.\n\n2. Gem in WHITE:  \n   - White has gems → B1 = F  \n   - W1 = F  \n   - Black empty → K1 = F  \n   All statements false, violating “at least one box all true.”\n\n3. Gem in BLACK:  \n   - White empty → B1 = T  \n   - W1 = T  \n   - Black has gems → K1 = T  \n   All statements true, violating “at least one box all false.”\n\nTherefore, the gems are in the BLUE box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.262, "ast_prob": 0.314, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00080", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS AND THIS BOX HAS AT LEAST ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- \\(B1\\): “A box next to this box contains the gems” = White contains the gems.\n- \\(W1\\): “The Blue box contains the gems.”\n- \\(K1\\): “Boxes next to the Black box contain gems and this box has at least one false statement.”\n\nFor \\(K1\\), since Black has only one statement, “this box has at least one false statement” means \\(K1\\) is false.  \nSo \\(K1\\) says: “White contains gems AND \\(K1\\) is false.”\n\nIf \\(K1\\) were true, then White would contain gems and \\(K1\\) would be false — contradiction.  \nThus \\(K1\\) must be false. For \\(K1\\) to be false, its first part must also be false, so White does **not** contain the gems.\n\nTherefore:\n- \\(B1\\) is false, because White does not contain the gems.\n- \\(K1\\) is false, as shown.\n\nAt least one box must be all-true, so \\(W1\\) must be true.  \nThus the Blue box contains the gems.\n\nFinal truth values:\n- \\(B1 = F\\)\n- \\(W1 = T\\)\n- \\(K1 = F\\)\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.297, "ast_prob": 0.266, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00081", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is “The Blue box is the middle box.” Since Blue is on the left, B1 is FALSE.\n\nW1 and K1 have identical wording: “The other two boxes relative to the Blue box are empty.” That means White and Black are both empty, which is true exactly when the gems are in Blue. So W1 and K1 must have the same truth value.\n\nBecause B1 is false, Blue is an all-false box. The rules require at least one all-true box, so at least one of W1 or K1 must be true. Since W1 and K1 share the same truth value, both must be true. Therefore the gems must be in Blue.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.331, "ast_prob": 0.151, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00082", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Each box has exactly one statement displayed, so no box can have “two false statements.” Therefore, W1 is false.\n\nK1 says “the statement above this one is true,” meaning K1 asserts W1 is true. Since W1 is false, K1 is also false.\n\nWe need at least one box with all statements true. Since W1 and K1 are false, B1 must be true. B1 says the Blue box contains the gems, so the gems are in Blue.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.781, "ast_prob": 0.188, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00083", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT LEAST THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(K1\\) mean “the White box is true,” since White is the only box next to Blue. So \\(K1 = W1\\).\n\nIf \\(W1\\) were true, then all three statements would be false, including \\(W1\\) itself — contradiction. Therefore \\(W1 = F\\), so \\(K1 = F\\).\n\nSince at least one statement must be true, \\(B1\\) must be \\(T\\). Thus Blue contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.663, "ast_prob": 0.464, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00084", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Each box has exactly one statement, so a box being “all true” just means its statement is true, and “all false” means its statement is false.\n\n- W1 says the Blue box has at least two true statements. But Blue has only one statement, B1. So W1 is always false.\n- K1 says the other two boxes relative to Blue are empty. Since exactly one box has the gems, that is equivalent to saying the gems are in Blue. So K1 has the same truth value as B1.\n- At least one box must be all true, so at least one statement must be true. But W1 is false, and B1 and K1 must have the same truth value. Therefore B1 and K1 must both be true.\n- Thus the gems are in Blue, B1 is true, W1 is false, and K1 is true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.286, "ast_prob": 0.478, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00085", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] AT LEAST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is true because the white box is indeed the middle box.  \nSince W1 is true, at least one statement in the puzzle is true, so B1 is also true.  \nTherefore Blue and White are both all-true boxes.  \nThe rules require at least one all-false box, so Black must be all-false, meaning K1 is false.  \nIf K1 is false, “The blue box is empty” is false, so the blue box contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.478, "ast_prob": 0.382, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00086", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS BLUE AND THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 states that the other two boxes (White and Black) are empty, which is equivalent to saying the gems are in the Blue box. So B1 is true iff Blue has the gems.\n\nW1 says “The Black box is Blue and the box with two false statements has the gems.” The Black box is not blue, so this conjunction is false. Therefore W1 = F.\n\nK1 says “It is not true that the White box is the middle box.” But the White box is the middle box, so K1 = F.\n\nSince W1 and K1 are both false, the only way to satisfy “at least one box has all statements true” is for B1 to be true. Therefore the gems are in the Blue box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.532, "ast_prob": 0.461, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00087", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n- \\(B1\\) = “The other two boxes are empty.”\n- \\(W1\\) = “The top statement of each box is true.”\n- \\(K1\\) = “All statements on the White box are true.”\n\nSince each box has only one statement, the “top statement” of each box is just that box’s only statement. Therefore:\n\n- \\(W1\\) asserts: \\(B1 \\land W1 \\land K1\\).\n- \\(K1\\) asserts: \\(W1\\). So \\(K1 = W1\\).\n\nAlso, since exactly one box has the gems:\n- \\(B1\\) is true iff the gems are in BLUE.\n\nNow solve the fixed-point equation for \\(W1\\):\n\\[\nW1 = B1 \\land W1 \\land K1 = B1 \\land W1\n\\]\nbecause \\(K1 = W1\\).\n\nIf \\(B1\\) is false, then \\(W1\\) must be false, and \\(K1\\) is false too. That would make all three statements false, violating the rule that at least one box is all-true.\n\nSo \\(B1\\) must be true, meaning the gems are in BLUE.\n\nWith \\(B1\\) true, the equation becomes:\n\\[\nW1 = W1\n\\]\nwhich allows \\(W1\\) either true or false. But if \\(W1\\) were true, then \\(K1\\) would also be true, making all three statements true, violating the rule that at least one box is all-false.\n\nThus:\n\\[\nW1 = F,\\quad K1 = F\n\\]\n\nSo the only consistent solution is:\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.699, "ast_prob": 0.172, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00088", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS OR THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT LEAST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EITHER THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY OR THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- \\(G\\) = box with gems.\n- \\(B1\\) = “Blue has gems OR White has gems” → \\(B1\\) is true iff gems are not in Black.\n- \\(W1\\) = “At least two statements are false.”\n- \\(K1\\) = “Other two boxes empty OR White empty” → \\(K1\\) is true iff gems are not in White.\n\nCheck each possible gem location:\n\n1. **Gems in Blue**  \n   - \\(B1\\) = true, because Blue has gems.  \n   - \\(K1\\) = true, because White is empty.  \n   - \\(W1\\): If it were true, then all three statements would be true, so fewer than two would be false — contradiction. So \\(W1\\) = false.  \n   - This is consistent: \\(B1=T, W1=F, K1=T\\).\n\n2. **Gems in White**  \n   - \\(B1\\) = true.  \n   - \\(K1\\) = false, because White is not empty.  \n   - If \\(W1\\) is true, only \\(K1\\) is false → not at least two false, contradiction.  \n   - If \\(W1\\) is false, then \\(K1\\) and \\(W1\\) are both false → exactly two false, making \\(W1\\) true, contradiction.  \n   - No consistent solution.\n\n3. **Gems in Black**  \n   - \\(B1\\) = false.  \n   - \\(K1\\) = true, because the other two boxes are empty.  \n   - If \\(W1\\) is true, only \\(B1\\) is false → not at least two false, contradiction.  \n   - If \\(W1\\) is false, then \\(B1\\) and \\(W1\\) are both false → exactly two false, making \\(W1\\) true, contradiction.  \n   - No consistent solution.\n\nTherefore, the only valid solution is gems in Blue.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.408, "ast_prob": 0.448, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00089", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 refers to a statement above it, but there is no such statement, so B1 is false.  \nW1 says the top statement of every box is true; since B1 is false, W1 cannot be true, so W1 is false.  \nThe rules require at least one all-true box, so K1 must be true.  \nK1 says the other two boxes relative to Blue are empty, so the gems must be in Blue.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.508, "ast_prob": 0.187, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00090", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EITHER BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS OR THE BLACK BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "- Black box has only one statement, K1. The first part of K1 (“boxes next to the white box contain gems”) would require both Blue and Black to contain gems, impossible since exactly one box has gems. The second part (“Black box has at least two true statements”) is impossible because Black has only one statement total. Therefore K1 is FALSE.\n\n- So Black is an all-false box. At least one box must be all-true, so either B1 or W1 must be true.\n\n- W1 says “a box next to the black box contains gems.” Black’s only neighbor is White, so W1 is true only if the gems are in WHITE.\n\n- Check gem = BLACK: W1 false, K1 false, so B1 would have to be true. But B1 says the unique no-false-statement box has the gems; if B1 were true, Blue would be that box, but the gems would be in Black. Contradiction. So gem ≠ BLACK.\n\n- Check gem = WHITE: W1 true, K1 false. White is then a no-false-statement box. If B1 were true, Blue would also be a no-false-statement box, making “the box with no false statements” non-unique, so B1 could not be true. If B1 were false, the only no-false-statement box would be White, which contains the gems, making B1 true. Contradiction. So gem ≠ WHITE.\n\n- Therefore the gems are in BLUE. Then W1 is false, K1 is false, and Blue is the unique no-false-statement box containing the gems, so B1 is true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.629, "ast_prob": 0.267, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00091", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EXACTLY ONE STATEMENT IS FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(B, W, K\\) be the truth values of B1, W1, K1. A box is “true” iff its statement is true, since each box has exactly one statement.\n\n- W1 says: \\(W = B \\lor K\\).\n- K1 says: exactly one statement is false.\n\nChecking consistent truth assignments:\n\n- If \\(B=W=T\\), K cannot be consistently assigned.\n- If \\(B=W=F\\), then \\(K=F\\), but then all three boxes are false, violating the rule that at least one box is all-true.\n- If \\(B=F, W=T\\), then \\(W = F \\lor K\\) forces \\(K=T\\). This works: exactly one statement is false.\n- If \\(B=T, W=F\\), then \\(W = T \\lor K = T\\), contradiction.\n\nSo:\n- B1 = False\n- W1 = True\n- K1 = True\n\nThus White and Black are true boxes, while Blue is false. B1 says the gems are in a true box. Since B1 is false, the gems are not in a true box, so they must be in Blue.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.751, "ast_prob": 0.135, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00092", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is true only if the White box contains the gems, because Black has only one adjacent box: White.\n\n- If gems are in BLUE: B1 is true, W1 is false, K1 is false. This gives at least one all-true box and at least one all-false box.\n- If gems are in WHITE: B1, W1, and K1 would all be true, leaving no all-false box.\n- If gems are in BLACK: B1, W1, and K1 would all be false, leaving no all-true box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.614, "ast_prob": 0.103, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00093", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] AT MOST THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE AND THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE AND THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "There are exactly 3 statements total.\n\n- B1 contains “at most three statements are false,” which is always true, but also “this box is the middle box,” which is false because BLUE is left. So B1 = False.\n- W1 is true exactly when WHITE does not contain the gems. So W1 = True if gems are BLUE or BLACK, False if gems are WHITE.\n- K1 requires BLACK to contain the gems and the gem-box to be false. If gems were in BLACK, K1 would create a self-contradiction: it would be true only if it were false, and false only if it were true. So gems cannot be BLACK.\n\nTesting gems in WHITE: B1=False, W1=False, K1=False → no true box, invalid.\n\nTesting gems in BLUE: B1=False, W1=True, K1=False → has both a true box and false boxes, valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.564, "ast_prob": 0.339, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00094", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX HAS EXACTLY ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 refers to a statement above it inside the White box; there is none, so W1 is false.  \nThus the White box has zero true statements, so B1 (“White box has exactly one true statement”) is false.  \nTo satisfy “at least one box is all-true,” the Black box must be all-true, so K1 is true.  \nK1 says the White and Black boxes are empty; therefore the gem must be in Blue.  \nThis is consistent: Blue and White are all-false, Black is all-true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.375, "ast_prob": 0.18, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00095", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT LEAST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the truth of each statement depend on where the gems actually are:\n\n- B1 means “White has the gems,” since then Blue and Black are empty.\n- W1 means “Black has the gems.”\n- K1 means “At least two of B1, W1, K1 are false.”\n\nCheck each possible gem location:\n\n1. Gem in WHITE: B1 = T, W1 = F.  \n   If K1 = F, then two statements are false, making K1 true — contradiction.  \n   If K1 = T, then only one statement is false, making K1 false — contradiction.  \n   Impossible.\n\n2. Gem in BLACK: B1 = F, W1 = T.  \n   If K1 = F, then two statements are false, making K1 true — contradiction.  \n   If K1 = T, then only one statement is false, making K1 false — contradiction.  \n   Impossible.\n\n3. Gem in BLUE: B1 = F, W1 = F.  \n   If K1 = F, all three are false, making K1 true — contradiction.  \n   If K1 = T, then exactly two statements are false, so K1 is true — consistent.  \n   This works.\n\nSo the gems are in the BLUE box, B1 is false, W1 is false, and K1 is true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.666, "ast_prob": 0.22, "max_depth": 2}}
