{"id": "direct_ft_train_00097", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Each box has only one statement.\n\n- **W1** says the White box has exactly two true statements, but the White box contains only W1 itself, so it can never have two true statements. Therefore **W1 = F**.\n- **B1** refers to “the box with two false statements.” But no box contains two statements at all, so no such box exists. Therefore **B1 = F**.\n- Since we must have at least one all-true box, the only remaining possible true statement is **K1**. Thus **K1 = T**, meaning the Blue box contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.545, "ast_prob": 0.184, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00098", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is false because White contains only one statement, so it cannot have at least two true statements.  \nK1 is false because it claims both boxes next to White contain gems, but exactly one box contains gems.  \nSince at least one box must be all-true, and White and Black are all-false, Blue must be all-true, so B1 is true.  \nTherefore, the box with no false statements is Blue, and it must contain the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.559, "ast_prob": 0.211, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00099", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 refers to the black box, which is on the right, so K1 is false.  \nW1 says that B1, W1, and K1 are all true, but since K1 is false, W1 cannot be true, so W1 is false.  \nB1 says the white and black boxes are empty. Since exactly one box has the gems, B1 is true exactly when the gems are in the blue box.  \nAt least one statement must be true, and W1 and K1 are false, so B1 must be true. Therefore the gems are in the blue box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.677, "ast_prob": 0.198, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00100", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 refers to the White box having at least two true statements, but the White box has only one statement, W1. So B1 must be false.\n\nW1 says the two boxes other than Blue (White and Black) are empty. That is true exactly when the gems are in the Blue box.\n\nK1 says all statements on the White box are true. Since the White box has only W1, K1 has the same truth value as W1.\n\nNow test gem locations:\n\n- If gems are in WHITE: W1 is false, so K1 is false. B1 is already false. All statements false, violating the rule that at least one box is all-true.\n- If gems are in BLACK: W1 is false, so K1 is false, and B1 is false. Again all false, invalid.\n- If gems are in BLUE: W1 is true, K1 is true, and B1 is false. This gives at least one true and at least one false, consistent.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.746, "ast_prob": 0.283, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00101", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Since each box contains exactly one statement:\n\n- BLUE has only B1, so B1 cannot be “exactly two true statements.” Therefore B1 = F.\n- WHITE has only W1, so K1’s claim that WHITE has “at least two true statements” is impossible. Therefore K1 = F.\n\nSince at least one box must be all-true, and B1 and K1 are both false, the only way to get an all-true box is for W1 to be true.\n\nIf W1 is true, then the gems must be in a box that is true. WHITE is true exactly because W1 is true, so the gems must be in WHITE. This is consistent:\n- BLUE all-false: B1 = F\n- BLACK all-false: K1 = F\n- WHITE all-true: W1 = T\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.539, "ast_prob": 0.435, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00102", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The WHITE box is in the middle, so W1 is false.  \nThe BLUE box is on the left, so its only adjacent box is WHITE; thus B1 is true exactly when WHITE contains the gems.  \nK1 says “the BLUE box is true,” meaning B1 is true, so K1 has the same truth value as B1.  \n\nSince W1 is false, the WHITE box is all-false, satisfying the “at least one all-false” rule.  \nFor at least one box to be all-true, either BLUE or BLACK must be all-true. Both depend on B1 being true, which happens only if WHITE contains the gems.  \nTherefore the gems are in WHITE, B1 is true, W1 is false, and K1 is true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.439, "ast_prob": 0.275, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00103", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Black is the rightmost box, so the only box next to it is WHITE. Therefore both B1 and K1 mean “WHITE contains the gems,” so B1 = K1 = true exactly when gem = WHITE.\n\nW1 says “B1, W1, and K1 are all true.” If the gems were not in WHITE, then B1 and K1 would both be false, and W1 would also be false, leaving no all-true box. So the gems must be in WHITE. Then B1 and K1 are true; to satisfy “at least one all-false box,” W1 must be false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.618, "ast_prob": 0.245, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00104", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS OR THE BLACK BOX IS BLUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE AND BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Adjacency: Blue is left, White is middle, Black is right.  \nSo the boxes next to White are Blue and Black; the only box next to Blue is White.\n\n- “Boxes next to the White box contain gems” would require both Blue and Black to contain gems, but exactly one box contains the gems, so that clause is false.\n- “The Black box is Blue” is false.\n- Therefore B1 is FALSE.\n\n- “A box next to the White box is true” will be true if either Blue or Black is true.\n- “Boxes next to the Blue box contain gems” means White contains the gems.  \nIf the gems are in White, then K1 is true, so Black is true, making “a box next to White is true” true as well.\n- Therefore W1 is TRUE and K1 is TRUE.\n\nIf the gems were in Blue or Black, both K1 and W1 would be false, and B1 would also be false, leaving no all-true box, which violates the rules. So the gems must be in White.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.301, "ast_prob": 0.426, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00105", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS BLACK\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because the blue box is not black.  \nW1 and K1 are both true exactly when the gems are in the white box.  \nIf the gems were in blue or black, all three statements would be false, leaving no all-true box, which violates the rules.  \nTherefore the gems must be in the white box.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.273, "ast_prob": 0.332, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00106", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS OR THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "We have three statements total: one on each box.\n\n- B1 is true only if the gems are in the WHITE box.\n- W1 says: “Either a box next to the BLUE box contains the gems, or this box contains the gems.” The only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so W1 is effectively true only if the gems are in WHITE.\n- K1 says: “The WHITE box has exactly two false statements.” But the WHITE box has only one statement, W1, so K1 can never be true. Therefore K1 = FALSE.\n\nNow test possible gem locations:\n\n1. If gems are in BLUE:\n   - B1 = FALSE\n   - W1 = FALSE\n   - K1 = FALSE  \n   This makes all boxes all-false, violating the rule that at least one box must be all-true.\n\n2. If gems are in WHITE:\n   - B1 = TRUE\n   - W1 = TRUE\n   - K1 = FALSE  \n   This satisfies exactly one gem box, at least one all-true box, and at least one all-false box.\n\n3. If gems are in BLACK:\n   - B1 = FALSE\n   - W1 = FALSE\n   - K1 = FALSE  \n   Again, all boxes false, violating the rule.\n\nThus the only valid solution is gems in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.371, "ast_prob": 0.297, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00107", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] AT LEAST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 asserts that both Blue and Black contain gems, but exactly one box contains gems, so K1 is always false.\n\nIf the gems were in Blue or Black, then W1 would be true. With K1 false, B1 would be true only if at least two statements were false, but only K1 would be false. If B1 were false, then B1 and K1 would both be false, making B1 true. Contradiction.\n\nSo the gems must be in White. Then W1 is false and K1 is false, giving exactly two false statements, so B1 is true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.556, "ast_prob": 0.111, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00108", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let each box’s single statement determine whether that box is “true” or “false.”\n\n- Black is the rightmost box, so its only neighbor is White.  \n  Thus K1 is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.\n\n- Blue’s statement B1 says the gem box is a true box.\n\n- White’s statement W1 says the unique box with no false statements has the gems. I read this as a definite description: W1 is true only if there is exactly one true box and that box contains the gems.\n\nCheck the three possible gem locations:\n\n1. Gems in BLACK:  \n   K1 is false because White does not contain gems.  \n   So Black is false, making B1 false.  \n   W1 would require the unique true box to contain gems, but Black is false, so W1 is false.  \n   All three false → violates “at least one true box.” Impossible.\n\n2. Gems in BLUE:  \n   K1 is false.  \n   If W1 were true, White would be true, but W1 also requires exactly one true box, so it cannot be true. Thus W1 is false.  \n   If B1 is true, then Blue is the only true box and contains gems, so W1 would be true — contradiction.  \n   If B1 is false, there are no true boxes — contradiction.  \n   Impossible.\n\n3. Gems in WHITE:  \n   K1 is true because White is next to Black and contains gems.  \n   If W1 were true, White would be true, making Blue true too, so there would be multiple true boxes, contradicting W1. So W1 is false.  \n   Therefore White is false, so B1 is false.  \n   Black is the unique true box, but it does not contain gems, so W1 is false consistently.  \n   This satisfies exactly one gem, at least one true box, and at least one false box.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.719, "ast_prob": 0.122, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00110", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Black is on the right, so the only box next to it is WHITE.  \nThus B1 is true exactly when WHITE contains the gems.\n\nWHITE is in the middle, so the boxes next to it are BLUE and BLACK.  \nThus K1 would require both BLUE and BLACK to contain gems, but exactly one box contains gems, so K1 is always false.\n\nW1 says every top statement is true, including K1. Since K1 is false, W1 is also false.\n\nAt least one box must have all true statements. The only remaining true possibility is B1, so WHITE must contain the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.55, "ast_prob": 0.136, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00111", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE OR THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT MOST ONE STATEMENT IS FALSE AND THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(B1, W1, K1\\) be the truth values of the statements.\n\n- Since each box has exactly one statement, “all true” means that statement is true, and “all false” means that statement is false.  \n  So at least one of \\(B1, W1, K1\\) must be true, and at least one must be false.\n\n- “White box is empty” is exactly equivalent to \\(W1\\), because the only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so \\(W1\\) is true iff WHITE does not contain the gems.\n\n- \\(B1\\) says: “All BLUE statements are true OR WHITE is empty.”  \n  Since BLUE has only \\(B1\\), this becomes: \\(B1 = B1 \\lor W1\\).\n\n- \\(K1\\) says: “At most one statement is false AND WHITE is true.”  \n  So \\(K1 = (\\text{number of false statements} \\le 1) \\land W1\\).\n\nCheck gem locations:\n\n1. Gem in BLUE: WHITE is empty, so \\(W1 = T\\). Then \\(B1 = T\\). That forces all statements true, violating the rule that at least one box is all-false. Invalid.\n\n2. Gem in BLACK: WHITE is empty, so \\(W1 = T\\). Then again \\(B1 = T\\), and \\(K1 = T\\). All true, invalid.\n\n3. Gem in WHITE: WHITE is not empty, so \\(W1 = F\\). Then \\(K1 = F\\). Since at least one statement must be true, \\(B1\\) must be \\(T\\). This is consistent:\n   - \\(B1 = T\\): “All BLUE statements true OR WHITE empty” = True or False = True.\n   - \\(W1 = F\\): WHITE has the gems.\n   - \\(K1 = F\\): More than one statement is false and WHITE is not true.\n\nThus the only valid solution is gem in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.334, "ast_prob": 0.458, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00112", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is on the WHITE box, and the WHITE box is indeed the middle box.  \nSo W1 (“It is not true that this box is the middle box”) is **false**.\n\nB1 says all statements on the WHITE box are true.  \nSince W1 is false, B1 is also **false**.\n\nK1 says boxes next to the BLACK box contain gems.  \nThe only box next to BLACK is WHITE, so K1 is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.\n\nAt least one box must be all-true. BLUE is false and WHITE is false, so BLACK must be all-true.  \nThus K1 must be true, meaning the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.348, "ast_prob": 0.317, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00113", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS WHITE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n\n1. The BLACK box is on the right, so its only adjacent box is the WHITE box.  \n   Thus W1 (“A box next to the BLACK box contains the gems”) is true exactly when the gems are in the WHITE box.  \n   So W1 = T iff gem = WHITE.\n\n2. B1 says “THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE.” The WHITE box has only W1, so White being true means W1 is true.  \n   Therefore B1 has the same truth value as W1.\n\n3. K1 says “THIS BOX IS WHITE.” But the box is the BLACK box, so K1 is always false.\n\n4. At least one box must be all-true, and at least one box must be all-false. Since K1 is false, the all-false condition is satisfied.  \n   For the all-true condition, at least one of B1 or W1 must be true. Since B1 and W1 have the same truth value, both must be true.\n\n5. Both B1 and W1 are true only if the gems are in the WHITE box, because W1 is true iff gem = WHITE.\n\nTherefore the gems are in the WHITE box, B1 and W1 are true, and K1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.341, "ast_prob": 0.25, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00114", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n1. K1 says “the statement above this one is true,” but the BLACK box contains only K1, so there is no statement above it. Therefore K1 is false.\n2. B1 is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE, because then the other two boxes (BLUE and BLACK) are empty.\n3. W1 is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE, because the only box next to BLUE is WHITE.\n4. If the gems were not in WHITE, then B1, W1, and K1 would all be false, leaving no all-true box.\n5. So the gems must be in WHITE, making B1 true and W1 true, while K1 remains false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.668, "ast_prob": 0.113, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00115", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step deduction:\n\n1. Each box has exactly one statement, so a box is “true” iff its single statement is true, and “false” iff its single statement is false.\n2. W1 says “The statement above this one is true.” The boxes are arranged left/middle/right, so there is no statement physically above the White box. Therefore W1 is false.\n3. Since W1 is false, the White box is not a true box.\n4. Black is next only to White. K1 says “A box next to this box is true,” but White is false. Therefore K1 is false.\n5. At least one box must be all-true. The only remaining candidate is Blue, so B1 must be true.\n6. B1 says the two boxes relative to White—Blue and Black—are empty. Thus the gems must be in the White box.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.725, "ast_prob": 0.177, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00116", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is false, because the Blue box is on the left, not the middle.  \nB1 is false, because there is no statement above it to be true.  \nSince at least one statement must be true, W1 must be true.  \nW1 says a box next to the Black box contains the gems; the only box next to Black is White. Therefore the gems are in White.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.203, "ast_prob": 0.399, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00117", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY OR THE WHITE BOX HAS AT MOST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT MOST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is always true because the White box contains only one statement, so it cannot have more than two true statements.  \nIf K1 were true, then B1 and K1 would both be true, making “at most one statement is true” false. So K1 is false.  \nSince K1 is false, at least two statements must be true; B1 is already true, so W1 must be true.  \nW1 says the boxes next to Black contain gems. Black is on the right, so only White is next to it. Therefore White contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.559, "ast_prob": 0.439, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00118", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS BLUE AND A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The box next to the BLACK box is the WHITE box.  \n- W1 says “The BLACK box is BLUE and a box next to the BLACK box contains the gems.” Since the BLACK box is not BLUE, the conjunction is false, so W1 = F. Thus WHITE is an all-false box.  \n- K1 says “A box next to the BLACK box contains the gems,” which means the WHITE box contains the gems. So K1 = T iff gems are in WHITE.  \n- B1 says “The statement above this one is true,” but there is no statement above it in the BLUE box, so B1 = F.  \n- Since at least one box must be all-true, and BLUE cannot be all-true, BLACK must be all-true. Therefore K1 = T, so the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.229, "ast_prob": 0.498, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00119", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT MOST TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is true because the White box is explicitly the middle box. So the Blue box is true.\n\nLet W1 and K1 be the truth values of the White and Black statements.  \nK1 says “at most two statements are true.” Since B1 is already true:\n\n- If K1 were false, then more than two statements would be true, meaning all three are true, making K1 true — contradiction.\n- So K1 must be true.\n- With B1 true and K1 true, there are already two true statements. Therefore W1 must be false for K1’s “at most two” claim to hold.\n\nSo: Blue and Black boxes are true, White box is false.  \nW1 says “the gems are in a box that is true,” but W1 is false, so the gems must be in the only false box: White.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.503, "ast_prob": 0.219, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00120", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS AND THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLACK\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n- BLUE is left, WHITE is middle, BLACK is right.\n- B1: The only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so B1 is true exactly when WHITE contains the gems.\n- W1: The box next to BLACK is WHITE, so the first part would be true only if WHITE contains gems. But the second part, “BLUE is the middle box,” is false. So W1 is always false.\n- K1: “The WHITE box is BLACK” is false, so K1 is always false.\n- Thus WHITE and BLACK are already all-false boxes. To satisfy “at least one box all-true,” BLUE must be all-true, so B1 must be true, meaning WHITE contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.468, "ast_prob": 0.29, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00121", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX HAS AT LEAST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "White has only one statement, so there is no statement above W1. Therefore W1 is false.\n\nSince W1 is false, at least one other box must be all-true. B1 and K1 are linked: B1 says Black has a true statement, which is exactly whether K1 is true. So B1 and K1 must both be true to satisfy “at least one all-true box.”\n\nK1 is therefore true, meaning the gems are in a box that is false. The only false box is White. So gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.686, "ast_prob": 0.418, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00122", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX HAS AT MOST ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLACK BOX ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n\n1. Blue has only one statement, B1. If B1 were false, Blue would contain exactly one false statement, making “at most one false statement” true. Contradiction. So B1 = T.\n2. White’s W1 says all Black statements are true. Black has only K1, so W1 is true exactly when K1 is true: W1 ↔ K1.\n3. At least one box must be all-false. Blue is all-true, so at least one of White/Black must be all-false. Since W1 and K1 have the same truth value, they must both be false. So W1 = F and K1 = F.\n4. K1 says a box next to White contains gems. The boxes next to White are Blue and Black. Since K1 is false, neither Blue nor Black has the gems. Therefore the gems must be in White.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.56, "ast_prob": 0.203, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00123", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n- BLUE is left, WHITE is middle, BLACK is right.\n- The only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so B1 is true exactly when WHITE contains the gems.\n- W1 and K1 both say “B1, W1, and K1 are all true.”\n\nIf the gems were in BLUE or BLACK, then B1 would be false, so W1 and K1 would also be false. That would make every statement false, violating the rule that at least one box must be all-true.\n\nTherefore the gems must be in WHITE, making B1 true.  \nWith B1 true, W1 and K1 cannot both be true, because then every box would be all-true, violating the rule that at least one box must be all-false. So W1 and K1 are false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.73, "ast_prob": 0.23, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00124", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "- Exactly one box contains the gems.\n- W1 says the boxes next to WHITE (BLUE and BLACK) contain gems. Since only one box contains gems, both adjacent boxes cannot contain gems, so W1 is false.\n- The WHITE box therefore has all its statements false, satisfying the “at least one all-false” rule.\n- We still need at least one all-true box. B1 and K1 are both true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.\n- If the gems were not in WHITE, then B1 and K1 would both be false, leaving no all-true box.\n- Therefore the gems must be in WHITE, making B1 true and K1 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.224, "ast_prob": 0.412, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00125", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because the blue box is on the left, not the middle.  \nIf the gems were in BLUE or BLACK, then both W1 and K1 would be false, making every statement false and violating the “at least one all-true box” rule.  \nTherefore the gems must be in WHITE, which makes W1 and K1 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.53, "ast_prob": 0.214, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00126", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT MOST TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because no box has two false statements.  \nW1 is true because not all three statements are true.  \nK1 is false because the gems are in the true WHITE box.  \n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.643, "ast_prob": 0.27, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00127", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS BLUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS WHITE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is on the BLUE box and says “THIS BOX IS BLUE,” so B1 is true.  \nW1 is on the WHITE box and says “THE WHITE BOX IS WHITE,” so W1 is true.  \n\nSince at least one box must have all statements false, and each box has only one statement, at least one statement must be false. B1 and W1 are true, so K1 must be false.  \n\nK1 = “THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY” is false, meaning the WHITE box is not empty. Since exactly one box contains the gems, the gems must be in the WHITE box.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.335, "ast_prob": 0.362, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00128", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let each box contain one statement, so a box is all-true iff its single statement is true, and all-false iff its single statement is false.\n\n- B1: The White box has only one statement, W1, so it cannot have “at least two false statements.” Thus B1 = FALSE.\n- K1: The boxes next to the White box are BLUE and BLACK. Since exactly one box contains gems, both adjacent boxes cannot contain gems. Thus K1 = FALSE.\n- To satisfy “at least one box has all statements true,” at least one of B1, W1, K1 must be true. Since B1 and K1 are false, W1 must be true.\n- W1 says “Boxes next to the BLUE box contain gems.” The only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so W1 true means the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.464, "ast_prob": 0.145, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00129", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS WHITE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EXACTLY THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n\n1. K1 claims “Exactly three statements are false.”  \n   If K1 were true, then K1 itself would not be false, so at most two statements could be false. That contradicts K1. Therefore K1 must be false.\n\n2. W1 says “The black box is white.” The black box is black, so W1 is false.\n\n3. Since K1 is false, not all three statements can be false. So at least one of B1 or W1 must be true. W1 is false, so B1 must be true.\n\n4. B1 says “Boxes next to the black box contain gems.” The black box is on the right, so the only box next to it is the middle WHITE box. Thus the gems are in WHITE.\n\n5. Final truth values: B1 = T, W1 = F, K1 = F.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.421, "ast_prob": 0.135, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00130", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS OR BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is logically “P or P”, so it reduces to P.  \nP is “boxes next to the white box contain gems.” Since exactly one box contains gems, the two boxes next to the white box cannot both contain gems, so P is false.  \nThus B1 = False.\n\nW1 says “The statement above this one is true,” meaning W1 = B1.  \nSo W1 = False.\n\nK1 says “The white box contains the gems,” so K1 is true exactly when the gems are in the WHITE box.\n\nAt least one statement must be true. B1 and W1 are false, so K1 must be true.  \nTherefore the gems are in the WHITE box, and K1 = True.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.251, "ast_prob": 0.28, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00131", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS BLUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because the black box is black, not blue.  \nSo the BLUE box is all-false.\n\nW1 says B1, W1, and K1 are all true. Since B1 is false, W1 cannot be true; therefore W1 is false.  \nSo the WHITE box is also all-false.\n\nSince at least one box must be all-true, the BLACK box must be the all-true box, so K1 must be true.\n\nK1 is true only if a box next to the BLACK box contains the gems. The BLACK box is on the right, so its only neighbor is the WHITE box. Therefore the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.465, "ast_prob": 0.423, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00132", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS BLUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is false, because the BLACK box is not the BLUE box.\n\nB1 is true only if the gems are in BLUE, because it claims the other two boxes are empty.\n\nNow test gem locations:\n\n- If gems were in BLACK: B1 would be false, K1 false. W1 would need to be true to have any all-true box, but W1 would claim the no-false-statement box has gems, which would not be BLACK. Contradiction.\n- If gems were in BLUE: B1 true, K1 false. If W1 is true, then both BLUE and WHITE would have no false statements, so there would not be a unique “box with no false statements” as W1 requires. If W1 is false, then BLUE would be the unique no-false-statement box and would contain gems, making W1 true. Contradiction.\n- Therefore gems must be in WHITE.\n\nWith gems in WHITE: B1 is false, K1 is false. W1 must be true, making WHITE the unique box with no false statements, and WHITE has the gems. This satisfies all rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.79, "ast_prob": 0.418, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00133", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT AT MOST TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS BLUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is false because the black box is not blue.  \nB1 says “it is not true that at most two statements are true,” which would require all three statements to be true. Since K1 is false, B1 must also be false.  \nAt least one box must be all-true, so W1 must be true.  \nW1 true means the two boxes beside the white box are empty, so the gems are in the white box.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.493, "ast_prob": 0.349, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00134", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 says “the statement above this one is true,” but there is no statement above B1, so B1 is false.\n\nSince at least one box must have all statements true, at least one of W1 or K1 must be true.  \n- W1 is true only if the gems are in WHITE.  \n- K1 is true only if the gems are in WHITE.  \n\nSo the gems must be in WHITE, making both W1 and K1 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.367, "ast_prob": 0.213, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00135", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 says “the statement above this one is true,” but there is no statement above B1, so B1 cannot be true. Therefore B1 is false.\n\nSince B1 is false, the statement “All statements on the blue box are true” is also false, so W1 is false.\n\nWith Blue and White both all-false, at least one box must be all-true. That must be the Black box, so K1 must be true.\n\nK1 says “The White box contains the gems,” so the gems must be in the White box.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.358, "ast_prob": 0.466, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00137", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS AND THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let B1, W1, K1 be the truth values of the three statements.\n\n1. B1 says the White box has at least two true statements. But the White box has only one statement, W1, so this is impossible. Therefore B1 is FALSE. So the Blue box is all-false.\n\n2. At least one box must be all-true, so at least one of W1 or K1 must be TRUE.\n\n3. Suppose W1 were TRUE. Then the White box has no false statements. W1 says “the box with no false statements has the gems,” so if W1 is true, White itself would have to be the unique no-false box containing the gems. But if gems are in White, then Blue is empty, making K1 TRUE, which would make Black also a no-false box. That contradicts W1’s definite “the box” claim. So W1 cannot be TRUE; therefore W1 is FALSE.\n\n4. Since W1 is FALSE and at least one statement must be true, K1 must be TRUE. K1 says “The Blue box is empty,” so the gems are not in Blue.\n\n5. With B1 FALSE and W1 FALSE, the only no-false box is Black, because K1 is TRUE. W1 says the no-false box has the gems, but W1 is FALSE, so the no-false box Black does NOT have the gems. Therefore the gems must be in White.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.459, "ast_prob": 0.132, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00138", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step by step:\n- The BLUE box is leftmost, so the only box next to it is WHITE. Thus B1 is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.\n- The BLACK box is rightmost, so the only box next to it is WHITE. Thus K1 is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.\n- The WHITE box is in the middle, with no statement physically above it. Therefore W1 is false.\n- Since at least one box must be all-true, the gems cannot be in BLUE or BLACK, because then B1 and K1 would also be false, leaving no true statement.\n- Therefore the gems must be in WHITE, making B1 true and K1 true, while W1 remains false. This satisfies at least one all-true box and at least one all-false box.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.731, "ast_prob": 0.349, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00140", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] AT MOST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS WHITE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the statements be:\n\n- \\(B1\\): \"At most one statement is true\"\n- \\(W1\\): \"The White box is white\"\n- \\(K1\\): \"A box next to this box contains the gems\"\n\nFirst, \\(W1\\) is true because the middle box is indeed the White box.\n\nSo:\n- \\(W1 = T\\)\n\nNow consider \\(K1\\). The Black box is on the right, so its only neighboring box is the White box. Therefore \\(K1\\) is true exactly when the White box contains the gems.\n\nLet \\(K1 = T\\) if gems are in WHITE, otherwise \\(K1 = F\\).\n\nNow consider \\(B1\\): \"At most one statement is true.\" Since \\(W1\\) is already true, the total number of true statements is at least 1.\n\nIf the gems are not in WHITE, then \\(K1 = F\\), so we would need \\(B1\\) to be the only true statement. But then the total true statements would include \\(W1\\) and \\(B1\\), making two true statements, so \\(B1\\) would be false. Contradiction.\n\nTherefore the gems must be in WHITE, making \\(K1 = T\\). Then the true statements are \\(W1\\) and \\(K1\\), so there are two true statements, making \\(B1\\) false.\n\nThus:\n- Gems: WHITE\n- \\(B1 = F\\)\n- \\(W1 = T\\)\n- \\(K1 = T\\)\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.71, "ast_prob": 0.341, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00141", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is true if and only if the White box contains the gems, since White is the only box next to Blue.  \nW1 is always false because Black is on the right, not the middle.  \nK1 is false because the White box has only one statement total (W1), and that statement is false, so it cannot have at least two true statements.\n\n- If gems were in BLUE: B1 false, W1 false, K1 false → no all-true box, invalid.\n- If gems were in WHITE: B1 true, W1 false, K1 false → Blue is all-true, White and Black are all-false, valid.\n- If gems were in BLACK: B1 false, W1 false, K1 false → no all-true box, invalid.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.389, "ast_prob": 0.409, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00142", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS AND THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. There is only one statement on the BLACK box: K1.  \n   K1 claims the BLACK box has at least two false statements, but it has only one statement total. So K1 must be FALSE.\n\n2. W1 says: “Boxes next to the WHITE box contain gems and the statement above this one is true.”  \n   The boxes next to WHITE are BLUE and BLACK. With exactly one gem, both BLUE and BLACK cannot contain gems. So the first part of W1 is FALSE, making W1 FALSE.\n\n3. B1 says: “A box next to the BLUE box contains the gems.”  \n   The only box next to BLUE is WHITE. So B1 is TRUE if and only if WHITE contains the gems.\n\n4. If the gems were in BLUE or BLACK, then B1 would be FALSE. Then all three statements would be false, leaving no all-true box, which violates the rule that at least one box must be all-true.\n\n5. Therefore B1 must be TRUE, so WHITE contains the gems.\n\nFinal check:\n- BLUE box: B1 = T → all-true.\n- WHITE box: W1 = F → all-false.\n- BLACK box: K1 = F → all-false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.312, "ast_prob": 0.265, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00143", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "There is one statement per box, so each box’s “top statement” is its only statement.\n\n- K1 says “The blue box is the middle box.” Since blue is left, K1 is **false**.\n- B1 says B1, W1, and K1 are all true. Because K1 is false, B1 must be **false**.\n- W1 says “Boxes next to the black box contain gems.” The black box is on the right, so its only adjacent box is WHITE. Thus W1 is true exactly when WHITE contains the gems.\n- Since at least one box must be all-true, and B1 and K1 are false, W1 must be true.\n- Therefore WHITE contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.222, "ast_prob": 0.42, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00144", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS AND THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step by step:\n\n1. The BLUE box is on the left, not the middle, so B1 is false.  \n   Thus the BLUE box is all-false.\n\n2. W1 says “the statement above this one is true.” The statement above W1 is B1, which is false.  \n   So W1 is false, and the WHITE box is also all-false.\n\n3. We already have at least one all-false box. We still need at least one all-true box. Since BLUE and WHITE are all-false, the BLACK box must be all-true, so K1 must be true.\n\n4. K1 says the boxes next to the BLUE box contain gems and the BLACK box is empty. The only box next to BLUE is WHITE.  \n   So K1 is true exactly when WHITE contains the gems and BLACK is empty.\n\n5. Therefore, the gems are in the WHITE box.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.505, "ast_prob": 0.38, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00145", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(b, w, k\\) be the truth values of B1, W1, K1.\n\n- K1 is true iff the WHITE box has the gems, since only WHITE is next to BLUE.\n- B1 and W1 are identical statements, so they must have the same truth value.\n\nIf the gems were in BLUE or BLACK, then K1 would be false. To satisfy “at least one true,” B1 and W1 would both have to be true. But then both BLUE and WHITE would be boxes with no false statements, so “THE box with no false statements” would not be unique, making B1/W1 false. Contradiction.\n\nTherefore the gems must be in WHITE, so K1 is true. Since at least one statement must be false and B1/W1 have the same truth value, B1 and W1 must both be false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.377, "ast_prob": 0.325, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00146", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX HAS EXACTLY ONE TRUE STATEMENT AND THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS BLUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because it contains “the blue box is the middle box,” which is false, so the whole conjunction is false.  \nK1 is false because the black box is not blue.  \nThus BLUE and BLACK are both all-false. Since at least one box must be all-true, WHITE must be all-true, so W1 is true.  \nW1 says the gems are in a true box, and WHITE is the only true box, so the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.465, "ast_prob": 0.47, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00147", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS WHITE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS BLACK\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 and K1 are false because the blue box is actually blue, not white or black.  \nW1 is true only if the white box contains the gems.\n\n- If gems are in BLUE: W1=false, so no box has all statements true → invalid.\n- If gems are in WHITE: W1=true; BLUE is all-false, WHITE is all-true, BLACK is all-false → valid.\n- If gems are in BLACK: W1=false, so no box has all statements true → invalid.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.454, "ast_prob": 0.138, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00148", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS AND THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EXACTLY ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(b,w,k\\) be the truth values of B1, W1, K1.\n\n- K1 is true iff the gems are in BLUE or BLACK, i.e. not in WHITE.\n- B1 requires both: gems in WHITE, and all three statements true.\n- W1 says exactly one of the three statements is true.\n\nIf the gems are in BLUE or BLACK, then K1 is true and B1 is false. That makes W1 impossible: if W1 is false, then exactly one statement is true, so W1 would be true; if W1 is true, then two statements are true, so W1 would be false.\n\nSo the gems must be in WHITE. Then K1 is false and B1 is false. W1 is true because exactly one statement — W1 itself — is true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.466, "ast_prob": 0.327, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00044", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY AND THIS BOX HAS AT LEAST ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 cannot be true: K1 says “Blue and Black are empty AND K1 is false,” so if K1 were true it would assert its own falsity. Therefore K1 is false. Since K1 is false, its second clause “this box has at least one false statement” is true, so its first clause must be false: it is not true that both Blue and Black are empty. Thus the gems are not in White.\n\nW1 says “the statement above this one is true,” but there is no statement above the White box’s single statement. So W1 is false.\n\nWhite and Black are now all-false boxes. At least one box must be all-true, so Blue must be all-true, meaning B1 is true. The only box with no false statements is therefore Blue, so B1’s statement means Blue has the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.734, "ast_prob": 0.274, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00109", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE AND THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(B1, W1, K1\\) be the truth values of the three statements.\n\n- \\(W1\\) says the other two boxes are empty, so \\(W1\\) is true exactly when the gems are in the WHITE box.\n- \\(K1\\) says “the statement above this one is true,” but the black box contains only \\(K1\\), with no statement above it. So \\(K1\\) is false.\n- Since \\(K1\\) is false, the black box is not true. Therefore \\(B1\\), which requires “the black box is true,” is false.\n- Now Blue and Black are all-false boxes. To satisfy “at least one box has all statements true,” White must be all-true, so \\(W1\\) must be true.\n- Therefore the gems are in the WHITE box.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.647, "ast_prob": 0.128, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00149", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE AND THE BLUE BOX IS BLUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY AND THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX HAS AT LEAST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 refers to a statement above it, but there is no statement above it, so that part is false; “the blue box is blue” is true, making B1 false.  \nSince BLUE contains only B1, K1 (“the blue box has at least one true statement”) is also false.  \nAt least one box must be all-true, and only WHITE remains, so W1 must be true.  \nW1 says BLACK is empty and the box with no false statements has the gems. Since W1 is the only true statement, WHITE is the box with no false statements, so WHITE must contain the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.382, "ast_prob": 0.393, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00150", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "- BLUE is stated to be the left box, so B1 (“The blue box is the middle box”) is **false**.\n- BLACK is stated to be the right box, so K1 (“The black box is the middle box”) is **false**.\n- Since at least one box must be all-true, and BLUE and BLACK both have false statements, the all-true box must be WHITE. Thus W1 must be **true**.\n- W1 says “Boxes next to the blue box contain gems.” The only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so W1 is true exactly when WHITE contains the gems.\n- Therefore the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.63, "ast_prob": 0.159, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00151", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE AND THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n- B1 = “The black box is true” = K1\n- K1 = “A box next to black contains the gems” = gems are in WHITE, since White is the only box next to Black\n- W1 = “All statements on the White box are true and the gems are in a box that is true”\n\nIf gems are in BLUE:\n- K1 = False → B1 = False\n- White box’s statement would require gems to be in a true box, but Blue is false, so W1 = False\n- All three boxes false → violates “at least one all-true”\n\nIf gems are in BLACK:\n- K1 = False → B1 = False\n- Black is false, so W1 = False\n- All three boxes false → violates “at least one all-true”\n\nIf gems are in WHITE:\n- K1 = True → B1 = True\n- White box is false because W1 = False, so White is the required all-false box\n- Blue and Black are true, satisfying at least one all-true and at least one all-false\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.663, "ast_prob": 0.432, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00152", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n\n1. BLUE is leftmost, so the only box next to BLUE is WHITE. Therefore B1 is true exactly when W1 is true: \\(B1 = W1\\).\n2. W1 says the other two boxes relative to WHITE are empty. Since exactly one box has the gems, W1 is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE: \\(W1 = (\\text{gem}=WHITE)\\).\n3. BLACK has only one statement, so there is no statement above K1. Thus K1’s claim is false: \\(K1 = F\\).\n4. If the gems were not in WHITE, then W1 would be false, so B1 would also be false, and K1 is false — all boxes false, violating the rule that at least one box is all-true.\n5. Therefore the gems must be in WHITE, making W1 true, B1 true, and K1 false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.789, "ast_prob": 0.365, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00153", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n\n1. **B1**: “The other two boxes relative to the White box” means the Blue and Black boxes. So B1 is true iff both Blue and Black are empty, which means the gems are in WHITE.\n2. **K1**: “Boxes next to the Blue box” means the White box only, since Blue is leftmost. So K1 is true iff the gems are in WHITE.\n3. **W1**: The White box contains only one statement, so there is no statement above W1. Thus “the statement above this one is true” is FALSE.\n4. If gems are in WHITE, then B1=T and K1=T, while W1=F. This gives at least one all-true box (Blue/Black) and at least one all-false box (White).\n5. If gems were in BLUE or BLACK, then B1 and K1 would both be false, and W1 would also be false, leaving no all-true box. So only gem=WHITE works.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.567, "ast_prob": 0.167, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00154", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE WHITE BOX HAS AT MOST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n\n1. The WHITE box contains only statement W1, so it always has at most one true statement.  \n   Therefore W1’s claim “It is not true that the WHITE box has at most one true statement” is false.  \n   So W1 = F.\n\n2. B1 says “The WHITE box is true.” Since W1 is false, the WHITE box is not true.  \n   So B1 = F.\n\n3. We need at least one box with all statements true. BLUE and WHITE are both false, so BLACK must be true.  \n   Therefore K1 = T.\n\n4. K1 says “A box next to this box contains the gems.” BLACK is the rightmost box, so its only adjacent box is WHITE.  \n   Thus the gems must be in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.267, "ast_prob": 0.124, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00156", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT LEAST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE AND THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "BLUE is left, WHITE is middle, BLACK is right.\n\n- W1 says “THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX,” which is false, so W1 is false regardless of the first part. Thus WHITE is all-false.\n- B1 says boxes next to BLUE contain gems. The only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so B1 is true iff the gems are in WHITE.\n- K1 says boxes next to BLACK contain gems. The only box next to BLACK is WHITE, so K1 is true iff the gems are in WHITE.\n- Since W1 is false and at least one statement must be true, at least one of B1/K1 must be true, so the gems must be in WHITE.\n- Therefore B1 and K1 are both true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.282, "ast_prob": 0.124, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00157", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 refers to a statement above it, but there is no statement above B1, so B1 is false.  \nW1 refers to the statement above it, which is B1; since B1 is false, W1 is also false.  \nThus BLUE and WHITE are all-false boxes. Since at least one box must be all-true, BLACK must be all-true, so K1 is true.  \nK1 says a box next to BLACK contains the gems; BLACK is rightmost, so the only adjacent box is WHITE. Therefore the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHI