{"id": "direct_ft_train_00158", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EITHER THE BLUE BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO FALSE STATEMENTS OR THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.584, "ast_prob": 0.382, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00160", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS BLACK\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS BLUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is true because the black box is indeed black.  \nK1 is true because the blue box is indeed blue.  \n\nSo the BLUE and BLACK boxes are already all-true boxes.  \nTo satisfy the rule that at least one box must have all statements false, WHITE's statement W1 must be false.  \n\nW1 says “A box next to this box contains the gems.” The boxes next to WHITE are BLUE and BLACK. For W1 to be false, the gems cannot be in BLUE or BLACK, so they must be in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.209, "ast_prob": 0.265, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00161", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY OR THIS BOX HAS AT LEAST ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE AND AT MOST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EITHER THE BLACK BOX IS BLACK OR THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let B1 be the statement on the BLUE box, W1 on WHITE, K1 on BLACK.\n\n- B1 says: “Either BLUE and BLACK are empty, or B1 is false.” If B1 were false, then its second clause would be true, making B1 true — contradiction. So B1 must be true. Therefore the first clause must be true: BLUE and BLACK are empty, so the gems are in WHITE.\n- K1 says: “Either the BLACK box is black, or the gems are in a true box.” Since the BLACK box is indeed black, K1 is true.\n- W1 says: “All top statements are true, and at most one statement is true.” But B1 and K1 are both true, so if W1 were true there would be three true statements, contradicting “at most one statement is true.” Therefore W1 is false.\n\nThis satisfies the rules: BLUE all-true, WHITE all-false, BLACK all-true, gems in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.655, "ast_prob": 0.458, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00162", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(B, W, K\\) be the truth values of B1, W1, K1. Since each box has exactly one statement, a box is “true” exactly when its statement is true.\n\n- If there were exactly one all-true box, then B1 and K1 would both assert that the gems are in that unique all-true box.  \n  - If the gems are there, then both B1 and K1 are true, making Blue and Black both all-true — contradiction.  \n  - If the gems are not there, then B1 and K1 are both false, so Blue and Black are false; the only all-true box would have to be White, but then White’s statement says the gems are in White, contradiction.  \n  So there cannot be exactly one all-true box.\n\n- Therefore there must be two all-true boxes and one all-false box.\n\n- Since B1 refers to “the box with no false statements” as a unique box, B1 is false when there are two all-true boxes. So Blue is the all-false box.\n\n- Thus the two all-true boxes are White and Black: W1 = T and K1 = T.\n\n- W1 says White contains the gems, so the gems are in White.\n\n- Check: K1 says the gems are in a true box. White is true and contains the gems, so K1 = T. B1 is false because there is no unique all-true box.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.508, "ast_prob": 0.403, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00163", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT MOST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is false because the blue box is on the left, not the middle.\n\nLet B1, W1, K1 be the truth values. Since K1 is false, the black box is all-false.  \nW1 says “at most one statement is true.” If W1 were false, then more than one statement would be true, but with K1 already false that would require both B1 and W1 true, which is impossible if W1 is false. So W1 must be true.\n\nWith W1 true, at most one statement is true. Since W1 itself is true, B1 must be false and K1 is false. So exactly one statement is true.\n\nB1 says “the gems are in a box that is false.” Since B1 is false, the gems are NOT in a false box. The false boxes are BLUE and BLACK. The true box is WHITE. Therefore the gems must be in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.429, "ast_prob": 0.103, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00164", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- \\(B1\\) = statement on BLUE box  \n- \\(W1\\) = statement on WHITE box  \n- \\(K1\\) = statement on BLACK box  \n\n\\(B1\\) and \\(K1\\) both say: “The gems are in a box that is false.”\n\nIf the gems were in BLUE, then \\(B1\\) would be true exactly if BLUE’s statement were false, i.e. \\(B1 = \\neg B1\\), impossible.  \nIf the gems were in BLACK, then \\(K1\\) would be true exactly if BLACK’s statement were false, i.e. \\(K1 = \\neg K1\\), impossible.  \n\nSo the gems must be in WHITE.\n\nThen \\(B1\\) and \\(K1\\) are true iff WHITE’s statement \\(W1\\) is false. So:\n\n\\[\nB1 = K1 = \\neg W1\n\\]\n\n\\(W1\\) says: “The top statement of each box is true,” meaning:\n\n\\[\nW1 = B1 \\land W1 \\land K1\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(B1 = K1 = \\neg W1\\):\n\n\\[\nW1 = (\\neg W1) \\land W1 \\land (\\neg W1) = \\text{false}\n\\]\n\nTherefore \\(W1 = F\\), so \\(B1 = T\\) and \\(K1 = T\\).\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.751, "ast_prob": 0.165, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00165", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS WHITE AND A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Positions: BLUE is left, WHITE is middle, BLACK is right.\n\n- K1 says “the BLUE box is the middle box.” This is false, so K1 = F.\n- W1 says “the BLACK box is WHITE and a box next to the BLUE box contains the gems.” The first part is false, so the whole conjunction is false, so W1 = F.\n- Since at least one box must have all true statements, and W1 and K1 are both false, B1 must be true.\n- B1 says “boxes next to the BLACK box contain gems.” BLACK is on the right, so its only neighboring box is WHITE. Therefore B1 is true iff WHITE contains the gems. Since B1 = T, the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.422, "ast_prob": 0.457, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00166", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY AND ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the boxes be in order: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right).\n\n- B1: “A box next to the BLACK box contains the gems.”  \n  The only box next to BLACK is WHITE, so B1 is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.  \n  So B1 = (gem = WHITE).\n\n- W1: “The WHITE box is empty AND all statements on the BLUE box are true.”  \n  The WHITE box is empty means gem ≠ WHITE.  \n  All statements on BLUE true means B1 is true, i.e. gem = WHITE.  \n  These cannot both be true, so W1 is always FALSE.\n\n- K1: “Boxes next to the BLUE box contain gems.”  \n  The only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so K1 is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.  \n  So K1 = (gem = WHITE).\n\nNow test gem locations:\n\n- If gem = BLUE: B1 = F, W1 = F, K1 = F. No true statement, violating the rule that at least one box is all-true.\n- If gem = BLACK: B1 = F, W1 = F, K1 = F. Same violation.\n- If gem = WHITE: B1 = T, W1 = F, K1 = T. There is at least one true box and at least one false box.\n\nThus the only consistent solution is gems in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.516, "ast_prob": 0.273, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00167", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLACK\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1: “The WHITE box is BLACK” is false, since the middle box is WHITE, not BLACK. So the BLUE box is all-false.\n\nK1: The only box next to the BLUE box is the WHITE box, so K1 is true exactly when the gems are in the WHITE box.\n\nW1: “The box with no false statements has the gems” refers to the unique all-true box. If W1 were true, the WHITE box would be all-true and would have to contain the gems. But if the gems were in WHITE, then K1 would also be true, making BLACK all-true too—so WHITE would not be the unique all-true box. Contradiction. Therefore W1 is false.\n\nSo WHITE is all-false. To satisfy the rule that at least one box is all-true, BLACK must be all-true, so K1 is true. Therefore the gems are in WHITE.\n\nCheck: B1 false, W1 false, K1 true. BLUE all-false, WHITE all-false, BLACK all-true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.617, "ast_prob": 0.223, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00168", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the boxes be in order: BLUE, WHITE, BLACK.\n\n- B1 says a box next to BLUE contains the gems. The only box next to BLUE is WHITE.  \n  So B1 is true iff the gems are in WHITE.\n\n- K1 says a box next to BLACK contains the gems. The only box next to BLACK is WHITE.  \n  So K1 is true iff the gems are in WHITE.\n\n- W1 says “The top statement of each box is true”, meaning B1, W1, and K1 are all true.  \n  So W1 = (B1 and W1 and K1). This allows W1 to be false while B1 and K1 are true.\n\nNow test gem locations:\n\n1. If gems are in BLUE: B1 = F, K1 = F, and W1 becomes F. All statements false, violating “at least one box all true”.\n2. If gems are in BLACK: same as above, B1 = F, K1 = F, W1 = F. Violates “at least one box all true”.\n3. If gems are in WHITE: B1 = T and K1 = T. W1 can be false, giving exactly one false statement. This satisfies at least one true and at least one false.\n\nThus the unique valid solution is:\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.358, "ast_prob": 0.362, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00169", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS WHITE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX AND THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS AND A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false: it says the BLUE box is WHITE, but the BLUE box is not WHITE.\n\nW1 is false: it includes “THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX,” but the BLUE box is on the left, not the middle. So the conjunction is false.\n\nK1 says both “a box next to this box contains the gems” and “a box next to the BLUE box contains the gems.” For the BLACK box, the only adjacent box is WHITE. For the BLUE box, the only adjacent box is also WHITE. So K1 is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.\n\nSince B1 and W1 are false, at least one box must be all-true, so K1 must be true. Therefore the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.373, "ast_prob": 0.346, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00170", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n- B1 = “The box next to BLUE contains gems” → since BLUE is leftmost, only WHITE is next to it. So B1 is true iff WHITE contains gems.\n- K1 = “The WHITE box is true” → K1 has the same truth value as W1, so K1 = W1.\n- W1 = “The top statement of each box is true” → W1 asserts B1, W1, and K1 are all true. Since K1 = W1, this becomes: W1 is true iff B1 and W1 are true.\n\nSolving W1:\n- If W1 is true, then B1 must also be true.\n- If W1 is false, the statement is false regardless of B1.\n\nSo possible truth pairs for (B1, W1) are:\n- (T, T) → all three statements true, violates “at least one false.”\n- (F, F) → all three statements false, violates “at least one true.”\n- (T, F) → satisfies both conditions.\n\nTherefore:\nB1 = T, W1 = F, K1 = F, and since B1 means WHITE contains gems, the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.744, "ast_prob": 0.101, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00171", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the actual gem location determine W1 directly:\n\n- W1 is true iff the gems are in the WHITE box.\n- K1 says “ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE,” and since BLUE has only B1, we get K1 = B1.\n- B1 says “THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE,” meaning B1, W1, and K1 are all true. So B1 is true iff (B1 ∧ W1 ∧ K1).\n\nSubstitute K1 = B1 into B1’s condition:\n\n- B1 iff (B1 ∧ W1)\n- Therefore, if W1 is false, B1 must also be false, and then K1 is false too. That would make all three statements false, violating the rule that at least one box is all-true.\n- So W1 must be true, meaning the gems are in the WHITE box.\n\nWith W1 true, B1 could in principle be true or false, but if B1 were true then K1 would also be true, making all three statements true, violating the rule that at least one box is all-false. Therefore B1 is false, and so K1 is false.\n\nFinal check: W1 is true because gems are in WHITE; B1 is false because not all top statements are true; K1 is false because not all statements on BLUE are true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.344, "ast_prob": 0.125, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00172", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS OR A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX IS NOT EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Since each box has only one statement, no box can have “two false statements,” so the first half of B1 is false.  \nThus B1 is true only if a box next to BLUE is true.\n\nIf K1 were true, it would require B1, W1, and K1 all to be true, making every statement true — but the rules require at least one all-false box. So K1 must be false.\n\nWith K1 false, B1 is true iff W1 is true. If W1 were false, then B1 would also be false and all statements would be false, violating the rule that at least one box is all-true. Therefore W1 is true, so WHITE has the gems. Then B1 is true because the adjacent WHITE box is true, and K1 is false because not all top statements are true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.582, "ast_prob": 0.294, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00173", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX IS BLUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- B1 = “boxes next to this box contain gems.” For the BLUE box, the only box next to it is WHITE, so B1 is true iff WHITE contains the gems.\n- W1 = “this box is BLUE.” The WHITE box is not BLUE, so W1 is false.\n- K1 = “the top statement of each box is true.” This means B1, W1, and K1 would all have to be true. But W1 is false, so K1 cannot be true. Therefore K1 is false.\n\nSo W1 = F and K1 = F.  \nAt least one box must be all-true. The only possible all-true box is BLUE, so B1 must be true.  \nTherefore WHITE contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.768, "ast_prob": 0.442, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00174", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE AND THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER AT LEAST THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE OR THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Each box has exactly one statement, so a “true box” means its single statement is true, and a “false box” means its single statement is false.\n\n- K1 says “the box with two false statements has the gems.” But no box has two false statements because each box has only one statement. Therefore K1 is false.\n- B1 says “a box next to this box is true and the statement above this one is true.” There is no statement above B1, so the second part is false. Therefore B1 is false.\n- Now Blue and Black are all-false boxes. Since at least one box must be all-true, White must be true, so W1 must be true.\n- With B1 and K1 false, there are only 2 false statements, so the first part of W1 (“at least three statements are false”) is false. Thus the second part must be true: the gems must be in a true box. The only true box is White.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.77, "ast_prob": 0.396, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00175", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Each box has only one statement, so a box is all-true iff its single statement is true, and all-false iff its single statement is false.\n\n- B1 says the Black box has exactly two false statements, but the Black box has only one statement. So B1 can never be true: **B1 = F**.\n- K1 says boxes next to the Black box contain gems. Black’s only adjacent box is White, so **K1 = T iff gems are in White**.\n- W1 says “the box with no false statements has the gems.” Since B1 is false, Blue is not no-false. The only possible no-false boxes are White and Black.\n  - If W1 = T and K1 = F, then the only no-false box is White, so W1 would require gems in White, but K1 = F means gems are not in White. Contradiction.\n  - If W1 = T and K1 = T, then both White and Black have no false statements, so there is no single “the box” with no false statements. W1 cannot be true.\n  - If W1 = F and K1 = T, then the unique no-false box is Black, and gems are in White, so W1 is false. Consistent.\n  - Both false would leave no all-true box, violating the rules.\n\nTherefore gems are in White, B1 is false, W1 is false, and K1 is true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.401, "ast_prob": 0.336, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00176", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EXACTLY THREE STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(B, W, K\\) be the truth values of B1, W1, K1.\n\n- B1 is true only if all three statements are true.\n- Since at least one statement must be true and at least one must be false, the all-true and all-false cases are impossible.\n- The possible remaining truth triples are: \\((F,T,T)\\), \\((F,T,F)\\), \\((F,F,T)\\).\n\nCheck each:\n\n1. \\((F,F,T)\\): K1 true means Black has the gems. The only no-false box is Black, so W1 would be true — contradiction.\n2. \\((F,T,T)\\): K1 true means Black has the gems. But there are two no-false boxes, White and Black, so “the box with no false statements” is not unique and W1 cannot be true — contradiction.\n3. \\((F,T,F)\\): K1 false means Black does not have gems. The only no-false box is White, and W1 says that box has the gems, so White must have the gems. This is consistent.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.566, "ast_prob": 0.195, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00177", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE AND THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(G\\) be the gem box, and let each box’s statement determine whether that box is “true.”\n\n- W1: “The other two boxes relative to the white box are empty” means BLUE and BLACK are empty. Since exactly one box has the gems, this is equivalent to: gem is in WHITE.  \n  So \\(W1 = T\\) iff \\(G = WHITE\\).\n\n- K1: “The white box contains the gems” means \\(K1 = T\\) iff \\(G = WHITE\\).\n\n- B1: “A box next to this box is true and the white box is empty.” The only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so “a box next to this box is true” means W1 is true. Also WHITE must be empty.  \n  So \\(B1 = W1 \\land (G \\neq WHITE)\\).\n\nCheck the three gem possibilities:\n\n1. Gem in WHITE:  \n   \\(W1 = T\\), \\(K1 = T\\), \\(B1 = T \\land F = F\\).  \n   Valid: at least one box true, at least one box false.\n\n2. Gem in BLUE:  \n   \\(W1 = F\\), \\(K1 = F\\), \\(B1 = F \\land T = F\\).  \n   All false — violates “at least one true.”\n\n3. Gem in BLACK:  \n   \\(W1 = F\\), \\(K1 = F\\), \\(B1 = F \\land T = F\\).  \n   All false — violates “at least one true.”\n\nTherefore, the unique solution is gem in WHITE, with B1 false, W1 true, K1 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.558, "ast_prob": 0.346, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00178", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS BLACK\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS WHITE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The statements W1 and K1 describe impossible color identities for the BLUE box, so both are always false.  \nTherefore, at least one all-false box is already satisfied.  \nTo also satisfy “at least one box has all statements true,” B1 must be true.  \nB1 is true only if the WHITE box contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.504, "ast_prob": 0.26, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00179", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLACK\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The BLUE box is on the left, so W1 (“The BLUE box is the middle box”) is false.  \nThe WHITE box is not black, so B1 (“The WHITE box is BLACK”) is false.  \nThus there is already at least one all-false box. Since the rules require at least one all-true box, and B1 and W1 are false, the BLACK box must be all-true. So K1 must be true.  \nK1 says a box next to the BLACK box contains the gems; the only box next to BLACK is WHITE, so the gems are in the WHITE box.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box":