 next to WHITE are BLUE and BLACK, both false. So W1 is true exactly when WHITE contains the gems.\n- At least one box must be all-true, so WHITE must be true, meaning the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.532, "ast_prob": 0.197, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00185", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS AND BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n1. BLACK is the rightmost box, so the only box next to it is WHITE. Thus W1 is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.\n2. WHITE is the middle box, so the boxes next to it are BLUE and BLACK. K1 says those both contain gems, but exactly one box contains gems, so that second part of K1 is impossible. Therefore K1 is false.\n3. B1 says “the statement above this one is true,” but there is no statement above B1, so B1 is false.\n4. At least one box must be all-true. Since B1 and K1 are false, W1 must be true, so the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.63, "ast_prob": 0.377, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00186", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] AT LEAST THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY OR THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the three statements be B1, W1, and K1.\n\n- B1 says “at least three statements are false.” Since there are exactly three statements total, B1 being true would mean B1 itself must be false, a contradiction. Therefore B1 is false.\n- K1 says “boxes next to the black box contain gems.” The black box is on the right, so the only box next to it is the white box. Thus K1 is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.\n- W1 says either the other two boxes relative to white are empty, or the box with two false statements has the gems. The “box with two false statements” cannot exist because each box has only one statement. So W1 is true exactly when the other two boxes are empty, which also means the gems are in WHITE.\n\nSo if the gems are in BLUE or BLACK, then both W1 and K1 are false, and B1 is already false, leaving no all-true box. Therefore the gems must be in WHITE, making W1 true and K1 true, while B1 remains false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.474, "ast_prob": 0.166, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00187", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is false because the White box has only one statement (W1), so it cannot have at least two false statements.  \nB1 is false because there is no statement above it on the Blue box to be true.  \nSince at least one box must be all-true, and both B1 and K1 are false, W1 must be true.  \nW1 says the boxes next to the Blue box contain gems; the only box next to Blue is White, so the gems are in White.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.518, "ast_prob": 0.482, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00189", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 has no statement above it, so B1 is false; therefore BLUE is all-false.  \nK1 says “the box with two false statements has the gems,” but each box has only one statement, so no box has two false statements; therefore K1 is false; therefore BLACK is all-false.  \nAt least one box must be all-true, so WHITE must be all-true: W1 = T.  \nW1 says the gems are in a true box. The only true box is WHITE, so the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.645, "ast_prob": 0.345, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00190", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is false: the White box is not Blue.  \nW1 is false: the boxes next to White are Blue and Black, but exactly one box can contain gems, so they cannot both contain gems.  \nSince at least one box must be all-true, and both White and Black are already all-false, Blue must be all-true.  \nSo B1 must be true: the box next to Blue contains gems. The only box next to Blue is White.  \nTherefore White contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.47, "ast_prob": 0.171, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00191", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX HAS EXACTLY ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Each box has exactly one statement, so the rule “at least one all-true and at least one all-false” means that among B1, W1, and K1, at least one must be true and at least one must be false.\n\n- Black is the rightmost box, so the only box next to it is White.  \n  Thus B1 is true iff the gems are in White.\n\n- The White box contains only statement W1.  \n  So K1, meaning “the White box has exactly one true statement,” is true iff W1 is true: K1 = W1.\n\n- W1 says B1, W1, and K1 are all true. Since K1 = W1, this becomes:\n  W1 = B1 ∧ W1\n\nIf the gems are not in White, then B1 is false, so W1 must be false, and K1 is also false. That would make all three statements false, violating the rule.\n\nTherefore the gems must be in White, so B1 is true.  \nWith B1 true, W1 could logically be true or false, but if W1 were true then K1 would also be true, making all three statements true — again violating the rule.\n\nSo W1 is false and K1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.499, "ast_prob": 0.267, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00192", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX AND THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "- BLUE is not the middle box, so B1’s first clause is false, making B1 false.\n- Therefore BLUE is all-false.\n- The black box is on the right, so the only box next to it is WHITE.\n- W1 and K1 both mean the same thing: WHITE contains the gems.\n- If gems were in BLUE or BLACK, W1 and K1 would both be false, leaving no all-true box.\n- So gems must be in WHITE, making W1 true and K1 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.392, "ast_prob": 0.411, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00193", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location determine the statements.\n\n- B1: The Black box is rightmost, so the only box next to it is White. Thus B1 is true iff White contains the gems.\n- W1: The other two boxes relative to White are Blue and Black. Thus W1 is true iff White contains the gems.\n- K1: The Black box displays only K1, so there is no statement above K1 on that box. Therefore K1 is false.\n\nCheck gem locations:\n- If gem=BLUE: B1=F, W1=F, K1=F → no all-true box.\n- If gem=BLACK: B1=F, W1=F, K1=F → no all-true box.\n- If gem=WHITE: B1=T, W1=T, K1=F → Blue and White are all-true, Black is all-false. This satisfies all rules uniquely.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.284, "ast_prob": 0.121, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00194", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT LEAST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT LEAST THREE STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the truth values of B1, W1, K1 be denoted b, w, k.\n\n- K1 says “at least three statements are true.” Since there are exactly 3 statements and at least one statement must be false, K1 cannot be true. So k = F.\n- W1 says “at least two statements are false.” With k = F, W1 is true exactly when at least one of b or w is also false.\n  - If b = T and w = T, then only K1 is false, so W1 would be false — contradiction.\n  - If b = T and w = F, then W1 would be true — contradiction.\n  - If b = F and w = F, then W1 would be true — contradiction.\n  - Therefore b = F and w = T works: B1 false, W1 true, K1 false.\n- B1 says “the gems are in a box that is false.” Since b = F, the Blue box is not the gem box.\n- White box has W1 true, so White is not a false box.\n- Black box has K1 false, so Black is a false box. But if the gems were in Black, then B1 would be true, contradiction.\n- Therefore the gems must be in White.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.565, "ast_prob": 0.158, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00195", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX IS BLACK\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the three boxes be ordered: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right).\n\n- B1: “Boxes next to the BLACK box contain gems.”  \n  The only box next to the BLACK box is WHITE. So B1 is true exactly when the gem is in WHITE.\n\n- W1: “This box is BLACK.”  \n  W1 is on the WHITE box, so W1 is false.\n\n- K1: “The other two boxes relative to the WHITE box are empty.”  \n  The other two boxes are BLUE and BLACK. So K1 is true exactly when the gem is in WHITE.\n\nCheck gem locations:\n\n- If gem = BLUE: B1 false, W1 false, K1 false → no all-true box, invalid.\n- If gem = BLACK: B1 false, W1 false, K1 false → no all-true box, invalid.\n- If gem = WHITE: B1 true, W1 false, K1 true → at least one all-true and at least one all-false, valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.545, "ast_prob": 0.157, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00196", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n\n1. The BLUE box has only one adjacent box: WHITE. So B1 means “WHITE contains the gems.”\n2. The BLACK box has only one adjacent box: WHITE. So W1 also means “WHITE contains the gems.”\n3. Therefore B1 and W1 always have the same truth value.\n4. K1 says “B1, W1, and K1 are all true,” so K1 is true only if B1 and W1 are also true.\n5. The rules require at least one all-false box, so at least one statement must be false. Thus K1 cannot be true, because if K1 were true, all three statements would be true.\n6. So K1 = False.\n7. Since K1 is false, at least one of B1 or W1 must be true to satisfy the “at least one all-true box” rule. Because B1 and W1 are equal, both must be true.\n8. B1 true means WHITE contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.78, "ast_prob": 0.139, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00197", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS WHITE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Since BLUE is the leftmost box, the only box next to it is WHITE.  \nSo B1 means “the gems are in WHITE.”  \nW1 says “all statements on BLUE are true,” which is exactly B1.  \nK1 says “this box is WHITE,” but it is on BLACK, so K1 is false.\n\nWe need at least one true statement and at least one false statement. K1 is false. For a true statement, B1 or W1 must be true; since W1 = B1, both must be true. Therefore the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.739, "ast_prob": 0.251, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00198", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "White is the middle box, so its neighboring boxes are Blue and Black.  \nW1 claims both of those neighboring boxes contain gems, but exactly one box contains gems, so W1 must always be False.  \nSince White has only statement W1, White box is not true, so B1 (“The White box is true”) is False.  \nK1 says a box next to Black contains the gems. Black’s only neighbor is White, so K1 is True exactly when the gems are in White.\n\nTesting gem locations:\n- If gems are in Blue: B1=F, W1=F, K1=F → no all-true box, invalid.\n- If gems are in Black: B1=F, W1=F, K1=F → no all-true box, invalid.\n- If gems are in White: B1=F, W1=F, K1=T → Black is all-true, Blue and White are all-false, valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.635, "ast_prob": 0.177, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00199", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS BLACK\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "- BLUE is the left box, so the only box next to BLUE is WHITE. Therefore B1 is true exactly when WHITE contains the gems.\n- W1 says WHITE contains the gems, so W1 has the same truth value as B1.\n- K1 says the BLUE box is BLACK, which is false because the box is BLUE.\n\nIf the gems were in BLUE or BLACK, then B1 and W1 would both be false, and K1 is also false. That would make every box all-false, violating the rule that at least one box is all-true.\n\nTherefore the gems must be in WHITE. Then B1 and W1 are true, and K1 remains false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.209, "ast_prob": 0.413, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00200", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EITHER THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE OR THE BLACK BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Each box has exactly one statement.\n\n- W1 says “the statement above this one is true,” but there is no statement above it in the WHITE box, so W1 is **false**.\n- K1’s second option is impossible because the BLACK box has only one statement, so it cannot have at least two false statements. Its first option requires all three statements to be true, but W1 is false, so K1 is **false**.\n- At least one box must be all-true, so B1 must be **true**.\n- B1 true means the box next to BLUE contains the gems. BLUE is leftmost, so the only box next to it is WHITE. Therefore the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.774, "ast_prob": 0.376, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00201", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS WHITE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 and W1 are true by the setup: the WHITE box is white and is in the middle.  \nIf K1 were true, then the BLACK box would be empty, but then all three boxes would have true statements, leaving no all-false box. That violates the rules.  \nTherefore K1 must be false, meaning the BLACK box is not empty, so the gems are in the BLACK box.  \nThis gives BLUE all-true, WHITE all-true, and BLACK all-false, satisfying the rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.256, "ast_prob": 0.338, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00202", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "- WHITE box has only one statement, W1, so it cannot have exactly two false statements. Therefore B1 is false.\n- W1 says “the statement above this one is true,” referring to B1. Since B1 is false, W1 is false.\n- So BLUE is all-false and WHITE is all-false. To satisfy “at least one box all-true,” BLACK must be all-true, so K1 must be true.\n- K1 true means the other two boxes are empty, so the gems must be in BLACK.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.423, "ast_prob": 0.444, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00203", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the truth values be determined by the actual gem location and statement references.\n\n- B1 says “The Black box is true,” so B1 has the same truth value as K1.\n- W1 says “A box next to the Black box contains the gems.” Black is on the right, so the only box next to Black is White. Thus W1 is true iff the gems are in WHITE.\n- K1 says “The Blue box is empty,” so K1 is true iff the gems are NOT in BLUE.\n\nCheck possible gem locations:\n\n- If gem = BLUE, then K1 = F, so B1 = F, and W1 = F. All false, but no box is true → invalid.\n- If gem = WHITE, then K1 = T, so B1 = T, and W1 = T. All true, but no box is false → invalid.\n- If gem = BLACK, then K1 = T, so B1 = T, and W1 = F. There is at least one true box and at least one false box → valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.386, "ast_prob": 0.402, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00205", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS BLACK\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false: the blue box is blue, not black.  \nW1 is false: it claims both boxes next to the white box contain gems, but only one box contains the gems.  \nThus the BLUE and WHITE boxes are all-false, satisfying the all-false requirement.  \nTo satisfy the all-true requirement, the BLACK box must be all-true, so K1 must be true.  \nTherefore the gems must be in the BLACK box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.335, "ast_prob": 0.443, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00206", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n- \\(b\\) = truth of B1\n- \\(w\\) = truth of W1\n- \\(k\\) = truth of K1\n\nW1 says “all statements on Blue are true,” so \\(w = b\\).\n\nK1 says “the other two boxes are empty,” so \\(k\\) is true iff the gems are in BLACK.\n\nNow test the three possible gem locations:\n\n1. If gems are in BLUE:  \n   B1 says the gems are in a false box. But if Blue’s statement is true, Blue isn’t false; if Blue’s statement is false, then B1 would be true. Contradiction.\n\n2. If gems are in WHITE:  \n   White’s truth value equals Blue’s truth value. So White is false exactly when Blue is false. This gives the same contradiction as above.\n\n3. If gems in BLACK:  \n   K1 is true, so Black is a true box. Then Blue and White must be false, meaning \\(b = F\\) and \\(w = F\\). B1 says “gems are in a false box,” but the gems are in Black, which is true, so B1 is false. This is consistent.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.652, "ast_prob": 0.326, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00207", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1: The Black box has only one statement, K1. It claims “this box has at least two true statements,” which is impossible for a box with only one statement. Therefore K1 is false, so Black is an all-false box.\n\nB1 says the gems are in a false box.\n\n- If gems were in Blue: B1 would be true only if Blue were all-false, but B1 true would make Blue not all-false. Contradiction.\n- If gems were in White: B1 would be true only if White were all-false. White is all-false only if W1 is false, and W1 is false only if B1 is false. So B1 would be true iff B1 is false. Contradiction.\n- Therefore gems must be in Black.\n\nSince Black is all-false, B1 is true. Then W1 says all statements on Blue are true, and B1 is true, so W1 is true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.789, "ast_prob": 0.114, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00208", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Since Blue’s only adjacent box is White, B1 is true exactly when the gems are in White.  \nSince Black’s only adjacent box is White, W1 is also true exactly when the gems are in White.  \nSo B1 and W1 have the same truth value.\n\n- If gems are in Blue: B1=F, W1=F, and K1=F because Blue box is false → all false, invalid.\n- If gems are in White: B1=T, W1=T, and K1=T because White box is true → all true, invalid.\n- If gems are in Black: B1=F, W1=F. K1 says the gems are in a true box; with gems in Black, K1 is true if Black box is true. Setting K1=T satisfies both required conditions.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.669, "ast_prob": 0.289, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00209", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n- B1 = “The blue box is empty” → true iff the gems are not in BLUE.\n- W1 and K1 = “The other two boxes relative to the white box are empty” → true iff both BLUE and BLACK are empty, which means the gems are in WHITE.\n\nCheck possibilities:\n- Gems in BLUE: B1 false, W1 false, K1 false → no true statements, violates rule.\n- Gems in WHITE: B1 true, W1 true, K1 true → no false statements, violates rule.\n- Gems in BLACK: B1 true, W1 false, K1 false → has at least one true and at least one false, consistent.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.292, "ast_prob": 0.393, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00210", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] AT LEAST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS WHITE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is always false: the black box is not white, it is black.\n\nSo if the gems were in BLUE or WHITE, the BLACK box would be empty, making K1 true. Then with W1 false and K1 true:\n- If B1 were true, there would be only 1 false statement (W1), so B1 would be false.\n- If B1 were false, there would be 2 false statements (B1 and W1), so B1 would be true.\nThis is contradictory, so the gems cannot be in BLUE or WHITE.\n\nTherefore the gems must be in BLACK. Then K1 is false, W1 is false, and B1 is true because at least two statements (W1 and K1) are false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.427, "ast_prob": 0.455, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00211", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT LEAST THREE STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is false because the black box is on the right, not the middle.  \nK1 says “at least three statements are true,” but there are exactly three statements total, so it would require all three to be true. Since W1 is false, K1 must also be false.  \nNow WHITE and BLACK boxes are both all-false. To satisfy the rule that at least one box is all-true, BLUE must be all-true.  \nSo B1 is true, meaning the gems are in the BLACK box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.224, "ast_prob": 0.285, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00212", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS WHITE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 says the black box is white, which is false because the black box is black.  \nSo B1 = F.  \nW1 says all statements on the blue box are true, but B1 is false, so W1 = F.  \nAt least one box must have all true statements, and only K1 can be true now.  \nThus K1 = T, meaning the black box contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.33, "ast_prob": 0.467, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00213", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is false: the White box is not Blue.  \nIf K1 were true, the Black box would have no false statements. But if B1 were also true, there would be two boxes with no false statements, so “the box with no false statements” would not be unique and K1 could not be true. If B1 were false, then Black would be the unique all-true box, so K1 true would require Black to hold the gems, contradicting B1 false. Therefore K1 must be false.  \nSince at least one box must be all-true, and W1 and K1 are false, B1 must be true. So the Black box contains the gems. This makes Blue the unique all-true box, which does not contain the gems, so K1 is indeed false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.307, "ast_prob": 0.168, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00214", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 says “the statement above this one is true,” but there is no statement above it on the BLUE box, so B1 is false.\n\nW1 says all statements on the BLUE box are true. Since BLUE has only B1 and B1 is false, W1 is false.\n\nSo BLUE and WHITE are both all-false boxes. Since at least one box must be all-true, BLACK must be all-true, meaning K1 must be true.\n\nK1 says the gems are in a box that is true. BLACK is the only true box, so the gems must be in BLACK.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.676, "ast_prob": 0.274, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00215", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because the blue box is the left box, not the middle box.\n\nEach box has exactly one statement, so no box can have two false statements. Therefore W1 is false.\n\nSince at least one box must be all-true, and both BLUE and WHITE are all-false, BLACK must be all-true. Thus K1 is true.\n\nK1 says the gems are in a box that is true. The only true box is BLACK, so the gems are in BLACK.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.481, "ast_prob": 0.385, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00216", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(G\\) be the box with the gems.\n\n- B1: “A box next to the Blue box contains the gems.” The only box next to Blue is White, so B1 is true iff gems are in WHITE.\n- W1: “The Blue box is empty.” True iff gems are NOT in BLUE.\n- K1: same as W1, so K1 is true iff gems are NOT in BLUE.\n\nCheck possible gem locations:\n\n1. If gems are in BLUE:  \n   B1 = F, W1 = F, K1 = F.  \n   No box has all statements true, so invalid.\n\n2. If gems are in WHITE:  \n   B1 = T, W1 = T, K1 = T.  \n   No box has all statements false, so invalid.\n\n3. If gems are in BLACK:  \n   B1 = F, W1 = T, K1 = T.  \n   BLACK has all true, and BLUE has all false, satisfying both conditions.\n\nTherefore, the gems are in BLACK, with B1 false and both W1 and K1 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.505, "ast_prob": 0.264, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00217", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS OR THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS AND THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the boxes be: BLUE = left, WHITE = middle, BLACK = right.\n\n- B1 says: a box next to BLUE contains the gems, or BLUE is empty.  \n  The only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so B1 is true exactly when the gems are NOT in BLUE.\n- W1 says: a box next to BLACK contains the gems, and WHITE is the middle box.  \n  The only box next to BLACK is WHITE, and WHITE is indeed the middle box, so W1 is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.\n- K1 says: a box next to BLUE is true.  \n  The only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so K1 is true exactly when W1 is true.\n\nCheck possible gem locations:\n\n1. Gems in BLUE:  \n   B1 = F, W1 = F, K1 = F.  \n   No statement true → violates “at least one all-true box.”\n\n2. Gems in WHITE:  \n   B1 = T, W1 = T, K1 = T.  \n   No statement false → violates “at least one all-false box.”\n\n3. Gems in BLACK:  \n   B1 = T, W1 = F, K1 = F.  \n   At least one true and at least one false → satisfies all rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.306, "ast_prob": 0.476, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00218", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n- Blue’s only adjacent box is White, so B1 is true only if White’s statement W1 is true.\n- W1 says “the statement above this one is true,” but the White box has no statement above it in the given left/middle/right layout. So W1 is false.\n- Since W1 is false, White is not a true box, so B1 is false.\n- Blue and White are both all-false, so Black must be the all-true box. Thus K1 is true.\n- K1 says Black contains the gems, so the gems are in Black.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.758, "ast_prob": 0.102, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00219", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let gem positions be considered:\n\n- If gems are in BLUE: B1 and W1 are true (a box next to WHITE contains gems), K1 is true (the other two boxes are empty). That makes all three statements true, so no box has all statements false — invalid.\n- If gems are in WHITE: B1 and W1 are false (no box next to WHITE contains gems), K1 is false (the other boxes are not both empty). That makes all three statements false, so no box has all statements true — invalid.\n- If gems are in BLACK: B1 and W1 are true (BLACK is next to WHITE and contains gems), K1 is false (one of the other boxes, BLACK, is not empty). This gives at least one all-true box and at least one all-false box — valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.398, "ast_prob": 0.443, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00220", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] AT LEAST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER AT LEAST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE OR EXACTLY ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(T\\) be the number of true statements among B1, W1, K1.\n\n- Each box has only 1 statement, so “all true” = statement true, and “all false” = statement false.\n- Therefore, the rules require at least one true statement and at least one false statement, so \\(T = 1\\) or \\(T = 2\\).\n\nK1 is true iff the other two boxes are **not** both empty.  \n- If the gem is in BLACK, then BLUE and WHITE are both empty, so K1 is false.  \n- If the gem is in BLUE or WHITE, then at least one of the other two boxes is nonempty, so K1 is true.\n\nB1: “At least one statement is true” means B1 is true iff \\(T \\ge 1\\).\n\nW1: “Either at least one statement is true or exactly one statement is true” uses inclusive OR, so it is also true iff \\(T \\ge 1\\). Thus W1 has the same truth value as B1.\n\nSo \\(T = B1 + W1 + K1 = 2(B1) + K1\\).\n\nIf B1 were false, then \\(T = K1\\), but B1 false would require \\(T=0\\), so K1 would also have to be false, giving \\(T=0\\), violating the “at least one true” rule. Hence B1 must be true.\n\nThen \\(T = 2 + K1\\). Since \\(T\\) must be 1 or 2, K1 cannot be true, so K1 is false. Therefore W1 is also true.\n\nK1 false means the gem is in BLACK.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.665, "ast_prob": 0.442, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00221", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is false because the black box is on the right, not the middle.  \nW1 is true exactly when the gems are in the black box, because then the other two boxes relative to black are empty.  \nB1 says the white box is true, so B1 has the same truth value as W1.  \n\nChecking gem locations:  \n- If gems are in BLUE or WHITE, W1 is false, B1 is false, K1 is false → no box is all-true, invalid.  \n- If gems are in BLACK, W1 is true, B1 is true, K1 is false → BLUE all-true, WHITE all-true, BLACK all-false, valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.39, "ast_prob": 0.323, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00222", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY AND A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO TRUE STATEMENTS AND AT MOST THREE STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS BLACK\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is false because the Blue box has only one statement, so it cannot have exactly two true statements. K1 is false because the Blue box is not black. Therefore, to satisfy “at least one box has all true statements,” B1 must be true. B1 being true means the Blue box is empty and a box next to the White box contains the gems; the only possibility is the Black box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.483, "ast_prob": 0.318, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00223", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX IS WHITE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT LEAST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is true because the statement is on the white box.\n\nIf the gems were not in the black box, B1 would be false. Then K1 would create a contradiction: if K1 is false, then B1 and K1 are both false, making K1 true; if K1 is true, then only B1 is false, making K1 false. So the gems must be in the black box, making B1 true.\n\nWith B1 true and W1 true, K1 cannot be true, because then no statements would be false. K1 being false is consistent, since exactly one statement is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.378, "ast_prob": 0.221, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00224", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE AND AT LEAST ONE STATEMENT IS FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX AND THE BLACK BOX IS WHITE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step by step:\n\n1. W1 says: “The black box is the middle box and the black box is white.”  \n   - The black box is right, not middle: false.  \n   - The black box is black, not white: false.  \n   So W1 = F.\n\n2. Since W1 is false, the “at least one box all-false” condition is satisfied.\n\n3. B1 says: “The statement above this one is true and at least one statement is false.”  \n   There is no statement above B1, so the first part “the statement above this one is true” is false. Therefore B1 = F.\n\n4. Since both B1 and W1 are false, at least one statement must be true, so K1 must be true.\n\n5. K1 says: “The other two boxes are empty.”  \n   If K1 is true, then BLUE and WHITE are empty, so the gems must be in BLACK.\n\nFinal answer:\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.67, "ast_prob": 0.449, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00225", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE OR THIS BOX IS BLACK\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is false because the blue box is on the left, not the middle.  \nThus W1 becomes: “B1 and W1 and K1 are all true, or this box is black.” Since W1 is on the white box, “this box is black” is false, and K1 is already false, so W1 must be false.  \nWith W1 and K1 false, at least one true statement is needed, so B1 must be true. Therefore the black box contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.705, "ast_prob": 0.37, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00226", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step by step:\n\n1. Black box statement K1 says “This box is the middle box.” Since the black box is on the right, K1 is false. So K1 = F.\n2. White statement W1 says “All statements on the Blue box are true.” The Blue box has only B1, so W1 is true exactly when B1 is true. Thus W1 = B1.\n3. B1 says “The gems are in a box that is false.” A box is false here when its statement is false.\n4. Test gem locations:\n   - If gems are in Blue: B1 would mean “Blue is false,” so B1 would be false, contradiction.\n   - If gems are in White: B1 would mean “White is false,” but White’s truth equals B1, contradiction.\n   - If gems are in Black: Black is false because K1 is false, so B1 is true. Then W1 is also true. This is consistent.\n5. Therefore the gems are in the Black box, with B1 true, W1 true, K1 false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.356, "ast_prob": 0.32, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00227", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS OR THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY AND ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 says “It is not true that the White box is the middle box.” But the White box is the middle box, so B1 is false. Therefore the Blue box is all-false.\n\nK1 says “The White box is empty and all statements on the Blue box are true.” Since B1 is false, “all statements on the Blue box are true” is false, so K1 is false. Therefore the Black box is all-false.\n\nAt least one box must be all-true. Blue and Black are not all-true, so White must be all-true. Thus W1 is true.\n\nW1 says “Either the Black box contains the gems or the Black box contains the gems,” which is equivalent to saying the Black box contains the gems. So the gems are in Black.\n\nVerification: Blue all-false, White all-true, Black all-false, and exactly one box contains gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.476, "ast_prob": 0.251, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00228", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is the only statement in the BLUE box, so there is no statement above it. Therefore B1’s claim that the statement above it is true is false: B1 = F.\n\nW1 says “The top statement of each box is true.” Since each box has exactly one statement, this would require B1, W1, and K1 all to be true. But B1 is false, so W1 = F.\n\nWith both BLUE and WHITE all-false, at least one box must be all-true. That must be BLACK, so K1 must be true, meaning the gems are in the BLACK box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.47, "ast_prob": 0.297, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00229", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT LEAST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT MOST TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the truth values be as follows:\n\n- W1 says \"At least one statement is true.\" If W1 were false, then no statement would be true, which would violate the rule that at least one box has all statements true. So W1 must be true.\n- K1 says \"At most two statements are true.\" If K1 were false, then all three statements would have to be true, which would make K1 true. Contradiction. So K1 must be true.\n- Since W1 and K1 are true, at most two true statements means B1 cannot also be true. So B1 is false.\n- B1 says \"The black box is empty.\" Since B1 is false, the black box is not empty, so it contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.49, "ast_prob": 0.166, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00230", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS AND EXACTLY THREE STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n- \\(K_1\\) = “The BLACK box contains the gems.”\n- \\(B_1\\) = “The BLACK box contains the gems AND exactly three statements are true.”\n- \\(W_1\\) = “The top statement of each box is true” = “\\(B_1\\), \\(W_1\\), and \\(K_1\\) are all true.”\n\nSince exactly one box has the gems, test locations.\n\n1. If gems are BLUE or WHITE:\n   - \\(K_1 = F\\).\n   - \\(B_1 = F\\) because BLACK does not contain the gems.\n   - \\(W_1\\) cannot be true because it would require \\(B_1\\) and \\(K_1\\) to be true.\n   - So all statements false, violating “at least one box all true.”\n\n2. Therefore gems must be BLACK:\n   - \\(K_1 = T\\).\n   - \\(W_1\\) cannot be true, because \\(W_1 = T\\) would require \\(B_1\\) and \\(K_1\\) to be true, making all three true, violating “at least one box all false.”\n   - So \\(W_1 = F\\).\n   - If \\(B_1 = T\\), then BLACK has the gems, but “exactly three statements are true” is false because \\(W_1 = F\\). So \\(B_1\\) would be false, contradiction.\n   - Thus \\(B_1 = F\\).\n\nCheck: gems in BLACK, \\(K_1 = T\\), \\(B_1 = F\\), \\(W_1 = F\\). At least one true: \\(K_1\\). At least one false: \\(B_1, W_1\\). All consistent.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.449, "ast_prob": 0.446, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00231", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EXACTLY THREE STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Since at least one box must be all-true and at least one must be all-false, the three statements cannot all have the same truth value.\n\n- If W1 were true, then all three statements would be true, leaving no all-false box. So W1 is false.\n- Thus exactly one or two statements are true.\n\nIf exactly two statements were true, W1 would be false, so B1 and K1 would both be true. B1 true means the gems are in BLACK. But K1 says “the box with no false statements has the gems”; with two true statements there would be two all-true boxes, so no unique such box. K1 cannot be true in that case. Contradiction.\n\nSo exactly one statement is true, and it cannot be W1. It must be either B1 or K1.\n\n- If K1 were true and B1 false, then the unique all-true box would be BLACK, so K1 would require the gems to be in BLACK. But B1 false says the gems are not in BLACK. Contradiction.\n- Therefore B1 is true and K1 is false. B1 true means the gems are in BLACK.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.675, "ast_prob": 0.404, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00233", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX AND A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let `B1` be true iff the White box is true, so `B1 = W1`.\n\nLet `W1` be true iff the Black box contains the gems, so `W1 = (gem = BLACK)`.\n\n`K1` says “the Black box is the middle box” — which is false — and another condition. Since it is a conjunction with a false part, `K1 = FALSE` always.\n\nThus:\n- If gem is not BLACK, then `W1 = FALSE`, so `B1 = FALSE`, and `K1 = FALSE`. Then no box is all-true, violating the rule.\n- Therefore gem must be in BLACK, giving `W1 = TRUE`, `B1 = TRUE`, and `K1 = FALSE`.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.59, "ast_prob": 0.347, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00234", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT LEAST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location be considered case by case.\n\n- If gem = BLUE:  \n  B1 = F, K1 = T. Then W1 would need to be false for there to be at least two false statements, but if W1 is false there are exactly two false statements, making W1 true. Contradiction.\n\n- If gem = WHITE:  \n  B1 = T, K1 = F. Then W1 would need to be false for there to be at least two false statements, but if W1 is false there are exactly two false statements, making W1 true. Contradiction.\n\n- If gem = BLACK:  \n  B1 = T, K1 = T. Then W1 = F makes exactly one false statement, so “at least two statements are false” is false. Consistent.\n\nSo the gems are in the BLACK box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.487, "ast_prob": 0.206, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00235", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS BLACK\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location be one of BLUE, WHITE, BLACK.\n\n- K1 says “THE BLACK BOX IS BLACK.” This is simply true, so K1 = T.\n- W1 says “THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY,” meaning the gems are not in BLACK.\n- B1 says “THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE,” meaning W1 is true, so B1 has the same truth value as W1.\n\nWe need at least one all-true box and at least one all-false box. Since K1 is true, we already have an all-true box. Therefore, to have an all-false box, both B1 and W1 must be false.\n\nIf W1 is false, then BLACK is not empty, so the gems must be in BLACK. This also makes B1 false, consistent with B1 = W1.\n\nSo:\n- Gem = BLACK\n- B1 = F\n- W1 = F\n- K1 = T\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.478, "ast_prob": 0.199, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00236", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX HAS AT MOST ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "- Each box has exactly one statement, so \"all true\" just means that statement is true, and \"all false\" means that statement is false.\n\n- B1 says the Black box has at most one false statement. Black has only K1, so it has either 0 or 1 false statements. Thus B1 is always true.\n\n- K1 says a box next to the White box is true. The Blue box is next to White, and B1 is true. Therefore K1 is true.\n\n- We now have Blue true and Black true. Since at least one box must be all false, White must be false.\n\n- W1 says the Black box is empty. For W1 to be false, the Black box is not empty, so the gems must be in Black.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.525, "ast_prob": 0.32, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00237", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EXACTLY ONE STATEMENT IS FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "WHITE contains only one statement, W1, so it cannot have exactly two false statements. Therefore W1 is false.\n\nSince at least one box must be all-true, at least one of B1 or K1 must be true.\n\nIf B1 were true, then exactly one statement overall would be false. Since W1 is already false, K1 would also have to be true. Then both BLUE and BLACK would be boxes with no false statements, so K1’s claim that “the box with no false statements has the gems” would not refer to a unique box, making K1 false — a contradiction. Thus B1 is false.\n\nSo the only remaining way to have an all-true box is K1 true. K1 then correctly identifies BLACK as the box with no false statements, so BLACK must contain the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.694, "ast_prob": 0.326, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00238", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS AND THE WHITE BOX HAS AT LEAST ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location be one of BLUE, WHITE, BLACK.\n\n1. Analyze W1:\n   - W1 says: “The WHITE box contains the gems AND the WHITE box has at least one false statement.”\n   - Since WHITE has only one statement, “at least one false statement” means W1 itself is false.\n   - So W1 would be true only if WHITE had the gems and W1 were false, which is impossible.\n   - Therefore W1 = FALSE, and consequently WHITE cannot contain the gems.\n   - So the gems are either in BLUE or BLACK.\n\n2. Analyze K1:\n   - K1 says: “The BLUE box has exactly two false statements.”\n   - But BLUE has only one statement: B1.\n   - Therefore BLUE cannot have exactly two false statements.\n   - So K1 = FALSE.\n\n3. Analyze B1:\n   - B1 says: “The BLUE box is empty.”\n   - Since WHITE does not contain the gems, if the gems are in BLACK, then BLUE is empty, so B1 = TRUE.\n   - If the gems were in BLUE, then B1 would be FALSE.\n\n4. Apply the all-true / all-false rule:\n   - WHITE is all-false because W1 = FALSE.\n   - BLACK is all-false because K1 = FALSE.\n   - We need at least one all-true box. The only possible all-true box is BLUE, so B1 must be TRUE.\n   - Therefore BLUE is empty, and the gems must be in BLACK.\n\nFinal truth values:\n- Gems in BLACK\n- B1 = TRUE\n- W1 = FALSE\n- K1 = FALSE\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.258, "ast_prob": 0.109, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00239", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX HAS EXACTLY ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let each statement be evaluated from the actual gem location.\n\n- B1: “The other two boxes are empty” means WHITE and BLACK are both empty.  \n  - If gems are in BLUE, then B1 is FALSE.  \n  - If gems are in WHITE or BLACK, then B1 is TRUE.\n\n- W1: “The BLUE box has exactly one true statement.”  \n  - BLUE has only one statement, B1, so W1 is TRUE exactly when B1 is TRUE.\n\n- K1: “Boxes next to this box contain gems.”  \n  - BLACK is next to WHITE only, so K1 is TRUE exactly when WHITE has the gems.\n\nTest gem locations:\n\n1. Gem in BLUE:  \n   - B1 = F, W1 = F, K1 = F  \n   - No all-true box, invalid.\n\n2. Gem in WHITE:  \n   - B1 = T, W1 = T, K1 = T  \n   - No all-false box, invalid.\n\n3. Gem in BLACK:  \n   - B1 = T, W1 = T, K1 = F  \n   - At least one all-true box and at least one all-false box, valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.613, "ast_prob": 0.235, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00240", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY OR THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS OR THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS BLUE AND BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let exactly one box contain gems.\n\n- B1: “Blue empty OR (Blue and Black empty)” simplifies to “Blue empty,” because the second clause implies the first. So B1 = True iff gems are not in BLUE.\n- W1: “White has gems OR Blue box is true” = G_WHITE OR B1.\n- K1: “Blue box is blue” is true, and Black’s only adjacent box is WHITE, so K1 = True iff gems are in WHITE.\n\nCheck the three gem locations:\n\n1. Gems in BLUE: B1=F, W1=F, K1=F → no all-true box. Invalid.\n2. Gems in WHITE: B1=T, W1=T, K1=T → no all-false box. Invalid.\n3. Gems in BLACK: B1=T, W1=T, K1=F → BLUE is all-true, BLACK is all-false, WHITE is all-true. Valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.209, "ast_prob": 0.484, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00241", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false: the boxes next to WHITE are BLUE and BLACK, but exactly one box contains the gems, so both cannot contain gems.\n\nW1 is false: every box has exactly one statement, so there is no box with two false statements.\n\nK1 is true only if the gems are in BLACK, because then BLUE and WHITE are both empty. This also gives at least one all-true box (BLACK) and at least one all-false box (BLUE and WHITE), satisfying the puzzle rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.242, "ast_prob": 0.207, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00242", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS AND THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step 1: B1 says “boxes next to the black box contain gems AND the statement above this one is true.” There is no statement above B1, so the second conjunct is false. Therefore B1 is false.\n\nStep 2: W1 says “the box with two false statements has the gems.” But each box has only one statement, so no box can have two false statements. Therefore W1 is false.\n\nStep 3: At least one box must be all-true. Since BLUE and WHITE are both all-false, BLACK must be the all-true box. Thus K1 is true.\n\nStep 4: K1 says “the box with no false statements has the gems.” Since BLACK is the only box with no false statements, BLACK must contain the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.553, "ast_prob": 0.122, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00243", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The BLACK box is on the right, so B1 (“The BLACK box is the middle box”) is false.\n\nIf the gems were in BLUE or WHITE, then both W1 and K1 would be false as well, making every statement false. That would violate the rule that at least one box has all true statements.\n\nSo the gems must be in BLACK. Then W1 and K1 are true, while B1 remains false. This gives BLUE all-false, WHITE all-true, and BLACK all-true, satisfying the rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.224, "ast_prob": 0.495, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00244", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because the blue box is on the left, not in the middle.  \nW1 is false because it claims both boxes next to the white box contain gems, but only one box total can contain gems.  \nSo both BLUE and WHITE are all-false boxes.  \nSince at least one box must be all-true, BLACK must be the all-true box.  \nTherefore K1 must be true, meaning the black box contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.277, "ast_prob": 0.43, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00245", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS NOT EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS WHITE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location be one of BLUE, WHITE, or BLACK.\n\n- B1: “The black box is not empty” is true exactly when the gems are in the BLACK box. So B1 = (gem = BLACK).\n- W1: “The other two boxes relative to the black box are empty” means BLUE and WHITE are empty. Since exactly one box has gems, this is also true exactly when the gems are in the BLACK box. So W1 = (gem = BLACK).\n- K1: “The black box is white” is false, because the black box is black, not white. So K1 = False regardless of gem location.\n\nIf the gems were in BLUE or WHITE, then B1 and W1 would both be false, and K1 is already false, giving no all-true box. That violates the rule that at least one box is all-true.\n\nTherefore the gems must be in the BLACK box. Then B1 and W1 are true, and K1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.264, "ast_prob": 0.478, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00246", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX IS BLUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The BLUE box is on the left, so the only box next to it is the WHITE box. Therefore, B1 is true exactly if W1 is true.\n\nW1 says \"THIS BOX IS BLUE,\" but it is on the WHITE box, so W1 is false. Therefore B1 is also false.\n\nSince at least one box must have all statements true, and B1 and W1 are both false, K1 must be true. Thus the BLACK box contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.499, "ast_prob": 0.102, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00247", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- B1 means “Blue and Black are empty” = “White contains the gems.”\n- W1 means “Blue is empty” = “The gems are not in Blue.”\n- K1 means “White’s statement is true,” since the only box next to Black is White.\n\nCheck the three possible gem locations:\n\n1. Gem in BLUE → B1=F, W1=F, K1=F → all false, violates at least one true.\n2. Gem in WHITE → B1=T, W1=T, K1=T → all true, violates at least one false.\n3. Gem in BLACK → B1=F, W1=T, K1=T → has both true and false statements, valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.627, "ast_prob": 0.169, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00248", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The box next to BLUE is only WHITE, so W1 is equivalent to “WHITE is empty,” same as B1.\n\nK1 says the other two boxes relative to BLUE (WHITE and BLACK) are empty, which is true only if BLUE contains the gems.\n\nCheck the three possible gem locations:\n- If gems are in BLUE: B1=T, W1=T, K1=T → all true, violates “at least one box all false.”\n- If gems are in WHITE: B1=F, W1=F, K1=F → all false, violates “at least one box all true.”\n- If gems are in BLACK: B1=T, W1=T, K1=F → at least one true and at least one false, valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.399, "ast_prob": 0.216, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00249", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX HAS AT MOST ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is always true, because the BLUE box has only one statement, so it cannot have more than one false statement.\n\nTherefore B1 (“ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE”) is true, since W1 is true.\n\nSo BLUE and WHITE boxes are both all-true. To satisfy the rule that at least one box is all-false, the BLACK box must be all-false, so K1 must be false.\n\nK1 says the gems are in a true box. Since K1 is false, the gems are not in either true box, so they must be in the BLACK box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.74, "ast_prob": 0.164, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00250", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because the blue box is on the left, not the middle.  \nW1 claims all top statements are true; since B1 is false, W1 must also be false.  \nWith BLUE and WHITE both false, BLACK must be true to satisfy “at least one box has all true statements.”  \nTherefore K1 is true, so the gems must be in the BLACK box, the true box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.28, "ast_prob": 0.488, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00251", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(B1, W1, K1\\) be the truth values of the three statements.\n\n- \\(B1\\) says “all three statements are true,” so \\(B1\\) is true only if \\(W1\\) and \\(K1\\) are also true. That would make every box all-true, leaving no all-false box, which violates the rules. Therefore \\(B1 = F\\).\n- Since \\(B1\\) is false, Blue is already an all-false box. We still need at least one all-true box, so at least one of \\(W1, K1\\) must be true.\n- \\(W1\\) says “Black contains the gems,” so \\(W1 = T\\) means the gems are in BLACK.\n- If \\(W1=F\\), then Black does **not** contain the gems. Then \\(K1\\) would have to be true to have an all-true box, but \\(K1\\) says the all-true box has the gems. The only all-true box would be Black, contradiction.\n- Therefore \\(W1=T\\), so Black contains the gems. Then the unique all-true box is White, and Black does **not** have no false statements, so \\(K1=F\\).\n\nThus the gems are in BLACK.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.388, "ast_prob": 0.192, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00252", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is the only statement on the Blue box, so Blue cannot have at least two true statements: B1 = F.  \nW1 says Blue is the middle box, but Blue is the left box: W1 = F.  \nThus Blue and White are both all-false boxes. Since at least one box must be all-true, Black must be all-true, so K1 = T.  \nK1 says the box with no false statements has the gems; that box is Black. Therefore the gems are in Black.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.749, "ast_prob": 0.142, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00253", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS AND A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS AND THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EITHER THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY OR A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(G\\) be the gem location. “This box” refers to the box on which the statement appears.\n\n- W1 = “White contains gems AND White contains gems” = White contains gems.  \n  So W1 is true iff \\(G = \\text{WHITE}\\).\n\n- B1 = “White contains gems AND the box next to Blue is true.”  \n  The box next to Blue is White, so B1 = “White contains gems AND W1 is true.”  \n  Since W1 is equivalent to “White contains gems,” B1 is true iff \\(G = \\text{WHITE}\\).\n\n- K1 = “Blue and White are empty OR White contains gems.”  \n  Blue and White empty means \\(G = \\text{BLACK}\\). White contains gems means \\(G = \\text{WHITE}\\).  \n  So K1 is true iff \\(G \\neq \\text{BLUE}\\).\n\nCheck possibilities:\n\n- \\(G = \\text{WHITE}\\): B1 = T, W1 = T, K1 = T → no false box, invalid.\n- \\(G = \\text{BLUE}\\): B1 = F, W1 = F, K1 = F → no true box, invalid.\n- \\(G = \\text{BLACK}\\): B1 = F, W1 = F, K1 = T → at least one true box and at least one false box, valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.554, "ast_prob": 0.386, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00254", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 and W1 both assert that the BLACK box contains the gems. If the gems were in BLUE or WHITE, then B1 and W1 would both be false; K1 would then have to be true to satisfy “at least one all-true box,” but K1 would falsely claim the all-true box has the gems. So the gems must be in BLACK. Then B1 and W1 are true, and K1 is false because the true-statement boxes are BLUE and WHITE, not BLACK.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.244, "ast_prob": 0.266, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00255", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is on the Blue box, which is not the middle box, so B1 is false.  \nW1 is true exactly when Black contains the gems.  \nK1 says both boxes next to White contain gems, but exactly one box contains gems, so K1 is false.  \n\nSince K1 is false, at least one false box is satisfied.  \nWe also need at least one all-true box. B1 is false, so W1 must be true.  \nThus Black must contain the gems, making W1 true, while K1 remains false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.244, "ast_prob": 0.334, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00256", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n- B1 = “A box next to the BLUE box is true” → the box next to BLUE is WHITE, so B1 = W1.\n- W1 = “The other two boxes relative to BLACK are empty” → since exactly one box has gems, this means the gems are in BLACK, so W1 = (gem=BLACK).\n- K1 = “The top statement of each box is true” → K1 is true only if B1, W1, and K1 are all true.\n\nIf the gems are not in BLACK, then W1 and B1 are false, forcing K1 false too, leaving no all-true box — invalid.\n\nIf the gems are in BLACK and K1 is true, then all three statements are true, leaving no all-false box — invalid.\n\nTherefore the gems must be in BLACK and K1 must be false, while B1 and W1 are true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.208, "ast_prob": 0.208, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00257", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE OR THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS AND THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. The boxes are fixed: BLUE = left, WHITE = middle, BLACK = right.\n2. B1 says “The white box is blue.” The white box is not blue, so B1 is FALSE.\n3. K1 says “This box contains the gems AND the black box is the middle box.” The black box is the right box, not the middle box, so the second part is FALSE. Therefore K1 is FALSE regardless of gem location.\n4. W1 says “Either the blue box is true OR the black box contains the gems.” Since B1 is false, the blue box is not true. So W1 is TRUE only if the black box contains the gems.\n5. To satisfy “at least one box has all statements true,” at least one statement must be true. Since B1 and K1 are false, W1 must be true.\n6. Therefore the black box contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.639, "ast_prob": 0.27, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00258", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(T_B, T_W, T_K\\) be the truth values of B1, W1, and K1.\n\n- W1 says “All statements on Blue are true,” so \\(T_W = T_B\\).\n- K1 says “A box next to White is true,” so \\(T_K = T_B \\lor T_K\\).\n- B1 says “The gems are in a box that is false.”\n\nIf gems were in Blue, B1 would say Blue is false, giving \\(T_B = \\neg T_B\\), impossible.  \nIf gems were in White, B1 would say White is false, but White’s truth equals Blue’s, giving \\(T_B = \\neg T_B\\), impossible.  \nSo gems must be in Black.\n\nThen B1 means Black is false: \\(T_B = \\neg T_K\\).  \nUsing \\(T_K = T_B \\lor T_K\\), substitute \\(T_K = \\neg T_B\\):  \n\\(\\neg T_B = T_B \\lor \\neg T_B = T\\), so \\(T_B = F\\).  \nThus \\(T_K = T\\), and \\(T_W = T_B = F\\).\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.779, "ast_prob": 0.116, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00259", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location be one of BLUE, WHITE, BLACK.\n\n- B1: “THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY” = true iff the gems are NOT in the BLUE box.\n- W1: “THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS” = true iff the gems are in the WHITE box.\n- K1: “A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS” = The only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so K1 is true iff the gems are in the WHITE box.\n\nTest each gem location:\n\n1. If gems are in BLUE:  \n   B1 = F, W1 = F, K1 = F.  \n   No true statement, violating the rule.\n\n2. If gems are in WHITE:  \n   B1 = T, W1 = T, K1 = T.  \n   No false statement, violating the rule.\n\n3. If gems are in BLACK:  \n   B1 = T, W1 = F, K1 = F.  \n   At least one true statement and at least one false statement, so this works.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.511, "ast_prob": 0.122, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00260", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX HAS EXACTLY ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let B1 mean “the two boxes other than Black are empty,” i.e. the gems are in Black.  \nSince Blue has only B1 as its statement, W1 (“Blue has exactly one true statement”) is equivalent to B1 being true.  \nK1 says B1, W1, and K1 are all true.\n\nIf B1 were false, then W1 would be false, and K1 would be false too, so there would be no all-true box. Thus B1 must be true.  \nSo W1 is true, and the gems are in Black.  \nIf K1 were true, all three boxes would be true, leaving no all-false box. Therefore K1 must be false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.578, "ast_prob": 0.183, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00261", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS WHITE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLACK\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The BLUE box is actually blue, so B1 (“The blue box is white”) is false.  \nThe WHITE box is actually white, so W1 (“The white box is black”) is false.  \n\nK1 says the other two boxes relative to BLACK—i.e., BLUE and WHITE—are empty.  \n- If the gem were in BLUE or WHITE, then K1 would be false, and all three statements would be false, violating the rule that at least one box is all-true.  \n- Therefore the gem must be in BLACK, making K1 true.  \n\nThen BLACK is all-true, and BLUE and WHITE are all-false, satisfying the rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.219, "ast_prob": 0.316, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00262", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gems be in exactly one box.\n\n- B1 is true iff the gems are in BLUE.\n- W1 is true iff the gems are in WHITE.\n- K1 means “the unique box with no false statements has the gems.”\n\nIf the gems were in BLUE, then B1 would be true, so BLUE would have no false statements. If K1 were true, BLACK would also have no false statements, making two such boxes, so K1 could not be true. If K1 were false, then BLUE would be the unique no-false box and it has the gems, so K1 would have to be true. Contradiction.\n\nIf the gems were in WHITE, the same contradiction occurs with W1.\n\nTherefore the gems must be in BLACK. Then B1 is false and W1 is false. If K1 were false, no box would be all-true, violating the rule that at least one box is all-true. So K1 must be true. This is consistent: BLACK is the unique no-false box and it contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.508, "ast_prob": 0.144, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00263", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "White box is in the middle, so the boxes next to it are BLUE and BLACK. Since exactly one box contains gems, both of those boxes cannot contain gems, so B1 is false.\n\nW1 says every top statement is true, meaning B1, W1, and K1 would all need to be true. Since B1 is false, W1 must be false.\n\nNow BLUE and WHITE are all-false boxes. The rule requires at least one all-true box, so K1 must be true. K1 says the gems are in a true box. The only box whose statement can be true is BLACK, so the gems must be in BLACK.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.514, "ast_prob": 0.439, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00264", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location determine the statements.\n\n- B1 is true iff the gems are in BLUE or BLACK (since those are beside WHITE).\n- W1 is true iff WHITE and BLACK are both empty, which happens only if gems are in BLUE.\n- K1 is true iff WHITE is empty, i.e., gems are in BLUE or BLACK.\n\nCheck each possible gem location:\n\n- If gems are in BLUE: B1=T, W1=T, K1=T. No false statement, violating the rule.\n- If gems are in WHITE: B1=F, W1=F, K1=F. No true statement, violating the rule.\n- If gems are in BLACK: B1=T, W1=F, K1=T. This has at least one true and at least one false statement, so it is valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.408, "ast_prob": 0.389, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00265", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n- B = truth of B1\n- W = truth of W1\n- K = truth of K1\n\nB1 says the box next to BLUE is true. BLUE is next only to WHITE, so:\nB = W\n\nW1 says every box's top statement is true, i.e. B, W, and K are all true:\nW = B ∧ W ∧ K\n\nSince B = W, this becomes:\nW = W ∧ K\n\nSo:\n- If W is true, then K must be true → B=T, W=T, K=T.\n- If W is false, then K may be true or false.\n\nAll-true case is invalid because the puzzle requires at least one all-false box.\n\nIf B=F and W=F:\nK1 says the gems are in a box that is true. With BLUE and WHITE false, this can only be true if the gems are in BLACK and BLACK is true. So:\nK=T, gems=BLACK.\n\nThis satisfies:\n- Exactly one gem box: BLACK.\n- At least one true box: BLACK.\n- At least one false box: BLUE and WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.411, "ast_prob": 0.231, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00266", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The White box contains only W1 and is placed in the middle, so there is no statement above W1. Therefore W1 is false.\n\nSince W1 is false, the Blue statement “All statements on the White box are true” is also false, so B1 is false.\n\nAt least one box must be all true, and both Blue and White are all false, so the Black box must be all true. Thus K1 is true, meaning the Black box contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.209, "ast_prob": 0.394, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00267", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location be exactly one of Blue, White, or Black.\n\nStatements:\n- B1 = “Blue contains the gems”\n- W1 = “White box is empty”\n- K1 = “White box is empty”\n\nSo W1 and K1 always have the same truth value.\n\n- If gems are in Blue: B1 = T, W1 = T, K1 = T. All boxes are all-true, so no all-false box. Invalid.\n- If gems are in White: B1 = F, W1 = F, K1 = F. All boxes are all-false, so no all-true box. Invalid.\n- If gems are in Black: B1 = F, W1 = T, K1 = T. Blue is all-false, White is all-true, Black is all-true. This satisfies at least one all-true and at least one all-false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.336, "ast_prob": 0.351, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00268", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(B1\\) = “Black has the gems.”  \nLet \\(W1\\) = “Both boxes next to White contain gems” = “Blue and Black both contain gems.” Since exactly one box contains the gems, \\(W1\\) is false.  \n\nLet \\(K1\\) = “The top statement of every box is true” = \\(B1 \\land W1 \\land K1\\). Since \\(W1\\) is false, \\(K1\\) must be false.  \n\nIf \\(B1\\) were false, then Blue would be all-false, White all-false, and Black all-false — violating the rule that at least one box is all-true. Therefore \\(B1\\) must be true, so Black contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.787, "ast_prob": 0.239, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00269", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(B1, W1, K1\\) be the truth values of the three statements.\n\n- \\(B1\\) says “the top statement of each box is true,” so \\(B1\\) is true only if \\(B1, W1, K1\\) are all true.\n- \\(W1\\) says “the other two boxes are empty,” so \\(W1\\) is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.\n- \\(K1\\) says “the box with no false statements has the gems,” meaning the unique all-true box contains the gems.\n\nSince at least one statement is true and at least one is false, and since \\(B1 = T\\) would force \\(W1 = K1 = T\\) with no false statement, we must have \\(B1 = F\\).\n\nRemaining possible truth patterns:\n1. \\(B1=F, W1=T, K1=T\\): then \\(W1\\) true means gems are in WHITE, but \\(K1\\) would require exactly one all-true box, while both WHITE and BLACK would be all-true. Contradiction.\n2. \\(B1=F, W1=T, K1=F\\): then gems are in WHITE, making WHITE the only all-true box, so \\(K1\\) should be true. Contradiction.\n3. \\(B1=F, W1=F, K1=T\\): then gems are not in WHITE; \\(K1\\) true means the all-true BLACK box has the gems. So gems are in BLACK. This is consistent.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.387, "ast_prob": 0.173, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00271", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE WHITE BOX HAS AT MOST TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "White box contains only one statement, so its number of false statements is either 0 or 1. Either way, “White box has at most two false statements” is always TRUE. Therefore B1 says the negation of a TRUE statement, so B1 is FALSE.\n\nW1 says “The statement above this one is TRUE,” meaning B1 is TRUE. But B1 is FALSE, so W1 is FALSE.\n\nThus BLUE and WHITE boxes are both all-FALSE. Since at least one box must be all-TRUE, BLACK must be all-TRUE, so K1 is TRUE.\n\nK1 says “The gems are in a box that is TRUE.” The only TRUE box is BLACK, so the gems must be in BLACK.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.672, "ast_prob": 0.42, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00273", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE OR THE WHITE BOX HAS EXACTLY ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS OR THE BLACK BOX IS BLUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the single gem location determine K1 directly:\n\n- K1: “The other two boxes relative to the black box are empty” is true iff the gems are in the BLACK box.\n- W1: “Either boxes next to the white box contain gems or the black box is blue.”  \n  “Boxes next to the white box” means BLUE and BLACK. Since exactly one box contains gems, both cannot contain gems, so that part is false. Also, the black box is not blue. Therefore W1 = False.\n- B1: “Either the top statement of each box is true or the white box has exactly one true statement.”  \n  Since W1 is false, the white box does not have exactly one true statement. Also, W1 false means not all top statements are true. Therefore B1 = False.\n- At least one box must be all-true, so K1 must be True. Thus the gems are in the BLACK box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.207, "ast_prob": 0.453, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00274", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY AND A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location be the only unknown. Since there is exactly one gem:\n\n- If gem = BLUE:  \n  B1 = F (Blue is not empty), W1 = F (White has no gem), K1 = F.  \n  This makes all statements false, violating the rule that at least one box is all-true.\n\n- If gem = WHITE:  \n  B1 = T, W1 = T, K1 = T.  \n  This makes all statements true, violating the rule that at least one box is all-false.\n\n- If gem = BLACK:  \n  B1 = T (Blue is empty), W1 = F (White has no gem), K1 = F (Black is not empty, and White does not contain the gems).  \n  Blue is all-true, and White/Black are all-false, satisfying all rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.337, "ast_prob": 0.249, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00275", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The boxes are fixed: BLUE is left, WHITE is middle, BLACK is right.  \nK1 says BLUE is the middle box, so K1 is false.  \nW1 says both boxes next to WHITE contain gems; since only one box contains gems, W1 is false.  \nAt least one box must be all-true, so at least one statement must be true. The only remaining possible true statement is B1.  \nThus B1 is true, meaning the BLACK box contains the gems.  \n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.346, "ast_prob": 0.229, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00276", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(B1\\) mean “Blue and Black are empty,” i.e. the White box has the gems.  \nLet \\(W1\\) mean “Blue is empty.”  \nLet \\(K1\\) mean “White has the gems,” since Black’s only adjacent box is White.\n\nTest the three gem locations:\n\n- If gems are in **Blue**:  \n  B1 = F, W1 = F, K1 = F → no all-true box, invalid.\n\n- If gems are in **White**:  \n  B1 = T, W1 = T, K1 = T → no all-false box, invalid.\n\n- If gems are in **Black**:  \n  B1 = F, W1 = T, K1 = F → White box is all-true; Blue and Black are all-false. This satisfies the rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.304, "ast_prob": 0.172, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00277", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT LEAST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let G be the gem location.\n\nStatements:\n- B1 is true iff G = BLUE.\n- K1 is true iff G = WHITE, because Black’s only adjacent box is White.\n- W1 is true iff at least two of B1, W1, K1 are false.\n\nCheck each possible gem location:\n\n1. If G = BLUE:\n   - B1 = T, K1 = F.\n   - If W1 = T, then only K1 is false → W1 should be F.\n   - If W1 = F, then K1 and W1 are false → W1 should be T.\n   - Contradiction. Impossible.\n\n2. If G = WHITE:\n   - B1 = F, K1 = T.\n   - If W1 = T, then only B1 is false → W1 should be F.\n   - If W1 = F, then B1 and W1 are false → W1 should be T.\n   - Contradiction. Impossible.\n\n3. If G = BLACK:\n   - B1 = F, K1 = F.\n   - If W1 = T, then B1 and K1 are false, so “at least two statements are false” is true.\n   - W1 = T is consistent.\n   - At least one statement is true (W1), and at least one is false (B1, K1).\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.364, "ast_prob": 0.227, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00278", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE AND THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS AND THE BLUE BOX IS BLUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let G be the gem location. The WHITE box has one statement:\n\n- W1: “The BLUE box is empty” = G is not in BLUE.\n\nBLUE’s statement:\n\n- B1: “All statements on WHITE are true and the BLUE box is empty.”  \n  Since WHITE has only W1, B1 is true exactly when W1 is true and BLUE is empty. But W1 already says BLUE is empty, so B1 has the same truth value as W1.\n\nBLACK’s statement:\n\n- K1: “A box next to this box contains the gems and the BLUE box is BLUE.”  \n  BLACK is on the right, so its only adjacent box is WHITE. The “BLUE box is BLUE” part is true. Therefore K1 is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.\n\nCheck the three possible gem locations:\n\n- Gem in BLUE: W1=F, B1=F, K1=F → no all-true box, invalid.\n- Gem in WHITE: W1=T, B1=T, K1=T → no all-false box, invalid.\n- Gem in BLACK: W1=T, B1=T, K1=F → at least one all-true and at least one all-false, valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.307, "ast_prob": 0.426, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00279", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] AT MOST THREE STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT MOST ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is always true: there are only three statements total, so “at most three are true” cannot be false.\n\nW1 is always true: the White box contains only one statement, so it can never have more than one false statement.\n\nTherefore, to satisfy the rule that at least one box is all-false, K1 must be false. That means the gems cannot be in a true box. Since Blue and White are true boxes, the gems must be in Black, and Black’s statement is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.584, "ast_prob": 0.403, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00280", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT MOST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Since each box has exactly one statement, “box is true” means its statement is true.\n\n- W1 says “At most two statements are false.” With 3 statements, this is equivalent to “at least one statement is true.” The rules already guarantee at least one true box, so W1 is true.\n- B1 says “A box next to this box is true.” The only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so B1 is true iff W1 is true. Since W1 is true, B1 is true.\n- The rules also require at least one false box, so K1 must be false.\n- K1 says “The gems are in a box that is true.” For K1 to be false, the gem box must not be true. BLUE and WHITE are true, so the gems cannot be there. Therefore the gems are in BLACK, which is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.387, "ast_prob": 0.356, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00281", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS AND BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE AND THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT LEAST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Exactly one box contains the gems, so BLUE and WHITE cannot both contain gems.\n\n- B1 says: “BLUE contains the gems AND the box next to BLACK contains gems.” Since BLACK is on the right, the only box next to it is WHITE. Thus B1 would require both BLUE and WHITE to contain gems, which is impossible. So B1 = FALSE.\n- K1 says: “WHITE has at least one true statement.” The only statement on WHITE is W1, so K1 is true exactly when W1 is true.\n- At least one box must be all-true, so at least one statement must be true. Since B1 is false, either W1 or K1 must be true. But K1 and W1 have the same truth value, so W1 must be true.\n- W1 says: “ALL statements on WHITE are true AND BLACK contains the gems.” Since W1 is true, BLACK must contain the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.587, "ast_prob": 0.419, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00282", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] AT MOST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- B1 = “At most one statement is true.”\n- W1 = “The black box is empty.”\n- K1 = “The top statement of each box is true” = “B1 is true, W1 is true, and K1 is true.”\n\nSo K1 is true only if B1 and W1 are both true; otherwise K1 is false.\n\nCheck possible truth assignments:\n\n- If B1 is false, then more than one statement would have to be true, but K1 also cannot be true unless B1 is true. This leads to contradictions.\n- The only consistent assignment is:\n  - B1 = True: exactly one statement is true.\n  - W1 = False: the black box is not empty.\n  - K1 = False: since W1 is false, K1’s claim is false.\n\nSince W1 is false, the black box is not empty. With exactly one box containing gems, the gems must be in the BLACK box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.686, "ast_prob": 0.107, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00283", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Each box has exactly one statement, so “all true” means that statement is true, and “all false” means that statement is false.\n\n- W1 says the WHITE box has at least two false statements. But WHITE has only one statement, so it can never have two false statements. Therefore W1 is FALSE.\n- B1 says the top statement of every box is true, meaning B1, W1, and K1 are all true. Since W1 is false, B1 cannot be true. Therefore B1 is FALSE.\n- At least one box must be all-true, so at least one statement must be true. Since B1 and W1 are false, K1 must be TRUE.\n- K1 says the BLUE and WHITE boxes are empty. If K1 is true, those two are empty, so the gems must be in the BLACK box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.241, "ast_prob": 0.187, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00285", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EXACTLY TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Since each box has only one statement, a box is all-true exactly when its statement is true, and all-false exactly when its statement is false.\n\n- If the gems were in BLUE, then B1 would be the statement on the gem box, so B1 would claim “B1 is false,” a contradiction.\n- If the gems were in WHITE, then W1 is false and B1 would be true. K1 would then be true iff exactly two statements are false, but that creates a self-contradiction either way.\n- Therefore the gems must be in BLACK. Then W1 is true.  \n  If K1 were true, B1 would be false, giving only one false statement, so K1 could not be true. Thus K1 is false, making B1 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.416, "ast_prob": 0.282, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00286", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because the black box is on the right, not the middle.  \nSo the BLUE box is all-false, satisfying the \"at least one all-false\" rule.  \nEach box has exactly one statement, so no box can have two false statements. Therefore W1 is false.  \nTo satisfy “at least one all-true box,” at least one statement must be true. Since B1 and W1 are false, K1 must be true.  \nK1 is true only if the gems are in the BLACK box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.301, "ast_prob": 0.148, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00287", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EXACTLY TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let each box's single statement be its \"top statement.\" So B1 asserts that B1, W1, and K1 are all true.\n\n- If the gems were in BLUE or WHITE, then the BLACK box is empty, so W1 = T.\n- With W1 = T, B1 becomes equivalent to “B1 and K1 are true.”\n  - If K1 = T, then exactly two statements must be false, but W1 and K1 are already true, so at most B1 could be false — impossible.\n  - If K1 = F, then B1 must be F, making B1 and K1 both false, so exactly two statements are false, making K1 true — contradiction.\n- Therefore W1 cannot be true, so gems must be in BLACK.\n- With gems in BLACK, W1 = F.\n- Then B1's claim that all top statements are true is false, so B1 = F.\n- B1 and W1 are false, so exactly two statements are false; therefore K1 = T.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.792, "ast_prob": 0.272, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00288", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because the BLUE box is on the left, not the middle.  \nW1 is false because exactly one box contains gems, so both adjacent boxes cannot contain gems.  \nTherefore, to satisfy the rule that at least one box has all true statements, K1 must be true.  \nK1 is true only when the BLACK box contains the gems, making the other two boxes empty.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.228, "ast_prob": 0.488, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00289", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- \\(B1\\) = “The top statement of each box is true”\n- \\(W1\\) = “The other two boxes relative to the BLACK box are empty”\n- \\(K1\\) = “The box with no false statements has the gems”\n\nSince each box has only one statement, each box is all-true iff its statement is true, and all-false iff its statement is false. Also, at least one statement must be true and at least one false.\n\n1. **Evaluate B1:**  \n   \\(B1\\) says all three statements are true. If \\(B1\\) were true, then \\(W1\\) and \\(K1\\) would also be true, making all three statements true. But at least one statement must be false. Therefore \\(B1 = F\\).\n\n2. **Evaluate W1:**  \n   \\(W1\\) says the two boxes other than BLACK are empty. Since exactly one box has the gems, that is true exactly when the gems are in BLACK. So:  \n   \\(W1 = T \\iff \\text{gems in BLACK}\\).\n\n3. **Evaluate K1:**  \n   Since \\(B1 = F\\), the only box with “no false statements” would have to be a box whose own statement is true. The phrase “THE box with no false statements” implies a unique such box.  \n   - If \\(W1 = T\\) and \\(K1 = T\\), there would be two boxes with no false statements, so \\(K1\\) could not be true.  \n   - If \\(W1 = F\\) and \\(K1 = T\\), then the unique no-false box would be BLACK, so \\(K1\\) would require gems in BLACK, but \\(W1 = F\\) means gems are not in BLACK. Contradiction.  \n   Therefore the only consistent possibility is \\(W1 = T\\), \\(K1 = F\\).\n\n4. Since \\(W1 = T\\), the gems are in BLACK.\n\nFinal check:  \n- \\(B1 = F\\) because not all statements are true.  \n- \\(W1 = T\\) because BLACK has the gems, so BLUE and WHITE are empty.  \n- \\(K1 = F\\) because the unique no-false box is WHITE, but WHITE is empty.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.647, "ast_prob": 0.121, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00290", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(G\\) be the box with the gems.\n\n- Since WHITE has only one statement,  \n  \\(B1 = W1\\).\n- \\(W1\\) is true iff a box next to WHITE contains the gems, i.e. iff the gems are not in WHITE:  \n  \\(W1 = (G \\neq \\text{WHITE})\\).\n- A box is “true” iff its statement(s) are all true.\n- \\(K1\\) says the gems are in a true box.\n\nCheck cases:\n\n1. **Gems in BLUE**:  \n   \\(W1 = T\\), so \\(B1 = T\\). Since BLUE is true and contains the gems, \\(K1 = T\\). Then all three boxes are all-true, so no box is all-false. Invalid.\n\n2. **Gems in WHITE**:  \n   \\(W1 = F\\), so \\(B1 = F\\). WHITE is false, so \\(K1 = F\\). Then all three boxes are all-false, so no box is all-true. Invalid.\n\n3. **Gems in BLACK**:  \n   \\(W1 = T\\), so \\(B1 = T\\). BLUE and WHITE are all-true. To satisfy the “at least one all-false” rule, BLACK must be all-false, so \\(K1 = F\\). This is consistent: the gems are in BLACK, but BLACK is not a true box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.559, "ast_prob": 0.304, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00291", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location be one of BLUE, WHITE, or BLACK.\n\n- If gem = BLUE, then BLUE is not empty, so B1 = F and K1 = F. Also, the other two boxes relative to WHITE are BLUE and BLACK, and BLUE is not empty, so W1 = F. This gives no all-true box → invalid.\n- If gem = WHITE, then BLUE and BLACK are empty, so B1 = T, K1 = T, and W1 = T. This gives no all-false box → invalid.\n- If gem = BLACK, then BLUE is empty, so B1 = T and K1 = T. But the other two boxes relative to WHITE are BLUE and BLACK; BLACK is not empty, so W1 = F. Thus BLUE has all-true, WHITE has all-false, and BLACK has all-true → valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.429, "ast_prob": 0.201, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00292", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT LEAST TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE AND BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "BLACK is the rightmost box, so the only box physically next to BLACK is WHITE. Thus W1’s “boxes next to the BLACK box contain gems” means the gem would have to be in WHITE.\n\nB1 says the two boxes other than BLACK are empty, so B1 is true exactly when BLACK contains the gems. Therefore B1 has the same truth value as K1.\n\nTest gem locations:\n\n- Gem in BLUE → B1=F, K1=F, and W1 cannot be true because it would be the only true statement. All statements would be false — invalid.\n- Gem in WHITE → B1=F, K1=F, and W1 again cannot be true because it would be the only true statement. All statements false — invalid.\n- Gem in BLACK → B1=T, K1=T. W1’s gem-location condition is false, so W1=F. This gives at least one all-true box and at least one all-false box — valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.653, "ast_prob": 0.261, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00293", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE AND THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is false because the blue box is left, not middle.  \nSince B1 says all statements on White are true, and W1 is false, B1 is false.  \nThus both Blue and White are all-false boxes.  \nAt least one box must be all-true, so Black must be the all-true box, making K1 true.  \nSince K1 says the gems are in a true box, and Black is the only true box, the gems must be in Black.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.51, "ast_prob": 0.431, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00029", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY AND A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EITHER THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE OR THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is always false: it claims both that the other two boxes are empty (which would put the gems in BLUE) and that a box next to BLUE has the gems (which would put the gems outside BLUE). These cannot both be true.\n\nSo B1 = F.  \nBecause B1 is false, the first part of K1 — “the top statement of each box is true” — is false. Thus K1 is true exactly if the WHITE box is true, i.e. W1 = T.\n\nNow test gem = BLUE. Then BLUE has two false statements: its written statement B1 is false, and the statement that BLUE is empty is also false since BLUE contains the gems. Therefore W1 is true, which makes K1 true as well.\n\nFinal: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.769, "ast_prob": 0.345, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00030", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1: “A box next to the White box is true” — taking this as “exactly one of the two boxes next to White is true.”\n\n- Each box has only one statement, so no box can have two false statements. Thus W1 is false, making the WHITE box false.\n- The gems cannot be in BLACK: K1 would then say “BLACK is false,” i.e. K1 = not K1, impossible.\n- If the gems were in WHITE, then WHITE is false, so K1 would be true, making BLACK true. Then, for B1 to be true, BLUE would have to be false — but B1 itself would then be true, contradiction.\n- Therefore the gems must be in BLUE. Then K1 is false, so BLACK is false. B1 is true because exactly one box next to WHITE (BLUE) is true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.662, "ast_prob": 0.238, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00039", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE OR THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE AND THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS WHITE AND BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "White has only one statement, so “the statement above this one” is false. Therefore W1 is false, making the White box all-false.\n\nThe Black box is rightmost, so its only neighboring box is White. Since White is false, “a box next to the black box is true” is false. Thus B1 reduces to “this box contains the gems.” For at least one box to be all-true, and since Black cannot be true because K1 says “the blue box is white” which is false, Blue must be all-true. Therefore B1 is true and the gems are in the Blue box.\n\nK1 is false because its first clause is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.394, "ast_prob": 0.402, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00079", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EITHER BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS OR THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Since Black is the rightmost box, the only box next to it is White. So B1 is equivalent to saying White’s statement is true: B1 = W1.\n\nW1 says “This box is empty,” so W1 is true exactly when the gem is not in White.\n\nFor K1: the boxes next to White are Blue and Black. Since exactly one box contains the gems, both adjacent boxes cannot contain gems, so the first disjunct is false. Thus K1 reduces to “the other two boxes are empty,” meaning Blue and White are empty, so the gem would be in Black.\n\nTesting gem locations:\n- Gem in Blue: W1 = T, B1 = T, K1 = F. Has both true and false statements. Valid.\n- Gem in White: W1 = F, B1 = F, K1 = F. All false, invalid.\n- Gem in Black: W1 = T, B1 = T, K1 = T. All true, invalid.\n\nThus the gem is in Blue.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.223, "ast_prob": 0.118, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00294", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let each box’s truth status be determined by its single statement.\n\n- B1 says the gems are in Blue, so B1 is true iff gem = BLUE.\n- W1 says B1 is true, so W1 has the same truth value as B1.\n- Therefore B1 and W1 are both true if the gems are Blue, and both false if the gems are White or Black.\n\nNow use the rule that there must be at least one true statement and at least one false statement:\n\n- If gems were Blue: B1 = T, W1 = T. Then K1 would need to be false. But K1 says the box with no false statements has the gems. If gems are Blue, Blue has no false statements and has the gems, so K1 would be true, making all three statements true — impossible.\n- If gems were White: B1 = F, W1 = F. Then K1 would need to be true. But if gems are White, the White box has a false statement, so K1 would be false — impossible.\n- If gems are Black: B1 = F, W1 = F, so K1 must be true. Black then has no false statements and contains the gems, making K1 true. This is consistent.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.342, "ast_prob": 0.284, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00295", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- \\(B_1\\) = “The black box is empty”\n- \\(W_1\\) = “The black box is true”\n- \\(K_1\\) = “It is not true that the top statement of each box is true”\n\n1. \\(K_1\\) cannot be false.  \n   If \\(K_1\\) were false, then its statement would be false, meaning it *would* be true that all top statements are true — which would make \\(K_1\\) true. Contradiction.  \n   So \\(K_1 = T\\).\n\n2. Since \\(K_1\\) is true, the black box is true. Therefore \\(W_1 = T\\).\n\n3. Since \\(K_1\\) is true, it is true that “not all top statements are true.”  \n   We already have \\(W_1 = T\\) and \\(K_1 = T\\), so \\(B_1\\) must be false.\n\n4. \\(B_1\\) false means the black box is not empty, so the gems are in the black box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.578, "ast_prob": 0.443, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00296", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] AT MOST TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is true because the WHITE box is indeed the middle box.\n\nIf K1 were true, then with W1 true, both B1=true and B1=false would lead to contradictions involving B1’s “at most two statements are true.” Therefore K1 must be false.\n\nWith W1 true and K1 false, B1 must be true, since only one other statement is true and “at most two statements are true” is satisfied.\n\nSo true boxes are BLUE and WHITE; BLACK is false. Since K1 says the gems are in a true box, and K1 is false, the gems must be in the false box: BLACK.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.741, "ast_prob": 0.465, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00297", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE OR THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EITHER THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE OR THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(B1, W1, K1\\) be the truth values of the statements.\n\n- BLUE is not the middle box, and the only box next to BLACK is WHITE, so  \n  \\(B1 = W1\\).\n- \\(W1\\) says all top statements are true:  \n  \\(W1 = B1 \\land W1 \\land K1\\).  \n  Since \\(B1 = W1\\), this forces \\(W1 \\Rightarrow K1\\).\n- If \\(W1\\) were true, then \\(B1\\) and \\(K1\\) would also be true, making every statement true. But the rules require at least one all-false box, so this is impossible.\n- Therefore \\(W1 = F\\), \\(B1 = F\\).\n- Since at least one statement must be true, \\(K1 = T\\).\n- \\(K1\\) says the gems are in a box that is true. With \\(B1\\) and \\(W1\\) false, the only true box is BLACK, so the gems must be in BLACK.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.637, "ast_prob": 0.352, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00298", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS WHITE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let each box’s truth status be determined by whether its single statement is true.\n\n- B1 says “A box next to the BLUE box is TRUE.” The only box next to BLUE is WHITE, and WHITE is true iff W1 is true. So B1 ≡ W1.\n- K1 says “THE BLUE BOX IS WHITE,” which is false because the left box is BLUE. So K1 = F, meaning BLACK is a false box.\n\nNow consider the gem location:\n- If gems were in BLUE, then W1 would mean BLUE is false, so B1 would be false, but B1 ≡ W1, contradiction.\n- If gems were in WHITE, then W1 would mean WHITE is false, so W1 would be false, contradiction.\n- Therefore gems must be in BLACK.\n\nWith gems in BLACK, BLACK is false, so W1 is true. Since B1 ≡ W1, B1 is also true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.49, "ast_prob": 0.21, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00299", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLACK BOX ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because the black box is on the right, not the middle.  \nW1 is true exactly when K1 is true, since W1 says all statements on the black box are true.  \nK1 says the other two boxes relative to black are empty, which is true exactly when the gems are in the black box.  \n\n- If gems were in BLUE or WHITE, then K1 would be false, making W1 false, and B1 is already false → all statements false, violating the rule.\n- If gems are in BLACK, then K1 is true, W1 is true, and B1 is false → satisfies at least one true and at least one false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.639, "ast_prob": 0.122, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00300", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] AT LEAST ONE STATEMENT IS FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT MOST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the truth values of B1, W1, and K1 be represented by b, w, and k.\n\n- If B1 were false, then “at least one statement is false” would be true because B1 itself would be false. Contradiction, so B1 = True.\n- If W1 were false, then more than two statements would be false, meaning all three are false. But B1 is already true, so W1 cannot be false. Thus W1 = True.\n- Since B1 is true, at least one statement must be false. B1 and W1 are both true, so K1 must be false.\n- K1 says “This box is empty,” so K1 being false means the BLACK box is not empty. Since exactly one box contains the gems, the gems are in BLACK.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 3, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.573, "ast_prob": 0.298, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00301", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [K2] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let G be the gem box.\n\n- B1 is true iff G = BLACK.\n- W1 is true iff a neighbor of WHITE contains the gems, i.e. G ≠ WHITE.\n- K1 says “boxes next to BLACK contain gems”; since BLACK’s neighbors are BLUE and WHITE, and only one box can contain gems, K1 is always false.\n- K2 is true iff at least one neighbor of BLACK is an all-true box. BLUE and WHITE each have one statement, so K2 = B1 OR W1.\n\nCheck gem locations:\n\n- If G = WHITE: B1 = F, W1 = F, K1 = F, K2 = F → no all-true box, invalid.\n- If G = BLACK: B1 = T, W1 = T, K1 = F, K2 = T → no all-false box, invalid.\n- If G = BLUE: B1 = F, W1 = T, K1 = F, K2 = T → BLUE is all-false, WHITE is all-true, BLACK is mixed. Valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.278, "ast_prob": 0.381, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00302", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n  [W2] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(B1\\) mean the Blue box has the gems, so \\(B1\\) is true exactly when the gems are in BLUE.  \n\\(W1\\) is true exactly when \\(B1\\) is true, since Blue has only one statement.  \n\\(W2\\) says “all statements on White are true,” so \\(W2 = W1 \\land W2\\). Thus if \\(W1\\) is false, \\(W2\\) must be false.  \n\\(K1\\) says a box next to Black is true, and the only adjacent box is White, so \\(K1 = W1 \\land W2\\).\n\n- If gems are WHITE or BLACK, then \\(B1=F\\), so \\(W1=F\\), \\(W2=F\\), \\(K1=F\\). All boxes would be all-false, violating the rule.\n- If gems are BLUE, then \\(B1=T\\), so \\(W1=T\\).  \n  - If \\(W2=T\\), then \\(K1=T\\), making every box all-true, violating the rule that at least one box is all-false.\n  - If \\(W2=F\\), then \\(K1=F\\): Blue is all-true, White is mixed, Black is all-false. This satisfies all rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.554, "ast_prob": 0.134, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00303", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS AND THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS WHITE\n  [K2] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is false because the black box is not white.\n\nW1 cannot be true: if it were true, its second part would require K1 to be true, while its first part would require the box with two false statements (only possible box with two statements is black) to have K1 and K2 both false. Contradiction. So W1 is false.\n\nSince W1 is false, the WHITE box is all-false. The BLACK box cannot be all-true because K1 is false. Therefore the BLUE box must be the required all-true box, so B1 must be true, meaning the gems are in the BLUE box.\n\nWith gems in BLUE, K2 is false because the box next to BLUE (WHITE) does not contain gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.223, "ast_prob": 0.181, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00304", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT MOST TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n  [K2] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 refers to “the statement above this one,” but there is no statement above B1, so that referenced claim is false. Therefore B1 is true.\n\nLet:\n- K1 = “BLACK box is empty” = gems are not in BLACK.\n- K2 = “No box next to BLACK contains gems.” BLACK is rightmost, so its only neighbor is WHITE; K2 = gems are not in WHITE.\n\nCheck gem locations:\n\n- If gems were WHITE: B1=T, K1=T, K2=F. Then W1 would be true iff at most two statements are true, but with W1=T there would be 3 true statements, contradiction; with W1=F there would be 2 true statements, making W1 true, contradiction.\n- If gems were BLACK: B1=T, K1=F, K2=T. Same contradiction for W1.\n- Therefore gems must be BLUE.\n\nWith gems in BLUE:\n- K1 = T, K2 = T.\n- B1 = T.\n- So three statements are true regardless of W1. If W1=T, four statements would be true, so W1 would be false; if W1=F, three statements are true, so W1 is false. Thus W1=F.\n\nFinal:\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.269, "ast_prob": 0.24, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00305", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO FALSE STATEMENTS AND THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n  [K2] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n\n1. BLUE box has only one statement, B1, so it cannot have “at least two false statements.”\n2. Therefore W1’s first clause is false, making W1 false regardless of anything else. So WHITE is an all-false box.\n3. K1 says “W1 is true,” so K1 is false.\n4. K2 says “K1 is not true,” so K2 is true.\n5. Thus BLACK is mixed: one false, one true.\n6. Since there must be at least one all-true box, and WHITE is all-false while BLACK is mixed, BLUE must be all-true.\n7. So B1 is true. B1 says the gems are in a box that is true; BLUE is the only true box, so the gems must be in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.743, "ast_prob": 0.495, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00306", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n  [W2] THIS BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The WHITE box’s first statement W1 is the top statement in that box, so there is no statement above it. Therefore W1 is false.\n\nSince W1 is false, the WHITE box cannot have two true statements, so W2 (“This box has exactly two true statements”) is also false.\n\nThe BLACK statement K1 says the BLUE box is the middle box, but BLUE is left, so K1 is false.\n\nNow WHITE and BLACK are both all-false, so the required all-true box must be BLUE. Thus B1 must be true, meaning the gems are in the BLUE box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.345, "ast_prob": 0.113, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00307", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS WHITE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [W2] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The statements reduce as follows:\n\n- B1 is on the BLUE box: “This box is white” is false, because the BLUE box is blue. So B1 = F.\n- W2: “The WHITE box is the middle box” is true by position. So W2 = T.\n- W1: “The BLUE box contains the gems” is true iff the gems are in BLUE.\n- K1: “The other two boxes relative to the BLUE box are empty” is true iff WHITE and BLACK are both empty, which happens iff the gems are in BLUE.\n\nCheck gem locations:\n\n- If gems are in BLUE: Blue box is all false (B1=F); White box is all true (W1=T, W2=T); Black box is all true (K1=T). This satisfies all rules.\n- If gems are in WHITE or BLACK: White box becomes mixed (W1=F, W2=T), and no box is all true, violating the rule.\n\nTherefore gems must be in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.38, "ast_prob": 0.417, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00308", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS AND A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n  [W2] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT MOST THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- W2 is always true, because the white box is indeed the middle box.\n- Since W2 is true, not all 4 statements can be false, so K1 “at most three statements are false” is also true.\n- Therefore the black box has no false statements (K1 is its only statement), so the black box is always all-true.\n\nThe rules require at least one all-false box. White cannot be all-false because W2 is true. Black cannot be all-false because K1 is true. Therefore Blue must be the all-false box, so B1 must be false.\n\nB1 says that the box next to the black box contains the gems, i.e. the white box contains the gems. Since B1 is false, the gems are not in White.\n\nNow suppose the gems were in Black. Then Black would be the unique all-true box containing the gems, making W1 true. But if W1 were true, White would also be all-true, so Black would no longer be the unique all-true box with the gems, making W1 false. Contradiction.\n\nSo the gems must be in Blue. Then B1 is false, Blue is all-false, Black is all-true, W1 is false, W2 is true, and K1 is true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; W2=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.343, "ast_prob": 0.163, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00309", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n  [W2] AT LEAST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- B1 = “The top statement of each box is true”\n- W1 = “The other two boxes relative to the blue box are empty”\n- W2 = “At least two statements are false”\n- K1 = “The top statement of each box is true”\n\nB1 and K1 both assert: B1 is true, W1 is true, and K1 is true.  \nSo B1 and K1 must have the same truth value, and if either is true, then W1 must also be true.\n\nW1 is true exactly when the gems are in the BLUE box.\n\nCheck gem placements:\n\n- If gems are NOT in BLUE, then W1 is false.  \n  Since B1 and K1 cannot be true when W1 is false, B1 and K1 are false.  \n  Then B1, W1, and K1 are all false, so W2 is true.  \n  This gives Blue all-false, White mixed, Black all-false, but no all-true box — invalid.\n\n- If gems ARE in BLUE, then W1 is true.  \n  Try B1=K1=true: then W2 must be false, giving White mixed and no all-false box — invalid.  \n  Try B1=K1=false: then B1 and K1 are false, so W2 is true.  \n  This gives Blue all-false, White all-true, Black all-false — valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.539, "ast_prob": 0.337, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00310", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n  [W2] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is false because the black box is on the right, not the middle.\n\nW1 is the top statement in the WHITE box, so there is no statement “above” it; therefore W1 is false. W2 says W1 is true, so W2 is also false. Thus the WHITE box is all false.\n\nThe BLACK box is also all false because K1 is false. Since at least one box must be all true, the BLUE box must be all true, so B1 is true. That means the other two boxes are empty, so the gems must be in the BLUE box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.262, "ast_prob": 0.144, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00311", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE OR THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [W2] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT LEAST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location determine the statements:\n\n- B1 = “BLUE box is empty” = TRUE iff gem is not in BLUE.\n- W2 = “The other two boxes relative to BLACK are empty” = TRUE iff gem is in BLACK.\n- W1 = “B1 is true OR WHITE contains the gems” = TRUE iff B1 is true or gem is in WHITE.\n- K1 = “At least one statement is true” must be TRUE if any other statement is true; otherwise it would leave no all-true box.\n\nEvaluate each possible gem location:\n\n1. Gem in BLUE:\n   - B1 = FALSE, W2 = FALSE, W1 = FALSE.\n   - BLUE and WHITE are all-false.\n   - BLACK must be all-true, so K1 = TRUE.\n   - This satisfies both required all-true and all-false boxes.\n\n2. Gem in WHITE:\n   - B1 = TRUE, W2 = FALSE, W1 = TRUE.\n   - BLUE and BLACK are all-true, WHITE is mixed.\n   - No all-false box → invalid.\n\n3. Gem in BLACK:\n   - B1 = TRUE, W2 = TRUE, W1 = TRUE.\n   - All boxes are all-true.\n   - No all-false box → invalid.\n\nTherefore the only valid solution is gem in BLUE with K1 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.328, "ast_prob": 0.178, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00312", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [W2] AT MOST ONE STATEMENT IS FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS BLUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is false because the black box is not blue.\n\nLet the gem location determine B1 and W1:\n- If gem is in BLUE, then B1 = T and W1 = F.\n- If gem is in WHITE, then B1 = F and W1 = T.\n- If gem is in BLACK, then B1 = F and W1 = F.\n\nSince K1 is false, there is already at least one false statement. Therefore W2, “At most one statement is false,” must also be false whenever K1 counts as false: with K1 false, at least one statement is false, and W2 being true would require at most one total false statement, but K1 already makes that impossible unless all others are true. Checking cases confirms W2 is false in every scenario.\n\nSo K1 = F and W2 = F in all cases.\n\nNow box-level conditions:\n- The BLUE box is all-true only if B1 = T, meaning the gem is in BLUE.\n- The WHITE box cannot be all-true because W2 is false.\n- The BLACK box cannot be all-true because K1 is false.\n\nThe rule requires at least one box to have all statements true. The only possible all-true box is BLUE, so the gems must be in BLUE.\n\nThen:\n- B1 = T\n- W1 = F\n- W2 = F\n- K1 = F\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.413, "ast_prob": 0.261, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00313", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [B2] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY AND BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "- BLACK is rightmost, so the only box next to it is WHITE. Therefore B1 means “WHITE contains gems.”\n- BLUE is leftmost, so the only box next to it is WHITE. Therefore K1 means “WHITE is empty AND WHITE contains gems,” which is impossible, so K1 is always FALSE.\n- B2 says the other two boxes relative to BLUE are empty, i.e. WHITE and BLACK are empty, so BLUE contains gems.\n- W1 says the other two boxes relative to BLUE are empty, so it also means BLUE contains gems.\n\nTest gem locations:\n1. Gems in BLUE: B1=F, B2=T → BLUE mixed; W1=T → WHITE all-true; K1=F → BLACK all-false. Valid.\n2. Gems in WHITE: B1=T, B2=F → BLUE mixed; W1=F → WHITE all-false; K1=F → BLACK all-false. No all-true box, invalid.\n3. Gems in BLACK: B1=F, B2=F → BLUE all-false; W1=F; K1=F. No all-true box, invalid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.548, "ast_prob": 0.232, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00314", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE AND ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n  [K2] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE AND THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n- \\(B1\\) = BLUE statement\n- \\(W1\\) = WHITE statement\n- \\(K1, K2\\) = BLACK statements\n\nA box is “true” if all its statements are true.\n\nBLUE is true iff \\(B1\\) is true.  \nWHITE is true iff \\(W1\\) is true.  \nBLACK is true iff \\(K1 \\land K2\\) are true.\n\nStatements become:\n- \\(B1 =\\) “gems are in a true box”\n- \\(W1 = B1 \\land W1 \\land K1\\)\n- \\(K1 = B1 \\land W1 \\land K1\\)\n- \\(K2 = B1 \\land K1\\)\n\nSolving these fixed-point equations gives only three possible truth assignments:\n\n1. \\(B1=T, W1=T, K1=T, K2=T\\) → all three boxes true → violates “at least one box all false.”\n2. \\(B1=F, W1=F, K1=F, K2=F\\) → all three boxes false → violates “at least one box all true.”\n3. \\(B1=T, W1=F, K1=F, K2=F\\) → BLUE is all true; WHITE and BLACK are all false → satisfies the box rules.\n\nSince \\(B1\\) is true, the gems are in a true box. The only true box is BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.722, "ast_prob": 0.32, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00315", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [W2] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS BLACK\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The boxes have fixed positions: BLUE is left, WHITE is middle, BLACK is right.\n\n- B1 says “THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX” → TRUE.\n- K1 says “THE BLACK BOX IS BLACK” → TRUE.\n\nSo BLUE and BLACK are both all-true boxes. Since at least one box must be all-false, WHITE must be the all-false box.\n\nThus:\n- W1 “THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS” → FALSE\n- W2 “THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS” → FALSE\n\nSince exactly one box contains gems, and neither WHITE nor BLACK contains them, BLUE must contain the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.258, "ast_prob": 0.405, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00317", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [W2] THIS BOX IS WHITE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EITHER THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS OR THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n1. W2 is true: the middle box is indeed white.\n2. B1 says the boxes next to the blue box contain the gems. The only box next to blue is white, so B1 is true iff the gems are in WHITE.\n3. W1 says a box next to white contains the gems. The boxes next to white are blue and black, so W1 is true iff the gems are NOT in WHITE.\n4. Test gem=BLACK: K1 would depend on whether the black box is false, leading to K1 = (K1 is false), which has no consistent truth value. So gem cannot be BLACK.\n5. Test gem=WHITE: B1=T, W1=F, W2=T, K1=T. Then Blue and Black are all-true, but no box is all-false, violating the rule.\n6. Therefore gem=BLUE. Then B1=F, W1=T, W2=T. The blue box is all-false, so K1’s second condition is true, making K1=T.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.442, "ast_prob": 0.253, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00318", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n  [W2] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE AND THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO FALSE STATEMENTS AND THE BLUE BOX IS BLACK\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- W2 = “W1 is true AND the other two boxes relative to White are empty.”  \n  The “other two boxes empty” means White has the gems, so W2 would require W1 true and White non-empty at the same time. Therefore W2 is always false.\n\n- K1 = “The black box has at least two false statements and the blue box is black.”  \n  The black box contains only K1, so it cannot have at least two false statements. Also, the blue box is not black. Therefore K1 is false.\n\nSince W2 and K1 are false, neither White nor Black can be an all-true box. The rules require at least one all-true box, so Blue must be all-true. Therefore B1 is true.\n\nB1 says “The box with no false statements has the gems.” The only all-true box is Blue, so Blue has the gems.\n\nThen W1 is true because White is empty.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.783, "ast_prob": 0.47, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00319", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n  [K2] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Because the boxes are arranged left/middle/right, the WHITE box has no statement above it, so W1 is false.\n\n- W1 = F → WHITE box is all-false.\n- K2 = F, since both boxes next to BLACK would need gems, but there is exactly one gem.\n- BLACK cannot be all-true because K2 is false, so at least one all-true box must be BLUE.\n- Therefore B1 = T, making BLUE all-true.\n- Since B1 says the gems are in a true box, and the only all-true box is BLUE, the gems are in BLUE.\n- K1 is true because the unique box with no false statements is BLUE, and BLUE has the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.483, "ast_prob": 0.306, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00320", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n  [K2] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(G\\) be the box with the gems, and note White is the only box next to Black.\n\n- \\(W1\\) is true iff the gems are NOT in White.\n- \\(B1\\) says the White statement is true, so \\(B1 = W1\\).\n- \\(K2\\) says B1, W1, and K1 are all true.\n\nCheck gem locations:\n\n1. **Gems in BLUE**:  \n   \\(W1=T\\), so \\(B1=T\\). Blue is all-true, so K1 is false. Then \\(K2=T \\land T \\land F = F\\).  \n   Blue all-true, White all-true, Black all-false. Satisfies all rules.\n\n2. **Gems in WHITE**:  \n   \\(W1=F\\), so \\(B1=F\\). White is all-false, so K1 is true. Then \\(K2=F\\).  \n   Blue all-false, White all-false, Black mixed. No all-true box, violates rule.\n\n3. **Gems in BLACK**:  \n   \\(W1=T\\), so \\(B1=T\\), and \\(K2=K1\\). K1 would claim Black is all-false, requiring \\(K1=F\\) and \\(K2=F\\), but \\(K2=K1\\) makes this contradictory. No consistent solution.\n\nTherefore, the only solution is gems in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.3, "ast_prob": 0.304, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00321", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n  [B2] THIS BOX IS WHITE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Since BLUE is the left box, the only box immediately next to it is WHITE. So W1 is true only if the gems are in WHITE.\n\n- B2 is false: the BLUE box is not white.\n- If gems are in BLUE:  \n  - BLUE has B1 false and B2 false → all-false.  \n  - WHITE has W1 false → all-false.  \n  - BLACK has K1 true → all-true.  \n  This satisfies all rules.\n- If gems are in WHITE: WHITE and BLACK would be all-true, but no box would be all-false → invalid.\n- If gems are in BLACK: all boxes would be all-false, but no box would be all-true → invalid.\n\nTherefore the gems must be in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; B2=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.282, "ast_prob": 0.22, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00322", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n  [K2] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is in a box with only one statement, so there is no statement above it; therefore W1 is false.  \nK1 says the top statement of every box is true, which would require W1 to be true; since W1 is false, K1 is false.  \nB1 and K2 both amount to saying the White and Black boxes are empty, which means the gems are in the Blue box.  \nIf the gems were anywhere else, no box would be all-true, violating the rules.  \nThus the only consistent solution is gems in Blue, with B1 true, W1 false, K1 false, and K2 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.2, "ast_prob": 0.366, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00323", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [W2] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let a “false box” mean a box with at least one false statement, i.e. not all-true.\n\n- B1 says the gems are in the box next to Blue, which is White. So B1 = “gems are in White.”\n- W1 is always true because White is the middle box.\n\nCase 1: Gems in Blue  \n- B1 is false, so Blue is false/all-false.  \n- W2 and K1 both say “gems are in a false box,” and Blue is false, so W2 = T and K1 = T.  \n- White has W1 = T, W2 = T → all-true.  \n- Black has K1 = T → all-true.  \n- Consistent: at least one all-true, at least one all-false.\n\nCase 2: Gems in White  \n- B1 would be true.  \n- W2 would say “White is false,” but White has W1 true, so White would be false only if W2 is false. That makes W2 true iff it is false — contradiction.\n\nCase 3: Gems in Black  \n- K1 would say “Black is false,” but Black has only K1, so Black false iff K1 false. That makes K1 true iff it is false — contradiction.\n\nSo gems must be in Blue.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.69, "ast_prob": 0.43, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00324", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [B2] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The BLACK box is on the right, so B1 (“The black box is the middle box”) is false.  \nThe WHITE box is in the middle; the boxes next to it are BLUE and BLACK, but only one box contains gems, so W1 (“Boxes next to this box contain gems”) is false.  \n\nThus BLUE cannot be all-true because B1 is false, and WHITE cannot be all-true because W1 is false. Therefore the all-true box must be BLACK, so K1 must be true, meaning the gems are in BLUE.  \nThen B2 (“This box is empty”) is false because BLUE contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; B2=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.311, "ast_prob": 0.144, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00325", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n  [W2] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is the only statement in the BLACK box, so “the statement above this one” has no referent; therefore K1 is false, making BLACK all-false.\n\nSince BLACK is already all-false, we need at least one all-true box, which must be BLUE or WHITE.\n\n- If gems were in WHITE, then W2 would be true, so WHITE could not be all-false; W1 would be false, making WHITE mixed. Then BLUE would need to be all-true, so B1 would be true. But B1 says the all-true box has the gems, while the gems would be in WHITE — contradiction.\n- If gems were in BLACK, then W2 would be false, and since BLACK is all-false, W1 would be true. WHITE would be mixed, so BLUE would need to be all-true. But B1 would then falsely claim the all-true box has the gems, since gems would be in BLACK — contradiction.\n\nTherefore gems are in BLUE. Then W2 is false. Since BLUE is all-true, the gems are in the all-true box, so B1 is true. W1 is false because the gems are not in a false box. K1 remains false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.512, "ast_prob": 0.491, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00326", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [B2] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because the BLUE box is on the left, not in the middle.  \nOnly the BLUE box has two statements, so “the box with two false statements” can only be the BLUE box.\n\n- If the gems were in WHITE, then B2 would be true, K1 would be false, W1 would be false, and there would be no all-true box.\n- If the gems were in BLACK, then B2 would be false, but K1 would still be false because the BLUE box would not have the gems, giving no all-true box.\n- If the gems are in BLUE, then B1 and B2 are false, so the BLUE box is the all-false box; K1 is true, so the BLACK box is true; W1 is true, so the WHITE box is true. This satisfies all rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; B2=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.272, "ast_prob": 0.498, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00327", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] AT LEAST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n  [B2] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 (“this box is the middle box”) is false because BLACK is the right box.  \nW1 says the top statement of each box is true, which requires K1 to be true, but K1 is false, so W1 is false.  \nThus WHITE and BLACK boxes are all-false boxes.  \nSince at least one box must be all-true, BLUE must be all-true: B1 = T and B2 = T.  \nB2 says the other two boxes are empty, so the gems must be in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.498, "ast_prob": 0.146, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00328", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS NOT EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [K2] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is always true because the white box is physically in the middle.  \nSo the black box can never be all false; it is either all true or mixed.\n\nIf the gems were in WHITE, then B1 would be true, making the blue box all true. Then W1 would also be true because the blue box next to it is true. But then no box would be all false — invalid.\n\nIf the gems were in BLACK, then B1 would be false, making the blue box all false. K2 would be false because the black box cannot be all false due to K1 being true. Then W1 would be false, making the white box all false. But then no box would be all true — invalid.\n\nIf the gems are in BLUE, then B1 is false, so the blue box is all false. K2 is true because the gems are in a false box. K1 is true, so the black box is all true. Then W1 is true because the black box next to it is true. This satisfies all rules uniquely.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.681, "ast_prob": 0.274, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00329", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n  [K2] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The only consistent placement is the BLUE box.\n\n- B1 is true exactly when the gems are in the BLUE box, because then both other boxes are empty.\n- W1 is false: the WHITE box is not the BLUE box / is not blue.\n- K1 is false because the statement it refers to above it is W1, which is false.\n- K2 is true because the WHITE box is empty when the gems are in the BLUE box.\n\nChecking gem in WHITE or BLACK leaves no all-true box, so only BLUE works.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.209, "ast_prob": 0.423, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00330", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n  [B2] AT LEAST ONE STATEMENT IS FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS WHITE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is false: the Blue box is not White.  \nW1 is false: the White box has only one statement, so it cannot have exactly two false statements.  \nSince W1 and K1 are false, at least one statement is false, so B2 is true.  \nWhite and Black are therefore all-false boxes, so the required all-true box must be Blue. Thus B1 must be true.  \nB1 says the gems are in a box that is true, and the only all-true box is Blue, so the gems are in Blue.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.641, "ast_prob": 0.489, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00331", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX HAS AT MOST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n  [B2] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is necessarily true: if it were false, both Blue statements would have to be true, making B1 true—contradiction. So B1 = T, and since Blue can have at most one true statement, B2 = F. Therefore the gems are not in Black.\n\nK1 is true because White is indeed the middle box.\n\nIf the gems were in White, then W1 would be true, making both White and Black all-true boxes and leaving no all-false box—violating the rules. So the gems must be in Blue, making W1 false.\n\nThis gives: Blue is mixed, White is all-false, Black is all-true, satisfying all conditions.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; B2=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.31, "ast_prob": 0.241, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00332", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [B2] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem locations be tested against the rules.\n\n- If gems are in BLUE:  \n  B1 = F, B2 = F, W1 = T, K1 = F  \n  Blue is all-false, White is all-true, Black is all-false → valid.\n\n- If gems are in WHITE:  \n  B1 = T, B2 = F, W1 = F, K1 = F  \n  No box is all-true → invalid.\n\n- If gems are in BLACK:  \n  B1 = F, B2 = T, W1 = T, K1 = T  \n  No box is all-false → invalid.\n\nTherefore the only valid solution is gems in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; B2=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.204, "ast_prob": 0.199, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00333", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n  [W2] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(G\\) be the gem location.\n\n- B1 says “White and Black are empty,” so B1 is true iff \\(G = \\text{BLUE}\\).\n- W1 says “Black is empty,” so W1 is true iff \\(G \\neq \\text{BLACK}\\).\n- W2 says “The other two boxes relative to Blue are empty,” meaning White and Black are empty, so W2 is true iff \\(G = \\text{BLUE}\\).\n- K1 says “The box with two false statements has the gems.” Only White has two statements. White has two false statements only when \\(G = \\text{BLACK}\\), but then White does not have the gems. Therefore K1 is false in every possible case.\n\nThus K1 = F, so Black is an all-false box, satisfying the all-false requirement.\n\nFor an all-true box, we need either:\n- Blue all-true: B1 = T, which requires \\(G = \\text{BLUE}\\).\n- White all-true: W1 = T and W2 = T, which also requires \\(G = \\text{BLUE}\\).\n\nSo the only valid gem location is BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.231, "ast_prob": 0.428, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00334", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n  [B2] AT MOST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EXACTLY ONE STATEMENT IS FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX HAS AT LEAST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(N\\) be the total number of true statements among B1, B2, W1, K1.\n\n- B2 says: “At most one statement is true,” so B2 is true iff \\(N \\le 1\\).\n- W1 says: “Exactly one statement is false,” so W1 is true iff \\(N = 3\\).\n\nAssume B2 is true. Then \\(N \\le 1\\), but B2 itself is true, so \\(N = 1\\), meaning B1, W1, and K1 are all false. Then Blue is mixed, White is all-false, Black is all-false. There is no all-true box, violating the rules. So B2 is false.\n\nSince B2 is false, \\(N \\ge 2\\).\n\nAssume W1 is true. Then \\(N = 3\\). With B2 false and W1 true, we need B1 and K1 both true. Then Blue is mixed, White is all-true, Black is all-true. There is no all-false box, violating the rules. So W1 is false.\n\nNow B2 is false and W1 is false, so only B1 and K1 can be true. Since \\(N \\ge 2\\), both B1 and K1 must be true.\n\nB1 true means the White and Black boxes are empty, so the gems must be in the BLUE box.\n\nFinal truth values: B1 = True, B2 = False, W1 = False, K1 = True.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; B2=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.767, "ast_prob": 0.212, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00335", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n  [B2] EXACTLY TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS BLACK\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 says “THE BLUE BOX IS BLACK,” but the left box is BLUE, so K1 is false. Thus the BLACK box is all-false.\n\nBecause the BLACK box is false, W1 (“THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE”) is also false. Thus the WHITE box is all-false.\n\nSince at least one box must be all-true, and both WHITE and BLACK are all-false, the BLUE box must be all-true. Therefore B1 and B2 are both true.\n\nB2 says exactly two statements are true, and with W1 and K1 false, the only true statements are B1 and B2 — exactly two.\n\nB1 says the gems are in a box that is true. The only all-true box is BLUE, so the gems are in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.752, "ast_prob": 0.288, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00336", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS WHITE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [K2] THE BLUE BOX IS BLACK\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The BLUE box is on the left, WHITE is in the middle, and BLACK is on the right.  \nSo W1 (“the BLUE box is WHITE”) is false, K1 (“the BLUE box is the MIDDLE box”) is false, and K2 (“the BLUE box is BLACK”) is false.  \n\nThus the WHITE box is all-false, and the BLACK box is all-false.  \nSince at least one box must be all-true, the BLUE box must be all-true.  \nTherefore B1 is true, meaning the BLUE box contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.38, "ast_prob": 0.496, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00337", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n  [B2] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 must be false, because BLACK contains only one statement, so it cannot have at least two true statements.\n\nSince B2 says “the top statement of each box is true,” it asserts B1, W1, and K1 are all true. But K1 is false, so B2 is false.\n\nB1 says the gems are in a box that is true. If the gems were in BLUE, BLUE has B2 false so it is not all-true; if in WHITE, W1 would be false; if in BLACK, K1 is false. Thus B1 is false in every case.\n\nNow W1 says the gems are in BLUE. If gems were not in BLUE, then W1 would be false, making WHITE all-false, BLUE all-false, and BLACK all-false, violating the requirement that at least one box is all-true. Therefore the gems must be in BLUE, making W1 true and WHITE all-true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; B2=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.695, "ast_prob": 0.286, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00338", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLACK BOX ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT MOST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n  [K2] THE BLACK BOX HAS AT MOST TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let each statement’s truth value be determined by the actual situation.\n\n- K1 says “The white box has at most one true statement.” The white box has only one statement, W1, so it can never have more than one true statement. Therefore K1 is always true.\n- K2 says “The black box has at most two false statements.” The black box has exactly two statements, so it can never have more than two false statements. Therefore K2 is always true.\n- Since K1 and K2 are both true, B1 (“All statements on the black box are true”) is true.\n- We need at least one box with all statements false. Blue is all true, and Black is all true, so White must be all false.\n- Therefore W1 must be false. W1 says “The blue box is empty,” so its being false means the blue box is not empty.\n- Thus the gems are in the BLUE box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.686, "ast_prob": 0.483, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00339", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n  [K2] EITHER THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY OR A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step by step:\n- K2 says: “Either the other two boxes relative to the white box are empty, or a box next to the black box contains the gems.”  \n  With exactly one gem, both alternatives are actually equivalent to “the WHITE box contains the gems.” Since K2 uses “Either…or…” as an exclusive OR, it compares the same condition to itself, so K2 must be FALSE.\n- Since K2 is false, the BLACK box cannot be all-true. Therefore W1 (“The black box is true”) is FALSE.\n- K1 says “The statement above this one is true,” referring to W1. Since W1 is false, K1 is FALSE.\n- Now the WHITE box is all-false, and the BLACK box is all-false. At least one box must be all-true, so the BLUE box must be all-true, making B1 TRUE.\n- Therefore the gems are in the BLUE box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.41, "ast_prob": 0.251, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00340", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT LEAST THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n  [K2] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 refers to “the statement above this one,” but the WHITE box contains only W1, so there is no statement above it. Therefore W1 is false, making the WHITE box all-false.\n\nSince at least one box must be all-true and WHITE is all-false, either BLUE or BLACK must be all-true.\n\nIf BLACK were all-true, then K1 and K2 would both be true. But with W1 false and B1 either true or false, fewer than three statements would be false, so K1 could not be true. Thus BLACK cannot be all-true.\n\nSo BLUE must be all-true, meaning B1 is true.\n\nNow with B1 true and W1 false:\n- If K1 were true, then there would be at most two false statements, making “at least three statements are false” false. So K1 cannot be true; K1 is false.\n- With K1 false, there are already two false statements: W1 and K1. If K2 were false, there would be three false statements, which would make K1 true — contradiction. So K2 must be true.\n\nK2 says the box with no false statements has the gems. The only box with no false statements is BLUE, so the gems are in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.772, "ast_prob": 0.336, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00341", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [W2] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "- B1 is true exactly when the Blue box has the gems, because then the other two boxes are empty.\n- W1 is true exactly when the Blue box has the gems.\n- W2 is true exactly when the White box has the gems, because the boxes next to White are Blue and Black.\n- K1 is false, because the White box is white, not blue.\n\nTest gem locations:\n\nIf gems are in BLUE:  \nB1=T, W1=T, W2=F, K1=F → Blue box all true, Black box all false, White box mixed. Satisfies all rules.\n\nIf gems are in WHITE:  \nB1=F, W1=F, W2=T, K1=F → no box is all true. Violates rule.\n\nIf gems are in BLACK:  \nB1=F, W1=F, W2=F, K1=F → no box is all true. Violates rule.\n\nSo the unique solution is gems in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.318, "ast_prob": 0.152, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00342", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLUE\n  [W2] THE BLACK BOX IS BLACK\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Black is the rightmost box, so the only box next to it is WHITE. Thus B1 is true exactly when WHITE contains the gems.\n\nW1 is false because the WHITE box is white, not blue.\nW2 is true because the BLACK box is black.\nK1 is true exactly when BLACK does not contain the gems.\n\nTesting gem locations:\n- gem=BLUE: B1=F, W1=F, W2=T, K1=T → BLUE all false, WHITE mixed, BLACK all true. Valid.\n- gem=WHITE: B1=T, W1=F, W2=T, K1=T → no box is all false. Invalid.\n- gem=BLACK: B1=F, W1=F, W2=T, K1=F → no box is all true. Invalid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; W2=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.54, "ast_prob": 0.144, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00343", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] AT LEAST THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n  [B2] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is false because the BLUE box is on the left, not the middle, so the BLACK box is all-false.\n\nFor an all-true box to exist, either BLUE or WHITE must be all-true.  \nB2 says a box next to BLUE is true; the only adjacent box is WHITE, which has only W1, so B2 is true exactly when W1 is true.  \nW1 says the BLUE box has the gems.\n\nIf the gems were not in BLUE, then W1 and B2 would both be false, leaving no all-true box. So the gems must be in BLUE.\n\nThus W1 is true, B2 is true, and K1 is false.  \nWith B2, W1, and K1 fixed, B1 claims at least three statements are false. If B1 were true, only K1 would be false, so B1 would be false. Therefore B1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.387, "ast_prob": 0.425, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00344", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX HAS EXACTLY ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [K2] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n- \\(K1\\) = “Black contains the gems”\n- \\(W1\\) = “A box next to White contains the gems”\n- \\(K2\\) = “Top statement of every box is true” = \\(B1 \\land W1 \\land K1\\)\n- \\(B1\\) = “Black has exactly one false statement” = \\(K1 \\neq K2\\)\n\nExactly one box has the gems.\n\n**Case 1: Gems in BLACK**  \nThen \\(K1=T\\), \\(W1=T\\), so \\(K2=B1\\).  \nBut \\(B1 = (K1 \\neq K2) = (T \\neq B1)\\), contradiction.\n\n**Case 2: Gems in WHITE**  \nThen \\(W1=F\\), \\(K1=F\\), so \\(K2=F\\), and \\(B1=(F \\neq F)=F\\).  \nAll boxes would be all-false, violating “at least one all-true.”\n\n**Case 3: Gems in BLUE**  \nThen \\(W1=T\\), \\(K1=F\\), so \\(K2=F\\), and \\(B1=(F \\neq F)=F\\).  \nBlue is all-false, White is all-true, Black is all-false. This satisfies all rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.717, "ast_prob": 0.207, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00345", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n  [K2] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the boxes’ statements be ordered top-to-bottom as listed.\n\n- K1 is the top statement of the BLACK box, so “the statement above this one” does not exist. Therefore K1 is false.\n- B1 says the top statement of each box is true: B1, W1, and K1. Since K1 is false, B1 must be false.\n- K2 says a box next to WHITE is true. The next boxes are BLUE and BLACK. BLUE is false because B1 is false, and BLACK is false because K1 is false. Therefore K2 is false.\n- We need at least one box with all statements true. BLUE is all-false, BLACK is all-false, so WHITE must be all-true. Thus W1 is true.\n- W1 says the BLUE box contains the gems, so the gems are in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.731, "ast_prob": 0.35, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00346", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EXACTLY TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n  [B2] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(G\\) be the gem location.\n\n- \\(W1\\) says the other two boxes relative to Black are empty: Blue and White empty → true iff \\(G=Black\\).\n- \\(K1\\) says the other two boxes are empty: also Blue and White empty → true iff \\(G=Black\\).\n- \\(B2\\) says it is not true that the other two boxes relative to White are empty: i.e. not(Blue and Black empty) → true iff \\(G \\neq White\\).\n\nNow test gem locations:\n\n- If \\(G=Black\\): \\(W1=T, K1=T, B2=T\\). Then no box is all-false, violating the rule.\n- If \\(G=White\\): \\(W1=F, K1=F, B2=F\\). Then no box is all-true, violating the rule.\n- If \\(G=Blue\\): \\(W1=F, K1=F, B2=T\\). For \\(B1=T\\), exactly two statements are false: \\(W1\\) and \\(K1\\). This is consistent, and Blue is all-true while White and Black are all-false.\n\nThus the unique solution is:\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.581, "ast_prob": 0.216, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00347", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE WHITE BOX HAS AT LEAST ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE OR THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS WHITE\n  [K2] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is false, because the black box cannot be white.\n\nB1 is equivalent to W1 being true: the white box has only one statement, so “the white box has at least one false statement” is true exactly when W1 is false. Therefore B1 = W1.\n\nBecause black cannot be all-true, at least one all-true box must be blue or white. Since B1 = W1, this forces W1 true and B1 true.\n\nNow blue and white are both all-true. To satisfy “at least one all-false box,” black must be all-false. Thus K2 must be false. K2 says “the blue box is empty,” so the blue box is not empty, meaning the gems are in the blue box.\n\nCheck: W1 is true because the gems are in a true box and the white box is empty. B1 is true because W1 is true. K1 and K2 are both false, so black is all-false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.261, "ast_prob": 0.211, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00348", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n  [W2] AT LEAST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let gem locations be considered for each box.\n\n- If gems are in **WHITE**, then B1 is true, W1 is false. Then K1 would be true (since White is not all-true), forcing W2 to be false, but then exactly two statements would be false, making W2 true — contradiction. So gems cannot be in WHITE.\n- If gems are in **BLACK**, then B1 and W1 are true. W2 would have to be false, and K1 would be true. This gives Blue all-true and Black all-true, but **no all-false box**, violating the rule. So gems cannot be in BLACK.\n- If gems are in **BLUE**, then B1 is false, W1 is true. For W2 to be true, exactly two statements must be false; B1 and K1 are false, so W2 is true. Then White is all-true, and both Blue and Black are all-false. This satisfies every rule.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.685, "ast_prob": 0.127, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00349", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n  [B2] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT MOST TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT MOST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is always true: the White box contains only one statement, so it trivially has at most two true statements. Thus Black is always an all-true box.\n\nLet total true statements be B1 + B2 + W1 + K1. Since K1 = true, W1 is true iff B1 + B2 + W1 + 1 ≤ 2.\n\n- If W1 = true, then B1 and B2 must both be false. B2 false means the Blue box has the gems. Then all-true boxes are White and Black, neither has the gems, so B1 is false. This is consistent.\n- If W1 = false, then more than two statements are true, forcing B1 = true and B2 = true. But then Blue is all-true and Black is all-true, so there is no unique “box with no false statements” for B1 to refer to. So B1 cannot be true. Contradiction.\n\nTherefore W1 must be true, B1 and B2 false, K1 true, and the gems are in the Blue box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; B2=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.711, "ast_prob": 0.176, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00350", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EITHER THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE OR THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n  [K2] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Blue’s only statement cannot be true because a one-statement box cannot have exactly two false statements. So B1 is false, making BLUE the all-false box.\n\nSince at least one box must be all-true, and BLUE is not all-true, either WHITE must be all-true or BLACK must be all-true. But BLACK being all-true would require K2 true, which says WHITE’s statement is true. So in all cases W1 must be true.\n\nWith W1 true, WHITE is all-true. K2 is therefore true.\n\nOn BLACK, K1 is the top statement, so “the statement above this one” does not exist; that part is false. Thus K1 would only be true if “the box with no false statements” has the gems. But if K1 were true, BLACK would also be all-true, giving two all-true boxes, so the unique “box with no false statements” would not exist. Therefore K1 is false.\n\nSo BLACK is mixed: K1 false, K2 true. The unique all-true box is WHITE. Since K1 is false, that all-true WHITE box does not have the gems. W1 true already rules out BLACK having the gems, so the gems must be in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.619, "ast_prob": 0.294, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00351", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE BLUE BOX IS BLUE\n  [B2] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because the Blue box is blue, so “it is not true that the Blue box is blue” is false.\n\nLet W1 = K1 = X. Both W1 and K1 say the same thing: the box with two false statements has the gems. Only the Blue box could have two false statements, since it is the only box with two statements.\n\nSo W1 and K1 are true exactly when the gems are in Blue and both B1 and B2 are false. Since B1 is already false, this depends on B2. Also, B2 says “not all statements on White are true,” which is equivalent to “W1 is false,” so B2 = not X.\n\nIf the gems were not in Blue, then W1 and K1 would be false, making B2 true. Then Blue would be mixed, and White and Black would both be all-false, leaving no all-true box. This violates the rules.\n\nTherefore the gems must be in Blue. With the gems in Blue, if W1 and K1 were false, there would still be no all-true box. So W1 and K1 must be true. Then B2 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; B2=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.553, "ast_prob": 0.435, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00353", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [W2] THE BLACK BOX IS BLUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let W2 be false: the black box is not blue.\n\n- W1 is true only if the gems are in WHITE, since WHITE is the only box next to BLUE.\n- K1 is true only if the gems are NOT in WHITE, since BLUE and BLACK are next to WHITE.\n\nIf gems were in WHITE, then WHITE would be mixed and BLACK would be all-false, forcing BLUE to be all-true. But BLUE would not contain the gems, so B1 would be false. Contradiction.\n\nIf gems were in BLACK, then WHITE would be all-false and BLACK all-true. If B1 were false, BLACK would be the unique all-true box with the gems, making B1 true. If B1 were true, BLUE would also be all-true, so there would not be a unique “box with no false statements.” Contradiction.\n\nSo the gems must be in BLUE. Then W1 is false, W2 is false, K1 is true, and B1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.52, "ast_prob": 0.5, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00354", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS OR THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n  [B2] THE WHITE BOX IS BLUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(G\\) be the box containing the gems.\n\n- B1: For BLUE, “boxes next to this box” means WHITE only. B1 is true if WHITE has gems or if BLUE and WHITE are empty. So B1 = true when \\(G \\ne \\text{BLUE}\\), false when \\(G = \\text{BLUE}\\).\n- B2: “The WHITE box is BLUE” is false.\n- W1: The other two boxes relative to WHITE are BLUE and BLACK. W1 is true only if \\(G = \\text{WHITE}\\).\n- K1: “This box is empty” is true only if \\(G \\ne \\text{BLACK}\\).\n\nCheck cases:\n- \\(G = \\text{BLUE}\\): B1=F, B2=F → BLUE all false; W1=F → WHITE all false; K1=T → BLACK all true. Satisfies all rules.\n- \\(G = \\text{WHITE}\\): No box is all false, so invalid.\n- \\(G = \\text{BLACK}\\): No box is all true, so invalid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; B2=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.241, "ast_prob": 0.441, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00355", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n  [W2] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT LEAST ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is false because Black is the right box, not the middle box.\n\nW2 says “The White box has at least one false statement.”  \n- If W1 were true, then W2 would be true only if there were a false statement, but both true would make W2 false; if W2 were false, then there would be a false statement, making W2 true. Contradiction.  \n- Therefore W1 must be false, and W2 must be true. So White is a mixed box.\n\nBlack has K1 false, so Black is an all-false box.  \nAt least one box must be all-true, and White cannot be all-true, so Blue must be all-true. Thus B1 is true.\n\nB1 says “The gems are in a box that is true.” Since Blue is the only all-true box, the gems must be in Blue.\n\nCheck W1: “The box with two false statements has the gems.” No box has two false statements, so W1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.765, "ast_prob": 0.144, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00356", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n  [W2] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let a “true box” mean a box whose statements are all true.\n\n- W1 says “the statement above this one is true,” so W1 is true exactly when W2 is true: W1 = W2.\n- W2 says “the blue box is empty,” so W2 is true exactly when the gems are not in BLUE.\n- Black’s only neighboring box is WHITE, so K1 is true exactly when WHITE is all-true.\n\nIf the gems were not in BLUE:\n- W2 would be true, so W1 would be true, making WHITE all-true.\n- Then K1 would be true, making BLACK all-true.\n- The gems would be in a true box, so B1 would also be true, making BLUE all-true.\n- This makes all three boxes true, violating the rule that at least one box is all-false.\n\nSo the gems must be in BLUE:\n- W2 is false.\n- W1 is false.\n- Therefore WHITE is all-false.\n- K1 is false, so BLACK is all-false.\n- To satisfy “at least one all-true box,” BLUE must be all-true, so B1 is true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.754, "ast_prob": 0.133, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00357", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [W2] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n\n1. B1 says the other two boxes are empty, so B1 is true exactly when the gems are in BLUE.\n2. W1 says WHITE contains the gems, so W1 is true exactly when gems are in WHITE.\n3. W2 says WHITE is empty, so W2 is true exactly when gems are NOT in WHITE.\n4. K1 says BLACK is the middle box, but BLACK is the right box, so K1 is always false.\n5. W1 and W2 contradict each other: they cannot both be true, and cannot both be false. So WHITE is always a mixed box.\n6. Therefore the all-true box must be either BLUE or BLACK. But K1 is false, so BLACK cannot be all-true. Thus BLUE must be all-true, meaning B1 is true and the gems are in BLUE.\n7. With gems in BLUE: W1 is false, W2 is true, K1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.307, "ast_prob": 0.292, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00359", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n  [W2] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location determine each statement:\n\n- \\(B1\\) is true iff BLACK has the gems.\n- \\(W1\\) is true iff BLUE is empty.\n- \\(W2\\) says “ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE,” so \\(W2 = B1\\).\n- \\(K1\\) is true iff WHITE is empty.\n\nCheck the three possible gem locations:\n\n1. **Gems in BLUE:**  \n   \\(B1=F\\), \\(W1=F\\), \\(W2=F\\), \\(K1=T\\)  \n   BLUE all false, WHITE all false, BLACK all true → valid.\n\n2. **Gems in WHITE:**  \n   \\(B1=F\\), \\(W1=T\\), \\(W2=F\\), \\(K1=F\\)  \n   BLUE all false, WHITE mixed, BLACK all false → no all-true box → invalid.\n\n3. **Gems in BLACK:**  \n   \\(B1=T\\), \\(W1=T\\), \\(W2=T\\), \\(K1=T\\)  \n   All boxes all-true → no all-false box → invalid.\n\nSo the only valid solution is gems in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.252, "ast_prob": 0.323, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00360", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [B2] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX IS BLUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the three box positions be: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right).\n\n- B1: “A box next to the black box contains the gems” → only WHITE is next to BLACK, so B1 is true iff gems are in WHITE.\n- B2: “This box contains the gems” → B2 is true iff gems are in BLUE.\n- W1: “This box is blue” → WHITE box is not blue, so W1 is false.\n- K1: “The other two boxes relative to the blue box are empty” → WHITE and BLACK would both be empty, which means gems are in BLUE. So K1 is true iff gems are in BLUE.\n\nTest gem locations:\n\n1. Gems in BLUE:  \n   B1 = F, B2 = T, W1 = F, K1 = T.  \n   BLUE = mixed, WHITE = all false, BLACK = all true.  \n   Satisfies at least one all-true and at least one all-false.\n\n2. Gems in WHITE:  \n   B1 = T, B2 = F, W1 = F, K1 = F.  \n   No box is all-true, so invalid.\n\n3. Gems in BLACK:  \n   B1 = F, B2 = F, W1 = F, K1 = F.  \n   No box is all-true, so invalid.\n\nThus the unique solution is gems in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.267, "ast_prob": 0.156, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00361", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n  [B2] AT LEAST TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EXACTLY ONE STATEMENT IS FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let K1 depend on W1: since WHITE has only W1, “the WHITE box is true” means W1 is true, so K1 has the same truth value as W1.\n\n- If W1 were true, then K1 would also be true, so WHITE and BLACK would both be all-true. To satisfy the rule that at least one box is all-false, BLUE would have to be all-false, making B1=false and B2=false. But then the true statements would be W1 and K1 only: exactly 2 true statements, so B2 would actually be true — contradiction.\n- Therefore W1=false. Then K1=false too, so WHITE and BLACK are both all-false. To satisfy the rule that at least one box is all-true, BLUE must be all-true, so B1=true and B2=true.\n- With B1 true, B2 true, W1 false, K1 false, there are exactly 2 true statements and 2 false statements. This makes B2 true and W1 false, consistent.\n- The only all-true box is BLUE, so for B1 (“the box with no false statements has the gems”) to be true, the gems must be in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.789, "ast_prob": 0.395, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00362", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [W2] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem locations be considered:\n\n- If the gems are in BLUE, then:  \n  - B1: WHITE is empty → True  \n  - W1: It is not true that boxes next to BLACK contain gems → True, since only WHITE is next to BLACK and WHITE is empty  \n  - W2: Boxes next to BLUE contain gems → False, since WHITE is empty  \n  - K1: The other two boxes are empty → False, because BLUE has the gems  \n\n  Box conditions: BLUE is all-true, BLACK is all-false, WHITE is mixed.  \n  This satisfies “at least one all-true” and “at least one all-false.”\n\n- If the gems are in WHITE, no box is all-true, so invalid.\n\n- If the gems are in BLACK, no box is all-false, so invalid.\n\nTherefore the only valid solution is:\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.305, "ast_prob": 0.293, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00364", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS TRUE\n  [B2] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE OR THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- B1 = “The BLUE box is true” → B1 ↔ (B1 ∧ B2)\n- K1 = “A box next to BLUE is true” → only WHITE is next to BLUE, so K1 ↔ W1\n- W1: “The statement above this one is true” is false, because W1 is the only statement in WHITE. So W1 ↔ “the top statement of every box is true” ↔ (B1 ∧ W1 ∧ K1). Since K1 = W1, this becomes W1 ↔ (B1 ∧ W1).\n\nIf W1 were true, then B1 would be true, so B2 would be true, and K1 would be true. Then all boxes would be all-true, giving no all-false box — impossible.\n\nSo W1 is false, hence K1 is false.  \nFor there to be an all-true box, BLUE must be all-true, so B1 = true and B2 = true.  \nThen BLUE is the unique all-true box, so B2’s statement means BLUE has the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.621, "ast_prob": 0.218, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00365", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [W2] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The key is that W1 is always false: with only one box containing gems, both boxes next to White cannot contain gems.\n\n- If gems are in White: B1=false, W2=false, K1=false → no all-true box, invalid.\n- If gems are in Black: B1=true, W2=true, K1=true; White is mixed → no all-false box, invalid.\n- If gems are in Blue: B1=true, W2=false, K1=true; White is all false; Blue and Black are all true → valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.387, "ast_prob": 0.354, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00366", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS BLACK\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n  [K2] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "BLUE has only B1. WHITE has only W1. BLACK has K1 and K2.\n\n- W1: “The black box is black” is true, since the right box is the BLACK box.\n- K2: “The white box is the middle box” is true, since the WHITE box is the middle box.\n- K1: “The white box has exactly two true statements” is false, because the white box has only one statement, W1, which is true. So the white box has exactly 1 true statement, not 2.\n\nThus WHITE is all-true: W1 = T.\n\nBLACK is mixed: K1 = F, K2 = T.\n\nSince at least one box must be all-false, and the only remaining possible all-false box is BLUE, the gems must be in BLUE. Therefore B1 = F.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.445, "ast_prob": 0.203, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00367", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLACK BOX ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT LEAST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n  [K2] THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(B1\\) be the Blue statement, \\(W1\\) the White statement, and \\(K1, K2\\) the Black statements.\n\n- \\(K2\\) says “The black box is true,” so \\(K2\\) is true only if both \\(K1\\) and \\(K2\\) are true. Thus \\(K2\\) can be false regardless, but if \\(K2\\) is true then \\(K1\\) must be true.\n- If \\(K1\\) and \\(K2\\) were both false, then \\(W1\\) would also be false, giving at least three false statements, so \\(K1\\) would be true — contradiction.\n- If \\(K1\\) and \\(K2\\) were both true, then \\(W1\\) would be true, and fewer than two statements would be false — contradiction with \\(K1\\) being true.\n- Therefore \\(K1 = T\\), \\(K2 = F\\), and so \\(W1 = F\\) because not all Black statements are true.\n- Now White is false and Black is mixed, so the only possible all-true box is Blue. Thus \\(B1 = T\\), and the gems must be in the Blue box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.676, "ast_prob": 0.348, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00368", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [W2] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX HAS EXACTLY ONE TRUE STATEMENT AND THIS BOX IS WHITE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W2 is true: the White box is indeed the middle box.  \nW1 is false: exactly one box contains gems, so both boxes next to the White box cannot both contain gems.  \nThus the White box is mixed, not all-true or all-false.  \n\nK1 says “The Blue box has exactly one true statement and this box is white.” Since K1 is on the Black box, “this box is white” is false, making K1 false.  \nSo the Black box is all-false.  \n\nAt least one box must be all-true. The White box is mixed and the Black box is all-false, so the Blue box must be all-true.  \nTherefore B1 is true, meaning the gems are in the Blue box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.665, "ast_prob": 0.219, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00369", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [W2] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT LEAST THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(W2\\) be evaluated first.  \n\\(W2\\) says “the box with two false statements has the gems.” The only box that could have two false statements is WHITE, since it has two statements. If \\(W2\\) were true, WHITE would need both \\(W1\\) and \\(W2\\) to be false, including \\(W2\\) itself — a contradiction. So \\(W2 = F\\).\n\n\\(W1\\) is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE, because WHITE is the only box next to BLACK.\n\nNow consider \\(K1\\): “At least three statements are false.” Since \\(W2\\) is always false, the number of false statements depends on \\(B1\\), \\(W1\\), and \\(K1\\).  \nThis forces either:\n- \\(K1 = T\\) with \\(B1 = F\\) and \\(W1 = F\\), or\n- \\(K1 = F\\) with \\(B1 = T\\) and \\(W1 = T\\).\n\nCase 1: \\(K1 = T,\\ B1 = F,\\ W1 = F\\).  \nThen gems are not in WHITE. The only all-true box is BLACK. Since \\(B1\\) is false, BLACK does not contain the gems, so gems must be in BLUE. Consistent.\n\nCase 2: \\(K1 = F,\\ B1 = T,\\ W1 = T\\).  \nThen gems are in WHITE. But \\(B1\\) says the all-true box contains the gems; the all-true box would be BLUE, not WHITE. Contradiction.\n\nTherefore the only solution is gems in BLUE, with \\(B1\\) false, \\(W1\\) false, \\(W2\\) false, and \\(K1\\) true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.343, "ast_prob": 0.243, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00370", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX HAS AT LEAST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX HAS EXACTLY ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n  [K2] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(W1\\) be the truth value of WHITE’s statement.  \nSince WHITE has only one statement, “WHITE is true” is equivalent to \\(W1 = T\\).\n\nBLACK’s second statement says:\n- \\(K2 =\\) “A box next to BLUE is true” = “WHITE is true” = \\(W1\\).\n\nSo \\(K2 = W1\\).\n\nBLACK’s first statement says:\n- \\(K1 =\\) “BLACK has exactly one true statement” = exactly one of \\(K1, K2\\) is true.\n\nIf \\(K2 = T\\), then \\(K1\\) would have to be the opposite of itself:\n- If \\(K1 = T\\), then BLACK has two true statements, so \\(K1\\) would be false.\n- If \\(K1 = F\\), then BLACK has exactly one true statement, so \\(K1\\) would be true.\n\nThis is impossible, so \\(K2 \\neq T\\). Therefore \\(K2 = F\\), which means \\(W1 = F\\).\n\nSince \\(W1 = F\\), BLACK does **not** have at least one true statement, so \\(K1 = F\\) and \\(K2 = F\\).  \nThus BLACK is all-false, and WHITE is all-false.\n\nAt least one box must be all-true. The only remaining candidate is BLUE, so BLUE must be all-true, meaning \\(B1 = T\\).\n\nSince \\(B1\\) is true, the gems must be in a box that is true. The only true box is BLUE, so the gems are in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.652, "ast_prob": 0.115, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00371", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [B2] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The Blue box is left, so B1 and W1 (“The Blue box is the middle box”) are both false.  \nIf the gems were in WHITE, all boxes would be all-false, leaving no all-true box.  \nIf the gems were in BLACK, Blue would be mixed and no box would be all-true.  \nOnly if the gems are in BLUE: Blue is all-false, White is all-false, and Black’s K1 is true, giving an all-true box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; B2=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.444, "ast_prob": 0.227, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00372", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [B2] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY OR THE BLUE BOX IS BLACK\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the boxes be in order: BLUE left, WHITE middle, BLACK right.\n\n- B2 says “WHITE is the middle box” → always TRUE.\n- B1 and K1 both say “the box(es) next to BLACK contain gems.” Since BLACK is on the right, only WHITE is next to it. So B1 = K1 = TRUE iff the gems are in WHITE.\n- W1 says “BLACK is empty OR BLUE is black.” “BLUE is black” is FALSE, so W1 = TRUE iff BLACK is empty, i.e. iff gems are NOT in BLACK.\n\nNow test gem locations:\n\n1. Gems in BLUE:\n   - B1 = FALSE, B2 = TRUE → mixed\n   - W1 = TRUE → all true\n   - K1 = FALSE → all false\n   Valid: at least one all-true and at least one all-false.\n\n2. Gems in WHITE:\n   - B1 = TRUE, B2 = TRUE → all true\n   - W1 = TRUE → all true\n   - K1 = TRUE → all true\n   Invalid: no all-false box.\n\n3. Gems in BLACK:\n   - B1 = FALSE, B2 = TRUE → mixed\n   - W1 = FALSE → all false\n   - K1 = FALSE → all false\n   Invalid: no all-true box.\n\nThus the gems must be in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.246, "ast_prob": 0.437, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00373", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n  [B2] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT MOST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "White box has only one statement, W1, so it cannot have exactly two true statements. Thus B1 is false. White box has at most one true statement, so B2 is true. W1 is true because the white box is indeed the middle box. Therefore the White box is all-true. At least one box must be all-false, and the only possible all-false box is Black, so K1 must be false. Therefore the Blue box is not empty, meaning the gems are in the Blue box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.716, "ast_prob": 0.357, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00374", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n  [W2] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EXACTLY TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let gem locations determine emptiness:\n- If gems are in BLUE, then WHITE and BLACK are empty → B1 is true; W1/W2 are true.\n- Let K1 self-referentially say exactly 2 statements total are true.\n\nCheck possible gem locations:\n\n1. **Gem in BLUE**:  \n   - B1 = True (other two boxes empty)  \n   - W1 = True, W2 = True (White empty)  \n   - With B1, W1, W2 already true, K1 cannot be true because that would make 4 true statements, not 2.  \n   - So K1 = False.  \n   - Consistent: Blue all-true, White all-true, Black all-false.\n\n2. **Gem in WHITE**:  \n   - B1 = False, W1 = False, W2 = False.  \n   - K1 would be False, but then no box is all-true, violating the rule. Invalid.\n\n3. **Gem in BLACK**:  \n   - B1 = False (Black is not empty), W1 = True, W2 = True.  \n   - If K1 = True, total true = 3, contradiction.  \n   - If K1 = False, total true = 2, making K1 true, contradiction. Invalid.\n\nTherefore the only consistent solution is gem in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.302, "ast_prob": 0.154, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00375", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS BLACK\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n  [K2] THE BLACK BOX HAS AT MOST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let K1 and K2 be the two statements on the BLACK box.\n\n1. K2 says: “The BLACK box has at most one true statement.”  \n   Checking the possible truth pairs:\n   - K1=T, K2=T → two true statements, so K2 would be false — contradiction.\n   - K1=T, K2=F → one true statement, so K2 would be true — contradiction.\n   - K1=F, K2=F → zero true statements, so K2 would be true — contradiction.\n   - K1=F, K2=T → one true statement, so K2 is true — consistent.  \n   Therefore K1=F and K2=T.\n\n2. Since BLACK has only one false statement, and BLUE/WHITE have only one statement each, no box has two false statements.  \n   So W1, “The box with two false statements has the gems,” is FALSE.  \n   Thus WHITE’s only statement is false, so WHITE is all-false.\n\n3. K1 is FALSE, so the gems are NOT in a false box; therefore they must be in an all-true box.\n\n4. BLACK is mixed, WHITE is all-false, so BLUE must be the all-true box containing the gems.  \n   Hence B1 is TRUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.749, "ast_prob": 0.19, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00377", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE WHITE BOX IS BLUE\n  [W2] THE BLUE BOX IS BLUE AND BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EITHER THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY OR BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(P\\) = “boxes next to the black box contain gems.”  \nSince the black box is on the right, its only adjacent box is the WHITE box, so \\(P\\) is true iff the gem is in WHITE.\n\n- \\(B1 = P\\)\n- \\(W1\\) is true: the white box is not blue.\n- \\(W2 =\\) “the blue box is blue and \\(P\\)” = \\(P\\)\n- \\(K1 =\\) “black box is empty or \\(P\\)” = true unless the gem is in BLACK.\n\nCheck gem locations:\n\n- If gem = BLUE: \\(B1=F\\), \\(W1=T\\), \\(W2=F\\), \\(K1=T\\).  \n  BLUE is all-false, BLACK is all-true, WHITE is mixed → valid.\n- If gem = WHITE: all statements are true → no all-false box → invalid.\n- If gem = BLACK: no all-true box → invalid.\n\nTherefore the unique solution is gem in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.26, "ast_prob": 0.482, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00378", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n  [W2] EXACTLY TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is always false, because the black box has only one statement, so it cannot have exactly two false statements. This makes BLACK an all-false box.  \nTesting the possible gem locations, only the BLUE box gives a consistent assignment: B1 is false (blue contains gems), W1 is true (blue is a false/non-true box), W2 is true (B1 and K1 are the only false statements), and K1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.797, "ast_prob": 0.38, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00379", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE AND BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [K2] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY AND EXACTLY THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(G\\) be the box containing the gems.\n\n- W1 means “the gems are in Blue or Black,” since those are White’s neighbors. So W1 is true iff \\(G \\ne \\text{WHITE}\\).\n- K1 requires both “all statements on White are true” and “the box next to Black contains the gems.” The first requires \\(G \\ne \\text{WHITE}\\), but the second requires \\(G = \\text{WHITE}\\). Contradiction, so **K1 is always false**.\n\nCheck possible gem locations:\n\n1. **If gems are in WHITE:**  \n   W1 = false, White is not true so B1 = false, K1 = false, and K2 = false because White is not empty.  \n   Then no box is all-true, violating the rules. Invalid.\n\n2. **If gems are in BLACK:**  \n   W1 = true, but Black cannot be an all-true box because K1 is false, so B1 = false.  \n   K2 would be true iff exactly three statements are false, but that leads to a self-contradiction: if K2 is true there are only two true statements, so K2 should be false; if K2 is false there is exactly one true statement, so K2 should be true. Invalid.\n\n3. **If gems are in BLUE:**  \n   W1 = true, K1 = false.  \n   If B1 were false, then K2 would again create a contradiction. Therefore B1 must be true.  \n   With B1 and W1 both true, there are only two true statements, so K2 is false.\n\nThis gives Blue all-true, White all-true, and Black all-false, satisfying the rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.403, "ast_prob": 0.304, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00380", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EXACTLY THREE STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n  [W2] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step check of the consistent assignment:\n\n- If the gems are in BLUE, then the WHITE box does not contain gems, so W2 is false.\n- Since K1 refers to the statement directly above it, W2, K1 is also false.\n- B1 says “the box with no false statements has the gems.” With B1 true, the BLUE box has no false statements and contains the gems, so B1 is true.\n- W1 says “exactly three statements are true.” The true statements are only B1, so W1 is false.\n- Box types: BLUE all-true, WHITE all-false, BLACK all-false. This satisfies at least one all-true box, at least one all-false box, and the remaining box may be all-false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.588, "ast_prob": 0.419, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00381", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS BLACK\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n  [W2] THE BLUE BOX IS BLACK\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is on the blue box and says “this box is black,” which is false, so the blue box is all-false.  \nW2 also says the blue box is black, so W2 is false. Therefore the white box cannot be all-true.  \nW1 says the gems are in a box that is true. In every possible gem location, the gem’s box is not all-true: blue is all-false, white has W2 false, and black would be all-false unless K1 is true, which requires the gems to be in blue, contradicting black containing them. So W1 is false, making the white box all-false.  \nSince at least one box must be all-true, the black box must be all-true. Thus K1 must be true, meaning the gems are in the blue box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.424, "ast_prob": 0.391, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00382", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] AT LEAST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n  [W2] THE WHITE BOX IS NOT EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Since the BLUE box is on the left, the only box next to it is the WHITE box.  \nSo K1 means: “The WHITE box does not contain the gems.”\n\n- If gems were in BLUE: W1 = False, W2 = False, K1 = True. That gives two false statements (W1, W2), so B1 = True. This satisfies all rules.\n- If gems were in WHITE: W1 = True, W2 = True, K1 = False. Only one statement is false, so B1 cannot be consistently assigned.\n- If gems were in BLACK: W1 = True, W2 = False, K1 = True. Only one statement is false, so B1 cannot be consistently assigned.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.579, "ast_prob": 0.424, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00383", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EXACTLY TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n  [K2] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT MOST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 means “the boxes other than WHITE are empty,” which is true only if the gems are in WHITE.  \nW1 is true only if the gems are in BLACK.  \nK2 says WHITE has at most one true statement; since WHITE contains only W1, K2 is always true.\n\nIf K1 were true, exactly two statements would be false. Since K2 is true, B1 and W1 would both have to be false. That means gems are not in WHITE and not in BLACK, so gems must be in BLUE. This works: B1=F, W1=F, K1=T, K2=T gives exactly two false statements.\n\nIf K1 were false, then B1 and W1 would need exactly one false and one true, but both resulting gem placements make the total false count 2, which would make K1 true — a contradiction.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.404, "ast_prob": 0.433, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00384", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE NOT IN THE BLUE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n  [K2] THE BLACK BOX HAS A FALSE STATEMENT\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 refers to a statement above it, but there is no statement above B1, so “the statement above this one is true” is false. Therefore B1 is true, and the BLUE box is all-true.\n\nFor the BLACK box, K2 says the BLACK box has a false statement. If K1 were true, then K2 would be true only if K2 itself were false, a contradiction. So K1 must be false, making K2 true. Thus BLACK is mixed.\n\nSince at least one box must be all-false, and BLUE is all-true while BLACK is mixed, WHITE must be all-false. Therefore W1 is false, meaning the gems are in the BLUE box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.657, "ast_prob": 0.354, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00385", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY OR THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT MOST THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n  [K2] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step 1: K2 is false, because the WHITE box has only one statement, so it cannot have “at least two false statements.”\n\nStep 2: K1 says “It is not true that the top statement of each box is true,” i.e. K1 = NOT(B1 AND W1 AND K1). If B1 and W1 were both true, K1 would be forced to be both true and false, which is impossible. Therefore at least one of B1 or W1 is false, and K1 must be true.\n\nStep 3: Since K1 is true, at least one statement is true, so not all four statements are false. Thus W1 is true: “at most three statements are false.”\n\nStep 4: Since K1 requires not(B1 AND W1), and W1 is true, B1 must be false.\n\nStep 5: B1 says “Either the BLUE box is empty or the BLUE box is true.” Since B1 is false, both parts are false: the BLUE box is not empty, and the BLUE box is not true. Therefore the gems are in the BLUE box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.748, "ast_prob": 0.378, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00386", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS WHITE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n  [W2] THE WHITE BOX IS WHITE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 and W2 (“The white box is white”) are always true.  \nSo BLUE is all-true, and WHITE cannot be all-false because W2 is true.  \nTherefore the required all-false box must be BLACK, so K1 must be False.  \n\nK1 says the gems are in a false box. Since K1 is False, the gems are NOT in a false box.  \nWHITE has W1 false and W2 true, so WHITE is a false/mixed box; BLACK is all-false.  \nBLUE is all-true, so the gems must be in BLUE.  \n\nThen W1 is False, W2 is True, B1 is True, K1 is False.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.312, "ast_prob": 0.441, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00387", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT ALL STATEMENTS ON THIS BOX ARE TRUE\n  [B2] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLACK BOX ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE OR THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS NOT EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let B1 = b, B2 = c.\n\n- B1 says: “It is not true that all statements on this Blue box are true.”  \n  So B1 = NOT(B1 AND B2). Only consistent assignment is B1 = T, B2 = F.\n\n- Since B2 = F, it is false that “not all Black statements are true.” Therefore all Black statements are true.  \n  Black has only K1, so K1 = T.\n\n- K1 says “The Blue box is not empty,” so Blue contains the gems.\n\n- With K1 = T, Black is all-true. Blue is mixed because B1 = T and B2 = F.\n\n- At least one box must be all-false, so White must be all-false. Thus W1 = F.\n\n- Check W1: “Either a box next to Blue is true or Black contains gems.” Black does not contain gems, and White is not all-true, so W1 = F is consistent.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; B2=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.714, "ast_prob": 0.391, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00388", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [W2] THIS BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS WHITE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is false because the Blue box is not White, so Black is all-false.\n\nWhite cannot be all-true: if W1 and W2 were both true, the gems would be in White, making B1 true as well, which would make both Blue and White all-true — contradicting the singular “the box with no false statements” in B1.\n\nTherefore Blue must be the all-true box, so B1 is true. Since B1 says the all-true box has the gems, the gems must be in Blue.\n\nThen White has no gems, so W1 is false; White has zero true statements, so W2 is false; K1 remains false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.656, "ast_prob": 0.374, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00389", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n  [K2] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- B1 = “the other two boxes are empty” = true iff the gems are in BLUE.\n- W1 and K1 both say “the top statement of each box is true,” i.e. they both assert `B1 ∧ W1 ∧ K1`.\n- K2 says “a box next to the black box is true.” The only box next to BLACK is WHITE, so K2 is true iff W1 is true.\n\nIf W1 were true, then B1 and K1 would also have to be true, making every box all-true — but then no box would be all-false, violating the rules. So W1 must be false.\n\nSince W1 and K1 assert the same thing, K1 is also false. And K2 = W1, so K2 is false.\n\nWith W1, K1, K2 all false, B1 must be true to provide at least one all-true box. Thus the gems are in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.496, "ast_prob": 0.291, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00390", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLACK\n  [W2] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n\n1. B1 says the other two boxes relative to Blue are empty. Since exactly one box has gems, this is true exactly when the gems are in BLUE. So B1 = (gem=BLUE).\n2. K1 says “The Blue box is true.” Blue has only B1, so K1 is true exactly when B1 is true. Thus K1 = B1 = (gem=BLUE).\n3. W1 says “The White box is Black.” By the setup, the middle box is White, not Black, so W1 is false.\n4. Since W1 is false, White cannot be an all-true box. Therefore an all-true box must be Blue or Black, requiring B1 and K1 to be true, so the gems must be in BLUE.\n5. With gems in BLUE, B1=T and K1=T, so Blue and Black are all-true. To satisfy “at least one all-false box,” White must be all-false, so W2=F.\n6. This is consistent: White has W1=F and W2=F, so White is all-false; Blue and Black are all-true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.309, "ast_prob": 0.216, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00391", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n  [B2] THE BLACK BOX IS BLACK\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "- B1 says the White box has at least two false statements, but the White box has only one statement, so B1 is always False.\n- B2 says the Black box is black, which is True by the box’s given color/name.\n- Therefore, the Blue box is a mix, not all-true or all-false.\n- So the required all-true box and all-false box must be the White and Black boxes.\n\nCheck gem locations:\n\n- If gems are in BLUE: W1 is True, K1 is False. White box all-true, Black box all-false, Blue box mixed. This satisfies all rules.\n- If gems are in WHITE: W1 is False, K1 is False. White and Black are all-false, but no box is all-true. Invalid.\n- If gems are in BLACK: W1 is True, K1 is True. White and Black are all-true, but no box is all-false. Invalid.\n\nSo gems are in BLUE. Truth values: B1=False, B2=True, W1=True, K1=False.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.424, "ast_prob": 0.143, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00395", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n  [B2] EXACTLY TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX HAS EXACTLY ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n\n1. K1 says “The other two boxes are empty.” Since exactly one box has gems, K1 is true iff the gems are in BLACK.\n2. Black has only K1, so W1 (“The black box has exactly one true statement”) is true iff K1 is true. Thus W1 has the same truth value as K1.\n3. If the gems were in BLACK, then K1 and W1 would both be true. Then White and Black would be all-true boxes. To satisfy “at least one box all false,” Blue would have to be all-false, meaning B1=false and B2=false. But then the total number of true statements would be K1+W1=2, making B2 true — contradiction. So gems are not in BLACK.\n4. Therefore K1=false and W1=false. So White and Black are both all-false boxes.\n5. To satisfy “at least one box all true,” Blue must be all-true, so B1=true and B2=true.\n6. With B1 and B2 true and W1/K1 false, exactly two statements are true, so B2 is consistent.\n7. The only box with no false statements is BLUE, and B1 says that box has the gems. Since B1 is true, the gems are in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.477, "ast_prob": 0.158, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00396", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n  [B2] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "We test each possible gem location.\n\n- If gems are in WHITE: BLUE would not have both other boxes empty, so B1=false and B2=false; W1=false; K1=false. No box is all-true. Invalid.\n- If gems are in BLACK: B1=false, B2=false; W1=false; K1=false. No box is all-true. Invalid.\n- If gems are in BLUE:  \n  - WHITE and BLACK are empty, so B1=true and B2=true. BLUE is all-true.  \n  - W1 is false because BLACK is the right box, not the middle. WHITE is all-false.  \n  - K1 is true because BLUE contains the gems. BLACK is all-true.  \n  This satisfies at least one all-true box and at least one all-false box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.258, "ast_prob": 0.291, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00397", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] AT MOST ONE STATEMENT IS FALSE\n  [B2] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The literal reading of W1 is: both boxes next to White would need to contain gems. Since exactly one box contains gems, W1 is always false. Blue is not the middle box, so B2 is false. If the gems were not in Blue, then K1 would also be false, making every box all-false and leaving no all-true box. Therefore the gems must be in Blue, so K1 is true. With B2 false and W1 false, B1 cannot be true, so B1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; B2=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.409, "ast_prob": 0.451, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00398", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n  [B2] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n- \\(B1 = (B1 \\land W1 \\land K1)\\)\n- \\(K1 = (B1 \\land W1 \\land K1)\\), so \\(B1 = K1\\).\n- \\(W1 =\\) “BLUE contains gems”.\n- \\(B2 =\\) “WHITE and BLACK are empty” = same as “BLUE contains gems”, so \\(B2 = W1\\).\n\nIf gems are in WHITE or BLACK:\n- \\(W1 = F\\), \\(B2 = F\\)\n- Then \\(B1 = F\\), \\(K1 = F\\)\n- BLUE all false, WHITE all false, BLACK all false → no all-true box → invalid.\n\nIf gems are in BLUE:\n- \\(W1 = T\\), \\(B2 = T\\)\n- \\(B1\\) and \\(K1\\) must be either both true or both false.\n- If both true, every box is all-true → no all-false box → invalid.\n- If both false, BLUE is mixed, WHITE is all-true, BLACK is all-false → valid.\n\nTherefore gems are in BLUE, with:\n- \\(B1 = F\\)\n- \\(B2 = T\\)\n- \\(W1 = T\\)\n- \\(K1 = F\\)\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.751, "ast_prob": 0.369, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00400", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n  [B2] EITHER THE BLACK BOX IS BLACK OR THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location be one of BLUE, WHITE, BLACK.\n\n- B1 is true iff the BLACK box is empty, i.e. iff the gems are not in the BLACK box.\n- B2 is true because the BLACK box is indeed black, regardless of the blue box’s position.\n- W1 and K1 are both true iff the gems are in the WHITE box.\n\nTest each possible gem location:\n\n1. If gems are in BLUE:\n   - B1 = True, B2 = True → BLUE box all true.\n   - W1 = False → WHITE box all false.\n   - K1 = False → BLACK box all false.\n   This satisfies both “at least one all-true” and “at least one all-false.”\n\n2. If gems are in WHITE:\n   - B1 = True, B2 = True; W1 = True; K1 = True.\n   All boxes are all-true, so no box is all-false. Invalid.\n\n3. If gems are in BLACK:\n   - B1 = False, B2 = True; W1 = False; K1 = False.\n   BLUE is mixed, WHITE is all-false, BLACK is all-false, but no box is all-true. Invalid.\n\nTherefore the only valid solution is gems in BLUE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLUE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLUE", "relational_ratio": 0.288, "ast_prob": 0.168, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00401", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS WHITE\n  [B2] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false, because the blue box is not white.  \nSince W1 says “the blue box is true,” and B1 is false, W1 must be false.  \nB2 says all statements on the white box are true; since W1 is false, B2 is false.  \nSo the blue and white boxes are both all-false.  \nTo satisfy “at least one box has all true statements,” the black box must be all-true, so K1 must be true.  \nK1 says a box next to the blue box contains the gems; the only box next to the blue box is the white box. Thus the gems are in the white box.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.404, "ast_prob": 0.206, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00402", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n  [B2] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS AND A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(W1\\) be false: the White box is not blue, so the White box is all-false.\n\nFor \\(B1\\): Blue has exactly two statements, \\(B1\\) and \\(B2\\). If \\(B1\\) were true, then both \\(B1\\) and \\(B2\\) would have to be false, a contradiction. So \\(B1\\) must be false, and therefore \\(B2\\) must be true to avoid Blue having exactly two false statements.\n\nSo Blue is mixed: \\(B1=F\\), \\(B2=T\\).\n\nSince at least one box must be all-true, and White is all-false while Blue is mixed, Black must be all-true. Thus \\(K1=T\\).\n\n\\(K1\\) says a box next to Black contains the gems. The box next to Black is White, so the gems are in the White box.\n\nFinal verification:  \n- \\(W1=F\\): White box is not blue.  \n- \\(K1=T\\): White, next to Black, has the gems.  \n- \\(B1=F\\): Blue has one false statement, not two.  \n- \\(B2=T\\): the second clause is true because White is next to Blue and contains gems; the “box with two false statements” clause is vacuously satisfied since no such box exists.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.206, "ast_prob": 0.125, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00403", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS OR THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n  [W2] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS BLACK AND THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is always true: if the gems are in BLUE or BLACK, an adjacent box contains them; if the gems are in WHITE, the BLUE box is empty.  \nW2 is true only if the gems are in WHITE.  \nB1 says both W1 and W2 are true, so B1 is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.  \nK1 includes “the BLUE box is BLACK,” which is false, so K1 is false.\n\nChecking gem locations:\n- Gem in BLUE: W2 false, so B1 false; WHITE is mixed; no all-true box.\n- Gem in BLACK: same issue; no all-true box.\n- Gem in WHITE: W1 true, W2 true, B1 true, K1 false; WHITE all true, BLUE all true, BLACK all false. Satisfies all rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.551, "ast_prob": 0.428, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00404", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n  [K2] THE WHITE BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- \\(B_1\\) = “The other two boxes relative to the Blue box are empty” → true iff the gems are in BLUE.\n- \\(W_1\\) = “The gems are in a box that is true” → true iff the box containing the gems has all its statements true.\n- \\(K_1\\) = “This box is empty” → true iff the gems are not in BLACK.\n- \\(K_2\\) = “The White box has exactly two true statements” → always false, because the White box has only one statement.\n\nReasoning:\n\n1. If the gems were in BLUE:  \n   - \\(B_1\\) = true, so BLUE is all-true.  \n   - \\(K_1\\) = true and \\(K_2\\) = false, so BLACK is mixed.  \n   - \\(W_1\\) = true because BLUE is true.  \n   - Then no box is all-false. Violates the rule. So gems are not in BLUE.\n\n2. If the gems were in BLACK:  \n   - \\(K_1\\) = false and \\(K_2\\) = false, so BLACK is all-false.  \n   - \\(B_1\\) = false, so BLUE is all-false.  \n   - \\(W_1\\) = false because BLACK is not true.  \n   - Then no box is all-true. Violates the rule. So gems are not in BLACK.\n\n3. Therefore the gems must be in WHITE.  \n   - \\(B_1\\) = false because WHITE is not empty.  \n   - \\(K_1\\) = true because BLACK is empty.  \n   - \\(K_2\\) = false because WHITE has only one statement.  \n   - \\(W_1\\) must be true: the gems are in WHITE, and WHITE is a true box because its only statement \\(W_1\\) is true.  \n   - Boxes: BLUE all-false, WHITE all-true, BLACK mixed. This satisfies all rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.4, "ast_prob": 0.423, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00405", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EXACTLY TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n  [B2] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(B2\\) be true exactly when the White box contains the gems, since Black is on the right and only White is next to it.\n\n- White box has only one statement, \\(W1\\), so there is no statement above it. Thus \\(W1\\) is false.\n- Since \\(W1\\) is false, the White box is not all-true, so \\(K1\\) (“The White box is true”) is false.\n- At this point \\(W1\\) and \\(K1\\) are both false. If \\(B1\\) were false, there would be at least three false statements, so no box could be all-true while satisfying the “at least one all-true box” rule. Therefore \\(B1\\) must be true.\n- Since \\(B1\\) says exactly two statements are false, and \\(W1\\) and \\(K1\\) are already the two false statements, \\(B2\\) must be true.\n- Therefore the White box contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.548, "ast_prob": 0.298, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00406", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [W2] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location be considered in cases.\n\n- W1 and W2 both mean the same thing: the gems are in WHITE.  \n  - If gems are in WHITE, both W1 and W2 are TRUE.  \n  - If gems are in BLUE or BLACK, both W1 and W2 are FALSE.\n\n- K1 says “BLACK is the middle box.” BLACK is on the right, so K1 is always FALSE.\n\n- B1 says “the box with two false statements has the gems.”  \n  The only box that can have two statements is WHITE.  \n  - If gems are in WHITE, W1 and W2 are true, so no box has two false statements → B1 is FALSE.  \n  - If gems are not in WHITE, W1 and W2 are false, so WHITE has two false statements, but WHITE does not have gems → B1 is FALSE.  \n  So B1 is always FALSE.\n\nThus BLUE always has all-false, and BLACK always has all-false. To satisfy the rule that at least one box has all statements TRUE, WHITE must be all-true, meaning the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.586, "ast_prob": 0.474, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00407", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [W2] THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX HAS EXACTLY ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(W2\\) mean “the BLACK box has all true statements,” which here is equivalent to \\(K1=T\\).\n\n- \\(K1\\) says: WHITE has exactly one true statement, so \\(K1 = (W1 \\oplus W2)\\).\n- But \\(W2 = K1\\). Thus \\(K1 = W1 \\oplus K1\\), which forces \\(W1=F\\).\n- Since \\(W1\\) says “a box next to WHITE contains the gems,” and WHITE’s neighbors are BLUE and BLACK, \\(W1=F\\) means the gems are not in BLUE or BLACK. Therefore gems must be in WHITE.\n\nNow consider \\(B1\\): it says the top statement of every box is true, i.e. \\(B1 \\land W1 \\land K1\\). Since \\(W1=F\\), this conjunction is false, so \\(B1=F\\).\n\nIf \\(K1=F\\), then \\(W2=F\\), and WHITE would be all-false, BLACK all-false, and BLUE all-false — no all-true box, violating the rules.  \nSo \\(K1=T\\), hence \\(W2=T\\). WHITE then has exactly one true statement, as \\(K1\\) claims.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; W2=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.695, "ast_prob": 0.426, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00408", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [K2] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because the blue box is on the left, not the middle.  \nSo the blue box is already an all-false box.\n\nLet W1’s truth value be \\(w\\). Since the white box has only W1, the white box is all-true exactly when \\(w=\\) T.  \nK2 says “a box next to the black box is true,” and the only box next to black is white, so K2 is true exactly when the white box is all-true. Thus K2 has the same truth value as W1.\n\nK1 says “boxes next to the blue box contain gems.” The only box next to blue is white, so K1 is true exactly when the gems are in the white box.\n\nAt least one box must be all-true. Blue cannot be all-true, so either white or black must be all-true.  \n- If white is all-true, then W1 is true.  \n- If black is all-true, then K1 and K2 are both true, which again makes W1 true because K2 equals W1.\n\nSo W1 is true. Therefore the gems must be in a true box. The white box is true, while the blue box is false. The black box would only be true if K1 is true, meaning the gems are in white. So the only consistent gem location is the white box.\n\nThen K1 is true, K2 is true, W1 is true, and B1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.268, "ast_prob": 0.428, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00409", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n  [B2] THE WHITE BOX IS NOT EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX AND A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 refers to a statement above it, but there is no statement above B1, so the embedded claim is false; hence B1 is true.  \nB2 says the White box is not empty, which is true exactly when White contains the gems.  \nW1 says boxes next to Blue contain gems; only White is next to Blue, so W1 is also true exactly when White contains the gems.  \nK1 says Black is the middle box, which is false, so the whole conjunction K1 is false.  \n\nChecking gem locations:  \n- If gems are Blue or Black, then B2 and W1 are false, and no box is all-true.  \n- If gems are White, then B1 and B2 make Blue all-true, W1 makes White all-true, and K1 makes Black all-false. This satisfies all rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.229, "ast_prob": 0.355, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00410", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY AND THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS WHITE AND THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [K2] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. Box positions: BLUE = left, WHITE = middle, BLACK = right.\n2. B1: “Boxes next to the black box contain gems.” The only box next to BLACK is WHITE, so B1 is true iff WHITE contains the gems. Thus B1 = T iff gem = WHITE.\n3. W1: “The other two boxes are empty and the other two boxes are empty.” For WHITE, the other two boxes are BLUE and BLACK, so W1 is true iff WHITE contains the gems. Thus W1 = T iff gem = WHITE.\n4. K1: “The white box is white and this box is the middle box.” The white box is white = true, but the BLACK box is not the middle box = false. Therefore K1 = F.\n5. K2: “This box is the middle box.” This is on the BLACK box, which is not the middle box. Therefore K2 = F.\n6. Check gem locations:\n   - If gem = WHITE: B1=T, W1=T, K1=F, K2=F. BLUE and WHITE are all-true; BLACK is all-false. Satisfies rules.\n   - If gem = BLUE or BLACK: B1=F, W1=F, K1=F, K2=F, so no box has all statements true. Violates rules.\n7. Therefore the only valid solution is gem = WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.257, "ast_prob": 0.215, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00411", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS BLUE\n  [K2] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS AND BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n- B1 says “The blue box is the middle box.” The blue box is on the left, so B1 is **false**.\n- K1 says “The black box is blue.” The black box is on the right, so K1 is **false**.\n- K2 says boxes next to White contain gems and boxes next to Black contain gems. Since exactly one box contains the gems, the boxes next to White (Blue and Black) cannot both contain gems. So K2 is **false**.\n- Therefore Blue box is all-false and Black box is all-false.\n- At least one box must be all-true, so White must be all-true. Thus W1 is **true**, meaning White contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.301, "ast_prob": 0.397, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00412", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX OR BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [K2] EITHER BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS OR THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "- Blue box is left, White is middle, Black is right.\n- W1 says both neighbors of White (Blue and Black) contain gems. Since only one box contains gems, W1 is always FALSE.\n- B1 = “Blue is middle” (false) OR “neighbors of Blue contain gems” (only White is next to Blue). So B1 is true iff White contains the gems.\n- K1 is also true iff White contains the gems.\n- K2 says “neighbors of Black contain gems” OR “White contains gems.” Both parts reduce to “White contains the gems,” so K2 is true iff White contains the gems.\n- White’s box is always all-false, so at least one all-true box must come from Blue or Black. If the gems are not in White, then B1, K1, and K2 are all false and there is no all-true box. Therefore the gems must be in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.337, "ast_prob": 0.436, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00413", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EXACTLY THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX HAS AT MOST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n  [K2] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the four statements be B1, W1, K1, and K2.\n\n- W1 says “The black box has at most two true statements.” Since the black box has exactly two statements, W1 is always true.  \n  So W1 = T.\n\n- K1 says “The top statement of each box is true,” meaning B1, W1, and K1 are all true. Since K1 depends on its own truth and on B1, once B1 is false, K1 is forced false.  \n  We will confirm B1 is false below.\n\n- B1 says “Exactly three statements are false.” Since there are four statements total, this means exactly one statement is true. But W1 is already true. If B1 were true, there would be at least two true statements, contradiction. So B1 = F.\n\n- Since B1 is false, K1’s claim is false, so K1 = F.\n\n- Now B1 says exactly three statements are false. Current truth values: B1 = F, W1 = T, K1 = F. So K2 must be true to make exactly two false statements? Wait: if K2 = F, then false statements are B1, K1, K2 = 3, which would make B1 true, contradiction. Therefore K2 = T.\n\n- K2 says “A box next to this box contains the gems.” The black box is next to the white box, so the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFinal checks:\n- Blue box: B1 = F → all false.\n- White box: W1 = T → all true.\n- Black box: K1 = F, K2 = T → mixed, allowed.\n- Exactly one gem box: WHITE.\n- Exactly three false statements? B1=F, K1=F, K2=T, W1=T → only two false, so B1 is correctly false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.309, "ast_prob": 0.311, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00414", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX HAS AT MOST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS AND BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS AND BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [K2] EXACTLY ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.594, "ast_prob": 0.492, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00415", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS AND THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n  [W2] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n\n1. The boxes are in order: BLUE – WHITE – BLACK.\n2. “Next to” means adjacent:\n   - BLUE is next only to WHITE.\n   - BLACK is next only to WHITE.\n3. So:\n   - B1 is true iff the gems are in WHITE.\n   - K1 is true iff the gems are in WHITE.\n4. W1 says “BLUE contains the gems AND BLUE is empty,” which is impossible, so W1 is always FALSE.\n5. W2 is true iff WHITE is empty, i.e., iff the gems are NOT in WHITE.\n\nCheck each possible gem location:\n\n- If gems in BLUE: B1=F, W1=F, W2=T, K1=F → no box is all-true. Invalid.\n- If gems in WHITE: B1=T, W1=F, W2=F, K1=T → BLUE is all-true, WHITE is all-false, BLACK is all-true. Valid.\n- If gems in BLACK: B1=F, W1=F, W2=T, K1=F → no box is all-true. Invalid.\n\nTherefore, the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.321, "ast_prob": 0.397, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00416", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n  [B2] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 has no statement above it, so B1 is false.  \nB2 says “B1 is true,” so B2 is false.  \nThus all Blue statements are false.  \nK1 says all Blue statements are true, so K1 is false.  \nTo have at least one all-true box, White must be all-true, so W1 must be true, meaning the White box contains the gems.  \nThis also satisfies exactly one gem box and at least one all-false box.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.604, "ast_prob": 0.479, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00417", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE AND THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT MOST ONE STATEMENT IS FALSE\n  [W2] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT MOST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE AND A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(T\\) = number of true statements among B1, W1, W2, K1. There are 4 statements total.\n\n- White is the middle box, so B1 is true exactly when the White box is all-true: \\(\\text{B1} = \\text{W1} \\land \\text{W2}\\).\n- W2 is true exactly when the gems are in White.\n- W1 says “at most one statement is false,” so W1 is true iff \\(T \\ge 3\\).\n- K1 says “at most one statement is true and a box next to this box contains the gems.” A box next to Black is White, so K1 = \\((T \\le 1) \\land \\text{W2}\\).\n\nCheck gem locations:\n\n- If gems are in Blue or Black, then W2 is false, so B1 is false. Then at most 2 statements could be true, making W1 false, and K1 also false. This leaves no all-true box, violating the rules.\n- If gems are in White, then W2 is true. W1 cannot be false because that would force a contradiction with K1. So W1 is true, making B1 true. Then T is at least 3, so K1 is false.\n\nThus the only consistent solution is gems in White, with B1, W1, W2 true and K1 false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.564, "ast_prob": 0.371, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00418", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS WHITE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [W2] AT MOST THREE STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false, since the blue box is not white.  \nW2 cannot be false: if W2 were false, then “at most three statements are true” would be false, meaning all four statements would be true, including W2 itself — contradiction. So W2 is true.  \nW1 and K1 both say “a box next to the blue box contains the gems.” The only box next to the blue box is the white box, so W1 and K1 are equivalent to “the gems are in the white box.”  \nIf the gems were not in the white box, then W1 and K1 would both be false, making the white box mixed and both the blue and black boxes all-false — but then no box would be all-true, violating the rules.  \nTherefore the gems must be in the white box, making W1 and K1 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.212, "ast_prob": 0.37, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00419", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n  [W2] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLACK\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is false: the White box is not black. So the Black box is all-false.\n\nW1 says the top statement of every box is true, which would require B1, W1, and K1 all true. Since K1 is false, W1 must be false.\n\nW2 says a box next to the White box is true. The boxes next to White are Blue and Black. Black is not true, so W2 is true exactly when Blue is true, i.e. when B1 is true.\n\nSince at least one box must be all-true, and White cannot be all-true (W1 is false) and Black cannot be all-true (K1 is false), Blue must be all-true. Therefore B1 is true, meaning the box next to Blue contains the gems: the White box.\n\nThus W2 is also true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.671, "ast_prob": 0.437, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00420", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [W2] AT MOST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT LEAST TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(W1\\) be false because the BLUE box is left, not middle.\n\nOnly the WHITE box has two statements, so the “box with two false statements” can only be WHITE, and only if both \\(W1\\) and \\(W2\\) are false.\n\nLet \\(N\\) be the total number of true statements among \\(B1, W1, W2, K1\\).  \n- \\(W2\\) is true iff \\(N \\le 1\\).  \n- \\(K1\\) is true iff \\(N \\ge 2\\).\n\nSo \\(W2\\) and \\(K1\\) are opposites, and \\(N = B1 + 1\\).\n\nIf \\(B1\\) is false, then \\(N=1\\), so \\(W2=T\\), \\(K1=F\\), and \\(W1=F\\). This makes BLUE all false, WHITE mixed, BLACK all false — no all-true box, violating the rules.\n\nThus \\(B1\\) must be true. Then \\(N=2\\), so \\(W2=F\\), \\(K1=T\\), and \\(W1=F\\). Since WHITE has two false statements and \\(B1\\) is true, WHITE must have the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.764, "ast_prob": 0.427, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00421", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n  [W2] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- B1 means “the boxes other than White are empty,” which is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.\n- W2 means “the boxes other than Black are empty,” which is true exactly when the gems are in BLACK.\n- K1 means “the box next to Black, i.e. White, contains gems,” which is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.\n- W1 means “B1, W1, and K1 are all true,” so W1 = B1 ∧ W1 ∧ K1.\n\nCheck gem locations:\n\n1. If gems are in BLUE:  \n   B1=F, W2=F, K1=F ⇒ W1=F.  \n   No box is all-true → violates rules.\n\n2. If gems are in BLACK:  \n   B1=F, W2=T, K1=F ⇒ W1=F.  \n   No box is all-true → violates rules.\n\n3. If gems are in WHITE:  \n   B1=T, W2=F, K1=T.  \n   Then W1’s equation becomes W1 = W1, so W1 could be T or F by self-reference.  \n   If W1=T, then no box is all-false → violates rules.  \n   Therefore W1=F.  \n   Then Blue is all-true, White is all-false, and Black is all-true, satisfying the rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.283, "ast_prob": 0.14, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00422", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLUE\n  [B2] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B2 is true because the WHITE box is the middle box.  \nB1 is false because the WHITE box is not blue.  \nSo the BLUE box is mixed (one true, one false).  \n\nW1 says “the statement above this one is true,” but there is no statement above it inside the WHITE box, so W1 is false. Thus the WHITE box is all false.  \n\nSince at least one box must be all true, and the BLUE box is mixed while the WHITE box is all false, the BLACK box must be all true. Therefore K1 is true.  \n\nK1 says the boxes next to the BLACK box contain gems. The BLACK box is on the right, so the only box next to it is the WHITE box. Therefore the gems are in the WHITE box.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.249, "ast_prob": 0.181, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00423", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS WHITE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n  [W2] AT MOST TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS WHITE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because the BLUE box is not white.  \nK1 is true because the WHITE box is white.\n\nIf the gems were not in the WHITE box, then W1 would be true.  \nThen with B1 false and K1 true, there would already be two true statements. W2 says “at most two statements are true,” but if W2 were true there would be three true statements, and if W2 were false there would be exactly two true statements, making W2 true. Contradiction.\n\nSo the gems must be in the WHITE box. Therefore W1 is false.  \nThen only K1 is true so far, so W2 is true because at most two statements are true overall.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; W2=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.328, "ast_prob": 0.39, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00424", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n  [B2] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT LEAST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(b\\) be the truth value of both B1 and B2: “The White box is empty.”  \nSo \\(b\\) is true iff the gems are not in the White box.  \nAlso, K1 says “The White box is blue,” but the White box is white, so K1 is false.\n\nNow evaluate W1: “At least two statements are false.”\n\n- If \\(b\\) is true, then gems are not in White, so B1 and B2 are true, and K1 is false.  \n  If W1 were true, only K1 would be false → contradiction.  \n  If W1 were false, K1 and W1 would be false → W1 would be true → contradiction.  \n  So \\(b\\) cannot be true.\n\n- Therefore \\(b\\) is false, so the gems must be in the White box. Then B1 and B2 are false, and K1 is false.  \n  For W1 to be true, there would be 3 false statements: B1, B2, K1, which satisfies “at least two are false.” This is consistent.  \n  For W1 to be false, all 4 statements would be false, making W1 true → contradiction.  \n  So W1 is true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.282, "ast_prob": 0.274, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00425", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT MOST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n  [W2] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT MOST ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EXACTLY ONE STATEMENT IS FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(B1\\) be true iff the White box (the only box next to Blue) contains the gems.\n\n- If \\(B1=T\\), gems are in WHITE.\n- \\(W2\\) is true unless both White statements are false, so \\(W1=T, W2=F\\) is impossible.\n- Checking consistent truth-count cases leaves only one valid solution satisfying all box rules:\n  - \\(B1=T\\), \\(W1=F\\), \\(W2=T\\), \\(K1=F\\)\n  - True statements: \\(B1, W2\\) → 2 true statements, so \\(W1\\) (“at most one true”) is false.\n  - False statements: \\(W1, K1\\) → 2 false statements, so \\(K1\\) (“exactly one false”) is false.\n  - White box has exactly one false statement, so \\(W2\\) is true.\n  - Blue box is all-true, Black box is all-false, White box is mixed, satisfying the rules.\n  - Since \\(B1=T\\), gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.763, "ast_prob": 0.261, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00426", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE NOT IN THIS BOX\n  [W2] EITHER A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS OR THE WHITE BOX HAS EXACTLY ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(G_W\\) mean “the gems are in the WHITE box.”\n\n- Only the WHITE box is next to the BLACK box, so B1 = “gems are not in WHITE” = \\(\\neg G_W\\).\n- W1 is also “gems are not in WHITE” = \\(\\neg G_W\\).\n- K1 = “BLACK box is empty” = \\(\\neg G_K\\).\n\nNow consider W2:  \nW2 says: “A box next to BLUE contains the gems OR the WHITE box has exactly one false statement.”  \nThe only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so the first part is \\(G_W\\).\n\nIf \\(G_W\\) is false:\n- W1 is true.\n- For WHITE to have exactly one false statement, W2 itself would have to be false.\n- Then W2 would be equivalent to “W2 is false,” which is a paradox.\n\nSo \\(G_W\\) cannot be false. Therefore the gems are in WHITE.\n\nThen:\n- W1 = false\n- W2 = true\n- B1 = false\n- K1 = true, since BLACK is empty\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; W2=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.335, "ast_prob": 0.498, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00427", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EITHER THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY OR THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [K2] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "- B1 is true only if the WHITE box (the only box next to BLUE) contains the gems.\n- W1 would require both boxes next to WHITE to contain gems, but exactly one box contains gems, so W1 is false.\n- K1’s first part is true only if both WHITE and BLACK are empty, i.e. the gems are in BLUE; its second part is false because BLACK is not the middle box.\n- K2 says B1 is true, so K2 is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.\n\nTry gem locations:\n- If gem=BLUE: B1=F, W1=F, K1=T, K2=F → no all-true box.\n- If gem=WHITE: B1=T, W1=F, K1=F, K2=T → BLUE all-true, WHITE all-false, BLACK mixed.\n- If gem=BLACK: B1=F, W1=F, K1=F, K2=F → no all-true box.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.221, "ast_prob": 0.372, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00429", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n  [K2] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n\n1. B1 says the White box has at least two true statements. But the White box contains only one statement, W1, so it cannot have at least two true statements. Therefore **B1 = F**. Thus the Blue box is all-false, satisfying the “at least one all-false box” rule.\n\n2. K1 says the Black box is empty, so **K1 = T iff the gems are not in Black**.\n   K2 says “a box next to this box contains the gems.” The Black box is only next to the White box, so **K2 = T iff the gems are in White**.\n\n3. Test gem locations:\n   - If gems are in Blue: Black is mixed (K1=T, K2=F). White would need to be all-true, so W1=T. But then the only no-false box is White, and White does not contain the gems, so W1 would be false. Contradiction.\n   - If gems are in Black: Black is all-false (K1=F, K2=F). White would need to be all-true, so W1=T. But then the only no-false box is White, and White does not contain the gems, so W1 would be false. Contradiction.\n   - If gems are in White: Black is all-true (K1=T, K2=T), satisfying the all-true rule. Then W1=F, because the only no-false box is Black, and Black does not contain the gems. This is consistent.\n\nFinal assignment: gems in White; B1 false; W1 false; K1 true; K2 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.318, "ast_prob": 0.174, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00430", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLACK AND THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY OR A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [W2] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE WHITE BOX IS BLUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX AND BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because the White box is not Black.  \nK1 is false because the Black box is not the middle box.  \nW2 is true because the White box is not Blue.  \nW1 is true only if the gems are in the White box: the other two boxes relative to White would be empty, and the only box next to Blue is White.  \nTo have at least one all-true box, the gems must be in White. Then White is all-true, while Blue and Black are all-false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.778, "ast_prob": 0.47, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00431", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX IS BLACK\n  [W2] THIS BOX IS WHITE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n- Positions: BLUE = left, WHITE = middle, BLACK = right.\n- W1 says “THIS BOX IS BLACK” on the WHITE box: false.\n- W2 says “THIS BOX IS WHITE” on the WHITE box: true.\n- So the WHITE box has mixed statements, not all-true or all-false.\n- K1 says the BLACK box is the MIDDLE box: false, since BLACK is rightmost.\n- Thus the BLACK box is all-false, satisfying the “at least one all-false” rule.\n- Since WHITE cannot be all-true and BLACK cannot be all-true, BLUE must be all-true.\n- Therefore B1 is true: a box next to the BLACK box contains the gems.\n- The only box next to the BLACK box is WHITE, so the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.248, "ast_prob": 0.386, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00432", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [K2] THIS BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K2 says the BLACK box has at least two false statements. Since BLACK has exactly two statements, K2 would be true only if both K1 and K2 were false. That is impossible. Therefore K2 is false, which forces K1 to be true.\n\nK1 says the boxes next to the BLUE box contain gems. The only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so WHITE must contain the gems.\n\nThus B1, which says the same thing about boxes next to the BLUE box, is also true. W1 says WHITE is empty, but WHITE contains the gems, so W1 is false.\n\nFinal check: BLUE is all-true, WHITE is all-false, BLACK is mixed. Rules satisfied.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.565, "ast_prob": 0.371, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00433", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n  [W2] THE BLUE BOX HAS AT MOST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The BLACK box has only one statement, so there is no statement above K1. Therefore K1 is false: K1 = F.\n\nSince BLUE has only one statement, W2 (“The BLUE box has at most one true statement”) is always true. So WHITE cannot be all-false.\n\nBecause K1 is false, B1’s claim that all top statements are true is false, so B1 = F. Thus BLUE is all-false.\n\nTo satisfy the requirement that at least one box is all-true, WHITE must be all-true, so W1 = T. That means the other two boxes are empty, so the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.309, "ast_prob": 0.271, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00434", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT LEAST THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n  [K2] EITHER ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLACK BOX ARE TRUE OR THE BLACK BOX IS WHITE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 must be false, because the BLUE box has only one statement, so it cannot have exactly two true statements.  \nSince K1 is false, “all statements on the BLACK box are true” is false; also “the BLACK box is WHITE” is false. Therefore K2 is false.\n\nSo K1 and K2 are both false, giving at least two false statements.  \nIf W1 were false, then there would be at least three false statements, making W1 true — contradiction. So W1 is true.  \nSince W1 says “at least three statements are false” and K1, K2 are already false, B1 must also be false.\n\nNow B1 says “the gems are in a box that is false.” Since B1 is false, the gems are not in an all-false box.  \nBLACK is all-false, so gems are not in BLACK. BLUE would be all-false only if B1 were false, but gems in BLUE would make B1 refer to BLUE itself, creating a contradiction.  \nThus the gems must be in WHITE, which is all-true because W1 is true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.587, "ast_prob": 0.341, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00435", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n  [K2] THE BLACK BOX IS WHITE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is the only statement in the BLUE box and has no statement above it, so B1 is false. Therefore the BLUE box is all-false.\n\nK2 says “The BLACK box is WHITE,” which is false, so K2 = F.  \nK1 says “The top statement of each box is true,” but B1 is false, so K1 cannot be true. Thus K1 = F, making the BLACK box all-false.\n\nSince at least one box must be all-true, the WHITE box must be all-true, so W1 = T.  \nW1 says “The gems are in a box that is true,” and the only all-true box is WHITE, so the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.331, "ast_prob": 0.336, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00436", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EITHER THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY OR THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n  [K2] THIS BOX IS BLUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The black box is on the right, so B1 is false.  \nK2 is on the black box, so “this box is blue” is false.  \n\nIf W1 were false, then the WHITE box would be all-false, the BLUE box would also be all-false because B1 is false, and the BLACK box could not be all-true because K2 is false. That would leave no all-true box, violating the rules. So W1 must be true.\n\nW1 says the boxes next to the BLACK box contain gems. The BLACK box is on the right, so its only neighboring box is WHITE. Therefore the gems must be in WHITE.\n\nWith gems in WHITE: B1=F, W1=T. The BLUE box is empty, so K1=T. K2=F.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.382, "ast_prob": 0.328, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00437", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE BLACK BOX IS BLACK\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [K2] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because the black box is indeed black, so “it is not true that the black box is black” is false. Therefore the BLUE box is all-false, satisfying the “at least one all-false” rule.\n\nSo at least one of WHITE or BLACK must be all-true.\n\n- W1 says a box next to WHITE is true. The boxes next to WHITE are BLUE and BLACK. BLUE is false, so W1 is true exactly if BLACK is all-true.\n- K1 says a box next to BLACK contains the gems. Only WHITE is next to BLACK, so K1 = “WHITE contains the gems.”\n- K2 says a box next to BLACK is true. Only WHITE is next to BLACK, so K2 = “WHITE is true.”\n- WHITE is true exactly when W1 is true, so K2 depends on W1, and W1 depends on BLACK being true.\n\nIf the gems were not in WHITE, then K1 would be false. Since BLACK being true requires K1 and K2 both true, BLACK could not be all-true. Then WHITE would also not be true, leaving no all-true box, which violates the rules. Therefore the gems must be in WHITE.\n\nWith gems in WHITE:\n- K1 = true\n- To satisfy “at least one all-true box,” BLACK must be all-true, so K2 = true\n- Then W1 = true because BLACK is a true box next to WHITE\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.594, "ast_prob": 0.375, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00438", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [K2] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is false, because there is no statement above it in the White box.  \nSo the White box is not a true box.  \n\nB1 says a box next to the Black box is true. The only box next to Black is White, which is not true, so B1 is false.  \nThus the Blue box is also all-false.  \n\nSince at least one box must be all-true, and Blue and White are all-false, the Black box must be all-true.  \nTherefore K1 and K2 are both true.  \n\nK1 says a box next to the Black box contains the gems. The only box next to Black is White, so the gems must be in White.  \n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.667, "ast_prob": 0.449, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00439", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\n  [K2] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(A\\) mean “White contains the gems.”\n\n- Blue is leftmost, so the only box next to Blue is White.  \n  Thus W1 and K2 both say the same thing: \\(W1 = K2 = A\\).\n- Black’s only neighboring box is White. A box is “true” if all its statements are true. White has only W1, so Black’s statement K1 is true iff White is true: \\(K1 = W1 = A\\).\n\nSo \\(W1 = K1 = K2 = A\\).\n\nNow B1 says: “The top statement of each box is true,” i.e. \\(B1 = (B1 \\land W1 \\land K1) = (B1 \\land A)\\).\n\nIf \\(A = \\text{false}\\), then \\(W1 = K1 = K2 = \\text{false}\\), and \\(B1 = \\text{false}\\). Then all three boxes would be all-false, violating the rule that at least one box is all-true. Therefore \\(A = \\text{true}\\), so the gems are in WHITE.\n\nWith \\(A = \\text{true}\\), we get \\(W1 = K1 = K2 = \\text{true}\\). Thus White and Black are all-true. To satisfy “at least one box is all-false,” Blue must be all-false, so \\(B1 = \\text{false}\\).\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.53, "ast_prob": 0.452, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00441", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [K2] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Positions: BLUE = left, WHITE = middle, BLACK = right.\n\n- B1: BLACK is rightmost, so the only box next to BLACK is WHITE. Thus B1 is true exactly when WHITE contains the gems.\n- W1: The boxes next to WHITE are BLUE and BLACK. Since exactly one box contains gems, both cannot contain gems, so W1 is always false.\n- K1: BLACK is not the middle box, so K1 is false.\n- K2: The other two boxes relative to WHITE are BLUE and BLACK. K2 is true exactly when both are empty, i.e. when WHITE contains the gems.\n\nCheck gem placements:\n- If gem = BLUE: B1 F, W1 F, K1 F, K2 F → no all-true box → invalid.\n- If gem = BLACK: B1 F, W1 F, K1 F, K2 F → no all-true box → invalid.\n- If gem = WHITE: B1 T, W1 F, K1 F, K2 T → BLUE all-true, WHITE all-false, BLACK mixed → valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.207, "ast_prob": 0.381, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00442", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n  [W2] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is always true because the White box is indeed the middle box, so the Black box is an all-true box.\n\n- If the gems were in BLUE, then B1 and W1 would both be true, and Blue plus Black would be all-true, making W2 true too. Then all three boxes would be all-true, leaving no all-false box — invalid.\n- If the gems were in BLACK, then B1 would be true and W1 false. Blue and Black would be all-true, so W2 would be true; White would be mixed. There would be no all-false box — invalid.\n- If the gems were in WHITE, then B1 is false, so Blue is all-false. W1 is true. If W2 were false, White would not be all-true, making Black the unique all-true box; but Black would not contain the gems, so W2 would actually be true — contradiction. Thus W2 is true. This makes White all-true, Black all-true, and Blue all-false, satisfying all rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.604, "ast_prob": 0.424, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00443", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n  [B2] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX HAS AT MOST ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(K1\\) be the truth value of the BLACK box statement.\n\n- BLACK has only one statement, \\(K1\\). So “THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE” means \\(K1\\) is true. Thus \\(B1 = K1\\).\n- BLUE’s \\(B2\\) says: “A box next to WHITE is true.” The boxes next to WHITE are BLUE and BLACK. BLUE is true only if both \\(B1\\) and \\(B2\\) are true; BLACK is true only if \\(K1\\) is true. So \\(B2 = (B1 \\land B2) \\lor K1\\). Since \\(B1 = K1\\), this simplifies to \\(B2 = K1\\). Therefore \\(B1 = B2 = K1\\).\n- WHITE’s \\(W1\\) says BLACK has at most one false statement. BLACK has only one statement total, so it can have at most one false statement regardless. Thus \\(W1 = T\\).\n- \\(K1\\) says a box next to WHITE contains the gems. The boxes next to WHITE are BLUE and BLACK, so \\(K1\\) is true iff the gems are in BLUE or BLACK, and false iff the gems are in WHITE.\n\nWe need at least one all-false box. BLUE is all-false only if \\(K1 = F\\), and BLACK is all-false only if \\(K1 = F\\). So \\(K1\\) must be false. Therefore the gems are not in BLUE or BLACK, so they must be in WHITE.\n\nThus:\n- \\(K1 = F\\)\n- \\(B1 = F\\)\n- \\(B2 = F\\)\n- \\(W1 = T\\)\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.414, "ast_prob": 0.419, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00444", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EXACTLY TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n  [K2] AT MOST TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let T be the total number of true statements among B1, W1, K1, K2.\n\n- B1 is true iff the gems are NOT in WHITE, since only BLUE and BLACK are next to WHITE.\n- W1 is true iff T = 2.\n- K1 is true iff the gem box is an all-false box.\n- K2 is true iff T ≤ 2.\n\nChecking possible gem locations:\n\n1. **Gem in BLUE**: B1 = T, K1 = F.  \n   Then T = 1 + W1 + K2.  \n   W1 would require exactly 2 true statements, but K2 also interacts such that any assignment forces a contradiction. No consistent fixed point.\n\n2. **Gem in BLACK**: B1 = T.  \n   K1 says the gem is in a false box, but BLACK is false only if K1 and K2 are both false, forcing K1 = F and K2 = T.  \n   Then T = 2 + W1, so W1 would have to be both true and false. Contradiction.\n\n3. **Gem in WHITE**: B1 = F.  \n   K1 = F because WHITE is all-true, not false.  \n   K2 = T because at most two statements are true.  \n   Then T = F + W1 + F + T = 2, so W1 = T.  \n   This is consistent: BLUE is all-false, WHITE is all-true, BLACK is mixed.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.458, "ast_prob": 0.19, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00445", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [B2] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the boxes be in order: BLUE, WHITE, BLACK.\n\n- BLUE is adjacent only to WHITE, so B1 means “WHITE contains the gems.”\n- B2 means “BLUE is empty,” i.e. the gems are not in BLUE.\n- WHITE has only one statement, W1, so “at least two true statements” is impossible: W1 = F.\n- BLACK is adjacent only to WHITE. Since WHITE has W1 = F, WHITE is not a true box, so K1 = F.\n\nCheck gem locations:\n\n- If gems in BLUE: B1 = F, B2 = F, W1 = F, K1 = F → no all-true box, invalid.\n- If gems in BLACK: B1 = F, B2 = T, W1 = F, K1 = F → no all-true box, invalid.\n- If gems in WHITE: B1 = T, B2 = T, W1 = F, K1 = F → BLUE is all-true, WHITE and BLACK are all-false, valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.601, "ast_prob": 0.258, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00446", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n  [B2] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location be tested.\n\n- If gems are in BLUE: B1=false, B2=false, W1=false, K1=false → no all-true box, invalid.\n- If gems are in BLACK: B1=true, B2=false, W1=true, K1=true → no all-false box, invalid.\n- Therefore gems must be in WHITE:  \n  - B1: Blue is empty → true  \n  - B2: White is next to Blue and contains gems → true  \n  - W1: Blue and White are not both empty → false  \n  - K1: Black contains gems → false  \n\nBLUE is all-true; WHITE and BLACK are all-false, satisfying the rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.229, "ast_prob": 0.336, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00447", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [B2] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 refers to a statement above it on the Black box, but there is none, so K1 is false. If White did not contain the gems, then B1, B2, and W1 would all be false, leaving no all-true box, which violates the rules. Therefore the gems must be in White, making B1, B2, and W1 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.679, "ast_prob": 0.203, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00448", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS BLUE AND THE BLUE BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [W2] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EITHER BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS OR BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "1. The WHITE box is indeed the middle box, so **W1 = T**.\n\n2. B1 says “The BLACK box is BLUE and the BLUE box has at least two true statements.”  \n   The BLACK box is not blue, so the first conjunct is false. Therefore **B1 = F**.\n\n3. W2 says “The other two boxes relative to the WHITE box are empty.”  \n   If the gem is in WHITE, then BLUE and BLACK are both empty, so W2 would be true. If the gem is in BLUE or BLACK, W2 would be false.\n\n4. K1 is logically equivalent to “boxes next to the BLUE box contain gems.” Since BLUE is the left box, the only box next to it is WHITE. So K1 is true exactly when the gem is in WHITE.\n\n5. If the gem were in BLUE or BLACK, then both W2 and K1 would be false, giving no all-true box. That violates the rule that at least one box has all statements true.\n\n6. Therefore the gem must be in WHITE. Then W2 = T and K1 = T; W1 is already T, so the WHITE box is all-true. BLUE remains all-false, satisfying the rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.337, "ast_prob": 0.254, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00449", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [K2] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 says BLUE is the middle box, but BLUE is left, so B1 is false.  \nW1 says the statement above it (B1) is true, so W1 is false.  \nThus BLUE and WHITE are both all-false boxes.  \nSince at least one box must be all-true, BLACK must be all-true, so K1 and K2 are true.  \nK2 true means BLACK is empty. K1 true means the box next to BLACK, which is WHITE, contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.484, "ast_prob": 0.151, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00451", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [B2] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS WHITE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B2 is true because the white box is the middle box.  \nW1 is false because the black box is not white.  \nK1 is false because the black box contains only one statement, so it cannot have exactly two false statements.  \n\nThus the WHITE box is all-false and the BLACK box is all-false.  \nSince at least one box must be all-true, the BLUE box must be all-true.  \nB2 is already true, so B1 must also be true.  \nB1 says no box next to the white box contains the gems. The boxes next to the white box are BLUE and BLACK, so neither contains the gems.  \nTherefore the gems must be in the WHITE box.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.59, "ast_prob": 0.322, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00452", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [W2] THE BLACK BOX IS BLUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step by step:\n- BLUE is the left box, WHITE is the middle box, BLACK is the right box.\n- B1 says the BLUE box is the middle box → **FALSE**.\n- W1 says the WHITE box is the middle box → **TRUE**.\n- W2 says the BLACK box is blue → **FALSE**.\n- So the BLUE box is all-false, and the WHITE box is mixed.\n- At least one box must be all-true. The only possible all-true box is BLACK, so K1 must be TRUE.\n- K1 says boxes next to the BLACK box contain gems. BLACK is on the right, so its only neighbor is WHITE. Therefore the WHITE box must contain the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.211, "ast_prob": 0.126, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00453", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [B2] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS OR THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n- B1 = “Boxes next to the BLACK box contain gems.” Only WHITE is next to BLACK, so B1 is true iff the gems are in WHITE.\n- B2 = “The WHITE box is the middle box.” This is always true.\n- Only BLUE has two statements. Since B2 is always true, BLUE can never have two false statements, so “the box with two false statements has the gems” is always false.\n- K1 = “A box next to the BLACK box is true.” Only WHITE is next to BLACK, so K1 = (W1 is true).\n- W1 = false OR (B1 ∧ W1 ∧ K1) = B1 ∧ W1 ∧ W1 = B1 ∧ W1.\n\nThus:\n- If gems are in BLUE: B1=false, W1=false, K1=false, and BLUE is mixed → no all-true box → invalid.\n- If gems are in BLACK: same: B1=false, W1=false, K1=false → no all-true box → invalid.\n- If gems are in WHITE: B1=true, B2=true, so BLUE is all-true. W1 is unconstrained by its own equation, but W1=true would make WHITE and BLACK all-true with no all-false box; so W1=false, K1=false. This satisfies one all-true box and two all-false boxes.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.553, "ast_prob": 0.322, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00454", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EXACTLY ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n  [K2] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(B1\\) mean “Blue box has the gems” (since then White and Black are empty).  \nLet \\(W1\\) mean “White box has the gems” (since then Blue and Black are empty).  \nLet \\(K2\\) mean “White box has the gems” for the same reason.\n\nSo:\n- \\(B1\\) is true iff gem = BLUE\n- \\(W1\\) is true iff gem = WHITE\n- \\(K2\\) is true iff gem = WHITE\n- \\(K1\\) says that exactly one of all four statements \\(B1, W1, K1, K2\\) is true.\n\nCheck each gem location:\n\n1. **Gem = BLUE**  \n   \\(B1=T,\\ W1=F,\\ K2=F\\).  \n   If \\(K1=T\\), there are 2 true statements, contradiction.  \n   If \\(K1=F\\), there is exactly 1 true statement, making \\(K1\\) true, contradiction.  \n   Impossible.\n\n2. **Gem = BLACK**  \n   \\(B1=F,\\ W1=F,\\ K2=F\\).  \n   No box could be all-true, because Blue and White would both be all-false and Black would be all-false or mixed.  \n   Violates “at least one box all true.”  \n   Impossible.\n\n3. **Gem = WHITE**  \n   \\(B1=F,\\ W1=T,\\ K2=T\\).  \n   If \\(K1=T\\), there are 3 true statements, contradiction.  \n   So \\(K1=F\\). Then there are exactly 2 true statements, so \\(K1\\) is correctly false.  \n   Blue box is all-false, White box is all-true, Black box is mixed.  \n   This satisfies every rule.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.548, "ast_prob": 0.265, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00455", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS OR THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n  [W2] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY AND THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- \\(B1\\) = “Blue contains the gems.”\n- \\(K1\\) = “The other two boxes relative to Blue are empty and Black is empty” = Blue contains the gems, so \\(K1 = B1\\).\n- \\(W2\\) = “Black is empty” = Black does **not** contain the gems.\n- \\(W1\\) = “Either Blue contains the gems, or the other two boxes are empty” = true if Blue or White contains the gems.\n\nCheck the three possible gem locations:\n\n- If **Blue** has gems: \\(B1=T, W1=T, W2=T, K1=T\\). All boxes are all-true, so no box is all-false → invalid.\n- If **White** has gems: \\(B1=F, W1=T, W2=T, K1=F\\). Blue is all-false, White is all-true, Black is all-false → valid.\n- If **Black** has gems: \\(B1=F, W1=F, W2=F, K1=F\\). No box is all-true → invalid.\n\nTherefore the gems are in White.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.246, "ast_prob": 0.319, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00456", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS WHITE\n  [B2] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.454, "ast_prob": 0.363, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00457", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [B2] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because BLACK is the right box, not the middle.  \nK1 is false for the same reason: BLACK is not the middle box.  \n\nSo BLACK is an all-false box.  \nBLUE cannot be all-true because B1 is false.  \nTherefore the required all-true box must be WHITE, so W1 must be true.  \n\nW1 says the gems are in a box that is true; the only true box is WHITE, so the gems are in WHITE.  \nThen B2 is also true, because the gems are in a true box.  \nBLUE is then mixed: B1 false, B2 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.416, "ast_prob": 0.439, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00458", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n  [K2] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location be G.\n\n- BLUE is leftmost, so the only box next to it is WHITE. Therefore:\n  - B1 is true iff G = WHITE.\n- K1 is true iff G ≠ BLACK.\n- K2 is true iff B1, W1, and K1 are all true.\n- W1 says the gems are in a box whose statements are all true.\n\nCheck possibilities:\n\n1. If G = BLUE:  \n   B1 = F, K1 = T, so K2 = F. W1 = F.  \n   BLUE is all false, BLACK is mixed, WHITE is all false → no all-true box. Invalid.\n\n2. If G = BLACK:  \n   B1 = F because BLACK is not next to BLUE. K1 = F, so K2 = F.  \n   BLUE is all false, BLACK is all false, WHITE is all false → no all-true box. Invalid.\n\n3. If G = WHITE:  \n   B1 = T, K1 = T.  \n   BLUE is all true. To satisfy “at least one all-false box,” WHITE must be all false, so W1 = F.  \n   Then K2 = B1 ∧ W1 ∧ K1 = T ∧ F ∧ T = F.  \n   BLACK is mixed. This satisfies all rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.512, "ast_prob": 0.209, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00459", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX HAS AT LEAST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EXACTLY TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [K2] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Since BLACK is the rightmost box, the only box next to BLACK is WHITE.\n\nThus:\n- K1: “A box next to the BLACK box contains the gems” means WHITE contains the gems.\n- K2: “Boxes next to this box contain gems” also refers to boxes next to BLACK, i.e. only WHITE, so it also means WHITE contains the gems.\n- B1: “The BLACK box has at least one true statement” is true iff K1 or K2 is true, which is true iff WHITE contains the gems.\n\nNow test gem locations:\n\n- If gem is in BLUE or BLACK: K1=false, K2=false, B1=false. Then W1 cannot be true, and if W1=false all boxes are all-false, violating the “at least one all-true box” rule. Invalid.\n- If gem is in WHITE: K1=true, K2=true, B1=true. Then W1 must be false, because only W1 is false among the four statements, so “exactly two statements are false” is false. This gives one all-true BLUE box, one all-true BLACK box, and one all-false WHITE box, satisfying all rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.466, "ast_prob": 0.296, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00460", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS BLACK\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS BLACK\n  [W2] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n1. The BLUE box is blue, so B1 “The blue box is black” is FALSE.\n2. The BLACK box is black, so W1 “The black box is black” is TRUE.\n3. The BLUE box is on the left, so the only box next to it is the WHITE box. Thus W2 is TRUE exactly when the gems are in WHITE.\n4. The BLACK box is on the right, so the only box next to it is the WHITE box. Thus K1 is TRUE exactly when the gems are in WHITE.\n5. Test gem locations:\n   - If gems are in BLUE: W2=F, K1=F, W1=T, B1=F → no all-true box. Invalid.\n   - If gems are in WHITE: W2=T, K1=T, W1=T, B1=F → BLUE is all-false, WHITE is all-true, BLACK is all-true. Valid.\n   - If gems are in BLACK: W2=F, K1=F, W1=T, B1=F → no all-true box. Invalid.\n6. Therefore the gems are in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.517, "ast_prob": 0.153, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00461", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n  [K2] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K2 is false because the BLUE box is on the left, not the middle.  \nK1 says K2 is true, so K1 is false.  \nB1 says B1, W1, and K1 are all true; since K1 is false, B1 is false.  \nThus BLUE and BLACK are both all-false boxes.  \nAt least one box must be all-true, so WHITE must be all-true, making W1 true.  \nW1 says the gems are in a true box; the only true box is WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.623, "ast_prob": 0.41, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00462", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n  [K2] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(G\\) be the box with the gems.\n\n- \\(B1\\): “The white box is blue” is false, because the middle box is white, not blue.\n- If \\(G = \\text{BLUE}\\), then \\(W1\\) and \\(K1\\) are false, \\(K2\\) is true, and no box is all-true. Invalid.\n- If \\(G = \\text{BLACK}\\), then \\(W1\\) and \\(K1\\) are false, \\(K2\\) is true, and no box is all-true. Invalid.\n- If \\(G = \\text{WHITE}\\), then:\n  - \\(B1 = F\\)\n  - \\(W1 = T\\), because the other two boxes are empty.\n  - \\(K1 = T\\), because the other two boxes relative to white are empty.\n  - \\(K2 = F\\), because the white box contains the gems.\n\nThus BLUE is all-false, WHITE is all-true, and BLACK is mixed, satisfying the rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.279, "ast_prob": 0.259, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00463", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [B2] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location determine the statements:\n\n- B1 is true iff the gems are in BLUE.\n- B2 is true iff the gems are NOT in BLUE, so B2 is always the opposite of B1. Thus the BLUE box is always a mix, never all-true or all-false.\n- W1 says “BLUE is the middle box,” but BLUE is left and WHITE is middle, so W1 is always false. Thus the WHITE box is always all-false.\n- K1 says “a box next to BLACK contains the gems.” Only WHITE is next to BLACK, so K1 is true iff the gems are in WHITE.\n\nSince at least one box must be all-true, and BLUE can never be all-true and WHITE is all-false, BLACK must be the all-true box. Therefore K1 must be true, so the gems are in WHITE.\n\nThen:\n- B1 = F\n- B2 = T\n- W1 = F\n- K1 = T\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.501, "ast_prob": 0.146, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00464", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n  [B2] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE AND BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "1. White has only one statement, W1. W1 says both adjacent boxes (Blue and Black) contain gems, but exactly one box contains gems, so W1 is false.  \n2. Since White has only one statement, it cannot have exactly two true statements, so B1 is false.  \n3. B2 says the top statement of every box is true: B1, W1, and K1. Since B1 is false, B2 is false.  \n4. Thus Blue and White are both all-false. The rules require at least one all-true box, so Black must be all-true: K1 is true.  \n5. K1 says “The black box is true and boxes next to the blue box contain gems.” Since K1 is true and Black is true, the boxes next to Blue must contain gems. The only box next to Blue is White, so White contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.367, "ast_prob": 0.275, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00465", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n  [W2] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(b = B1\\), \\(x = W1\\), \\(y = K1\\), and \\(w = W2\\).\n\n- \\(W1\\) and \\(K1\\) both say: “B1 is true, W1 is true, and K1 is true.”\n  So \\(x = b \\land x \\land y\\) and \\(y = b \\land x \\land y\\).\n  Thus \\(x = y\\), and:\n  - If \\(b = F\\), then \\(x = y = F\\).\n  - If \\(b = T\\), then \\(x = y = T\\) or \\(x = y = F\\).\n\nCheck gem locations:\n\n- **BLUE**: \\(B1 = F\\), so \\(W1 = K1 = F\\). Also \\(W2 = F\\) because BLUE is not true. Then all boxes are all-false, violating “at least one all-true.”\n\n- **BLACK**: \\(B1 = F\\), so again \\(W1 = K1 = F\\) and \\(W2 = F\\). All boxes all-false, violating “at least one all-true.”\n\n- **WHITE**: \\(B1 = T\\).  \n  If \\(W1 = K1 = T\\), then BLUE and BLACK are all-true, and WHITE cannot be all-false, so no box is all-false — invalid.  \n  If \\(W1 = K1 = F\\), then \\(W2 = F\\) because WHITE is not all-true. Then:\n  - BLUE is all-true,\n  - WHITE is all-false,\n  - BLACK is all-false.\n\nThis satisfies all conditions uniquely.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.379, "ast_prob": 0.474, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00466", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [B2] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 and B2 are both true iff the WHITE box contains the gems. Since BLUE is the left box, the only box next to it is WHITE, so B1 and B2 have the same truth value. W1 is false because BLUE is not the middle box. K1 is false because WHITE has only one statement (W1), so it cannot have exactly two true statements.\n\nIf WHITE contains the gems, BLUE is all-true, WHITE is all-false, and BLACK is all-false, satisfying the uniqueness rules. If WHITE did not contain the gems, every box would be all-false, violating the requirement that at least one box be all-true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.52, "ast_prob": 0.497, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00467", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n  [W2] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step 1: The BLUE box has only one statement, so it cannot have exactly two false statements. Therefore W1 (“The BLUE box has exactly two false statements”) is FALSE.\n\nStep 2: W2 says “The top statement of each box is true,” meaning B1, W1, and K1 are all true. Since W1 is false, W2 is FALSE.\n\nStep 3: Therefore the WHITE box has both statements false, so the WHITE box is all-false.\n\nStep 4: B1 says “The WHITE box is true,” but the WHITE box is all-false, so B1 is FALSE. Thus the BLUE box is also all-false.\n\nStep 5: Since at least one box must be all-true, and both BLUE and WHITE are all-false, the BLACK box must be all-true. So K1 is TRUE.\n\nStep 6: K1 says the other two boxes relative to WHITE are empty, meaning BLUE and BLACK are empty. Therefore the gems must be in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.626, "ast_prob": 0.396, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00468", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [K2] THE WHITE BOX IS BLACK\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because the BLUE box is on the left, not the middle.  \nK2 is false because the WHITE box is not black.  \nIf the gems were in BLUE, then all statements would be false, violating the rule that at least one box is all-true.  \nIf the gems were in BLACK, then again all boxes would be all-false, violating the rule.  \nIf the gems are in WHITE, then W1 is true, K1 is true (White is next to Black and contains gems), Blue is all-false, White is all-true, and Black is mixed. This satisfies all rules uniquely.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.343, "ast_prob": 0.322, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00469", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE OR THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n  [B2] THIS BOX HAS AT MOST ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EXACTLY TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n1. If B1 were true, then B2 must also be true, and K1 would equal W1. Then the true-statement count would be 2 + 2·W1, which cannot make W1 (“exactly two statements are true”) consistent. So B1 is false.\n2. With B1 false, K1 is false. If B2 were also false, then Blue would be all false, and W1 would be forced false, leaving no all-true box — violating the rules. So B2 must be true.\n3. With B1 false, B2 true, and K1 false, the only way W1 (“exactly two statements are true”) is true is if W1 itself is true. Thus the true statements are B2 and W1.\n4. Since B1 is false, the gems are not in a false box and the top statements are not all true. The only non-false box is White, so the gems must be in White.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.486, "ast_prob": 0.386, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00470", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [B2] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the boxes be in order: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right).\n\n- W1 says “The black box is the middle box.” This is always false, because BLACK is on the right.\n- B1 says “Boxes next to the blue box contain gems.” The only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so B1 is true iff WHITE has the gems.\n- B2 says “A box next to the black box contains the gems.” The only box next to BLACK is WHITE, so B2 is also true iff WHITE has the gems.\n- K1 says “A box next to the black box is true.” The box next to BLACK is WHITE, which contains only W1, and W1 is false. So WHITE is not an all-true box; therefore K1 is false.\n\nNow test gem locations:\n\n- If gems are in BLUE: B1=false, B2=false, W1=false, K1=false. No box is all-true. Invalid.\n- If gems are in BLACK: B1=false, B2=false, W1=false, K1=false. No box is all-true. Invalid.\n- If gems are in WHITE: B1=true, B2=true, W1=false, K1=false. BLUE is all-true, and WHITE/BLACK are all-false. This satisfies the rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.304, "ast_prob": 0.183, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00471", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n  [B2] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n- B1 is true iff the gems are in WHITE (so Blue and Black are empty).\n- B2 is true iff the gems are NOT in WHITE (a box next to White contains them).\n- W1 is true iff the gems are in WHITE.\n- K1 claims: B1, W1, and K1 are all true. This gives the self-consistent condition K1 = (B1 ∧ W1 ∧ K1).\n\nCheck gem locations:\n\n1. **Gems in BLUE**:  \n   B1 = F, B2 = T, W1 = F, K1 = F.  \n   Blue is mixed, White is all-false, Black is all-false.  \n   No all-true box → invalid.\n\n2. **Gems in BLACK**:  \n   B1 = F, B2 = T, W1 = F, K1 = F.  \n   Same as above: no all-true box → invalid.\n\n3. **Gems in WHITE**:  \n   B1 = T, B2 = F, W1 = T.  \n   K1 can be T or F self-consistently:  \n   - If K1 = T: White and Black are all-true, Blue is mixed → no all-false box → invalid.  \n   - If K1 = F: White is all-true, Black is all-false, Blue is mixed → satisfies all rules.\n\nTherefore the unique solution is gems in WHITE, with K1 false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.288, "ast_prob": 0.387, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00472", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\n  [B2] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location decide the statements.\n\n- W1 says the other two boxes (Blue and Black) are empty. Since exactly one box has the gems, W1 is true iff the gems are in WHITE.\n- B2 says the box next to Blue contains gems. The only box next to Blue is White, so B2 is true iff the gems are in WHITE.\n- B1 says a box next to Blue is true. The only box next to Blue is White, so B1 is true iff W1 is true.\n- K1 says “the statement above this one is true,” but there is no statement above K1 in the Black box, so K1 is false.\n\nIf the gems were in Blue or Black, then W1, B1, and B2 would all be false, and K1 is false — all three boxes would be all-false, violating the rule that at least one box must be all-true.\n\nTherefore the gems must be in WHITE, making B1, B2, and W1 all true, while K1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.533, "ast_prob": 0.134, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00473", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THIS BOX ARE TRUE\n  [W2] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE AND THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EITHER AT LEAST THREE STATEMENTS ARE TRUE OR A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- `B1` = \"A box next to BLUE contains the gems\" → true iff gems are in WHITE.\n- `W1` = \"All statements on WHITE are true\" → true iff both W1 and W2 are true.\n- `W2` = \"A box next to WHITE is true and the top statement of each box is true\" → this requires B1, W1, and K1 all true.\n- `K1` = \"At least three statements are true OR a box next to BLACK contains the gems\" → true iff total true ≥ 3 OR gems are in WHITE.\n\nFrom W1, if W1 is true then W2 must also be true. From W2, if W2 is true then B1, W1, and K1 must all be true. So W1 and W2 must have the same truth value.\n\nIf W1 and W2 were both true, then B1 and K1 would also be true, making every box all-true. That would leave no all-false box, violating the rules. So W1 and W2 must both be false.\n\nWith W1 and W2 false, total true statements are just B1 + K1.\n\nIf B1 were false, then gems are not in WHITE, so K1 would be false too, making all three boxes all-false, violating the rule that at least one box is all-true.\n\nTherefore B1 must be true, so the gems are in WHITE. Then K1 is automatically true because a box next to BLACK contains the gems. This gives exactly two true statements, so K1's \"at least three statements\" part is false, but its other condition is true.\n\nSolution: gems in WHITE; B1 true, W1 false, W2 false, K1 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.503, "ast_prob": 0.287, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00474", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX HAS AT MOST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n  [W2] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(W1\\) refer to the statement above it in the White box, which is \\(W2\\). So \\(W1 \\leftrightarrow W2\\).\n\n- Blue has only \\(B1\\). “At most one true statement” in a one-statement box must be true, so \\(B1 = T\\). Blue is all-true.\n- Since Blue is next to White and is all-true, \\(K1 = T\\). Black is all-true.\n- Blue and Black are already all-true, so the required all-false box must be White. Thus \\(W1 = F\\) and \\(W2 = F\\).\n- \\(W2\\) says the gems are in a true box. Since it is false, the gems are not in Blue or Black; therefore they are in White.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.616, "ast_prob": 0.34, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00475", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS OR BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE AND THE BLUE BOX HAS AT MOST TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n  [K2] EXACTLY ONE STATEMENT IS FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location be determined first.\n\n- BLUE has only one neighboring box: WHITE. So B1 is true exactly when WHITE contains the gems.\n- BLACK also has only one neighboring box: WHITE. So W1 is true exactly when WHITE contains the gems.\n- Thus B1 and W1 have the same truth value: both are true if the gems are in WHITE, both false otherwise.\n- BLUE has only 1 statement, so “BLUE has at most two false statements” is always true.\n- Therefore K1 reduces to: “The BLACK box is true.” Since BLACK contains K1 and K2, this means K1 = K1 ∧ K2. So K1 cannot be true while K2 is false.\n\nCase 1: Gems in WHITE.\n- B1 = T, W1 = T.\n- To satisfy “at least one box is all-false,” BLACK must be all-false: K1 = F, K2 = F.\n- Check: B1 and W1 true, K1 and K2 false → K2 (“exactly one statement is false”) is false, consistent.\n- K1 (“BLACK box is true and BLUE has at most two false statements”) is false because BLACK is not true, consistent.\n- This works.\n\nCase 2: Gems not in WHITE.\n- Then B1 = F and W1 = F, so BLUE and WHITE are all-false.\n- To satisfy “at least one box is all-true,” BLACK would need to be all-true: K1 = T, K2 = T.\n- But K2 would then claim “exactly one statement is false,” while B1 and W1 are already two false statements. Contradiction.\n- So this case is impossible.\n\nThus the only valid solution is gems in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.633, "ast_prob": 0.301, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00476", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT MOST ONE STATEMENT IS FALSE\n  [K2] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is always false, because the black box is on the right, not in the middle.\n\n- If the gems were in BLUE: B1=F, W1=F (white is next to blue but empty), K2=F. Then at least B1, W1, and K2 are false, so K1=F. No box is all-true, so invalid.\n- If the gems were in BLACK: B1=F, W1=F, K2=T. With B1 and W1 already false, K1 must be false. No box is all-true, so invalid.\n- If the gems were in WHITE: B1=F, W1=T, K2=F. Since B1 and K2 are already false, K1 must also be false. Then BLUE is all-false, WHITE is all-true, and BLACK is all-false. This satisfies all rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.484, "ast_prob": 0.416, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00477", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [B2] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location determine B1 and K1, since the only box next to both Blue and Black is White.\n\n- If the gem is in WHITE, then B1 = T and K1 = T.\n- B2 says “the statement above is true,” so B2 = B1 = T.\n- W1 says “this box has at least two false statements,” but the White box has only one statement, so W1 = F.\n- This gives: Blue all-true, White all-false, Black all-true, satisfying the rule of at least one all-true and at least one all-false.\n\n- If the gem is in BLUE or BLACK, then White is empty, so B1 = F, B2 = F, K1 = F, and W1 = F. Then every box is all-false, violating the requirement of at least one all-true box.\n\nThus the only solution is gem in WHITE.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.753, "ast_prob": 0.436, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00478", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n  [K2] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n- A box being “true” means all of its statements are true; “false” means all of its statements are false.\n- Blue has only one neighbor: White. So B1 means “White is true,” i.e. W1 is true. Thus B1 = W1.\n- White’s statement refers to the statement above it, B1, so W1 means “B1 is true.” Thus W1 = B1.\n- Therefore B1 and W1 have the same truth value.\n\nIf B1 = W1 = True:\n- Blue and White are both all-true.\n- To have at least one all-false box, Black must be all-false: K1 = False and K2 = False.\n- K1 false means Blue has the gems.\n- K2 false means neither adjacent box to Black has the gems, so Black has the gems.\n- Contradiction.\n\nSo B1 = W1 = False:\n- Blue and White are both all-false.\n- To have at least one all-true box, Black must be all-true: K1 = True and K2 = True.\n- K1 true means Blue is empty.\n- K2 true means one of Blue or White contains the gems.\n- Since Blue is empty, White must contain the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.615, "ast_prob": 0.413, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00479", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n  [W2] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The BLACK box is on the right, so B1 (“The black box is the middle box”) is FALSE.  \nThe WHITE box is in the middle, so W2 (“This box is the middle box”) is TRUE.  \nW1 is true only if both other boxes are empty, meaning the gems must be in WHITE.  \nK1 is true only if the box next to BLACK contains gems, which is also only true if the gems are in WHITE.\n\n- If gems are in BLUE: no box is all-true → invalid.\n- If gems in WHITE: BLUE is all-false, WHITE is all-true, BLACK is all-true → valid.\n- If gems in BLACK: no box is all-true → invalid.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.364, "ast_prob": 0.362, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00480", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n  [K2] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY AND THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let gem location determine statement truth values, then check the global rules.\n\n- If gems are in BLUE: B1 is false (Blue is not empty), W1 is false, K1 is true, K2 is false. No box is all-true. Invalid.\n- If gems are in WHITE: B1 is true (Blue and Black are empty), W1 is false, K1 is false (White is not empty), K2 is true. BLUE is all-true, WHITE is all-false, BLACK is mixed. Valid.\n- If gems are in BLACK: B1 is false (Black is not empty), W1 is false, K1 is false, K2 is true. No box is all-true. Invalid.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.343, "ast_prob": 0.257, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00481", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [B2] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the boxes be in fixed positions: BLUE = left, WHITE = middle, BLACK = right.\n\n- B1: “THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX” is false, because BLUE is left.\n- B2: “ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE” would require B1 true, but B1 is false, so B2 is false.\n  - Therefore BLUE is all-false.\n- W1: “THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX” is false, because BLACK is right.\n  - Therefore WHITE is all-false.\n- Since at least one box must be all-true, BLACK must be the all-true box.\n- K1: “BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS” means the WHITE box must contain the gems, because BLACK’s only neighboring box is WHITE.\n  - So gems are in WHITE, making K1 true and BLACK all-true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.489, "ast_prob": 0.149, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00482", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n  [K2] THIS BOX HAS AT MOST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let K2 be analyzed first:  \n- If K2 were false, the Black box would have more than one true statement, meaning K1 and K2 would both be true — a contradiction.  \n- Therefore K2 is true, and K1 must be false.  \nSo the Black box is a mixed box, not all-true or all-false.\n\nThus the all-true and all-false boxes must come from Blue and White. Since each has only one statement, one of B1 or W1 must be true and the other false.\n\nIf B1 were true, the gems would be in Blue, making W1 also true. Then there would be no all-false box. So B1 must be false and W1 true.\n\nW1 true means Black is empty. B1 false means White is not empty, or Black is not empty — but Black is empty, so White must contain the gems.\n\nFinal truth values:  \n- gem = WHITE  \n- B1 = F  \n- W1 = T  \n- K1 = F  \n- K2 = T\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.273, "ast_prob": 0.148, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00483", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] AT MOST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [K2] EXACTLY THREE STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(t\\) be the number of true statements among B1, W1, K1, K2.\n\n- B1 is true iff \\(t \\le 1\\).\n- W1 is true iff the gems are in BLACK.\n- K1 is true iff the gems are in BLUE.\n- K2 is true iff \\(t = 3\\).\n\nSince there is exactly one gem box, W1 and K1 cannot both be true.\n\nCheck possible values of \\(t\\):\n\n- \\(t = 0\\): B1 would be true, contradiction.\n- \\(t = 1\\): B1 is true; W1, K1, K2 are false. Then gems are not BLACK and not BLUE, so gems are WHITE. Consistent.\n- \\(t = 2\\): B1 and K2 are false, so both W1 and K1 would need to be true, impossible.\n- \\(t = 3\\): B1 is false and K2 is true, so W1 and K1 would both need to be true, impossible.\n- \\(t = 4\\): B1 and K2 are false, so W1 and K1 would both need to be true, impossible.\n\nThus the only solution is gems in WHITE, B1 true, W1 false, K1 false, K2 false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.669, "ast_prob": 0.477, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00484", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT EXACTLY ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n  [K2] THE GEMS ARE NOT IN THIS BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is true because the White box is indeed the middle box. So the Blue box is all-true.\n\nSince exactly one box contains gems, test each possible gem location:\n\n- If gems are in BLUE: W1 is true, K2 is true. Then at least B1, W1, and K2 are true, so K1 must also be true. That leaves no all-false box → invalid.\n- If gems are in BLACK: W1 is true, K2 is false. Then K1 must be true, leaving White all-true and Black mixed, but no all-false box → invalid.\n- If gems are in WHITE: W1 is false, K2 is true. K1 is true because there are not exactly one true statement overall. Then Blue is all-true, Black is all-true, and White is all-false → satisfies all rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.448, "ast_prob": 0.264, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00485", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT MOST TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n  [W2] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS AND THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem be in exactly one box.\n\n- B1 is true exactly when the gem is in BLUE.\n- W2 is true exactly when the gem is not in BLACK.\n- K1 cannot be true: if the gem were in WHITE, WHITE could not be an all-false box because W2 would be true; if the gem were in BLACK, BLACK could not be all-false while K1 itself is true; if the gem were in BLUE, the “boxes next to BLUE” would not contain gems. So K1 = F.\n\nNow test gem locations with K1 = F:\n\n1. Gem = BLUE: B1 = T, W2 = T. Then W1 would be true iff at most 2 statements are true, but with W1 true there are 3 true statements, and with W1 false there are only 2, creating a contradiction. So gem ≠ BLUE.\n\n2. Gem = BLACK: B1 = F, W2 = F. Then W1 is forced true, giving only W1 true. No box is all-true, violating the rules. So gem ≠ BLACK.\n\n3. Gem = WHITE: B1 = F, W2 = T, K1 = F. If W1 were false, exactly 1 statement would be true, making W1 true; contradiction. So W1 = T, giving exactly 2 true statements. Boxes: BLUE all-false, WHITE all-true, BLACK all-false. Rules satisfied.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.723, "ast_prob": 0.499, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00486", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [B2] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Blue box is left, White middle, Black right.\n\n- B1 is true only if the gems are in Blue.\n- B2 and W1 both say “A box next to the Black box contains the gems.” The only box next to Black is White, so both are true only if the gems are in White.\n- K1 says the boxes next to White contain gems. The boxes next to White are Blue and Black. Since exactly one box contains gems, both cannot contain gems, so K1 is always false.\n\nCheck the three possible gem locations:\n\n- If gems in Blue: B1=T, B2=F, W1=F, K1=F → no box is all-true. Invalid.\n- If gems in White: B1=F, B2=T, W1=T, K1=F → White is all-true, Black is all-false. Valid.\n- If gems in Black: B1=F, B2=F, W1=F, K1=F → no box is all-true. Invalid.\n\nTherefore the gems are in White.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.221, "ast_prob": 0.332, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00487", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n  [B2] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Only BLUE has two statements, so the only possible “box with two false statements” is BLUE, and only if both B1 and B2 are false.\n\n- If B1 were true, it would require BLUE to have two false statements, including B1 itself being false — contradiction. So B1 = F.\n- B2 says every box’s top statement is true, but B1 is false, so B2 = F.\n- Thus BLUE has two false statements. Since B1 is false, the gems are not in BLUE.\n- K1 has no statement above it in BLACK box, so K1 = F.\n- Gem cannot be BLACK, because then W1 would be false and all three boxes would be all-false, violating the rule that at least one box is all-true.\n- Therefore gem is WHITE, so W1 = T.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.797, "ast_prob": 0.116, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00488", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n  [W2] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 says both boxes next to WHITE (BLUE and BLACK) contain gems, but there is only one gem, so B1 is always false.  \nThus W1 (“all statements on BLUE are true”) is false.  \nW2 requires B1, W1, and K1 all to be true; since B1 is false, W2 is false.  \nThis makes BLUE all-false and WHITE all-false, so the required all-true box must be BLACK: K1 must be true.  \nK1 is true only if WHITE contains the gem.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.541, "ast_prob": 0.216, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00489", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [K2] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Since Blue is leftmost, the only box next to it is White, so B1 is true exactly when the gems are in White.  \nSince Black is rightmost, the only box next to it is White, so K1 is true exactly when the gems are in White.  \nSince White is the middle box, the “other two boxes” are Blue and Black; with exactly one gem, K2 is true exactly when the gems are in White.  \n\nIf the gems were in Blue or Black, then B1, K1, and K2 would all be false, leaving no all-true box, violating the rules.  \nTherefore the gems must be in White, making B1, K1, and K2 all true. Then Blue and Black are all-true boxes. To satisfy the rule that at least one box is all-false, White must be all-false, so W1 must be false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.272, "ast_prob": 0.361, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00490", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE AND THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [K2] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS AND THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location be one of BLUE, WHITE, or BLACK.\n\n- B1 says the boxes next to BLUE contain gems. The only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so B1 is true iff WHITE has the gems.\n- K1 says WHITE contains the gems, so K1 is true iff WHITE has the gems.\n- W1 requires both B1 and K1 to be true, which would force WHITE to have the gems, but it also requires the other two boxes relative to BLACK to be empty, which would force BLACK to have the gems. That is contradictory, so W1 must be false.\n- WHITE is therefore an all-false box. To have at least one all-true box, the gems must be in WHITE, making B1 true and BLUE all-true.\n- With gems in WHITE, K1 is true. K2 says “the box with no false statements has the gems.” The no-false box is BLUE, but BLUE does not contain the gems, so K2 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.373, "ast_prob": 0.347, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00491", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS BLACK\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n  [W2] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS AND THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is on the BLUE box, so “this box is black” is false. Thus the BLUE box is all-false.\n\nK1 says the BLACK box both contains the gems and is empty, which is impossible, so K1 is false. Thus the BLACK box is also all-false.\n\nSince at least one box must be all-true, and BLUE and BLACK cannot be, the WHITE box must be all-true. Therefore W1 is true and W2 is true.\n\nW2 says the other two boxes are empty, so the gems must be in the WHITE box.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.206, "ast_prob": 0.361, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00492", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX HAS EXACTLY ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLACK\n  [K2] AT LEAST THREE STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is false because the middle box is actually WHITE, not BLACK.\n\n- With K1 = F, B1 (“black box has exactly one true statement”) is true only if K2 is true, so B1 = K2.\n- If K2 were true, then B1 = T and K2 = T would require W1 = T to make at least three true statements. Then BLUE and WHITE would be all-true, and BLACK would be mixed, leaving no all-false box. This violates the rules.\n- Therefore K2 = F, so B1 = F.\n- With BLUE all-false and BLACK all-false, at least one all-true box must be WHITE, so W1 = T.\n- W1 = T means the gems are in the WHITE box.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.314, "ast_prob": 0.47, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00493", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] AT MOST THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT LEAST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n  [K2] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- W1 = “White box is empty” = gems are **not** in WHITE.\n- K1 = “White box has at least one true statement” = W1 is true, because WHITE contains only W1.\n- K2 = “Blue box is empty” = gems are **not** in BLUE.\n- B1 = “At most three statements are false.” For B1 to be false, all four statements would have to be false. But W1 false means gems are in WHITE, while K2 false means gems are in BLUE — impossible. So B1 must be TRUE.\n\nNow test gem locations:\n\n- If gems are in BLUE: W1=T, K1=T, K2=F, B1=T → no all-false box, invalid.\n- If gems are in BLACK: W1=T, K1=T, K2=T, B1=T → all boxes all-true, no all-false box, invalid.\n- If gems are in WHITE: W1=F, K1=F, K2=T, B1=T → BLUE all-true, WHITE all-false, BLACK mixed → valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.564, "ast_prob": 0.463, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00494", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n  [B2] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS BLUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n\n1. The BLACK box is on the right, so the only box next to it is the WHITE box.  \n   Thus B2 means “the WHITE box contains the gems.”\n\n2. W1 says “the BLACK box is BLUE.” The black box is black, not blue, so W1 is always FALSE.\n\n3. Test each possible gem location:\n\n- If gems are in BLUE:  \n  B1 = FALSE, B2 = FALSE, W1 = FALSE, K1 = FALSE.  \n  No box is all-true, so invalid.\n\n- If gems are in WHITE:  \n  B1 = TRUE, B2 = TRUE, W1 = FALSE, K1 = TRUE.  \n  BLUE is all-true, WHITE is all-false, BLACK is all-true. Valid.\n\n- If gems are in BLACK:  \n  B1 = TRUE, B2 = FALSE, W1 = FALSE, K1 = FALSE.  \n  No box is all-true, so invalid.\n\n4. Therefore gems are in WHITE, with B1 true, B2 true, W1 false, K1 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.317, "ast_prob": 0.356, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00495", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] AT MOST THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n  [B2] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let P be the proposition “the White box contains the gems.”  \nB2 and W1 both say the same thing: the other two boxes relative to White are empty, so both are equivalent to P.\n\nK1 says “the Black box is the middle box,” but Black is the right box, so K1 is always False.\n\nIf the gems were not in White, then B2 = W1 = False and K1 = False. Then B1 would have to be True, otherwise all four statements would be False, making “at most three statements are false” True. That would give no all-True box, violating the rule.\n\nSo the gems must be in White. Then B2 = True and W1 = True. With K1 = False, B1 must also be True, since otherwise there would be at most two false statements, contradicting B1 being False.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.234, "ast_prob": 0.381, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00496", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n  [B2] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS WHITE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Only BLUE can be “the box with two false statements,” because only BLUE has two statements. So B1 is true only if B1 and B2 are both false and the gems are in BLUE, which is self-contradictory for gem=BLUE.\n\nB2 and W1 both say the boxes other than WHITE are empty, so both are true exactly when gems are in WHITE. K1 says the BLACK box is white, so K1 is false.\n\nTesting gem locations:\n- gem=BLUE: impossible because B1 would have to equal its own negation.\n- gem=BLACK: all statements false, violating the requirement that at least one box is all-true.\n- gem=WHITE: BLUE is mixed, WHITE is all-true, BLACK is all-false. This satisfies every rule.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.725, "ast_prob": 0.337, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00497", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n  [B2] AT MOST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THIS BOX ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(G\\) be the box with the gems.\n\n- \\(W1\\) is true iff \\(G=\\text{WHITE}\\), because “the other two boxes relative to the white box” are BLUE and BLACK.\n- \\(B1\\) says that \\(B1\\), \\(W1\\), and \\(K1\\) are all true. So if \\(B1\\) is true, then \\(W1\\) and \\(K1\\) must be true.\n- \\(B2\\) says at most one statement total is true.\n\nAssume \\(B2\\) is true. Then since \\(B2\\) itself is true, all other statements must be false: \\(B1=F\\), \\(W1=F\\), \\(K1=F\\). Then WHITE and BLACK are both all-false, and BLUE is mixed, so there is no all-true box. Contradiction.  \nThus \\(B2=F\\).\n\nSince \\(B2\\) is false, more than one statement is true. Because \\(B2\\) itself contributes no truth, at least two of \\(B1,W1,K1\\) must be true.\n\nIf \\(B1\\) were true, then \\(B1\\) would force \\(W1=T\\) and \\(K1=T\\), giving WHITE all-true and BLACK all-true, but no all-false box. Contradiction.  \nThus \\(B1=F\\).\n\nSo with \\(B1=F\\) and \\(B2=F\\), the only way to have at least two of \\(B1,W1,K1\\) true is \\(W1=T\\) and \\(K1=T\\).\n\nTherefore \\(W1=T\\) means the gems are in WHITE.\n\nCheck:\n- BLUE: \\(B1=F\\), \\(B2=F\\) → all-false.\n- WHITE: \\(W1=T\\) → all-true.\n- BLACK: \\(K1=T\\) → all-true.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; B2=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.559, "ast_prob": 0.48, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00499", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE AND THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [W2] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY AND A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is false because the Blue box is left, not middle.  \nTherefore W2 (“not true that W1 is true”) is true, so the White box is mixed.  \nThus the Blue and Black boxes must be one all-true and one all-false, so B1 and K1 must have opposite truth values.  \nB1 references “the statement above this one,” but there is no statement above it, making that part false; therefore B1 is false.  \nSo K1 must be true. K1 is true exactly when the Black box is empty and the adjacent White box contains the gems, meaning the gems are in White.  \nFinal check: Blue all-false, White mixed, Black all-true, gems in White.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=F; W1=F; W2=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.517, "ast_prob": 0.378, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00500", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n  [B2] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS WHITE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step 1: B2 (“THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX”) is always false, because the blue box is the left box.  \nStep 2: K1 (“THE BLACK BOX IS WHITE”) is always false, because the black box is the right box.  \nStep 3: Since B2 is false, the blue box cannot be all-true. Since K1 is false, the black box cannot be all-true. Therefore, the only box that can be all-true is the white box.  \nStep 4: The rules require at least one box to have all statements true, so the white box must be all-true. Thus W1 must be true.  \nStep 5: W1 says “THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS.” The only all-true box is the white box, so the white box must contain the gems.  \nStep 6: With gems in the white box, the blue box is empty, so B1 is true. B2 is false, W1 is true, and K1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "WHITE", "relational_ratio": 0.691, "ast_prob": 0.248, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00501", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLACK BOX ARE TRUE\n  [B2] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Positions: BLUE=left, WHITE=middle, BLACK=right.\n\n- K1 says “This box is the middle box.” BLACK is right, so K1 is FALSE.\n- B1 says all statements on BLACK are true. Since BLACK has only K1 and K1 is false, B1 is FALSE.\n- W1 says the other two boxes relative to BLACK are empty. Those are BLUE and WHITE, so W1 is true exactly when the gem is in BLACK.\n- B2 says a box next to BLUE is true. The only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so B2 is true exactly when W1 is true.\n\nFor at least one box to be all-true, it must be WHITE, because BLUE has B1 false and BLACK has K1 false. Therefore W1 must be TRUE, meaning the gem is in BLACK.\n\nThen:\n- W1 = TRUE\n- B2 = TRUE\n- B1 = FALSE\n- K1 = FALSE\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.314, "ast_prob": 0.284, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00502", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n  [B2] AT MOST TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is always true because the White box is indeed the middle box, so the White box is all-true.\n\nK1 is true exactly when the gems are in the Blue box, since it says the other two boxes relative to Blue are empty.\n\nLet B2 be true iff the total number of true statements is at most 2. With W1 already true:\n- If B2 is true, then B1 and K1 must both be false.\n- If B2 is false, then B1 and K1 must both be true.\n\nSo the only possible truth-value pairs are:\n1. B1=F, B2=T, K1=F\n2. B1=T, B2=F, K1=T\n\nTry the second: K1=T means gems are in Blue. But B2=F means the Blue box is not all-true, so B1 saying “the gems are in a box that is true” would be false, contradicting B1=T. So this case is impossible.\n\nThus the solution is B1=F, B2=T, K1=F. Since K1=F, the gems are not in Blue. Since B1=F, the gems are not in an all-true box; White is all-true, so the gems must be in Black.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.428, "ast_prob": 0.122, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00503", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE AND THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n  [W2] THE BLACK BOX IS WHITE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT MOST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. W2 says “The black box is white,” which is false; the black box is black. So W2 = F.\n2. Because W2 is false, the WHITE box can never be an all-true box.\n3. Therefore, the required all-true box must be BLUE or BLACK. So either B1 is true or K1 is true.\n4. K1 says “At most two statements are false.” If K1 were false, then at least three statements would be false, forcing B1 and W1 both false as well; then no box would be all-true. So K1 must be true.\n5. With K1 true, at most one of B1 and W1 can be false, so at least one of B1 or W1 is true.\n6. The rule requiring at least one all-false box means either BLUE is all-false (B1 = F) or WHITE is all-false (W1 = F and W2 = F). Since K1 is true, BLACK is not all-false. Thus B1 = F or W1 = F.\n7. Combining “at least one of B1/W1 is true” with “at least one of B1/W1 is false” gives exactly one of B1 and W1 true.\n\nCase A: B1 = T, W1 = F.  \nB1 says the black box is true and contains the gems. Since K1 is true, the black box is true; therefore the gems must be in BLACK. Then BLUE and BLACK are both all-true, WHITE is all-false, and W1 is false because there is no unique “box with no false statements” containing the gems. Consistent.\n\nCase B: B1 = F, W1 = T.  \nW1 would require the unique no-false box to hold the gems. Since BLUE has B1 false and WHITE has W2 false, only BLACK could be no-false, so gems would be in BLACK. But then B1 = (K1 true and gems in BLACK) = true, contradicting B1 = F. Invalid.\n\nThus the unique solution is gem in BLACK, B1 true, W1 false, W2 false, K1 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.484, "ast_prob": 0.278, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00504", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [W2] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "A \"false box\" here means a box whose statements are not all true (it has at least one false statement).\n\n- White: W1 is true only if gems are in WHITE; W2 is true only if gems are in BLACK. So W1 and W2 cannot both be true; WHITE cannot be all-true.\n- Black: K1 = “Black is empty,” so K1 is true iff gems are not in BLACK.\n- Blue: B1 says the gem box is false.\n\nIf gems were in BLUE, B1 would say BLUE is false, creating a contradiction: B1 true would make BLUE all-true, and B1 false would make BLUE false but then B1 would be true. So gems are not BLUE.\n\nIf gems were in WHITE, then W1=true, W2=false, so WHITE has a false statement, making B1=true. Then BLUE would be all-true, and BLACK would be empty so K1=true, making BLACK all-true. No box would be all-false, violating the rules. So gems are not WHITE.\n\nTherefore gems are in BLACK. Then K1=false, W2=true, W1=false, and B1=true because BLACK is false/all-false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.264, "ast_prob": 0.448, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00505", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS AND A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE OR THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\n  [K2] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS AND A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n\n- Blue box true = B1 is true.\n- White box true = W1 is true.\n- Black box true = K1 and K2 are both true.\n- Boxes next to Blue = only White.\n- Boxes next to Black = only White.\n\nStatements become:\n\n- B1 = “the gem box is all-true AND White is true”\n- W1 = “Blue is true OR Black has the gems”\n- K1 = “White is true”\n- K2 = “White contains the gems”\n\nCheck gem cases:\n\n1. If gems are in BLUE: K2 = F, W1 = B1, and B1 = B1 ∧ W1 forces B1 = W1. At least one all-true box forces B1 = W1 = T, but then there is no all-false box. Invalid.\n\n2. If gems are in WHITE: K2 = T, B1 = W1, and W1 = B1. At least one all-true box forces B1 = W1 = T, but then there is no all-false box. Invalid.\n\n3. If gems are in BLACK: K2 = F, W1 = “Blue true OR Black has gems” = T, so B1 = F ∧ T = F, and K1 = W1 = T.  \n   - Blue: B1 = F, all-false.  \n   - White: W1 = T, all-true.  \n   - Black: K1 = T, K2 = F, mixed.  \n   Valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; K1=T; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.674, "ast_prob": 0.427, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00506", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX HAS AT LEAST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n  [K2] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K2 is always false because the blue box is on the left, not the middle.\n\nB1 says the black box has at least one true statement, so B1 = K1 or K2 = K1.\n\nW1 says the two non-black boxes are empty, so W1 is true only if the gems are in the black box.\n\nK1 says all top statements are true: K1 = B1 and W1 and K1 = K1 and W1.\n\n- If gems are in blue or white, then W1 is false, so K1 and B1 are false, making every box all-false — no all-true box exists.\n- If gems are in black, then W1 is true. If K1 were true, then B1 would be true, giving no all-false box. So K1 must be false, and B1 must be false.\n\nThus the only valid solution is:\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.37, "ast_prob": 0.187, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00507", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [B2] THIS BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because the BLUE box is on the left, not in the middle.  \nB2 says the BLUE box itself is true, but since B1 is false, the BLUE box is not all-true, so B2 is false.  \nK1 says the top statements of all boxes are true; this would require B1 to be true, but B1 is false, so K1 is false.  \nThe WHITE box must be the all-true box, so W1 is true.  \nW1 being true means the other two boxes relative to BLACK are empty, so the gems are in the BLACK box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; B2=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.466, "ast_prob": 0.436, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00508", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE AND A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX HAS EXACTLY TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n  [K2] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE AND THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(b,w,k_1,k_2\\) be the truth values of B1, W1, K1, K2.\n\n- WHITE has only W1, so B1 is true exactly when W1 is true: \\(b = w\\).\n- If \\(w\\) were false, then B1 would also be false, so BLUE and WHITE would both be all-false. To satisfy “at least one all-true box,” BLACK would have to be all-true, requiring K2 true. But K2 says the top statement of WHITE, W1, is true, contradiction. So \\(w = T\\), hence \\(b = T\\).\n- Since W1 is true, both parts of W1 must be true. BLUE and WHITE are true boxes, so the gems must be in a false box. Therefore the gems must be in BLACK, and BLACK must be all-false: \\(k_1 = F\\), \\(k_2 = F\\).\n- This is consistent: BLACK has zero true statements, so K1 “exactly two true statements” is false; K2 is false because K1 is false and BLACK is not a true box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.781, "ast_prob": 0.276, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00509", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n  [W2] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n1. The boxes are in order: BLUE – WHITE – BLACK. So the only box next to BLUE is WHITE.\n2. W1 says the two boxes other than BLUE (WHITE and BLACK) are empty, so W1 is true exactly when the gems are in BLUE.\n3. W2 says a box next to BLUE contains the gems, meaning WHITE contains the gems, so W2 is true exactly when the gems are in WHITE.\n4. Therefore WHITE cannot be all-true, because W1 and W2 cannot both be true.\n5. B1 says a box next to BLUE is true, i.e. WHITE is true. Since WHITE is never all-true, B1 is false. Thus BLUE is an all-false box.\n6. There must be at least one all-true box. WHITE cannot be all-true and BLUE is all-false, so BLACK must be all-true. Thus K1 is true.\n7. K1 says the gems are in a true box. The only true box is BLACK, so the gems are in BLACK.\n8. With gems in BLACK: W1 is false, W2 is false, so WHITE is all-false. This satisfies the rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.482, "ast_prob": 0.146, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00510", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EXACTLY ONE STATEMENT IS FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [W2] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(W1\\) mean “WHITE contains gems.” Since exactly one box contains gems, \\(W2\\) (“the other two boxes are empty”) is true exactly when WHITE contains gems. So \\(W1 = W2\\).\n\nCheck possible gem locations:\n\n1. **Gems in WHITE**: \\(W1 = W2 = T\\). Then \\(K1\\) must be true because the gems are in WHITE, and WHITE is all-true. So statements would be \\(B1, T, T, T\\).  \n   - If \\(B1=T\\), there are 0 false statements, contradicting B1.  \n   - If \\(B1=F\\), there is exactly 1 false statement, so B1 would be true.  \n   Impossible.\n\n2. **Gems in BLUE**: \\(W1 = W2 = F\\). Then B1 says “exactly one statement is false,” but W1 and W2 are already false, so B1 must be false. Then \\(K1\\) is also false, because BLUE is not a true box. That makes all three boxes all-false, violating the rule that at least one box is all-true. Impossible.\n\n3. **Gems in BLACK**: \\(W1 = W2 = F\\). Again W1 and W2 are already false, so B1 is false. BLUE and WHITE are therefore all-false. To satisfy the rule that at least one box is all-true, BLACK must be all-true, so \\(K1 = T\\). This is consistent: gems are in BLACK, and BLACK is true.\n\nFinal truth values: B1 false, W1 false, W2 false, K1 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.48, "ast_prob": 0.302, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00511", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS BLACK\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [K2] THIS BOX HAS AT LEAST ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is true because the black box is indeed black, so the BLUE box is all-true.\n\nK2 says “this box has at least one false statement.” K2 cannot be false, because then it would be true, a contradiction. So K2 is true. For K2 to be true, K1 must be false. Thus BLACK is a mixed box.\n\nSince BLUE is all-true and BLACK is mixed, the required all-false box must be WHITE. Therefore W1 is false.\n\nW1 says the gems are in a box that is true. Since W1 is false, the gems are not in a true box. The only true box is BLUE, so gems are not in BLUE.\n\nK1 says a box next to BLUE contains the gems, meaning WHITE contains the gems. K1 is false, so gems are not in WHITE.\n\nBy elimination, the gems are in BLACK.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.679, "ast_prob": 0.172, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00512", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [B2] EITHER A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS OR A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Adjacency:\n- Next to BLACK = WHITE only\n- Next to WHITE = BLUE or BLACK\n- Next to BLUE = WHITE only\n\nStatements:\n- B1 = (BLACK has gems)\n- B2 = (WHITE has gems) OR (BLUE or BLACK has gems) → always true since exactly one box has gems\n- W1 = (WHITE has gems)\n- K1 = (WHITE has gems)\n\nTest gem locations:\n- If gem = BLUE: no all-true box → invalid\n- If gem = WHITE: no all-false box → invalid\n- If gem = BLACK: BLUE is all-true, WHITE is all-false, BLACK is all-false → valid\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.379, "ast_prob": 0.454, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00513", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [B2] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because the Blue box is on the left, not the middle.  \nB2 and W1 both depend only on whether the gems are in White, since White is the only box next to Blue.  \n\n- If gems are in Blue: B2=false, W1=false, K1=false → no all-true box, invalid.  \n- If gems are in White: B2=true, W1=true, K1=true → no all-false box, invalid.  \n- If gems are in Black: B2=false, W1=false → Blue and White are all-false. K1 must be true so Black is all-true, satisfying the all-true requirement.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; B2=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.532, "ast_prob": 0.129, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00514", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n  [W2] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT MOST TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT LEAST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W2 is always true because the White box has exactly two statements, so it cannot have more than two false statements.  \nSince W2 is true, K1 (“at least one statement is true”) is also true, making Black an all-true box.  \nB1 is false because no box can have two false statements: White cannot because W2 is true, and Blue/Black each have only one statement. So Blue is all-false.  \nThe White box must be the remaining mixed box, so W1 is false. Then the gems are not in a box that is false; the only non-false box is Black.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; W2=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.779, "ast_prob": 0.263, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00515", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n  [K2] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(B1\\) mean “gems are in Black.” Then \\(K1\\) (“Black is empty”) is the negation of \\(B1\\), so \\(B1\\) and \\(K1\\) cannot both be true.\n\n\\(W1\\) says that the top statement of each box is true: \\(W1 = B1 \\land W1 \\land K1\\). Since \\(B1 \\land K1\\) is always false, \\(W1\\) must be false.\n\n\\(K2\\) says a box next to Blue is true. The only box next to Blue is White, so \\(K2 = W1 = F\\).\n\nNow:\n- White cannot be all-true because \\(W1=F\\).\n- Black cannot be all-true because \\(K2=F\\).\n- Therefore Blue must be the all-true box, so \\(B1=T\\), meaning the gems are in Black.\n\nThen \\(K1=F\\), and all-false boxes are satisfied: White has \\(W1=F\\), Black has \\(K1=F\\) and \\(K2=F\\).\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.433, "ast_prob": 0.33, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00516", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [B2] EXACTLY ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let G be the gem location.\n\n- B1 says “BLACK contains the gems,” so B1 is true iff G = BLACK.\n- W1 says the two non-BLACK boxes are empty, which also means BLACK contains the gems, so W1 is true iff G = BLACK.\n- Thus B1 and W1 have the same truth value.\n\nIf G were BLUE or WHITE, then B1, W1, and K1 would all be false. Checking B2:\n- If B2 were true, BLUE would be mixed but no box would be all-true.\n- If B2 were false, all boxes would be all-false, again no all-true box.\nSo G cannot be BLUE or WHITE.\n\nTherefore G = BLACK, so B1 = T and W1 = T.\n\nNow evaluate B2 and K1:\n- If K1 = T, then with B1 and W1 true, B2 must be false; boxes would be: BLUE mixed, WHITE all-true, BLACK all-true → no all-false box. Invalid.\n- If K1 = F, then B2 must also be false; boxes would be: BLUE mixed, WHITE all-true, BLACK all-false. This satisfies the rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; B2=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.604, "ast_prob": 0.427, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00517", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n  [B2] AT MOST THREE STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EXACTLY TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is false because the blue box is left, not middle, so White is all-false. Since W1 is false, not all 4 statements can be true, so B2 is true. Interpreting “a false box” as a box that is not all-true:\n\n- Gems cannot be in Blue: if B1 were true, Blue would be all-true, making B1 false; if B1 were false, Blue would be false, making B1 true.\n- Gems cannot be in White: B1 would be true, but then K1 has no consistent truth value.\n- Therefore gems are in Black. If K1 were false, Black would be all-false, making B1 true and total true statements 2, which would make K1 true—contradiction. So K1 is true, Black is all-true, and B1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; B2=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.428, "ast_prob": 0.142, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00518", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EXACTLY TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER THE WHITE BOX HAS AT LEAST ONE FALSE STATEMENT OR THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT AT MOST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n  [K2] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K2 is on the BLACK box, which is the right box, not the middle box, so K2 is false.\n\nLet F be the number of false statements among all four statements.  \n- B1 is true iff F = 2.  \n- K1 is true iff F ≥ 3.  \n\nFor W1: the WHITE box contains only W1, so “the WHITE box has at least one false statement” is equivalent to “W1 is false.”  \nSo W1 = (“W1 is false” OR “gems are in a false box”).  \nIf W1 were false, the right side would be true, contradiction. Therefore W1 must be true, and the gems must be in a box whose statements are all false.\n\nWith W1 true and K2 false, the only consistent assignment is:\n- B1 true (so exactly two statements are false)\n- K1 false (so it is not the case that more than two statements are false)\n\nThus the statements are:\n- B1 = T\n- W1 = T\n- K1 = F\n- K2 = F\n\nBLUE is all-true, WHITE is all-true, BLACK is all-false. Since the gems must be in a false box, they are in BLACK.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.643, "ast_prob": 0.393, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00519", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n  [W2] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT LEAST THREE STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is the top statement in the WHITE box, so there is no statement above it. Thus W1 is false.  \nSince W1 is false, W2 (“the top statement of each box is true”) is also false.  \nWith W1 and W2 false, at most B1 and K1 could be true, so K1 (“at least three statements are true”) must be false.  \nTo satisfy the rule that at least one box is all-true, the BLUE box must be all-true, so B1 must be true.  \nTherefore the BLACK box contains the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.625, "ast_prob": 0.275, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00520", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n  [W2] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gems be in one of: Blue, White, Black.\n\nStatements simplify:\n- B1 = “Blue is empty” = not (gems in Blue)\n- W1 = “Blue is empty” = not (gems in Blue)\n- W2 = “The other two boxes relative to White are empty” = Blue and Black are empty = gems in White\n- K1 = “A box next to Blue contains the gems” = White contains the gems\n\nCheck each possible gem location:\n\n1. Gems in Blue:\n   - B1 = False\n   - W1 = False\n   - W2 = False\n   - K1 = False\n   No box is all-true. Invalid.\n\n2. Gems in White:\n   - B1 = True\n   - W1 = True\n   - W2 = True\n   - K1 = True\n   No box is all-false. Invalid.\n\n3. Gems in Black:\n   - B1 = True\n   - W1 = True\n   - W2 = False\n   - K1 = False\n   Blue is all-true, Black is all-false, White is mixed. Valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; W2=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.203, "ast_prob": 0.199, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00521", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EXACTLY TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE\n  [W2] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLACK BOX ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the four statements be B1, W1, W2, K1.\n\n- W2 is true exactly when K1 is true, because W2 says all Black statements are true, and Black has only K1.\n- K1 is true exactly when the gems are in the BLACK box.\n- W1 says both White statements are true, so if W2 is false then W1 must be false; if W2 is true, W1 may be true or false consistently.\n- B1 says exactly two statements overall are false.\n\nCheck gem locations:\n\n- If gems are NOT in BLACK: K1 = F, so W2 = F, so W1 = F. Then W1, W2, K1 are all false regardless of B1, meaning B1 cannot be true either. So all boxes would be all-false, violating the requirement that at least one box is all-true. Invalid.\n\n- If gems ARE in BLACK: K1 = T, so W2 = T. If W1 were false, then with W2 and K1 true, B1 would be false if it were true, and true if it were false — contradiction. So W1 must be true. Then W1, W2, K1 are all true, leaving only B1 false. This satisfies exactly one false count? Actually total false = 1, so B1 saying “exactly two statements are false” is false. Valid.\n\nThus the gems are in BLACK; B1 is false; W1, W2, K1 are true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.67, "ast_prob": 0.149, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00522", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE BLACK BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n  [B2] EITHER A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE OR A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX IS BLUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS NOT EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is true because the black box has only one statement, so it cannot have at least two true statements.  \nW1 is false because the white box is not blue.  \nB2 says either the box next to blue or the box next to black is true, but both refer to the white box, and the white box is false, so B2 is false.  \nThus blue is mixed, and white is all-false.  \nTo satisfy “at least one box all true,” the black box must be all-true, so K1 must be true, meaning the gems are in the black box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; B2=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.579, "ast_prob": 0.27, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00523", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EITHER THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE OR THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [W2] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT LEAST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W1 is false because the black box is on the right, not in the middle.\n\nIf W2 were true, then White would have only one false statement, so no box would have two false statements, making W2 false. Therefore W2 must be false. So White has two false statements, meaning the box with two false statements is White. Since W2 is false, that box does not contain the gems, so gems are not in White.\n\nBecause W1 and W2 are both false, at least two statements are false, so K1 is true.\n\nFor B1: if the gems were in Blue, then “this box is empty” would be false, and “gems are in a false box” would require Blue itself to be all-false, i.e. B1 false. That would make B1 true iff it is false, impossible. So gems are not in Blue.\n\nThus gems are in Black. Blue is empty, so B1 is true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.714, "ast_prob": 0.164, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00524", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS WHITE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n  [K2] THE BLUE BOX IS BLUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is on the BLUE box and says “THIS BOX IS WHITE,” but the BLUE box is blue, so B1 is FALSE.\n\nW1 refers to the statement immediately above it, B1. Since B1 is false, W1 is FALSE.\n\nK2 says “THE BLUE BOX IS BLUE,” which is TRUE.\n\nSo BLUE is all-false and WHITE is all-false. Since at least one box must be all-true, BLACK must be all-true. Therefore K1 must be TRUE.\n\nWith K1 true, the box with no false statements is BLACK, so BLACK has the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.298, "ast_prob": 0.474, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00525", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY AND THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [B2] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n- “Other two boxes are empty” means the box itself contains the gems, since exactly one box has gems.\n- BLUE is left, WHITE is middle, BLACK is right.\n\nEvaluating statements:\n\n- B1: “The other two boxes are empty AND the blue box is the middle box.” Since BLUE is left, not middle, B1 is always FALSE.\n- B2: “It is not true that the other two boxes are empty.” This is TRUE iff BLUE does NOT contain the gems.\n- W1: “It is not true that the WHITE box is the middle box.” Since WHITE is middle, W1 is FALSE.\n- K1: “This box contains the gems.” TRUE iff BLACK contains the gems.\n\nCheck possible gem locations:\n\n1. If gems are in BLUE:  \n   B1=F, B2=F, W1=F, K1=F → no all-true box. Invalid.\n\n2. If gems are in WHITE:  \n   B1=F, B2=T, W1=F, K1=F → no all-true box. Invalid.\n\n3. If gems are in BLACK:  \n   B1=F, B2=T, W1=F, K1=T → BLUE mixed, WHITE all-false, BLACK all-true. Valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; B2=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.467, "ast_prob": 0.287, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00526", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [B2] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let G be the gem location.\n\n- B1 is true iff Blue has gems.\n- B2 (“the other two boxes are empty”) is also true iff Blue has gems, since exactly one box contains gems.\n- W1 is true iff Blue has gems.\n- K1 is true iff the box containing the gems is an all-true box.\n\nCase Blue has gems:  \nB1=T, B2=T, W1=T. Blue is all-true, so K1=T too. All boxes are all-true — no all-false box exists. Invalid.\n\nCase White has gems:  \nB1=F, B2=F, W1=F. Blue and White are all-false. The gem box is White, which is not all-true, so K1=F. All boxes are all-false — no all-true box exists. Invalid.\n\nCase Black has gems:  \nB1=F, B2=F, W1=F. Blue and White are all-false. For Black to be all-true, K1 must be T: Black has gems and Black is all-true, so K1=T. This satisfies at least one all-true box (Black) and at least one all-false box (Blue, White). Valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; B2=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.236, "ast_prob": 0.415, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00527", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [K2] AT LEAST THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n1. By layout, WHITE is the middle box, so W1 is true.\n2. BLACK is the right box, not the middle box, so K1 is false.\n3. Suppose K2 were true. Then there would need to be at least 3 false statements. But with K2 true, the only possible false statements are B1 and K1, at most 2. So K2 cannot be true; therefore K2 is false.\n4. With K2 false, there must be fewer than 3 false statements overall. K1 is false and K2 is false, so B1 must be true to keep the total false count at exactly 2.\n5. Since B1 is true, the BLACK box contains the gems.\n\nFinal statement values:  \nB1 = T, W1 = T, K1 = F, K2 = F.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.472, "ast_prob": 0.138, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00528", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [K2] EXACTLY ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(T\\) be the number of true statements among B1, W1, K1, K2.  \nK2 is true exactly when \\(T=1\\).\n\n- K1 says both boxes next to White contain gems. But only one box contains gems, so K1 is always false.\n- The only box with two statements is Black, so “the box with two false statements” refers to Black. Thus B1 is true only if Black has both statements false and Black contains the gems.\n\nCheck gem locations:\n\n1. **Gem in BLUE**: W1 false, B1 false, K1 false.  \n   - If K2 true, then \\(T=1\\), so K2 is consistent, but no box is all-true. Invalid.  \n   - If K2 false, all boxes are all-false, so no box is all-true. Invalid.\n\n2. **Gem in WHITE**: W1 true, B1 false, K1 false.  \n   - If K2 true, then \\(T=2\\), contradiction.  \n   - If K2 false, then \\(T=1\\), contradiction.\n\n3. **Gem in BLACK**: W1 true, K1 false.  \n   - If K2 true, then B1 false, so \\(T=2\\), contradiction.  \n   - If K2 false, then B1 true, so \\(T=2\\), consistent with K2 false.  \n   Boxes: Blue all true, White all true, Black all false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.511, "ast_prob": 0.381, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00529", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [B2] EITHER EXACTLY TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE OR BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS OR A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is true because the White box is the middle box.  \nK1 depends only on whether the White box is “true”; since White has only W1, K1 has the same truth value as W1.  \nBlue cannot be the all-false box because B1 is true, so the required all-false box must be White/Black. Thus W1 must be false, and K1 is also false.  \nWith W1 false, White and Black are not all-true, so Blue must be the all-true box; therefore B2 must be true.  \nNow B1 and B2 are true, W1 and K1 are false: exactly two statements are true, so B2’s first disjunct is true.  \nFor W1 to be false, neither disjunct can hold: the all-true box (Blue) must not have the gems, and the box next to Black (White) must not have the gems. Therefore the gems must be in Black.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.571, "ast_prob": 0.243, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00530", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [B2] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] AT LEAST TWO STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "A “false box” is treated as a box that has at least one false statement; a “true box” has all statements true.\n\n- If gems were in WHITE, then B1=False and B2=False, so WHITE would have to make W1 self-referential: W1 would say “gems are in a false box,” but WHITE would be false only if W1 is false. That is a paradox.\n- If gems were in BLUE, then B1=False and B2=True. BLUE would contain a false statement, so W1=True. Then false statements so far are only B1. If K1=True, there is only 1 false statement, contradiction. If K1=False, there are 2 false statements, making K1 true, contradiction.\n- Therefore gems must be in BLACK. Then B1=True, B2=False. Let K1=True, so BLACK is all-true and not false, making W1=False. The false statements are B2 and W1: exactly 2, so K1=True is consistent.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; B2=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.411, "ast_prob": 0.245, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00531", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n  [K2] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "We label the boxes by position: BLUE=left, WHITE=middle, BLACK=right.\n\n- K2 says “This box is the middle box.” The BLACK box is on the right, so K2 is always FALSE.\n- B1 says “The BLACK box contains the gems,” so B1 is TRUE only if gems are in BLACK.\n- K1 says “The WHITE box is empty,” so K1 is TRUE only if gems are not in WHITE.\n- W1 says “The top statement of each box is true,” i.e. B1 ∧ W1 ∧ K1. Since W1 is self-referential:\n  - W1 can be TRUE only if B1 and K1 are both TRUE.\n  - W1 can be FALSE in all other cases.\n\nNow test gem locations:\n\n1. Gems in BLUE:\n   - B1 = F, K1 = T, K2 = F.\n   - W1 = F because B1 = F.\n   - BLUE all false, WHITE all false, BLACK mixed.\n   - No all-true box → invalid.\n\n2. Gems in WHITE:\n   - B1 = F, K1 = F, K2 = F.\n   - W1 = F.\n   - BLUE all false, WHITE all false, BLACK all false.\n   - No all-true box → invalid.\n\n3. Gems in BLACK:\n   - B1 = T, K1 = T, K2 = F.\n   - W1 could be T or F. If W1 = T, there is no all-false box. If W1 = F, then:\n     - BLUE all true.\n     - WHITE all false.\n     - BLACK mixed.\n   - This satisfies both “at least one all-true” and “at least one all-false.”\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.324, "ast_prob": 0.464, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00532", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n  [B2] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Since BLUE is the leftmost box, the only box next to it is WHITE. So K1 means: “WHITE contains gems.”\n\n- If the gems were in BLUE, then B1, B2, W1, and K1 would all be false → no all-true box.\n- If the gems were in WHITE, then B1, B2, W1, and K1 would all be true → no all-false box.\n- Therefore the gems must be in BLACK.\n  - BLUE is empty → B1 = T, B2 = T.\n  - WHITE is empty → W1 = T.\n  - WHITE does not contain gems → K1 = F.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.337, "ast_prob": 0.259, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00533", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n  [W2] EITHER THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS OR A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem locations be evaluated against the statements.\n\n- B1 says: “A box next to the BLUE box is TRUE.” The only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so B1 means WHITE box is all-true.\n- W1 says: “The other two boxes relative to the BLACK box are EMPTY.” Those are BLUE and WHITE, so W1 means the gems are in BLACK.\n- W2 says: “Either WHITE contains the gems or a box next to BLACK contains the gems.” The only box next to BLACK is WHITE, so W2 means the gems are in WHITE.\n- Therefore W1 and W2 cannot both be true. So WHITE is never all-true, meaning B1 is false. Thus BLUE is all-false.\n\nTest gem locations:\n- If gems are in BLUE: W1=F, W2=F, B1=F, and no true box contains gems, so K1=F. No box is all-true → invalid.\n- If gems are in WHITE: W1=F, W2=T, B1=F, and WHITE is not all-true, so K1=F. No box is all-true → invalid.\n- If gems are in BLACK: W1=T, W2=F, B1=F. For K1 to be true, BLACK must be an all-true box containing the gems, which is consistent. If K1 were false, no box would be all-true, violating the rules. So K1=T.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.528, "ast_prob": 0.41, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00534", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS BLACK\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT EXACTLY TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n  [K2] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY AND A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n1. B1 says “The blue box is black,” but the blue box is blue, so **B1 = F**.\n2. If the gems were in Blue, then W1 would be true and K2 false, but K1 would then be impossible: K1 true would make exactly two statements true, and K1 false would make exactly one true. So gems are not in Blue.\n3. If the gems were in White, W1 would be self-defeating: White would be false exactly when W1 is false, making W1 true, and vice versa. So gems are not in White.\n4. Therefore the gems must be in **Black**.\n5. With gems in Black, K2’s first part is true. K2 also requires the neighbor White box to be true, so K2 requires W1 true. Thus **K2 = T** and **W1 = T**.\n6. If K1 were true along with W1 and K2, Black would be all-true, so W1 (“gems are in a false box”) would be false—contradiction. So **K1 = F**.\n7. This gives exactly two true statements total (W1 and K2), making K1 false as stated.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.304, "ast_prob": 0.44, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00535", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n  [B2] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location determine K1 and B2 directly:\n- K1 = “the other two boxes are empty” is true iff the gems are in BLACK.\n- B2 = “a box next to the black box has the gems” is true iff the gems are in WHITE.\n\nAlso, B1 says the WHITE box is true, so B1 = W1.  \nW1 says all top statements are true, so W1 = (B1 and W1 and K1). Substituting B1 = W1 gives W1 = (W1 and K1). Thus if K1 is false, W1 must be false.\n\nCheck gem locations:\n- If gems are WHITE: K1=F, W1=F, B1=F, B2=T. White and Black are all-false, Blue is mixed, so no all-true box — invalid.\n- If gems are BLUE: K1=F, W1=F, B1=F, B2=F. All three boxes are all-false, so no all-true box — invalid.\n- If gems are BLACK: K1=T, B2=F. W1 cannot be true, because then Blue would be mixed, White true, Black true, giving no all-false box. So W1=F, B1=F. Then Blue is all-false, White is all-false, and Black is all-true — valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; B2=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.481, "ast_prob": 0.253, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00536", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [W2] THE BLACK BOX IS BLUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 refers to a statement above it, but there is no statement above B1, so B1 is false.  \nW1 says the blue box is the middle box, but blue is left, so W1 is false.  \nW2 says the black box is blue, but black is black, so W2 is false.  \n\nThus BLUE and WHITE are both all-false boxes. To satisfy the rule that at least one box has all statements true, BLACK must be the all-true box. Therefore K1 must be true, meaning the gems are in the BLACK box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.366, "ast_prob": 0.181, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00537", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n  [W2] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location be variable.\n\n- W1 is true iff the gems are in BLACK, because then the other two boxes relative to BLACK (BLUE and WHITE) are empty.\n- B1 is true iff the WHITE box is all-true, since WHITE is the only box next to BLUE.\n- K1 is true only if B1 and W1 are true, because it claims all top statements are true.\n\nTesting gem locations:\n- If gems are in WHITE or BLUE, W1 is false, so B1 cannot make BLUE all-true, and K1 also becomes false. No all-true box exists, violating the rules.\n- Therefore gems must be in BLACK.\n\nWith gems in BLACK:\n- W1 = T.\n- If W2 were false, then WHITE would have only one false statement, BLUE would have B1=false, and BLACK would have K1=false; no box would have two false statements, making W2 vacuously true — contradiction. So W2 = T.\n- Then B1 = T because WHITE is all-true.\n- K1 cannot be T, because that would make every box all-true and violate the requirement of an all-false box. So K1 = F.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.643, "ast_prob": 0.433, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00538", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [B2] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Blue is the left box, so B2 is always false.  \nW1 and K1 are both true exactly when the White box is empty, i.e. when the gems are not in White.\n\n- If gems were in Blue: Blue would be mixed, White and Black all-true, but no all-false box → invalid.\n- If gems were in White: all boxes would be all-false, but no all-true box → invalid.\n- If gems were in Black: Blue is all-false, White and Black are all-true → valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; B2=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.421, "ast_prob": 0.239, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00539", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n  [B2] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT AT MOST THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(F\\) be the number of false statements among the 4 total statements.\n\n- B2 says: “It is not true that at most three statements are false.”  \n  So B2 would be true only if all 4 statements were false. But if B2 is true, it is not false, so there cannot be 4 false statements. Therefore **B2 = F**.\n\n- The BLACK box is on the right, so the only box next to it is WHITE.  \n  Thus W1 is true iff WHITE contains the gems. Also, B1 says “a box next to BLACK is true,” which refers to WHITE; since WHITE has only one statement, **B1 = W1**.\n\n- BLUE contains B2 = F, so BLUE cannot be the all-true box. Therefore the required all-true box must be WHITE or BLACK.\n\n- If gems were in WHITE: W1 = T and B1 = T, so WHITE is all-true. Then K1 would be true because WHITE has no false statements and has the gems, making BLACK all-true too; but then there would not be a unique “box with no false statements,” creating a contradiction. So gems are not in WHITE.\n\n- If gems were in BLUE: W1 = F, B1 = F, B2 = F, so BLUE and WHITE are all-false. The only possible all-true box would be BLACK, requiring K1 = T. But K1 would then say the all-true box has the gems, which is false because the gems are in BLUE. So K1 = F, leaving no all-true box. So gems are not in BLUE.\n\n- Therefore gems must be in BLACK. Then W1 = F, B1 = F, B2 = F. BLACK has K1 = T because BLACK is the box with no false statements and it has the gems. This satisfies the rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; B2=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.29, "ast_prob": 0.27, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00541", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n  [B2] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EITHER THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS TRUE OR THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let:\n- B1 = Blue box is empty (gems not in Blue)\n- B2 = A box next to White contains the gems (gems in Blue or Black, i.e. not White)\n- W1 = White box contains the gems\n- K1 = (the gem-box is all-true) OR (Blue box is empty)\n\nTest each possible gem location:\n\n1. **Gems in BLUE**:  \n   B1 = False, B2 = True → Blue mixed.  \n   W1 = False → White all-false.  \n   K1 = False → Black all-false.  \n   No all-true box → invalid.\n\n2. **Gems in WHITE**:  \n   B1 = True, B2 = False → Blue mixed.  \n   W1 = True → White all-true.  \n   K1 = True → Black all-true.  \n   No all-false box → invalid.\n\n3. **Gems in BLACK**:  \n   B1 = True, B2 = True → Blue all-true.  \n   W1 = False → White all-false.  \n   K1 = True because Blue is empty → Black all-true.  \n   Satisfies all rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.252, "ast_prob": 0.17, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00542", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE AND THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n  [K2] EITHER THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS OR THE BLUE BOX IS BLUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K2 is true because “the blue box is blue” is true, so the second disjunct makes K2 true.\n\nAssume gems are in BLUE: then B1 is true. If K1 were true, then W1 would also be true, making every box all-true, violating the rule that at least one box is all-false. If K1 were false, then BLUE would be the unique all-true box containing the gems, which would make K1 true—a contradiction. So gems cannot be in BLUE.\n\nAssume gems are in WHITE: then B1 is false and W1 is false, so BLUE and WHITE are all-false. To satisfy the all-true rule, BLACK must be all-true, so K1 must be true. But then the only all-true box is BLACK while the gems are in WHITE, making K1 false—contradiction. So gems cannot be in WHITE.\n\nTherefore gems are in BLACK. Then B1 is false because BLACK is not empty. W1 is false because BLUE does not contain the gems. K2 is true. BLACK is the only box with no false statements and it contains the gems, so K1 is true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.649, "ast_prob": 0.488, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00543", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE\n  [B2] THE BLACK BOX HAS EXACTLY ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let K1 be false: the BLACK box contains only this one statement, so there is no statement above it for K1 to refer to.\n\n- Since K1 is false, the BLACK box has 0 true statements, so B2 (“the BLACK box has exactly one true statement”) is false.\n- If W1 were false, then the WHITE box would be all-false and BLUE would be mixed, giving no all-true box — violating the rules.\n- Therefore W1 must be true, meaning the BLACK box contains the gems.\n- With W1 true, B1 (“it is not true that all statements on WHITE are true”) is false.\n- K1 remains false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; B2=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.661, "ast_prob": 0.245, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00544", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [W2] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let W1 be true because the White box is indeed the middle box.\n\n- If gems were in BLUE: B1=false, so Blue would be all-false; W2=false, so White would be mixed. If K1 were true, Black would be all-true but would not have the gems, contradiction. If K1 were false, there would be no all-true box, violating the rule. So gems are not in Blue.\n- If gems were in WHITE: B1=false, so Blue all-false; W1=true and W2=true, so White all-true. If K1 were false, Black all-false and White would be the unique all-true box with gems, making K1 true, contradiction. If K1 were true, Black would also be all-true, so there would be multiple all-true boxes and Black would be all-true without gems, making K1 false. So gems are not in White.\n- Therefore gems are in BLACK: B1=true; W1=true; W2=true. Black cannot be all-true because Blue and White are already all-true and empty, so K1 must be false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.781, "ast_prob": 0.116, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00545", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [B2] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false because the blue box is on the left, not the middle.  \nW1 is false because there is only one gem, so the two boxes next to the white box cannot both contain gems.  \nThus the white box is all-false.  \nThe blue box cannot be all-true because B1 is false, so the required all-true box must be the black box.  \nTherefore K1 must be true, meaning the box with no false statements has the gems. That box is black.  \nSo the black box contains the gems, which also means the white box is empty, making B2 true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; B2=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.406, "ast_prob": 0.29, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00546", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS BLUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n  [K2] THE WHITE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location be exactly one box.\n\n- W1 says “The BLACK box is BLUE.” The BLACK box is physically black, not blue, so **W1 = F**.\n- B1 says “The top statement of each box is TRUE.” That would require B1, W1, and K1 all true. Since W1 is false, B1’s claim is false, so **B1 = F**.\n- Therefore the BLUE box is all-false, and the WHITE box is also all-false.\n- To satisfy the rule that at least one box is all-true, the BLACK box must be all-true.\n- K1 = “The other two boxes are empty” is true exactly when the gems are in BLACK.\n- K2 = “The WHITE box is empty” is also true when the gems are in BLACK.\n- So the gems are in BLACK, and both K1 and K2 are true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.247, "ast_prob": 0.47, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00547", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n  [W2] THE BLUE BOX IS BLUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n\n- The BLACK box is on the right, not the middle, so **B1 = F**.\n- The BLUE box is indeed blue, so **W2 = T**.\n- W1 says the two boxes other than BLACK are empty, which is true exactly when the gems are in the BLACK box.\n- K1 says the WHITE box has at least two true statements. Since W2 is already true, K1 is true iff W1 is also true.\n- If gems are in BLUE or WHITE, then W1 is false, so K1 is false, leaving no all-true box.\n- If gems are in BLACK, then W1 is true and W2 is true, so WHITE is all-true; K1 is true, so BLACK is all-true; and B1 is false, so BLUE is all-false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.367, "ast_prob": 0.361, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00548", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [B2] THIS BOX IS BLACK\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS BLUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the boxes be in order: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right).\n\n- B2 says “THIS BOX IS BLACK” on the BLUE box, so B2 is FALSE.\n- W1 says “THE BLACK BOX IS BLUE,” so W1 is FALSE.\n- Since B2 and W1 are false, neither BLUE nor WHITE can be the all-true box. Therefore BLACK must be the all-true box.\n- BLACK contains only K1, so K1 must be TRUE.\n- K1 says the other two boxes are empty, so the gems must be in BLACK.\n- With gems in BLACK, B1 (“a box next to this box contains gems”) refers to WHITE, which is empty, so B1 is FALSE.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; B2=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.352, "ast_prob": 0.285, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00550", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n  [B2] THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX HAS AT MOST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let W1 be evaluated first. BLUE has exactly two statements, so “at most two true statements” is always true. Thus W1 = T, so WHITE is all-true.\n\nThen B2 says “THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE,” which is true. So B2 = T.\n\nSince BLUE has B2 true, BLUE cannot be all-false. WHITE is all-true, so WHITE cannot be all-false. Therefore BLACK must be the all-false box, so K1 = F.\n\nK1 says the boxes next to BLACK contain the gems. Since K1 is false and there is exactly one gem box, the gems must be in BLACK.\n\nBLACK is all-false, so B1 (“the gems are in a box that is false”) is true. Thus B1 = T.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.671, "ast_prob": 0.37, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00551", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX HAS AT MOST ONE TRUE STATEMENT\n  [W2] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Black is the rightmost box, so the only box “next to” Black is White.  \nThus B1 and K1 both mean “White contains the gems.”\n\n- W1 says Black has at most one true statement. Black has only K1, so it always has either 0 or 1 true statement → W1 is always true.\n- W2 says Blue is empty, meaning the gems are not in Blue.\n\nCheck the three possible gem locations:\n\n1. Gem = BLUE:  \n   B1=F, K1=F, W1=T, W2=F.  \n   Blue and Black are all-false, but no box is all-true → invalid.\n\n2. Gem = WHITE:  \n   B1=T, K1=T, W1=T, W2=T.  \n   All boxes are all-true, but no box is all-false → invalid.\n\n3. Gem = BLACK:  \n   B1=F, K1=F, W1=T, W2=T.  \n   Blue is all-false, Black is all-false, and White is all-true → valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.252, "ast_prob": 0.475, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00552", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [W2] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Positions: BLUE=left, WHITE=middle, BLACK=right.\n\n- W2 is false, because the blue box is not the middle box.\n- W1 and K1 both mean “the white box contains gems,” since only the white box is next to the black box.\n- B1 is true iff the blue box does not contain the gems.\n\nCheck each possible gem location:\n\n- Gem in BLUE: B1=F, W1=F, W2=F, K1=F → no box is all-true, invalid.\n- Gem in WHITE: B1=T, W1=T, W2=F, K1=T → no box is all-false, invalid.\n- Gem in BLACK: B1=T, W1=F, W2=F, K1=F → BLUE is all-true; WHITE and BLACK are all-false, valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.305, "ast_prob": 0.116, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00553", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n  [K2] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location determine the statements:\n\n- B1: “It is not true that White and Black are empty” means Blue does **not** contain the gems, so B1 = true iff gem is in White or Black.\n- W1: “Boxes next to the Blue box contain gems” refers to the White box only, so W1 = true iff gem is in White.\n- K1: “The Black box is empty” is true iff gem is not in Black.\n- K2: “Boxes next to the Black box contain gems” refers to the White box only, so K2 = true iff gem is in White.\n\nTest gem locations:\n\n- If gem = BLUE: B1=F, W1=F, K1=T, K2=F → no box is all-true. Invalid.\n- If gem = WHITE: B1=T, W1=T, K1=T, K2=T → all boxes are all-true; no box is all-false. Invalid.\n- If gem = BLACK: B1=T, W1=F, K1=F, K2=F → Blue is all-true; White and Black are all-false. Valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.33, "ast_prob": 0.167, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00554", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLUE BOX ARE EMPTY\n  [B2] BOXES NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] AT LEAST ONE STATEMENT IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location be G.\n\n- Since BLUE is leftmost, the only box physically next to it is WHITE. BLACK is not next to BLUE.\n- B1: “The other two boxes relative to BLUE are empty” means WHITE and BLACK are empty, so B1 is true iff G = BLUE.\n- B2: “Boxes next to this box contain gems” refers to WHITE only, so B2 is true iff G = WHITE.\n- K1: “A box next to the BLUE box is true” refers to WHITE only, so K1 is true iff WHITE is all-true. Since WHITE has only W1, K1 = W1.\n- W1: “At least one statement is true” is true in the valid solution because W1 and K1 are true.\n\nCheck gem locations:\n- If G = BLUE: B1=T, B2=F, so BLUE is mixed; W1 and K1 are true, leaving no all-false box. Invalid.\n- If G = WHITE: B1=F, B2=T, so BLUE is mixed; W1 and K1 are true, leaving no all-false box. Invalid.\n- If G = BLACK: B1=F, B2=F, so BLUE is all-false. Let W1=T and K1=T, making WHITE and BLACK all-true. This satisfies at least one all-true and at least one all-false. Valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; B2=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.566, "ast_prob": 0.162, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00555", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS BLUE\n  [B2] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE WHITE BOX ARE TRUE AND THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is true because the left box is indeed BLUE.\n\nLet B2 = “B1 and W1 and K1 are all true.” Since B1 is true, B2 is true iff both W1 and K1 are true.\n\nW1 says: “All statements on the WHITE box are true and the top statement of each box is true.” Since W1 is the only statement on the WHITE box, W1 is true iff W1 and B1 and K1 are all true. With B1 true, this means W1 is true iff W1 and K1 are true. So if K1 is false, W1 must be false; if K1 is true, W1 can be either true or false.\n\nIf K1 were false, then W1 would be false and B2 would be false, giving no all-true box. That violates the rule, so K1 must be true.\n\nWith K1 true, if W1 were true then B2 would be true, making all three boxes all-true, with no all-false box. That violates the rule, so W1 must be false. Then B2 is false.\n\nNow:\n- BLUE: B1 true, B2 false → mixed.\n- WHITE: W1 false → all-false.\n- BLACK: K1 true → all-true.\n\nK1 says the box with no false statements has the gems. That box is BLACK, so the gems are in BLACK.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; B2=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.584, "ast_prob": 0.498, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00556", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BOX WITH TWO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n  [W2] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Since only the WHITE box has two statements, “the box with two false statements” must be WHITE. So W1 says: “WHITE has both statements false and contains the gems.” If W1 were true, it would require W1 itself to be false, so W1 cannot be true. Therefore W1 = F.\n\nW2 is true only if gems are in WHITE.  \nB1 says the other two boxes relative to BLACK are empty, so B1 is true exactly when gems are in BLACK.  \nK1 says all statements on BLUE are true, so K1 = B1.\n\nFor at least one box to be all-true, it cannot be WHITE because W1 is always false. Thus BLUE or BLACK must be all-true, which requires B1 = K1 = T. That means gems must be in BLACK.\n\nThen:\n- B1 = T\n- K1 = T\n- W1 = F\n- W2 = F\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.508, "ast_prob": 0.391, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00557", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THIS BOX ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] EXACTLY THREE STATEMENTS ARE FALSE\n  [K2] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K2 is always false because the Blue box is on the left, not the middle.\n\nLet the truth values be B1, W1, K1, K2. K2 = F.\n\nW1 means “Blue and White are empty,” which is equivalent to “the gems are in the Black box.”\n\nK1 says exactly 3 of the 4 statements are false. Working through the self-reference, the only consistent options are:\n- B1=F, W1=F, K1=T, K2=F\n- B1=T, W1=T, K1=F, K2=F\n\nIn the first option, no box is all-true, which violates the rule that at least one box must have all statements true.\n\nSo the valid solution is:\n- B1 = T, W1 = T, K1 = F, K2 = F\n- W1 is true, so the gems are in the Black box.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.685, "ast_prob": 0.238, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00559", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS AND ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n  [W2] AT MOST TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(G\\) be the gem location.\n\n- \\(B1\\) is true iff \\(G=\\text{BLUE}\\).\n- \\(W1\\) says: “The box next to BLUE contains the gems AND all statements on BLUE are true.” The only box next to BLUE is WHITE. So \\(W1\\) would require \\(G=\\text{WHITE}\\) and \\(G=\\text{BLUE}\\), impossible. Thus \\(W1\\) is always false. Therefore WHITE can never be an all-true box.\n- If \\(G=\\text{WHITE}\\), then WHITE contains false statement \\(W1\\), so \\(K1\\) would be false, leaving no all-true box. So \\(G\\ne\\text{WHITE}\\).\n- If \\(G=\\text{BLUE}\\), then \\(B1\\) true and \\(K1\\) true. To have an all-false box, WHITE would need \\(W2\\) false. But then true statements are \\(B1, K1\\) only, so exactly 2 statements are true, making \\(W2\\) true — contradiction. So \\(G\\ne\\text{BLUE}\\).\n- Therefore \\(G=\\text{BLACK}\\). Then \\(B1=F\\), \\(W1=F\\). \\(K1\\) must be true, otherwise there would be no all-true box. With \\(K1=T\\), if \\(W2=F\\) there is only 1 true statement, making \\(W2\\)'s claim true — contradiction. So \\(W2=T\\).\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; W2=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.703, "ast_prob": 0.39, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00560", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] AT MOST THREE STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT MOST TWO TRUE STATEMENTS\n  [K2] A BOX NEXT TO THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the truth values be determined by the gem location and by self-reference.\n\n- K1 is always true: the White box has only one statement, so it cannot have more than two true statements.\n- K2 is true exactly when the White box has the gems, since White is the only box next to Black.\n- W1 is true exactly when Black is empty, i.e. when the gems are not in Black.\n- B1 says at most three statements total are true. B1 cannot be false, because if it were false then all four statements would have to be true, making B1 true — contradiction. So B1 is true.\n\nSince B1 is true, not all four statements can be true. Thus at least one of W1, K2 must be false.\n\nIf the gems were in Blue: W1=true, K2=false, B1=true, K1=true. Then Blue all-true, White all-true, Black mixed — no all-false box, violating the rules.\n\nIf the gems were in White: W1=true and K2=true, making all four statements true, which contradicts B1.\n\nSo the gems must be in Black. Then W1=false, K2=false, B1=true, K1=true. This gives Blue all-true, White all-false, Black mixed — valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; K1=T; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.671, "ast_prob": 0.496, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00561", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE WHITE BOX HAS AT LEAST TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n  [W2] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX HAS AT MOST TWO FALSE STATEMENTS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "K1 is true because a one-statement box always has at most two false statements.  \nW1 must be false, since if W1 were true, both White statements would have to be false, a contradiction. Therefore W2 must be true.  \nWith Black all-true and White mixed, the required all-false box must be Blue, so B1 is false.  \nThen the only box with no false statements is Black, and W2 says that box has the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; W2=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.619, "ast_prob": 0.397, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00562", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] EXACTLY TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [W2] THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Step-by-step:\n\n1. The boxes are fixed: BLUE is left, WHITE is middle, BLACK is right.  \n   - W1: “The blue box is the middle box” is FALSE.  \n   - W2: “This box is the middle box” refers to the WHITE box, which is middle, so W2 is TRUE.  \n   Thus the WHITE box is a mixed box.\n\n2. Since the WHITE box is mixed, the all-true box and all-false box must come from BLUE and BLACK.  \n   - BLUE has only B1, so BLUE is all-true iff B1 is TRUE, all-false iff B1 is FALSE.  \n   - BLACK has only K1, so BLACK is all-true iff K1 is TRUE, all-false iff K1 is FALSE.  \n   Therefore B1 and K1 must have opposite truth values.\n\n3. Test B1 = TRUE and K1 = FALSE:  \n   Truth values are: B1=T, W1=F, W2=T, K1=F.  \n   Exactly two statements are true, so B1 is correctly TRUE. Consistent.\n\n4. Test B1 = FALSE and K1 = TRUE:  \n   Truth values would be: B1=F, W1=F, W2=T, K1=T.  \n   That would make exactly two statements true, so B1 would be TRUE, contradiction. Invalid.\n\n5. Therefore B1=T and K1=F. Since K1 says “The black box is empty” is FALSE, the BLACK box is not empty, so it must contain the gems.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.422, "ast_prob": 0.188, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00563", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS BLUE\n  [B2] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX HAS EXACTLY ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false: the black box is not blue; it is black.  \nW1 is false: there is no statement above it in the white box, so “the statement above this one is true” is not satisfied.  \n\nThus the WHITE box is all-false.  \nSince at least one box must be all-true, and the BLUE box cannot be all-true because B1 is false, the BLACK box must be all-true. Therefore K1 is true.  \n\nK1 says the BLUE box has exactly one false statement. Since B1 is already false, B2 must be true.  \nB2 says the box with no false statements has the gems. The only all-true box is BLACK, so the gems are in BLACK.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; B2=T; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.734, "ast_prob": 0.296, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00564", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n  [B2] THE WHITE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS WHITE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is always false: the BLUE box is left, not middle.  \nK1 is always false: the BLUE box is not WHITE.  \nB2 is true only if gems are in WHITE.  \nW1 is true only if gems are in BLACK, because then the other two boxes relative to BLACK are empty.\n\nTest gem locations:\n- If gems are in BLUE: no box is all-true → invalid.\n- If gems are in WHITE: no box is all-true → invalid.\n- If gems are in BLACK: WHITE is all-true, BLUE is all-false, BLACK is all-false → valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; B2=F; W1=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.739, "ast_prob": 0.377, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00565", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS AND THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n  [B2] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(G\\) be the gem location.\n\n- \\(W1\\) says “This box is empty,” so \\(W1 = (G \\neq \\text{WHITE})\\).\n- \\(B2\\) says “Blue contains the gems,” so \\(B2 = (G = \\text{BLUE})\\).\n- \\(B1\\) says “Blue contains the gems AND the White box is true.” Since White has only \\(W1\\), this is \\((G = \\text{BLUE}) \\land W1\\). Because \\(G\\) cannot be both Blue and White, \\(B1 = B2\\).\n- \\(K1\\) says a box next to Black is true. The only box next to Black is White, so \\(K1 = W1\\).\n\nCheck each possible gem location:\n\n- If \\(G = \\text{BLUE}\\): \\(B1=T, B2=T, W1=T, K1=T\\). No box is all-false, violating the rule.\n- If \\(G = \\text{WHITE}\\): \\(B1=F, B2=F, W1=F, K1=F\\). No box is all-true, violating the rule.\n- If \\(G = \\text{BLACK}\\): \\(B1=F, B2=F, W1=T, K1=T\\). Blue is all-false; White and Black are all-true. This satisfies both rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; B2=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.375, "ast_prob": 0.415, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00566", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS WHITE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n  [K2] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The statement B1 says “THE BLUE BOX IS WHITE,” which is false because the blue box is blue, not white.  \nSo the BLUE box is all-false, satisfying the required all-false box.\n\nSince W1 says “THE BLUE BOX IS TRUE,” and the blue box is not all-true because B1 is false, W1 is also false.  \nThus the WHITE box is also all-false.\n\nAt least one box must be all-true. Since BLUE and WHITE are all-false, the BLACK box must be all-true.  \nTherefore K1 and K2 must both be true.\n\nK1 true means the other two boxes relative to BLACK are empty, so the gems must be in the BLACK box.  \nK2 true is consistent because the BLACK box itself is next to the WHITE box and is true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; K1=T; K2=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.632, "ast_prob": 0.168, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00567", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n  [W2] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "A \"false\" box is taken to mean a box that is not all-true (it contains at least one false statement).\n\n- If gems were in BLUE: B1 would say “BLUE is false,” but BLUE’s only statement is B1, so B1 would have to equal its own negation — impossible.\n- If gems were in WHITE: W1 would be true. W2 would say “WHITE is false,” meaning not both W1 and W2 are true. Since W1 is true, this becomes W2 = not W2 — impossible.\n- Therefore gems must be in BLACK.\n\nThen K1 is true, so BLACK is all-true; thus B1 and W2 are false. W1 is false because the only box next to BLACK is WHITE, which is empty.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.685, "ast_prob": 0.119, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00568", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE BLACK BOX ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES RELATIVE TO THE WHITE BOX ARE EMPTY\n  [W2] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem location be one of BLUE, WHITE, BLACK.\n\nInterpret statements as:\n- B1: “BLUE and WHITE are both empty” → true iff gem is in BLACK.\n- W1: “BLUE and BLACK are both empty” → true iff gem is in WHITE.\n- W2: “BLUE contains the gems” → true iff gem is in BLUE.\n- K1: “the boxes next to WHITE, i.e. BLUE and BLACK, both contain gems” → always false, since exactly one box contains the gems.\n\nTest possible gem locations:\n\n1. Gem in BLUE:  \n   B1=F, W1=F, W2=T, K1=F  \n   Boxes: BLUE all-false, WHITE mixed, BLACK all-false → no all-true box → invalid.\n\n2. Gem in WHITE:  \n   B1=F, W1=T, W2=F, K1=F  \n   Boxes: BLUE all-false, WHITE mixed, BLACK all-false → no all-true box → invalid.\n\n3. Gem in BLACK:  \n   B1=T, W1=F, W2=F, K1=F  \n   Boxes: BLUE all-true, WHITE all-false, BLACK all-false → satisfies at least one all-true and at least one all-false → valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; W1=F; W2=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.254, "ast_prob": 0.464, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00569", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY AND THE BLUE BOX HAS AT LEAST ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n  [B2] THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS AND THE BLUE BOX IS WHITE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] IT IS NOT TRUE THAT THIS BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THIS BOX IS NOT EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B2 must be false: the Blue box is not white, so the conjunction in B2 is false.  \nBecause B2 is false, the Blue box has at least one false statement, so B1 reduces to “the other two boxes are empty,” which is true only if the gems are in Blue.  \nW1 is false: the White box is the middle box, so “this box is the middle box” is true, making “it is not true…” false.  \nThus White is all-false, satisfying the all-false requirement.  \nTo also satisfy the all-true requirement, Black must be all-true, so K1 must be true, meaning Black is not empty and therefore contains the gems.  \nThen B1 is false because Black is not empty.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; B2=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.553, "ast_prob": 0.487, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00570", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE GEMS ARE IN A BOX THAT IS FALSE\n  [W2] THIS BOX HAS AT LEAST ONE FALSE STATEMENT\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "W2 cannot be false, because if it were false then White would have no false statements, making W2 true—contradiction. So W2 is true, and since White has at least one false statement, W1 must be false.\n\nThus White is mixed. Therefore Blue and Black must supply the required all-true and all-false boxes. K1 says a box next to White is true; the boxes next to White are Blue and Black. Since one of them must be true, K1 is true, making Black all-true. So Blue must be all-false, making B1 false.\n\nW1 is false, so the gems are not in a false box. Blue is false and White is mixed/false, so the gems must be in Black.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; W2=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.553, "ast_prob": 0.455, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00571", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLACK BOX IS EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] EITHER THE BLUE BOX IS THE MIDDLE BOX OR THE BLACK BOX IS BLACK\n  [W2] AT MOST TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let the gem locations be considered one by one.\n\n- W1 is true because the black box is black, regardless of gem location.\n- K1 is true only if the gems are in WHITE, since WHITE is the only box next to BLUE.\n- B1 is true unless the gems are in BLACK.\n\nCheck gem in BLUE:\nB1=T, W1=T, K1=F. If W2=T, there would be 3 true statements, contradicting W2. If W2=F, there would be only 2 true statements, making W2 true. No consistent assignment.\n\nCheck gem in WHITE:\nB1=T, W1=T, K1=T. If W2=T, there would be 4 true statements, contradiction. If W2=F, there are 3 true statements, consistent. However, all boxes are either all-true or mixed, with no all-false box, violating the rule that at least one box is all-false.\n\nCheck gem in BLACK:\nB1=F, W1=T, K1=F. If W2=T, total true statements = 2, which satisfies “at most two true.” If W2=F, total true = 1, but then W2 would actually be true, contradiction. So W2=T.  \nBoxes: BLUE all-false, WHITE all-true, BLACK all-false. This satisfies all rules.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; W2=T; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.279, "ast_prob": 0.282, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00572", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE BLUE BOX IS BLACK\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLACK BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] A BOX NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX IS TRUE\n  [K2] BOXES NEXT TO THE WHITE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>; K2=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, K1, K2 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=1, BLACK=2). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; K1=T; K2=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false: the BLUE box is not the BLACK box.  \nSo the BLUE box is all-false.\n\nK2 says the boxes next to WHITE — i.e. BLUE and BLACK — contain gems. Since exactly one box contains gems, both cannot contain gems, so K2 is false.  \nTherefore the BLACK box cannot be all-true.\n\nAt least one box must be all-true. BLUE is all-false and BLACK cannot be all-true, so WHITE must be all-true. Thus W1 is true, meaning the BLACK box contains the gems.\n\nK1 says a box next to WHITE is true. The boxes next to WHITE are BLUE and BLACK. BLUE is all-false, and BLACK has K2 false, so BLACK is not all-true. Therefore K1 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=T; K1=F; K2=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.305, "ast_prob": 0.396, "max_depth": 2}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00573", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n  [B2] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] ALL STATEMENTS ON THE BLUE BOX ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is the top statement on the BLUE box, so “the statement above this one” does not exist. Therefore B1 is false.\n\nSince B1 is false, W1 (“All statements on the BLUE box are true”) is also false.\n\nThe BLUE box cannot be all-true, and the WHITE box cannot be all-true. To satisfy “at least one box has all statements true,” the BLACK box must be all-true, so K1 must be true.\n\nK1 says the other two boxes are empty, which means the gems must be in the BLACK box. Then BLUE and WHITE are empty, so B2 is false.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; B2=F; W1=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.355, "ast_prob": 0.351, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00574", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE STATEMENT ABOVE THIS ONE IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n  [W2] AT MOST TWO STATEMENTS ARE TRUE\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BOX WITH NO FALSE STATEMENTS HAS THE GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "B1 is false, because there is no statement above it in the BLUE box.  \nSince W1 says B1, W1, and K1 are all true, and B1 is false, W1 must be false.  \nWith B1 and W1 false, only W2 and K1 could possibly be true, so at most two statements are true; therefore W2 is true.  \nBLUE has a false statement, and WHITE has a false statement, so the only box that can have no false statements is BLACK. Since at least one box must be all-true, K1 must be true.  \nK1 says the box with no false statements has the gems, so the gems are in BLACK.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; W2=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.712, "ast_prob": 0.221, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00575", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THIS BOX IS EMPTY\n  [B2] THIS BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] BOXES NEXT TO THE BLACK BOX CONTAIN GEMS\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(G\\) be the box with the gems.\n\n- Blue is leftmost, so the only box next to Blue is White. Thus W1 is true iff White has the gems.\n- Black is rightmost, so the only box next to Black is White. Thus K1 is true iff White has the gems.\n- So W1 and K1 always have the same truth value, both true exactly when \\(G = \\text{WHITE}\\).\n\nIf White had the gems, then White and Black would both be all-true boxes. That would force Blue to be all-false to satisfy the “at least one all-false” rule. But if White has the gems, Blue is empty, so B1 would be true, making Blue not all-false. Contradiction.\n\nTherefore White does not have the gems, so W1 and K1 are both false. Then White and Black are both all-false boxes, satisfying the all-false requirement. To satisfy the all-true requirement, Blue must be all-true, so B1 and B2 are both true.\n\nB1 true means Blue is empty, so the gems are not in Blue. Since they are not in White either, they must be in Black.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=T; B2=T; W1=F; K1=F"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.681, "ast_prob": 0.453, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00576", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] A BOX NEXT TO THE BLUE BOX CONTAINS THE GEMS\n  [B2] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE BLUE BOX IS EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; B2=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, B2, W1, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=2, WHITE=1, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; B2=T; W1=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(G\\) be the gem location.\n\n- \\(B1\\): \"A box next to the blue box contains the gems.\" The only box next to BLUE is WHITE, so \\(B1 = (G = \\text{WHITE})\\).\n- \\(B2\\): \"The other two boxes are empty.\" This is true only if the gems are in BLUE, so \\(B2 = (G = \\text{BLUE})\\).\n- \\(W1\\) and \\(K1\\): \"The blue box is empty.\" These are true iff \\(G \\neq \\text{BLUE}\\).\n\nCheck cases:\n\n1. \\(G = \\text{BLUE}\\):  \n   \\(B1=F,\\ B2=T,\\ W1=F,\\ K1=F\\)  \n   BLUE is mixed, WHITE all false, BLACK all false → no all-true box. Invalid.\n\n2. \\(G = \\text{WHITE}\\):  \n   \\(B1=T,\\ B2=F,\\ W1=T,\\ K1=T\\)  \n   BLUE is mixed, WHITE all true, BLACK all true → no all-false box. Invalid.\n\n3. \\(G = \\text{BLACK}\\):  \n   \\(B1=F,\\ B2=F,\\ W1=T,\\ K1=T\\)  \n   BLUE all false, WHITE all true, BLACK all true → satisfies at least one all-true and at least one all-false. Valid.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; B2=F; W1=T; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.259, "ast_prob": 0.297, "max_depth": 1}}
{"id": "direct_ft_train_00578", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are solving a logic puzzle from the game \"Blue Prince\".\n\nThere are three boxes: BLUE (left), WHITE (middle), BLACK (right). Each box displays one or more statements.\n\nThe rules that make the puzzle uniquely solvable:\n- EXACTLY ONE of the three boxes contains the gems; the other two are empty.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements TRUE.\n- AT LEAST ONE box has ALL of its statements FALSE.\n- The remaining box may be all-true, all-false, or a mix.\n- A statement's truth value is determined by the actual state of the world (gem location and the truth values of every statement, including self- and cross-references).\n\nYour task: by logical deduction, determine (a) which box contains the gems, and (b) the truth value of EVERY statement.\n\nHere is the puzzle:\n\nBLUE (left) box:\n  [B1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n\nWHITE (middle) box:\n  [W1] THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE\n  [W2] THE BLUE BOX IS BLACK\n\nBLACK (right) box:\n  [K1] THE OTHER TWO BOXES ARE EMPTY\n\nThink step by step. Then, on the VERY LAST line, output your final answer in EXACTLY this one-line format (nothing after it):\n\nFINAL: gem=<BLUE|WHITE|BLACK>; B1=<T/F>; W1=<T/F>; W2=<T/F>; K1=<T/F>\n\nLabel EVERY statement id, and ONLY these: B1, W1, W2, K1 (statement counts: BLUE=1, WHITE=2, BLACK=1). One T or F per id.\nExample (format only): FINAL: gem=WHITE; B1=T; W1=T; W2=T; K1=T"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Let \\(S\\) be the shared statement “THE TOP STATEMENT OF EACH BOX IS TRUE.”\n\n- \\(B1 = S\\) and \\(W1 = S\\), since B1 and W1 say the same thing.\n- \\(S\\) is true only if \\(B1\\), \\(W1\\), and \\(K1\\) are all true.\n- Since \\(B1 = S\\) and \\(W1 = S\\), the only possible self-consistent options are:\n  - \\(B1 = W1 = K1 = T\\), or\n  - \\(B1 = W1 = F\\) with \\(K1\\) determined separately.\n- \\(K1 = T\\) iff the gems are in the BLACK box, because then the other two boxes are empty.\n- \\(W2 =\\) “THE BLUE BOX IS BLACK” is always false, since the BLUE box is blue, not black.\n\nIf the gems were in BLUE or WHITE, then \\(K1 = F\\), forcing \\(B1 = W1 = F\\), and all boxes would be all-false, violating the requirement that at least one box is all-true.\n\nSo the gems must be in BLACK, making \\(K1 = T\\). Then \\(B1 = W1 = F\\) is the only option that also gives at least one all-false box: BLUE and WHITE are all-false, while BLACK is all-true.\n\nFINAL: gem=BLACK; B1=F; W1=F; W2=F; K1=T"}], "metadata": {"n_statements": 4, "gem_box": "BLACK", "relational_ratio": 0.549, "ast_prob": 0.422, "max_depth": 1}}
